(323, 2, 2, 3, 'Managing LLM Risks in Academic Publishing', 'managing-llm-risks-in-academic-publishing', 'Learn how academic publishers can manage LLM risks through responsible AI, human oversight, transparency, governance, and risk-based controls.', '[\"LLM\",\"Large Language Models\",\"Academic Publishing\",\"Generative AI\",\"AI Governance\",\"Responsible AI\",\"AI Ethics\",\"Scholarly Publishing\",\"AI Risk Management\",\"Research Integrity\",\"AI in Publishing\",\"Human Oversight\",\"Machine Learning\",\"Editorial Technology\"]', 'banner_image-1786362885086-265337310.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Managing LLM Risks in Academic Publishing: A Practical Framework for Responsible AI</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly changing the way academic publishers, researchers, editors and technology teams create, process and distribute scholarly information. Their ability to summarize complex research, assist with translation, improve accessibility and automate repetitive processes can deliver significant operational benefits.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, introducing LLMs into academic publishing also creates risks that extend far beyond technical accuracy. Academic publishing is responsible for maintaining the integrity of the scientific record, protecting authors, supporting fair evaluation and ensuring that research is represented accurately. If an AI system introduces bias, fabricates information, misrepresents research or influences an important editorial decision, the consequences can affect individual careers as well as the wider body of knowledge.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Historically, technology and editorial leadership have often approached these challenges from different perspectives. Editorial teams have focused primarily on research integrity, quality and fairness, while technology teams have concentrated on efficiency, scalability, cost and technical capability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The increasing adoption of LLMs makes it necessary for these perspectives to converge.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology leaders, editors, governance committees and publishing organizations need a structured way to determine where LLMs can safely provide value and where their use should be restricted or prohibited.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This framework applies a risk-based approach to LLM deployment. It combines responsible technology principles with established practices from the academic publishing community and guidance from organizations such as the International Committee of Medical Journal Editors (ICMJE), Committee on Publication Ethics (COPE) and STM.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not to prevent publishers from using AI. Instead, the objective is to ensure that AI supports knowledge production without weakening the transparency, accountability and human judgment on which scholarly publishing depends.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Five Critical Questions Before Deploying an LLM</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before introducing an LLM into any publishing workflow, organizations should evaluate its potential systemic impact. Five questions can help technology and editorial leaders determine whether a proposed application is appropriate.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. The Stakes Question</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>If this system makes a biased or incorrect decision, what is the worst possible consequence for a researcher, author or field of knowledge?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The greater the potential impact, the greater the level of caution required.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An error in an automated formatting task may be inconvenient but relatively easy to correct. An incorrect decision affecting whether research is published, how an author is evaluated or which research receives visibility can have long-term consequences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-impact applications should therefore receive the strongest controls or be excluded entirely.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. The Transparency Question</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Can the organization clearly explain how the LLM was used and what factors influenced its output?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Authors, reviewers and researchers should not be subjected to opaque AI-driven decisions in situations where the outcome materially affects them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If an organization cannot explain the role played by an LLM, its limitations or how the resulting decision was reached, deploying it in a high-stakes context becomes difficult to justify.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. The Oversight Question</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Does the organization have sufficient expertise and resources to continuously evaluate the system for bias and unintended consequences?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI governance is not a one-time activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Models, prompts, data, workflows and user behavior can change over time. Organizations therefore need ongoing monitoring, testing and auditing rather than relying solely on the results of an initial evaluation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If continuous oversight cannot be supported, the organization must reconsider the use case or introduce stronger mitigation measures.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. The Reversibility Question</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Can qualified humans review, override and correct an LLM-supported outcome?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systems that allow human intervention are generally easier to manage than systems that create irreversible decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human reviewers should have meaningful authority to challenge an AI output rather than simply approving recommendations generated by a model.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The more difficult an outcome is to reverse, the higher the associated risk.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. The Necessity Question</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Does using an LLM actually improve outcomes for researchers and knowledge production, or does it primarily improve internal efficiency?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Efficiency alone should not automatically justify introducing AI into a publishing workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should first determine whether the same objective can be achieved through conventional software, process redesign, better data integration or workflow improvements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where traditional technology can solve the problem without introducing significant AI-related risks, it may be the more responsible option.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should also consider the environmental impact of unnecessary model usage and establish clear criteria for discontinuing AI applications that do not provide sufficient value.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Risk Stratification Across the Academic Publishing Value Chain</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every application of an LLM carries the same level of risk.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A useful approach is to classify applications according to their position in the publishing value chain and the potential consequences of AI-generated outputs.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pre-Submission: Author Support and Discovery</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: Low</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLMs can provide useful assistance before a manuscript enters the formal publishing process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential applications include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Literature search assistance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Plain-language research summaries</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Translation support</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Language and grammar improvement</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reading-list generation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research discovery assistance</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These applications should primarily support the author rather than replace their judgment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Key safeguards:</strong></p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Treat AI output as an initial aid rather than authoritative information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Require authors to verify important claims.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Encourage disclosure of relevant LLM usage.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ensure authors retain control over the final manuscript.</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Submission Management: Administrative Processing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: Low</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLMs can also support administrative activities that do not directly determine the academic quality or acceptance of a submission.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Possible applications include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Formatting assistance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Copyediting support</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Submission categorization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Metadata summarization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Scheduling support</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Drafting routine correspondence</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizing submission information</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although the overall risk is relatively low, AI-generated administrative information can still contain errors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Key safeguards:</strong></p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conduct human review before external communication.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Spot-check categorization results.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Verify metadata summaries.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Avoid allowing automated classifications to become final decisions without review.</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Initial Screening: Scope and Completeness</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: Moderate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLMs can assist editorial teams with preliminary checks such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Determining whether a submission appears to fit a journal\'s scope</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Checking whether required information is present</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying technical formatting issues</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Detecting missing submission components</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The risk increases when these systems influence whether a manuscript moves forward.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Key safeguards:</strong></p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every negative decision should receive human review.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conduct regular bias assessments.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establish an accessible appeals process.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inform authors when AI-assisted screening is used.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitor outcomes across different author groups and research areas.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An LLM can support preliminary screening, but it should not become an invisible gatekeeper.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Peer Review: Supporting Reviewers Without Replacing Them</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: Moderate to High</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Peer review represents one of the more sensitive areas for AI deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potentially appropriate applications may include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supplementary methodology checks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Literature coverage assistance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Statistical approach review</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying areas that a reviewer may wish to investigate further</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, these tools should remain optional and supplementary.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLMs should not independently generate peer-review judgments or encourage reviewers to accept model-generated conclusions without verification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Key safeguards include:</strong></p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provide extensive training on LLM limitations.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Make AI assistance optional.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Require reviewers to independently assess the research.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitor for systematic bias.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protect confidential manuscript information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prevent AI-generated analysis from becoming a substitute for scholarly expertise.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The reviewer remains accountable for the assessment.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Editorial Decision-Making: A High-Risk Area</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: High - Do Not Use for Final Decisions</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLMs should not be used to make or determine:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accept/reject decisions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Publication priority rankings</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research merit assessments</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Novelty evaluations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quality scores</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Final editorial judgments</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reviewer performance evaluations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Decisions that materially determine an author\'s academic trajectory</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Editorial decisions can directly influence careers, funding opportunities, institutional reputation and the development of entire research fields.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Current LLMs cannot reliably determine scientific significance, research rigor or scholarly contribution in a way that can replace qualified human judgment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For these reasons, final editorial accountability must remain with humans.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research Integrity and Ethical Quality Assurance</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: Moderate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can assist with identifying potential integrity issues, but its output should be treated as a signal rather than a verdict.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential applications include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Flagging possible plagiarism</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying unusual submission patterns</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Detecting potentially manipulated content</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying possible AI-generated material</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Highlighting inconsistencies for expert review</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Key safeguards:</strong></p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use AI for flagging rather than automatic enforcement.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Require expert review of every significant flag.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Test systems for demographic and linguistic bias.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Take additional care when evaluating work from non-native English speakers.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regularly evaluate false-positive and false-negative rates.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An AI-generated flag should never automatically become an accusation or adverse decision.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Post-Publication Communication and Engagement</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk Level: Mixed</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some post-publication activities can benefit from AI, particularly those focused on accessibility and content presentation.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Appropriate applications</h3><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Translating abstracts</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accessibility assistance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Formatting supplementary content</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generating alternative content representations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting content discovery</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inappropriate applications</h3><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automated rejection communication</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generating sensitive editorial feedback</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Replacing human responses to authors</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Producing official editorial statements without human review</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Official communication represents the organization and should preserve authenticity, accountability and transparency.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology Leaders as Partners in Editorial Governance</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology advisers in academic publishing cannot focus solely on technical performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI becomes integrated into publishing workflows, technology leaders need to understand editorial integrity and actively participate in governance decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They may face pressure to approve technologies because they promise faster processing, lower costs or higher content throughput. However, operational efficiency should not become the only measure of success.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology leaders should focus on four responsibilities.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prioritize Outcomes Over Automation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The first question should not be:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>\"Can we automate this process?\"</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, teams should ask:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>\"Will automation improve outcomes for researchers, authors and the quality of knowledge?\"</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This distinction helps organizations avoid introducing AI simply because it is available.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Communicate Risks Clearly</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technical concepts such as model hallucination, bias, probabilistic output and training-data limitations need to be translated into language that editorial teams can understand and act upon.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Good governance depends on shared understanding.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Champion Continuous Oversight</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should dedicate resources to ongoing AI monitoring and establish governance structures that include representatives from:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Editorial</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research integrity</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Legal and compliance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ethics</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Relevant academic disciplines</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A diverse governance structure can identify risks that a purely technical review may miss.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preserve Meaningful Human Judgment</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human oversight must be real rather than symbolic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Editors, reviewers and other qualified professionals should have the authority and ability to:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Review AI outputs</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Challenge recommendations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Override decisions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Correct errors</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Escalate questionable results</p></li></ul><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Practical Implementation Roadmap</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A responsible AI strategy does not require an organization to transform every publishing workflow at once.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A more effective approach is to start with limited, lower-risk applications, learn from implementation and gradually expand where evidence supports doing so.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pre-Phase: Evaluate - Weeks 1 to 4</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Begin by mapping the existing publishing workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where LLMs are already being used</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where employees are experimenting with public AI tools</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which processes could potentially benefit from AI</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which workflows involve sensitive information</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which decisions have significant consequences</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Existing technical and governance controls</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Classify each potential application as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Low risk</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moderate risk</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High risk</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This initial inventory creates a baseline for future governance.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase One: Experiment - Months 2 to 4</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The next stage should focus on controlled experimentation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establish an AI ethics or governance committee.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connect AI governance with existing editorial integrity teams.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Train employees on LLM capabilities and limitations.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Select low-risk use cases.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Run controlled pilots.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Define success and failure criteria.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Document lessons learned.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Training activities can include practical exercises and simulations that help teams understand how AI systems can behave unexpectedly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is not simply to demonstrate that an AI tool works. The goal is to determine whether it works safely and responsibly in the organization\'s specific environment.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase Two: Evolve - Months 5 to 12</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">After low-risk pilots have been evaluated, organizations can consider carefully selected moderate-risk applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At this stage, publishers should:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measure pilot performance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evaluate bias and accuracy.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Review user feedback.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strengthen governance policies.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Introduce regular auditing.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improve documentation.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expand training.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establish clear escalation procedures.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expansion should depend on evidence rather than enthusiasm for AI.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phase Three: Expand - Month 12 and Beyond</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Long-term adoption requires continuous governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should establish:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regular AI audits</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Public or internal reporting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy reviews</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Model and workflow reassessment</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Incident response procedures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ongoing staff training</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear restrictions for high-risk applications</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-risk prohibitions should remain explicit rather than becoming diluted as AI capabilities evolve.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Risk Also Depends on the Type of Academic Content</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The risk of AI influence is not determined only by the publishing workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The nature of the research itself also matters.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Academic publishing commonly includes several forms of scholarly content.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Original Research</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Original research presents new findings based on experiments, observations, datasets or other primary evidence.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systematic Reviews</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systematic reviews use structured methodologies to identify, evaluate and synthesize existing research around a specific question.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Narrative Reviews</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Narrative reviews provide expert perspectives and summaries of existing literature but generally involve more flexible and subjective selection of evidence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Narrative reviews may present greater risk when AI significantly influences content because they depend heavily on expert interpretation and judgment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For this reason, publishers should consider applying stronger safeguards to content types where subjective synthesis plays a larger role.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protecting these areas can have an outsized effect on maintaining the quality and reliability of the broader knowledge base.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improve Publishing Cycle Time Without Automatically Adding AI</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every publishing bottleneck requires an LLM.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some delays originate from inefficient workflows rather than insufficient automation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can often improve cycle times through:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Better data integration</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cleaner handoffs between teams</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved metadata management</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Workflow redesign</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clearer ownership</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Better process visibility</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Removal of unnecessary approval steps</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved internal tooling</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These improvements can increase efficiency without creating additional AI-related risks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Peer review is a different challenge because it is constrained by human availability, disciplinary requirements and academic norms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It may be tempting to use AI to accelerate reviewer selection, reviewer workload management or review generation. However, these applications can introduce concerns involving bias, confidentiality, copyright and reduced scholarly rigor.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI should therefore be introduced only after fundamental workflow problems have been addressed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most importantly, AI recommendations should never become a hidden substitute for independent academic judgment.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Essential Organizational Safeguards for Responsible LLM Use</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responsible AI adoption requires organizational controls, not just technical controls.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Governance</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should establish clear accountability structures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A governance framework may include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A multidisciplinary AI ethics committee</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology representatives</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Editorial representatives</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research integrity specialists</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ethics and compliance professionals</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clearly defined approval processes</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Executive or board-level accountability</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every significant AI application should have a clear owner.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Bias Auditing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLM outputs should be evaluated systematically rather than relying on anecdotal observations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should consider:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Demographic bias testing</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Linguistic bias testing</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regular spot checks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">External audits</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Long-term performance monitoring</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">False-positive and false-negative analysis</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Public or internal reporting</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Risk assessment should also be incorporated into software development and governance ceremonies.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Transparency</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Publishers should clearly communicate:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where AI is being used</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What role the AI performs</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What limitations exist</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How humans remain involved</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How researchers can raise concerns</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Whether users can opt out where appropriate</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transparency helps preserve confidence in the publishing process.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Human-Centred Design</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The fundamental principle should be:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>LLMs augment human expertise; they do not replace it.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systems should be designed so that human professionals remain meaningfully involved in significant decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Training is equally important. Staff need to understand that fluent or confident AI output is not necessarily accurate.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. Continuous Learning</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI governance must evolve alongside the technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitor new research</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Follow developments in AI governance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Participate in industry discussions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Review emerging risks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Update internal policies</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Be willing to pause or withdraw deployments</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ability to reverse course is an important component of responsible innovation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">6. Clear Data Ownership and Protection</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Publishers should define how data can and cannot be used with AI systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policies should address:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Manuscript confidentiality</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reviewer information</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Researcher data</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Copyrighted material</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Training-data usage</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vendor data retention</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ownership of AI-generated outputs</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Third-party AI services</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data transfer and security requirements</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sensitive academic content should not be submitted to external AI systems without appropriate authorization and safeguards.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Central Principle: Human Accountability Must Remain</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">LLMs can provide substantial benefits to academic publishing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They can make information more accessible, assist with translation, support content discovery and reduce the effort required for routine administrative activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But the most consequential decisions should remain under human control.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Publishers should use AI to support:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Routine processes</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Content discovery</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accessibility</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Translation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Administrative assistance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preliminary analysis</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They should not use AI as a replacement for human responsibility when decisions influence:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What research gets published</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which research receives attention</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How researchers are evaluated</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Whose work is considered significant</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How scholarly disagreements are resolved</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The integrity of the academic record depends on maintaining this distinction.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust Is the Foundation of Academic Publishing</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Innovation cannot thrive if researchers and readers lose confidence in the integrity of the scholarly record.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust depends heavily on:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transparency</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accountability</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accuracy</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fairness</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human responsibility</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reliable citations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear governance</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations such as COPE, STM and other research-integrity bodies continue to develop principles and guidance around responsible scholarly communication.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The pressure to publish faster should never result in lower-quality content, unsupported claims, fabricated references or inaccurate information entering the scientific record.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responsible AI Is Also Good Engineering</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There are significant opportunities to improve academic publishing without using LLMs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Processes that depend heavily on human expertise, including peer review, can often be improved through better workflow design, data integration and existing software capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI should therefore be considered one possible component of a broader technology strategy rather than the default solution to every process problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI adoption increases, publishers will face growing pressure from researchers, funders, technology vendors and internal stakeholders to implement new tools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology leaders have an opportunity to become trusted partners to editorial teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their role should extend beyond selecting and deploying technology. They should help the organization understand the consequences of that technology and establish appropriate boundaries around its use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This requires continuous collaboration between technology, editorial, research integrity and governance teams.</p><h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conclusion</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large language models can create meaningful improvements in academic publishing, but responsible adoption requires more than technical implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations need to evaluate each use case according to its potential impact, transparency, oversight requirements, reversibility and actual necessity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Low-risk applications such as translation, accessibility, administrative support and content discovery can provide substantial benefits when appropriate safeguards are applied.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moderate-risk applications require stronger monitoring, human review and bias testing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-risk applications involving final editorial judgments, publication decisions and research evaluation should remain under direct human control and, where appropriate, be prohibited from LLM use entirely.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most effective strategy is to begin with low-risk experiments, strengthen governance through practical experience and expand only when evidence demonstrates that the benefits justify the risks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Academic publishing ultimately depends on trust.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective of responsible AI is therefore not to slow innovation. It is to ensure that innovation strengthens rather than undermines the integrity of the knowledge ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ethics should be treated as an engineering requirement-alongside security, reliability, scalability and performance.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology leaders and product teams have a responsibility to understand how their systems affect researchers, authors, reviewers and society. By working closely with editorial and research-integrity teams, they can help build AI-enabled publishing systems that are efficient, transparent, accountable and worthy of trust.</p>', 'Managing LLM Risks in Academic Publishing', 'Explore a practical framework for managing LLM risks in academic publishing with responsible AI, human oversight, transparency, and governance.', 'LLM risks in academic publishing, AI in academic publishing, responsible AI, LLM governance, AI ethics, academic publishing AI, AI risk management, generative AI governance, research integrity, human oversight, AI bias, scholarly publishing, LLM governance framework, responsible LLM deployment', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-05-31 15:47:59', 'published', 23229, 0, 18, NULL, '2024-05-31 15:47:59', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(324, 2, 2, 3, 'Physical AI in Healthcare: Building the Foundation for Safe and Effective Adoption', 'physical-ai-in-healthcare-building-the-foundation-for-safe-and-effective-adoption', 'Healthcare leaders explore how physical AI can transform clinical workflows, improve efficiency and extend care teams while maintaining safety, governance and human oversight.', '[\"Physical AI\",\"Healthcare AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Robotics\",\"Healthcare Robotics\",\"Generative AI\",\"Digital AI\",\"Medical Technology\",\"Clinical Workflows\",\"Surgical Innovation\",\"Hospital Automation\",\"AI Governance\",\"AI Safety\",\"Healthcare CIO\",\"Healthcare Automation\",\"Multimodal AI\",\"AI Infrastructure\",\"Healthcare Innovation\"]', 'banner_image-1786363312049-758852363.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Physical AI in Healthcare: Building the Foundation for Safe and Effective Adoption</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The first part of this feature examined the concept of physical AI and why healthcare organizations increasingly view it as a potential next phase of artificial intelligence. Unlike conventional AI systems that primarily operate within digital environments, physical AI brings intelligence into the real world through robotics, connected devices and intelligent machines.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As hospitals begin exploring these technologies, the focus is shifting from experimentation toward practical implementation. Healthcare leaders from Northwell Health, NVIDIA and Expper Technologies are examining how intelligent machines could eventually become part of clinical and operational workflows, how digital and physical AI may work together, and what safeguards will be required before these systems can become trusted components of healthcare infrastructure.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Physical AI Could Become an Extension of the Care Team</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The transition toward physical AI is likely to happen gradually. Healthcare leaders do not expect autonomous machines to take over clinical decision-making. Instead, the technology is more likely to serve as an extension of existing care teams, taking on repetitive physical responsibilities while clinicians continue to make critical decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Davit Martirosyan, cofounder and head of engineering at Expper Technologies, believes some of the earliest opportunities could appear in patient populations that find traditional digital interfaces difficult to use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Children, older adults and patients who require repeated assistance or companionship could particularly benefit from physical AI, he explained. Unlike a conventional screen-based interface, an intelligent physical system can establish a sense of presence, maintain engagement and adjust its communication according to the patient\'s responses.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This could make physical AI useful in situations where interaction, guidance and ongoing engagement are important parts of care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Martirosyan also sees a longer-term possibility in which hospitals operate interconnected networks of intelligent machines rather than deploying individual robots for isolated tasks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In such an environment, multiple robots and intelligent devices could share information, coordinate responsibilities and operate according to common safety policies. The real value would therefore come not only from the capabilities of individual machines but also from how effectively the entire physical environment works together.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital and Physical AI Agents Could Work as a Coordinated System</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">David Niewolny, senior director of business development for healthcare and medtech at NVIDIA, similarly anticipates a shift away from standalone AI applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, hospitals could eventually rely on coordinated digital and physical AI agents that exchange information continuously and assist healthcare professionals across different stages of the care journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital AI systems can manage information-heavy activities such as analyzing data, preparing documentation, supporting decision-making and coordinating workflows. Physical AI, meanwhile, can interact with the physical environment by assisting with logistics, monitoring, movement and selected procedures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these technologies could create a more adaptive healthcare operating model.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than deploying AI as a collection of disconnected applications, hospitals could create environments in which digital systems understand what is happening, determine appropriate actions and coordinate physical systems capable of carrying out bounded tasks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach could allow healthcare organizations to extend clinical expertise without removing humans from the decision-making process.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intelligence Could Become Embedded in Surgical Workflows</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Physical AI could also have a significant impact inside operating rooms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dr. Filippo Filicori, system chief of surgical innovation at Northwell Health, expects future surgical technologies to combine multiple streams of information, including video, robotic movement, force data, device telemetry and workflow events.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By bringing these data sources together, AI systems could develop a more complete understanding of what is happening during a procedure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Such systems could potentially recognize different stages of surgery, identify developing issues and provide relevant assistance at the appropriate moment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential applications could include automatically adjusting camera positioning or exposure based on surgical activity, recognizing when a surgeon is experiencing difficulty, providing targeted assistance, automating tightly controlled tasks and anticipating the instruments or supplies that may be needed next.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, these capabilities are intended to support clinicians rather than replace them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most valuable physical AI systems may ultimately be those that quietly reduce delays, minimize unnecessary variation and give clinicians more time to concentrate on patients instead of the most routine parts of their work.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance Must Evolve Alongside the Technology</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The potential of physical AI is significant, but healthcare leaders also emphasize that these systems cannot be governed exactly like conventional software.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional software failures can produce incorrect information or disrupt a workflow. Physical AI introduces another dimension because an intelligent system can interact directly with people, equipment and the physical environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That makes governance, safety and operational oversight essential from the beginning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Martirosyan recommends that healthcare organizations view physical AI as operational infrastructure rather than simply another software product.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every intelligent device should have a clearly defined role and a tightly controlled operating environment. Devices should have secure identities, restricted permissions and explicit limitations regarding what they can access, sense and physically manipulate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hospitals should also be able to understand what an AI-enabled machine is doing and why.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This includes maintaining visibility into system actions, understanding the factors behind important decisions and recording instances where human intervention changes or overrides automated behavior.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Multiple Safety Layers Are Essential</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Physical AI systems need safeguards that go beyond standard cybersecurity and software controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Martirosyan highlights several layers that should be incorporated into deployments, including human takeover mechanisms, emergency stop capabilities, safe operation during network disruptions and predictable fallback behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These safeguards are particularly important in healthcare environments where even a short interruption or unexpected machine action could affect patients or clinical staff.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Filicori argues that responsibility for these systems should not sit exclusively with IT teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Because physical AI can directly influence clinical environments, governance needs to involve clinical, operational and administrative stakeholders.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A failure involving generative AI may result in an inaccurate response. A physical AI failure could have much more direct consequences, such as moving an object incorrectly, colliding with a person, disrupting a medical procedure or causing physical harm.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That difference means healthcare organizations need stronger evaluation and approval processes before introducing physical AI into patient-facing environments.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Four Key Governance Areas for Healthcare Organizations</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Filicori recommends organizing physical AI governance around four broad areas:</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Technical Infrastructure</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations need reliable infrastructure capable of supporting intelligent machines, connected devices, data exchange and real-time operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This includes network reliability, device identity, cybersecurity, interoperability and the ability to monitor machine activity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Governance and Accountability</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every physical AI system should have a clearly documented purpose, defined autonomy level, operating boundaries and an accountable human owner.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance should also address issues such as ownership of sensor and video data, vendor access, audit trails, patient consent, liability and incident reporting.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Safety and Lifecycle Monitoring</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Safety cannot end once a system receives approval.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations need continuous monitoring to identify unexpected behavior, performance degradation and new risks as systems interact with changing environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Testing, incident reviews, software updates and periodic reassessment should remain part of the system lifecycle.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Workforce Readiness</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Employees must understand how physical AI systems operate, where their limitations exist and when human intervention is required.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance should therefore include clinicians, nurses, operations teams, IT professionals, biomedical engineers, quality and risk teams, legal departments, cybersecurity specialists and patient representatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A multidisciplinary approach can help organizations evaluate technology from both technical and patient-care perspectives.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Start With the Problem, Not the Robot</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Niewolny recommends that healthcare CIOs avoid starting their physical AI strategy with a particular robot or technology vendor.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, organizations should first identify operational problems that technology could realistically address.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where are clinicians experiencing the greatest workload pressure? Where are patients waiting unnecessarily? Which areas are facing shortages of specialized expertise? Which repetitive tasks consume valuable staff time?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once these problems have been identified, organizations can determine whether physical AI is actually the appropriate solution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach reduces the risk of adopting technology simply because it is innovative and instead connects investment to measurable operational outcomes.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital AI and Physical AI Will Increasingly Work Together</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Physical AI should not be viewed as a replacement for generative AI or other digital intelligence technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The emerging healthcare model is more likely to involve both working together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital AI can analyze information, reason over large datasets, generate documentation and coordinate workflows. Physical AI can interact with patients, equipment and the surrounding environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Niewolny describes the future hospital as an environment where digital agents and physical agents operate as coordinated teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This could create a system in which information is continuously exchanged between software-based intelligence and physical devices, allowing healthcare organizations to respond more effectively to changing conditions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Such integration could eventually move hospitals away from isolated AI applications toward connected, adaptive environments.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simulation-First Validation Can Reduce Deployment Risks</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before physical AI systems are introduced into real clinical environments, healthcare organizations will need robust methods for testing their behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Niewolny advocates a simulation-first approach in which intelligent systems are extensively evaluated in virtual environments before being exposed to patients.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These simulations could reproduce hospital layouts, equipment interactions, different patient conditions and potential system failures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simulation can help organizations identify problems earlier, improve development speed and test scenarios that may be difficult or dangerous to reproduce in real-world environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, simulation alone cannot establish clinical safety.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real-world hospital validation, clinical evidence and appropriate regulatory review will remain essential before technologies can be deployed at scale.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust Must Be Designed Into Physical AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Martirosyan emphasizes that safety should not begin when a machine reaches a hospital floor.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust needs to be built into the technology, its operating procedures and the people responsible for managing it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That means healthcare organizations must understand how systems behave, define the boundaries within which they are allowed to operate and ensure that human operators can intervene whenever necessary.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not simply to deploy intelligent machines. It is to create systems whose behavior is understandable, controlled and dependable enough to operate within sensitive healthcare environments.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preparing Healthcare for the Next Phase of AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The rise of physical AI does not represent the end of generative AI. Instead, the technologies are likely to become increasingly interconnected.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large language models, multimodal AI, robotics, simulation platforms and edge computing can work together to create systems capable of understanding digital information while also responding to physical environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare CIOs, this means infrastructure decisions being made today could influence how effectively their organizations adopt future AI capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than focusing primarily on purchasing the most advanced robot, healthcare leaders should identify operational problems that are measurable, repetitive and sufficiently constrained to demonstrate meaningful results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The long-term success of physical AI will depend on more than hardware capabilities. It will require redesigned workflows, strong governance, rigorous validation, appropriate cybersecurity, workforce preparation and continuous human oversight.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Autonomous Machines to Carefully Controlled Infrastructure</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of physical AI in healthcare is unlikely to resemble science-fiction scenarios involving fully autonomous humanoid caregivers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A more realistic path is the gradual introduction of hundreds of specialized and carefully controlled systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These machines could quietly perform repetitive physical tasks, support patient engagement, improve logistics, assist surgical teams, monitor environments and reduce operational friction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The result could be a healthcare environment in which clinicians spend less time on routine activities and more time delivering direct patient care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The key question for healthcare leaders, therefore, should not simply be which robot offers the most impressive capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, organizations should ask which physical tasks currently create the greatest risk, delay or workforce burden-and what measurable evidence would demonstrate that an AI-enabled solution has genuinely improved the situation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That shift in perspective could define healthcare\'s transition from primarily digital AI toward a future where digital intelligence and physical intelligence work together.</p>', 'Physical AI in Healthcare: Safe and Effective Adoption', 'Explore how physical AI can transform healthcare workflows through robotics, automation and digital AI while maintaining safety, governance and human oversight.', 'physical AI in healthcare, healthcare AI, AI robotics, healthcare robotics, AI in hospitals, physical artificial intelligence, healthcare automation, AI governance, AI safety, digital AI, generative AI healthcare, medical robotics, hospital automation, surgical AI, healthcare technology', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-04 13:30:30', 'published', 149384, 0, 11, NULL, '2024-06-04 13:30:30', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(325, 2, 2, 5, 'Operation Vital Signs: First-of-its-kind Exercise Stress Tests Healthcare Cyber Resilience', 'operation-vital-signs-first-of-its-kind-exercise-stress-tests-healthcare-cyber-resilience', 'Operation Vital Signs tested healthcare organizations’ ability to coordinate, respond and recover from major cyber disruptions across the sector.', '[\"Healthcare Cybersecurity\",\"Cyber Resilience\",\"Health-ISAC\",\"Operation Vital Signs\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Cyber Incident Response\",\"Information Sharing\",\"Healthcare Security\",\"Critical Infrastructure\",\"Incident Response\"]', 'banner_image-1786363593417-818108555.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1>Operation Vital Signs: First-of-its-kind Exercise Stress Tests Healthcare Cyber Resilience</h1><p>Healthcare organizations across the United States recently took part in <strong>Operation Vital Signs (OVS)</strong>, a first-of-its-kind national tabletop exercise designed to test how the healthcare sector would respond to a large-scale cyber disruption.</p><p>Co-sponsored by <strong>Health-ISAC</strong> and the <strong>Health Sector Coordinating Council (HSCC) Cybersecurity Working Group</strong>, the virtual exercise brought together more than 500 participants representing a broad range of healthcare organizations. Participants included hospitals, pharmaceutical companies, medical device manufacturers, health insurers, health IT providers, laboratories, pharmacies and other organizations supporting the healthcare ecosystem.</p><p>Rather than focusing only on the ability of an individual organization to recover from an attack, OVS examined the broader dependencies that connect the healthcare sector. The exercise tested organizational response plans while also examining how organizations, industry groups and government partners could coordinate when a widespread cyber event affects multiple entities simultaneously.</p><h2>A Scenario Built Around Digital Dependencies</h2><p>Operation Vital Signs placed participants in realistic situations designed to test their ability to manage disruption, make decisions under uncertainty and move toward recovery.</p><p>The first day centered on a simulated nation-state cyber campaign targeting the identity infrastructure of a cloud service provider. The upstream compromise created uncertainty around digital identity and authentication, forcing participating organizations to determine how they would respond to authentication failures and whether maintaining or disconnecting cloud connectivity would be the safer operational decision.</p><p>The scenario was intentionally designed around a realistic disruption rather than a complete digital shutdown. Leaders had to make important decisions while dealing with incomplete information, uncertain consequences and limited visibility into the evolving incident.</p><p>This approach demonstrated that cyber resilience is not simply about restoring systems after an outage. It also involves making informed operational decisions when organizations do not yet have a complete understanding of what has happened.</p><h2>Moving From Organizational Response to Sector Coordination</h2><p>One of the strongest lessons from OVS was the importance of coordination across the healthcare ecosystem.</p><p>Participants were able to identify trusted contacts, communication channels and coordination mechanisms that could help organizations maintain critical operations while transitioning from immediate response toward recovery.</p><p>Teams also had to balance speed with accuracy. Moving too slowly could increase operational and patient-care risks, while acting too quickly on incomplete or inaccurate information could create additional disruption.</p><p>The exercise also demonstrated how defensive actions taken by one organization or technology partner can sometimes create unexpected consequences for downstream organizations.</p><p>Establishing a common operating picture quickly became a critical requirement. Organizations needed to understand what was happening across the sector, what information could be trusted, and what assistance they could expect from government and private-sector partners as the situation developed.</p><p>During the early stages of an incident, participants identified several information requirements as especially important. These included vendor updates, indicators of compromise, verified intelligence and information about broader sector impacts.</p><p>The exercise reinforced the value of a trusted information-sharing environment where organizations can receive validated and actionable information while reducing duplication, conflicting reports and misinformation.</p><h2>Staffing Pressure, Uncertainty and the Cost of Recovery</h2><p>The second day of the exercise introduced another important dimension of cyber resilience: the human and operational cost of sustaining a prolonged response.</p><p>As teams relied on manual workarounds and faced increasing external pressure, including media leaks, response fatigue began to emerge.</p><p>A major cyber incident can create significant operational strain long after the initial technical problem has been identified. Organizations may face employee fatigue, increased cross-functional coordination requirements, dependence on vendors, delayed answers, conflicting information and repeated executive-level decisions.</p><p>Even containment measures can require substantial effort.</p><p>For example, rotating security keys may appear to be a straightforward protective action, but organizations still need to re-establish trust and validate affected relationships. Logging gaps, local workarounds and dependencies between federated systems can make this process significantly more complicated.</p><p>These challenges demonstrate why cyber resilience must account for people, processes and business operations in addition to technology.</p><h2>Connecting the Healthcare Sector</h2><p>Operation Vital Signs reinforced a fundamental principle of sector-wide resilience: <strong>no single organization can manage a major ecosystem-wide cyber incident alone.</strong></p><p>Healthcare organizations operate within an interconnected environment where hospitals, technology providers, pharmaceutical companies, medical device manufacturers, insurers, laboratories, pharmacies and public-sector organizations depend on one another.</p><p>In a widespread incident, coordination becomes just as important as technical response.</p><p>Health-ISAC plays an important role as a trusted information-sharing and coordination hub for the healthcare sector. Its function includes helping organizations connect with public-sector partners, improve situational awareness, share actionable intelligence and coordinate responses while protecting the confidentiality of affected organizations.</p><p>Effective coordination requires communication to work in both directions. Organizations need mechanisms for submitting relevant information while also receiving verified intelligence and guidance that can support their own response decisions.</p><p>The ability to scale these coordination mechanisms during a crisis can make a significant difference in how quickly the sector understands an incident and responds to it.</p><h2>Breaking Down Information-Sharing Barriers</h2><p>Another recurring challenge during the exercise was the difficulty organizations face when deciding whether to share information before all facts have been confirmed.</p><p>Organizations may hesitate because of potential legal exposure, reputational concerns, competitive considerations or the possibility that incomplete information could create unnecessary confusion.</p><p>At the same time, delaying communication can prevent other organizations from recognizing indicators of a developing threat or taking protective measures.</p><p>OVS provided an opportunity to explore this tension in a controlled environment. Participants experienced situations involving increasing pressure, incomplete reporting, competing priorities and uncertainty around how much information should be shared.</p><p>The exercise highlighted why trusted and appropriately structured information-sharing mechanisms are essential during sector-wide incidents.</p><p>Health-ISAC has continued to emphasize the benefits of responsible information sharing. Its updated <strong>Information Sharing Best Practices</strong>, published in September 2023, includes guidance around incident-response information sharing and coordinated media response.</p><p>Such frameworks can help organizations understand how to exchange information effectively while addressing the risks that become more significant when an incident affects the wider sector.</p><h2>Lessons for Future Exercises</h2><p>Participants described Operation Vital Signs as a valuable opportunity to identify gaps, understand available resources and evaluate how sector coordination could be improved.</p><p>Feedback from participants and the exercise planning team points toward several opportunities for future iterations.</p><h3>Integrating Legal Expertise</h3><p>Future exercises could incorporate legal teams more directly into the scenario. This would allow organizations to develop practical decision frameworks for sharing information quickly while managing legal and regulatory risks.</p><h3>Creating a Dedicated Sector Responder Breakout</h3><p>A structured discussion among organizations responsible for sector-level response functions could provide greater clarity during a large-scale incident.</p><p>Participants could include Health-ISAC, HSCC, HHS, ASPR, FDA and other relevant public-sector partners. Greater transparency around responsibilities and escalation pathways could help organizations understand where to turn as sector impacts expand.</p><h3>Adding Real-Time External Communications</h3><p>Corporate communications and public relations teams could also play a larger role in future exercises.</p><p>Including these teams would allow participants to practice developing consistent external messaging while technical teams continue responding to the incident. Coordinated messaging can help maintain confidence and reduce uncertainty among customers, employees, partners and the public.</p><h2>Building Collective Cyber Resilience</h2><p>Operation Vital Signs demonstrated that healthcare organizations are willing to collaborate, compare response strategies and strengthen sector-wide coordination.</p><p>However, resilience across the healthcare ecosystem cannot depend solely on security controls implemented within individual organizations.</p><p>It also depends on <strong>trust, relationships, communication channels and established coordination mechanisms</strong> that enable organizations to work together when a shared technology provider, infrastructure component or digital dependency fails.</p><p>The inaugural Operation Vital Signs exercise represents an important starting point. Future exercises can build on these lessons by improving communication, incorporating additional stakeholders and creating more realistic opportunities to test sector-level decision-making.</p><p>The most important work begins between exercises: turning lessons learned into practical improvements.</p><p>By strengthening information sharing, clarifying responsibilities and improving coordination before the next major cyber disruption occurs, the healthcare sector can be better prepared to make faster decisions, reduce operational disruption and protect patient care when a crisis emerges.</p>', 'Operation Vital Signs Tests Healthcare Cyber Resilience', 'Operation Vital Signs tested how healthcare organizations coordinate, respond and recover during large-scale cyber disruptions and sector-wide emergencies.', 'Operation Vital Signs, healthcare cyber resilience, healthcare cybersecurity, Health-ISAC, healthcare cyber attacks, cyber incident response, healthcare IT security, sector-wide cyber disruption, healthcare information sharing, cyber resilience', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-05 17:13:01', 'published', 147234, 0, 7, NULL, '2024-06-05 17:13:01', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(326, 2, 1, 14, 'How Big Is the Provider Transparency in Coverage Data Gap?', 'how-big-is-the-provider-transparency-in-coverage-data-gap', 'Providers are leaving reimbursement opportunities untapped as limited use of Transparency in Coverage data creates gaps in payer negotiations and financial planning.', '[\"Transparency in Coverage\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Payer Negotiations\",\"Healthcare Finance\",\"Reimbursement Data\",\"Revenue Cycle Management\",\"Healthcare Analytics\",\"Payer Data\",\"Provider Organizations\",\"Financial Planning\"]', 'banner_image-1786363769430-654517533.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The large volume of reimbursement information available through <strong>Transparency in Coverage (TIC)</strong> files could become a valuable strategic resource for healthcare providers. However, many provider organizations are still struggling to turn this publicly available data into actionable insights that can support payer negotiations, reimbursement planning and long-term financial decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">New research analyzed by <strong>Trek Health</strong> for the <strong>Healthcare Financial Management Association (HFMA)</strong> highlights a significant gap between the availability of payer data and how effectively providers are using it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">TIC files contain extensive machine-readable information about negotiated healthcare rates. While the data is publicly accessible, transforming these large and complex files into standardized benchmarks requires significant technical infrastructure, data engineering capabilities and specialized analytics expertise. Many provider organizations do not currently have these resources in place.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Providers Face Challenges Using TIC Data</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">HFMA gathered feedback from <strong>161 provider organization executives</strong> through a Qualtrics survey for its <strong>2026 State of Payer Negotiations</strong> report. The survey examined how organizations currently use reimbursement data, the challenges they encounter and the role of payer information in contract negotiations and financial planning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The findings show that <strong>40% of providers are not using Transparency in Coverage data when preparing for payer contract negotiations</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resource limitations are one of the biggest obstacles. About <strong>44% of respondents identified a lack of time and resources</strong> as a barrier to incorporating TIC information into payment negotiations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Forecasting reimbursement also remains difficult. More than <strong>60% of surveyed organizations reported challenges with reimbursement forecasting</strong>, demonstrating that providers still face difficulties translating payer information into reliable financial projections.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The report also found that only <strong>26% of organizations proactively model the financial impact of payer policy changes before those changes affect reimbursement</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a significant opportunity for healthcare organizations to improve how they monitor payer activity and prepare for changes that could affect revenue.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Raw Payer Data Into Actionable Intelligence</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The complexity of TIC files is another major barrier. Simply accessing machine-readable files does not automatically provide providers with useful reimbursement intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations need processes that can ingest, normalize, validate and analyze the raw information before it can be compared across payers, providers and markets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite these challenges, approximately <strong>60% of organizations are using TIC data in at least some part of their payer negotiation preparation</strong>, according to the research.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Among organizations that use the data, <strong>rate benchmarking</strong> is the most common application, representing roughly 25% of respondents. Other major applications include financial modeling and developing negotiation strategies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These use cases demonstrate how reimbursement data can move beyond simple benchmarking and become part of broader financial and contracting decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data Silos Create Another Problem</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology and resources are not the only challenges. Healthcare organizations can also struggle with internal visibility into how TIC data is being used.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Approximately <strong>23% of respondents said they did not know how their organization was using the data</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This suggests that the problem is not necessarily a lack of digital capabilities. Instead, it may be a knowledge-sharing and organizational fragmentation issue.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In larger health systems, TIC analysis may be handled by dedicated analytics teams or outside vendors. However, the resulting insights may not always reach the people responsible for negotiating payer contracts and managing financial performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contract managers, managed care leaders and finance teams need access to reimbursement intelligence at the point when they are making decisions about payer agreements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Without that connection, valuable data can remain isolated within analytics functions rather than becoming part of the organization\'s negotiation strategy.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuous Monitoring Can Help Reduce Revenue Leakage</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare providers can potentially improve payer performance by establishing a continuous process for collecting and analyzing TIC information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This process can include regular ingestion of raw files, normalization of reimbursement information and validation of data before it is used for financial analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The next step is connecting reimbursement intelligence with existing payer contracts. Doing so can help organizations identify differences between negotiated rates, market benchmarks and current payment arrangements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These insights can then be used to identify negotiation opportunities and prioritize contracts where reimbursement improvements could have the greatest financial impact.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Broader Shift in Payer Negotiations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing importance of TIC data reflects a broader change in how providers approach payer negotiations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Earlier this year, <strong>Dilpreet Sahota</strong>, founder and CEO of Trek Health, discussed the importance of contract lifecycle management for provider organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before Transparency in Coverage requirements were introduced, providers often had limited visibility into what comparable or fair reimbursement rates looked like in their markets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The availability of publicly reported payer pricing has changed that situation by giving providers an additional source of market intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">TIC data can provide organizations with a framework for analyzing market rates and comparing reimbursement levels across competitors and payers. Providers can use those comparisons to develop more informed expectations about target reimbursement rates during negotiations.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contract Modeling Can Improve Financial Decisions</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Providers can also benefit from incorporating contract modeling into payer review processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before agreeing to new reimbursement terms, organizations can model how different billing methodologies and payment structures could affect their overall financial performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach allows finance and managed care teams to evaluate potential consequences before a contract is finalized rather than discovering unfavorable financial effects after implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ability to connect payer data, contract terms and financial modeling could therefore become an important component of modern revenue cycle and payer management strategies.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regulatory Background</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The federal <strong>Transparency in Coverage rule took effect in 2022</strong>, requiring health plans to make negotiated rate information available through machine-readable files. The information is published on a recurring basis for plans and coverage options offered by health insurers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS)</strong> is currently reviewing proposed changes intended to streamline how this information is transmitted.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As regulatory requirements and data formats continue to evolve, providers will need processes that can adapt to changes while maintaining consistent access to reimbursement intelligence.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Revenue Leakage Risk</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The financial implications of ineffective payer data management are significant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the report\'s findings, nearly <strong>70% of organizations identified payer policy changes that are not reflected in contracts as their greatest revenue leakage risk</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inconsistent payment practices across payers were identified as another major concern.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These findings highlight why payer intelligence should not remain isolated from contract management. Changes in payer policies, reimbursement rates and payment practices can directly affect provider revenue if they are not identified and reflected in contractual agreements.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Providers Should Do Next</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research points toward a more integrated approach to payer management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than treating Transparency in Coverage data as a static source of information, healthcare organizations can incorporate it into ongoing financial and contracting workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A stronger approach can include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuously collecting and processing TIC data.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Standardizing reimbursement information across payers and markets.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Validating data before using it for financial decisions.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Comparing negotiated rates against relevant market benchmarks.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connecting payer intelligence with contract management systems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modeling the financial impact of potential contract changes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sharing reimbursement insights with managed care, finance and contracting teams.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prioritizing payer agreements with the greatest potential financial impact.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge is no longer simply gaining access to payer reimbursement information. The bigger issue is building the capabilities needed to transform that information into timely and actionable intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For provider organizations, closing the Transparency in Coverage data gap could provide stronger negotiating leverage, improve reimbursement forecasting and help reduce preventable revenue leakage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As payer pricing information becomes increasingly available, organizations that can effectively integrate and operationalize that data may have a significant advantage in future contract negotiations.</p>', 'Provider Transparency in Coverage Data Gap', 'Providers are underusing Transparency in Coverage data, limiting reimbursement insights, payer negotiation leverage and financial planning opportunities.', 'Transparency in Coverage data, provider reimbursement data, payer negotiations, healthcare financial management, healthcare analytics, reimbursement benchmarking, payer contracts, healthcare revenue cycle, TIC data, healthcare finance', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-07 12:56:32', 'published', 150462, 0, 7, NULL, '2024-06-07 12:56:32', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(327, 2, 2, 14, 'Medicaid Cuts Are Coming: How Health Systems Can Respond With Integrity', 'medicaid-cuts-are-coming-how-health-systems-can-respond-with-integrity', 'Medicaid cuts will pressure health systems to balance financial sustainability with behavioral health, maternal care and community needs.', '[\"Medicaid\",\"Healthcare\",\"Health Systems\",\"Healthcare Policy\",\"Medicaid Cuts\",\"Behavioral Health\",\"Mental Health\",\"Maternal Health\",\"Healthcare Leadership\",\"Healthcare Finance\",\"Population Health\",\"Patient Care\",\"Health Equity\",\"Care Coordination\",\"Healthcare Strategy\"]', 'banner_image-1786364009076-453859094.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Medicaid Cuts Are Coming: How Health Systems Can Respond With Integrity</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders have spent years navigating changes in federal policy, evolving patient demographics and growing financial pressures. Yet few challenges are as consequential as the Medicaid cuts currently moving through Congress.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These reductions will not exist solely on balance sheets. They will affect patients, clinicians and entire communities. Health systems will be forced to make difficult decisions about where limited resources should be directed, making financial discipline essential-but so is a clear commitment to organizational values.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mission and margin do not always move in the same direction. During periods of financial strain, healthcare organizations must determine which services are essential, which can be redesigned and which may no longer be sustainable. Behavioral health, maternal care and services for vulnerable populations should receive particular attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that openly acknowledge difficult tradeoffs, engage their communities and advocate for adequate reimbursement before reducing services will be better positioned to preserve public trust.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Behavioral Health Paradox</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mental health and substance use disorders remain among the country\'s most widespread and underfunded healthcare needs. Medicaid is also the largest payer for behavioral health services, making potential reimbursement reductions especially significant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">More than 65% of the geographic United States is designated as a mental health Health Professional Shortage Area (HPSA) by the Health Resources and Services Administration.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders therefore face an important question: what happens if that shortage expands even further?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When reimbursement declines, behavioral health programs that already operate with limited margins can become immediate targets for reductions. Staff workloads increase, caseloads expand and clinician burnout can accelerate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, patients do not simply disappear when services become unavailable. Many eventually arrive in emergency departments, experience crises in their communities or require more expensive forms of care. In the long run, reducing access can shift costs elsewhere in the healthcare system.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge for health system leaders is therefore not simply whether behavioral health services can absorb reductions, but how organizations can maintain safe, effective and compassionate care while resources become tighter.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanding Care Coordination</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Care coordination offers one potential path forward.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Integrated behavioral health models can place mental health professionals within primary care, OB/GYN and pediatric settings. By identifying patients earlier and coordinating treatment across specialties, these models can reduce dependence on episodic crisis care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although integrated care may initially appear to require additional resources, it can function as a broader cost-management strategy. Connecting behavioral health with primary and specialty care can improve continuity while reducing unnecessary barriers for patients.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems can also strengthen direct relationships between behavioral health programs and primary care, obstetrics and pediatric teams. Creating smoother referral processes and connections with community organizations can help patients reach the appropriate level of care without unnecessary delays.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protecting Clinical Quality</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial pressure should not automatically translate into deteriorating clinical relationships.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There is an important difference between increasing a clinician\'s workload while preserving supervision, peer support and sufficient documentation time and pushing staff beyond sustainable limits.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When workloads become excessive, compassion fatigue and burnout can affect the quality of care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In many situations, serving a smaller population effectively may be better than attempting to serve everyone with inadequate resources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems should therefore consider regular staff pulse surveys, targeted administrative support and meaningful investments in workforce well-being. These measures should not be viewed merely as employee benefits. They can help preserve clinical expertise and organizational capacity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying Mission-Critical Services</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another important step is distinguishing between services that are essential to the organization\'s mission and programs that may be supplementary.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">No health system can necessarily provide every behavioral health service. Instead of maintaining under-resourced programs across every organization, health systems could coordinate regionally, establish referral networks and develop stronger community partnerships.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A transparent referral relationship with another capable provider can ultimately serve patients better than maintaining an in-house service that cannot consistently deliver high-quality care.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maternal Health Cannot Be Treated as a Budget Line</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maternal health represents another area where financial reductions can have serious downstream consequences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The United States continues to experience a higher maternal mortality rate than other high-income countries, with Black women experiencing substantially higher maternal mortality than white women.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Medicaid finances more than 40% of births in the United States. Consequently, reductions affecting prenatal services, postpartum follow-up or the workforce delivering maternal care can have consequences far beyond an organization\'s immediate financial position.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prenatal access should remain a population health priority. Delays in an initial prenatal visit can contribute to missed opportunities to identify gestational diabetes, manage hypertension, detect fetal abnormalities and provide appropriate preventive guidance, including folate supplementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These risks are not theoretical. They can translate into preventable complications and poorer outcomes for mothers and infants.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maintaining Obstetric Readiness</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial constraints can also threaten continuing education and emergency preparedness, particularly within smaller and rural health systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Obstetric emergency training should not become an easy budget target. Conditions such as hemorrhage, eclampsia and preterm labor do not follow organizational budget cycles.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems can consider protecting core obstetric training and sharing simulation resources with regional partners when internal budgets are constrained.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Midwifery-led care, doula programs and community health workers can also extend the reach of maternal health services when appropriately integrated into care models. These approaches should complement-not replace-physician-led care and should operate within appropriate clinical safeguards.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Postpartum Care Matters</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Postpartum services are another essential component of maternal healthcare.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The expansion of Medicaid postpartum coverage to 12 months in many states represents an important policy development. Maintaining continuity after delivery can help identify and address health issues that might otherwise go untreated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems facing financial pressure should be cautious about reducing postpartum services in ways that create gaps in follow-up care. A short-term reduction in service availability can produce longer-term clinical and financial consequences.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rethinking the Healthcare Market</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Medicaid pressures are also forcing health systems to reconsider which markets and service lines they can realistically sustain.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Historically, many organizations operated under an assumption that they should provide comprehensive care to everyone who needed it. Commercial reimbursement frequently helped offset lower Medicaid reimbursement, making this cross-subsidization model possible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That financial equation is becoming increasingly difficult.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As Medicaid reimbursement declines and competition within commercial markets increases, organizations may have less flexibility to subsidize services that consistently operate at a loss.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems serving communities with high Medicaid enrollment and relatively limited commercial insurance volume may face particularly difficult strategic decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some organizations may ultimately need to withdraw from specific markets or discontinue certain services. When that becomes necessary, leadership has an ethical responsibility to provide sufficient notice, create transition plans for affected patients and work with community partners to identify alternative sources of care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simply closing a service without a transition strategy can leave patients and communities carrying the consequences.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Exploring Alternative Models</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other organizations may be able to maintain services through alternative approaches.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Value-based care arrangements can reward organizations for improving outcomes and managing resources efficiently rather than relying solely on service volume. Partnerships with federally qualified health centers can expand access, while affiliations with larger systems can provide greater financial and operational diversification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">None of these approaches is simple. However, they may be preferable to allowing a health system or service line to deteriorate gradually while attempting to remain in a market it can no longer adequately serve.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most concerning approach is one that treats these decisions exclusively as financial calculations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When a community loses a hospital, birthing center or behavioral health program, residents experience more than a change to an organization\'s budget. They experience reduced access, disruption and the loss of an important community institution.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leadership During Financial Pressure</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders cannot independently redesign the nation\'s reimbursement system. They cannot single-handedly resolve inadequate behavioral health funding, maternal health disparities or the broader challenges facing Medicaid.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But that does not mean they are powerless.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders can choose transparency with boards, employees, patients and communities rather than relying on overly optimistic narratives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They can prioritize services that have the greatest impact on vulnerable populations, including behavioral health and maternal care, even when financial pressures make those services difficult to sustain.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They can advocate at the state and federal levels for reimbursement policies that more accurately reflect the cost of delivering high-quality care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most importantly, leaders can allow organizational values to guide difficult decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The current period of financial uncertainty will not last forever. The decisions health systems make during this period, however, will influence whether communities continue to trust them long after the immediate pressure has passed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Balancing mission and margin does not mean ignoring financial realities. It means using financial strategy to protect the most important elements of the healthcare mission.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For health systems facing Medicaid reductions, integrity is not simply an ethical principle. It can also be a practical leadership strategy for navigating uncertainty while preserving the trust of patients, clinicians and communities.</p>', 'Medicaid Cuts: How Health Systems Can Respond', 'Learn how health systems can navigate Medicaid cuts while protecting behavioral health, maternal care, clinical quality and community trust.', 'Medicaid cuts, Medicaid policy, healthcare systems, healthcare leadership, behavioral health, maternal health, healthcare finance, healthcare strategy, Medicaid reimbursement, health system strategy, healthcare policy, patient care, health equity, care coordination', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-11 14:39:03', 'published', 93395, 0, 8, NULL, '2024-06-11 14:39:03', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(328, 2, 2, 14, 'From AI Ambition to Health System Execution: Closing the Gap Between Promise and Practice', 'from-ai-ambition-to-health-system-execution-closing-the-gap-between-promise-and-practice', 'Health systems are moving from AI pilots to measurable results by aligning priorities, governance, execution, vendor strategy and value measurement.', '[\"Healthcare AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Health Systems\",\"Digital Health\",\"AI Governance\",\"AI Strategy\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"AI Implementation\",\"HealthTech\",\"Clinical AI\",\"AI ROI\",\"Healthcare Innovation\"]', 'banner_image-1786364179247-497798055.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From AI Ambition to Health System Execution: Closing the Gap Between Promise and Practice</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems have invested heavily in artificial intelligence, but the pressure is now shifting from experimentation to measurable outcomes. While AI continues to generate significant interest across healthcare, many initiatives struggle to move beyond the pilot stage or demonstrate a sustainable return on investment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Industry estimates suggest that as many as 80% of AI projects fail to progress beyond pilots, while approximately 95% of AI initiatives fail to generate measurable ROI. Hospital executives are also encountering another challenge: evaluating AI vendors and determining whether a solution is likely to perform effectively before making a significant investment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">To better understand these challenges, the Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) and Qualified Health recently convened healthcare leaders representing 12 health systems and six AI technology companies. The closed-door working session examined how organizations are currently selecting, deploying and evaluating AI solutions in real-world healthcare environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective was practical rather than theoretical: identify the operating practices that can help health systems generate value from AI today while building the capabilities needed to adapt as AI technologies continue to evolve.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Five key lessons emerged from the discussion.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. AI Portfolios Must Connect Directly to Enterprise Priorities</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the most common challenges facing health systems is the disconnect between AI investments and broader organizational strategy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI initiatives can emerge from vendor outreach, individual departments, clinician interest, peer organizations or executive mandates. Each individual project may have a reasonable business case, but collectively these investments can lack a common strategic direction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The problem becomes particularly complicated in academic medical centers, where care delivery, research and education operate with different priorities, incentives and timelines. Financial objectives and clinical priorities can also compete, making it difficult to establish a consistent definition of success.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Without an enterprise-level framework, organizations can accumulate numerous AI applications without a clear understanding of how those investments contribute to larger institutional goals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems making meaningful progress are approaching the problem differently. They identify a limited number of enterprise priorities and use those priorities as the foundation for AI selection, implementation and evaluation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Typical priorities include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanding patient access</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving financial performance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Addressing workforce shortages</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reducing administrative workload</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increasing operational capacity</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving clinical workflows</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These priorities provide a practical filter for AI investments. They help organizations determine which opportunities deserve funding, which should be delayed and which should not move forward.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This ability to say \"no\" is becoming increasingly important as the number of AI opportunities continues to grow.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Prioritization Has Become an Essential Operating Discipline</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI demand is coming from virtually every part of the healthcare organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Executives are looking for measurable improvements in cost, access and workforce productivity. Clinicians are identifying opportunities to reduce administrative tasks and improve patient care. Operational teams are looking for ways to remove bottlenecks and increase efficiency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many of these requests are legitimate. The challenge is that a health system cannot implement every promising AI use case simultaneously.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This makes prioritization one of the most important capabilities for organizations moving from experimentation toward scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Selecting one initiative often means postponing another. Yet organizations frequently avoid these trade-offs, resulting in portfolios filled with pilots that have no clearly defined path toward broader deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems seeing stronger results are applying more disciplined selection criteria. They generally prioritize opportunities that have:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A clearly defined business or clinical problem</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A measurable outcome</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A realistic implementation pathway</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strong alignment with enterprise objectives</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Appropriate organizational readiness</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A viable path to scaling</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Several areas are already demonstrating tangible potential, including revenue cycle operations, ambient clinical documentation, referral management and targeted clinical workflows such as cardiology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can also contribute to improved patient access by helping organizations reduce backlogs, improve scheduling and increase available capacity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Funding structures influence prioritization as well. Centrally funded programs can be more closely aligned with enterprise strategy, while department-led projects may move faster because they involve fewer organizational layers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neither approach is inherently better. The challenge is coordinating them so that local innovation does not conflict with enterprise objectives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizational readiness is equally important. Workforce training, data interoperability, operational processes and change-management capabilities can determine whether a promising AI application produces lasting results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once teams invest time, money and organizational resources into an AI implementation, changing direction becomes increasingly expensive. Consequently, prioritization should not be treated as a one-time exercise. It needs to remain an ongoing management discipline.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. AI Governance Must Become an Operational Process</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance is frequently identified as one of the biggest obstacles to healthcare AI adoption. However, the problem is often not the absence of governance principles but the lack of operational clarity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations may have committees, policies and risk frameworks, yet still struggle to answer basic questions:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Who approves an AI solution?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Who owns implementation?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Who monitors performance?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Who is responsible when performance changes?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What conditions trigger reassessment?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When should an AI application be expanded or discontinued?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When governance remains primarily conceptual, approval processes can become slow without necessarily improving decision quality.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Effective governance needs to support three core activities: selecting appropriate solutions, implementing them effectively and maintaining performance after deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems that are progressing toward scale are increasingly creating structured pathways for these activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These models can include standardized evaluation processes, controlled pilot environments, defined implementation requirements and ongoing performance monitoring.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The underlying shift is significant. Governance is no longer simply an oversight mechanism. It is becoming an operating capability that enables responsible AI adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another important issue is the division of responsibility between healthcare organizations and vendors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI becomes embedded within commercial platforms, vendors are increasingly involved in validation, monitoring, model management and risk controls. However, the health system remains accountable for how the technology performs within its own environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear responsibility boundaries therefore need to be established before deployment rather than after problems emerge.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Decision-Making Cycles Must Keep Pace With AI Development</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The speed of AI innovation presents a fundamental operating challenge for healthcare organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">New capabilities are emerging faster than many health systems can evaluate, approve, contract for and deploy them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional decision-making structures can make this problem worse. Committee-based approval processes that meet once a month may have worked for conventional technology investments, but they can become a major constraint when organizations need to continuously evaluate rapidly changing AI capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A missed meeting can delay an initiative by weeks. Delays then accumulate across procurement, contracting, technical integration, workflow redesign and implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, AI deployments require continuous attention after launch. Performance must be monitored across users, departments and clinical environments, with adjustments made as workflows and technology evolve.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems that are advancing more quickly are beginning to redesign their decision structures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than requiring every decision to pass through large committees, some organizations are using smaller groups with clearly defined authority. These teams can evaluate use cases, approve appropriate initiatives and shorten the time between testing and implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Shorter experimentation cycles also create faster feedback. Organizations can learn what works, identify problems earlier and make decisions based on real-world performance rather than theoretical assumptions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vendor strategy is changing alongside these internal processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growth of major technology platforms does not mean health systems should automatically depend on a single provider. If a required capability is unavailable within the necessary timeframe, organizations may consider alternative solutions while evaluating the long-term cost of changing platforms later.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates greater flexibility and can strengthen the negotiating position of healthcare organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contract structures are evolving as well. Long-term agreements with rigid escalation mechanisms may not align with the rapid pace of AI development. Health systems are increasingly interested in shorter commitments, greater flexibility and explicit accountability for technology performance.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. AI Value Should Be Evaluated Across the Portfolio</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measuring AI value remains one of the most difficult challenges for healthcare organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many systems can identify individual improvements following an AI implementation. However, translating those improvements into consistent metrics that support investment decisions is considerably more difficult.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial performance naturally receives significant attention from CFOs and executives responsible for organizational profitability. Yet some of AI\'s most immediate benefits may not appear directly as revenue or cost savings.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Examples include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reduced clinician administrative workload</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved provider efficiency</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Faster workflows</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increased operational capacity</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Better patient access</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reduced delays</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved employee experience</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Different stakeholders therefore define value differently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">CFOs may prioritize financial returns. Clinical leaders may focus on quality and workflow integration. Operational executives may evaluate throughput and capacity. Workforce leaders may emphasize employee experience and workload reduction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These perspectives do not necessarily conflict, but they cannot always be represented through one financial metric.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations making greater progress are responding by defining value across multiple dimensions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In practice, three areas appear particularly important:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial health</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provider and workforce experience</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Patient access and care delivery</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This portfolio-based approach provides a broader picture of whether an AI investment is producing meaningful organizational value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measurement also needs to continue after implementation. A solution that produces strong results during an initial pilot may perform differently once it is deployed across additional departments, user groups or workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuous monitoring is therefore essential for maintaining trust and identifying performance degradation early.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy and Payment Models Will Influence AI Adoption</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The operational decisions made by health systems and AI vendors represent only part of the larger AI adoption equation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment structures and regulatory expectations also influence which technologies organizations can justify, fund and scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations respond strongly to financial incentives. Under fee-for-service models, investments designed to improve access or efficiency may sometimes conflict with existing revenue structures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Under value-based care models, improvements in outcomes, efficiency and access may align more naturally with organizational financial objectives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This difference can influence which AI applications receive priority.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Solutions focused on revenue cycle optimization, documentation and billing can be easier to justify because their financial impact may be visible relatively quickly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI applications designed to improve clinical outcomes, reduce provider burden or expand access may require a broader definition of value, particularly when benefits accumulate over a longer period.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regulation introduces another important consideration.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Requirements surrounding safety, performance, transparency and accountability are essential for responsible healthcare AI. However, how those requirements are translated into operational processes will influence whether they accelerate responsible deployment or create additional delays.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems are increasingly expected to monitor AI performance and manage associated risks, yet many organizations do not have sufficient infrastructure or standardized approaches to perform these responsibilities effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Greater alignment between payment models, regulatory expectations and desired healthcare outcomes will therefore be important for unlocking AI\'s full potential.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Value From AI Is Already Being Realized</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite the challenges, health systems are already generating meaningful value from AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations making the most progress are not necessarily those with the largest number of AI deployments. Instead, they tend to have stronger processes for deciding what to implement, how to deploy it and how to maintain performance after launch.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their approach is characterized by greater discipline around:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strategic prioritization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Implementation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vendor management</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Performance monitoring</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Value measurement</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, these practices remain inconsistent across the healthcare industry.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Different organizations use different decision frameworks, governance structures and measurement approaches. This variability makes it difficult to consistently reproduce successful outcomes or determine which practices are most effective across different environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Much of the existing healthcare AI literature also does not fully reflect this operational complexity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research and industry discussions frequently focus on high-level frameworks or individual AI applications. While those perspectives remain useful, they do not always explain how multiple AI solutions interact inside a health system or how organizational decisions influence their collective performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The next phase of healthcare AI therefore requires a stronger focus on execution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems need the capabilities to evaluate competing opportunities, prioritize investments against enterprise objectives, implement solutions across real-world environments and remain accountable for performance over time.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving From AI Potential to Sustainable Impact</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The healthcare industry has moved beyond the question of whether AI can create value. The more pressing question is whether health systems can consistently convert AI potential into measurable and sustainable outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Doing so requires more than selecting sophisticated technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It requires organizations to establish clear priorities, make deliberate investment decisions, build practical governance processes, accelerate decision cycles and measure value across multiple dimensions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI adoption will also continue to be influenced by reimbursement structures, regulatory requirements and the evolving responsibilities shared between health systems and technology vendors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations that succeed will be those that treat AI not simply as a technology initiative, but as an enterprise operating capability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">DiMe and Qualified Health intend to continue advancing this work through practical engagement with health systems and AI technology developers. Their focus includes developing open resources and sharing lessons from real-world implementation to help organizations move beyond experimentation and capture meaningful value for healthcare organizations, clinicians and patients.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Jennifer C. Goldsack</strong> is CEO of the Digital Medicine Society (DiMe). <strong>Justin Norden, MD</strong>, is an adjunct professor at Stanford Medicine in the Department of Biomedical Informatics Research and co-founder and CEO of Qualified Health.</p>', 'AI in Healthcare: From Ambition to Execution', 'Learn how health systems can turn AI ambition into measurable value through better prioritization, governance, execution and ROI measurement.', 'healthcare AI, AI in healthcare, health system AI, healthcare AI strategy, AI implementation, AI governance, healthcare technology, clinical AI, AI ROI, digital health', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-13 10:22:34', 'published', 137919, 0, 12, NULL, '2024-06-13 10:22:34', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(329, 2, 2, 14, 'AI Won’t Fix Healthcare Until We Fix the Infrastructure', 'ai-wont-fix-healthcare-until-we-fix-the-infrastructure', 'Healthcare AI needs more than advanced models. Strong data, governance, workflows, and monitoring are essential to scale AI safely.', '[\"Healthcare AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"AI Infrastructure\",\"Health Systems\",\"Clinical AI\",\"Healthcare Data\",\"AI Governance\",\"Data Architecture\",\"AI Adoption\",\"Digital Health\",\"Healthcare Innovation\",\"Machine Learning\"]', 'banner_image-1786364371430-656049681.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Won’t Fix Healthcare Until We Fix the Infrastructure</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Infrastructure is often overlooked and underfunded. Roads, bridges, and electrical grids remain invisible until a breakdown makes their importance impossible to ignore.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare AI is reaching a similar point. Over the past several years, health systems have developed, tested, or evaluated hundreds of AI solutions. Many demonstrate impressive technical performance, yet their clinical and operational impact remains limited. Some solutions have struggled to work with existing technology environments, while others have failed to move beyond pilot programs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The result is a healthcare AI landscape filled with isolated proofs of concept rather than sustainable, enterprise-wide solutions that consistently deliver measurable value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The problem isn\'t necessarily the AI tools themselves. The bigger challenge is that many healthcare organizations lack the infrastructure needed to deploy AI safely and effectively at scale. Reliable data architecture, governance, monitoring, security, and workflow integration are becoming essential components of successful AI adoption.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Missing Infrastructure Layer</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite their advanced capabilities, AI applications depend on many of the same fundamentals as traditional software. They need accurate and accessible data, reliable technology environments, clear oversight, and integration into the workflows where people actually work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Without these foundations, even sophisticated AI models can struggle to generate meaningful outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems that have progressed beyond experimentation typically have several infrastructure capabilities in place.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reliable Data Architecture</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI systems are only as dependable as the data supporting them. Yet many health systems continue to operate across hundreds of disconnected applications, databases, and platforms with inconsistent formats and varying data quality.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A model developed using clean and standardized information can perform very differently when exposed to fragmented real-world healthcare data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creating a unified data foundation is therefore critical. Organizations need architecture that supports data integrity, security, accessibility, and consistency across departments. A strong data governance and management strategy can provide the foundation for an enterprise data warehouse and create a more reliable environment for future AI initiatives.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Self-Authoring Capabilities</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every health system operates differently. Clinical protocols, patient populations, operational procedures, and local practices can vary significantly from one organization to another.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI infrastructure should therefore support controlled customization. Health systems should be able to adapt approved AI tools to their specific requirements without depending entirely on vendors for every modification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When properly governed, self-authoring capabilities can shorten the path from identifying an insight to implementing it locally. Clear standards and controls are necessary to ensure that customized solutions remain reliable, consistent, secure, and aligned with organizational objectives.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Workflow Integration and Redesign</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI solutions that operate outside established clinical and operational workflows often struggle to achieve meaningful adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Effective implementations place AI directly where work happens. Examples include E/M coding tools integrated into billing workflows, chart abstraction solutions positioned alongside reference information, and medication safety checks embedded within computerized provider order entry systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, simply adding AI to an inefficient process does not necessarily create value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should also reconsider whether the workflow itself needs to change. For example, an emergency department triage system could make nurse assessments faster, but patients may still experience delays if there are no available beds.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A more effective approach could use AI to identify the appropriate destination for patients and improve bed allocation and patient flow. The objective should not simply be to automate an existing bottleneck. Otherwise, organizations may end up scaling inefficient processes at machine speed instead of solving the underlying problem.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Structured Governance</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional IT governance structures were not designed for the unique challenges and risks associated with AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As a result, some healthcare organizations face governance bottlenecks where even relatively low-risk AI applications require months of review. Without defined risk classifications, monitoring requirements, and approval pathways, innovation can become unnecessarily slow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A tiered governance framework can address this challenge by matching oversight to the potential level of risk. Lower-risk applications can follow streamlined review processes, while high-impact clinical applications receive more comprehensive evaluation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear ownership, documented audit trails, ongoing monitoring, and the ability to pause or roll back an AI model are essential safeguards. These controls allow organizations to move faster while maintaining patient safety and organizational accountability.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuous Monitoring and Feedback</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI governance cannot end when a model goes into production.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many organizations still depend heavily on retrospective reviews, meaning problems may only become visible weeks or months after they occur. Modern AI systems require more continuous monitoring to identify data drift, performance changes, unexpected behavior, and potential safety concerns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Effective monitoring frameworks can track model performance, clinical outcomes, user behavior, and other operational indicators in near real time. Automated alerts can help identify inappropriate use or deteriorating performance before those issues become larger problems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Early detection supports faster intervention while helping maintain clinician confidence and trust in AI-enabled systems.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Infrastructure Creates Compounding Benefits</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A strong AI infrastructure layer does more than reduce implementation risks. It can create a cycle of continuous improvement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reliable data and active governance support better AI outcomes. Better outcomes increase clinician confidence. Increased confidence encourages broader adoption, which generates more operational experience and feedback. That feedback can then improve future implementations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The right infrastructure can also make it significantly easier to introduce new AI capabilities as clinical evidence evolves.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, if an organization has already established standardized data pipelines, governance processes, monitoring capabilities, and workflow integration patterns, a new drug-interaction checking capability could potentially be deployed across relevant workflows in days instead of requiring months of preparation.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Healthcare Organizations Are Still Struggling</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many health systems are still in the early stages of developing this foundation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial and technical limitations can certainly slow progress, but they are not the only barriers. Organizational alignment, prioritization, and collaboration can be equally important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI initiatives are often developed independently by clinical teams, operations groups, IT departments, and informatics leaders. When these efforts remain disconnected, organizations can end up with duplicated investments, inconsistent standards, and fragmented implementations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A successful AI strategy therefore requires more than technology investment. It requires coordinated leadership and a shared understanding of which problems the organization is trying to solve.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where Health System Leaders Can Begin</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations that have already encountered AI implementation roadblocks do not need to start over. They can use existing experiences to build a more sustainable foundation.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify the Problems That Matter Most</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI should not be treated as a solution in search of a problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders should begin with measurable clinical or business objectives where AI can have a meaningful near-term impact. Potential priorities could include reducing readmissions, decreasing documentation workloads, improving patient access, reducing administrative burden, or increasing operational throughput.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Starting with the problem rather than the technology helps organizations determine whether AI is actually the appropriate solution.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Build a Unified Data Platform</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A trusted and consistent data foundation is essential for analytics, AI development, and enterprise-scale deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consolidating fragmented data sources can improve data quality and make it easier to develop, validate, deploy, and scale future AI applications across the organization.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establish Efficient, Risk-Based Governance</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations need governance that supports innovation without compromising safety.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A risk-based framework can enable low-risk AI applications to move through streamlined approval processes while requiring more comprehensive evaluation for high-risk clinical systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance should also continue after deployment through technology-enabled monitoring, performance assessment, and defined intervention procedures.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Start With One High-Value Use Case</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations do not need to transform every workflow at once.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A better strategy is to select one high-value use case and implement it end to end. This provides an opportunity to demonstrate how data architecture, governance, workflow integration, monitoring, and operational delivery work together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A successful implementation can then become a repeatable blueprint for additional AI initiatives across the health system.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Looking Ahead</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The AI systems introduced over the next three to five years are likely to make many of today\'s models look relatively primitive.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI will increasingly become embedded within real-world clinical and operational workflows. The distinction between traditional digital technology and AI-enabled technology will continue to become less obvious as intelligent capabilities become part of everyday healthcare processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">New infrastructure models could accelerate this transition. Infrastructure-as-a-service platforms may increasingly automate areas such as data normalization, governance, model deployment, and monitoring, reducing the distance between model development and safe production use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intelligent workflows are also likely to become standard. Instead of operating as separate applications, AI capabilities will increasingly augment individual steps within clinical and administrative processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But this evolution requires more than better technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations need an operating model and culture that support continuous experimentation, evaluation, learning, and improvement. Successful AI adoption will require balanced investment across people, processes, data, governance, and technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems that build this foundation today will be better positioned to adapt as AI capabilities evolve. They can deploy new solutions faster, respond to emerging evidence more effectively, and deliver safer, more efficient, and more responsive care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that delay infrastructure investment risk creating an expanding gap between AI experimentation and meaningful enterprise adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>The future of healthcare AI will not be determined only by how powerful the models become. It will also depend on whether health systems build the infrastructure required to make those models useful, trusted, and scalable.</strong></p>', 'Healthcare AI Infrastructure: Building for Scalable AI', 'Healthcare AI needs strong data, governance, workflows, and monitoring. Learn how infrastructure enables safer, scalable AI adoption.', 'healthcare AI, AI infrastructure, healthcare technology, healthcare artificial intelligence, clinical AI, healthcare data architecture, AI governance, healthcare AI adoption, health system AI, digital health, AI monitoring, healthcare innovation, clinical workflows, enterprise AI, healthcare data management', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-14 16:05:05', 'published', 54408, 0, 8, NULL, '2024-06-14 16:05:05', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(330, 2, 2, 14, 'Key Steps Toward Optimizing Transformation in Healthcare in 2025', 'key-steps-toward-optimizing-transformation-in-healthcare-in-2025', 'Healthcare leaders can balance proven practices and emerging technology through six steps that improve efficiency, innovation, and patient care.', '[\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Healthcare Transformation\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Digital Health\",\"Healthcare Innovation\",\"Generative AI\",\"Organizational Transformation\",\"Healthcare Leadership\",\"Clinical Workflows\",\"Digital Transformation\"]', 'banner_image-1786364575753-161620009.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Steps Toward Optimizing Transformation in Healthcare in 2025</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence and other emerging technologies have generated enormous enthusiasm across the healthcare industry. From generative AI to ambient clinical intelligence, healthcare organizations are being presented with an expanding range of technologies that promise to improve patient care, reduce administrative workloads, and transform how healthcare is delivered.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But innovation alone does not guarantee better outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare leaders, the challenge in 2025 is not simply deciding which new technology to adopt. It is determining where transformation can create meaningful value while continuing to strengthen the systems, processes, and capabilities that already work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This requires what is often described as <strong>organizational ambidexterity</strong> - the ability to improve and maximize existing capabilities while simultaneously exploring new opportunities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In healthcare, that means protecting the value of established clinical practices and operational processes while remaining open to technologies, treatments, workflows, and business models that could significantly improve patient outcomes and organizational performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations best positioned for long-term success will be those that can manage both sides of this equation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizational Ambidexterity in Healthcare</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations have decades of accumulated clinical knowledge, operational experience, policies, and established workflows. These capabilities form the foundation of safe and effective care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, healthcare is experiencing rapid technological change. Artificial intelligence, digital health platforms, advanced analytics, virtual care, automation, and new patient engagement models are creating opportunities that did not exist just a few years ago.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare executives are therefore operating between two competing priorities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If organizations focus too heavily on established practices, they may miss opportunities to improve efficiency and patient care. But if they pursue transformation without sufficient discipline, they can spend heavily on technologies that fail to solve meaningful problems or introduce unnecessary operational and patient-safety risks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A balanced approach allows organizations to optimize what they already have while deliberately exploring what comes next.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The following six steps can help healthcare leaders achieve that balance.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Step 1: Establish Clear Objectives Before Choosing Technology</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transformation should begin with a clearly defined problem rather than a technology trend.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should first determine what they are trying to improve, how success will be measured, and whether the problem can be addressed using existing resources.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Optimize Existing Capabilities First</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">UNC Health provides an example of how improving established systems can deliver substantial results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization identified an opportunity to improve its patient messaging process and reduce the workload placed on clinicians. Rather than immediately introducing an entirely new technology platform, UNC Health modified policies, educated patients and staff, adjusted EHR configurations, and introduced team-based workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The result was a 25% reduction in patient messages reaching clinicians\' inboxes and a reduction of as much as 60% in the time staff spent handling messages.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The lesson is straightforward: organizations should not automatically associate transformation with purchasing new technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders can begin by:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying specific operational pain points.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Auditing existing workflows and systems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establishing measurable improvement targets.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Training employees on existing capabilities.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Testing incremental process changes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measuring results before making major investments.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The key question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What can we improve using the resources and systems we already have?</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Explore New Technology When Existing Tools Are Not Enough</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sometimes, however, existing systems cannot solve the problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Permanente Medical Group (TPMG), for example, explored ambient intelligence to reduce the documentation burden experienced by clinicians. During a 10-week pilot, approximately one-third of eligible clinicians used technology capable of automatically generating clinical notes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The participating physicians were able to reduce documentation time without compromising note quality.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this case, the objective could not be fully achieved through existing capabilities, creating a strong reason to investigate an emerging technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should therefore identify capability gaps first and then evaluate technologies such as generative AI, automation, or ambient intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question becomes:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Does the proposed technology solve a defined problem that our existing systems cannot adequately address?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology should be the answer to a business or clinical need - not the starting point.</p><hr><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Step 2: Build Cross-Functional Teams</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare transformation rarely succeeds when decisions are made by a single department.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical teams understand patient-care requirements. IT teams understand technology infrastructure. Data specialists understand analytics. Legal and compliance professionals understand regulatory risks. Operations teams understand how processes function in practice.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bringing these perspectives together can reduce blind spots and improve decision-making.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use Collaboration to Improve Existing Processes</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Texas Children\'s demonstrated the value of cross-functional collaboration by bringing together clinicians, research nurses, data specialists, and other stakeholders to implement a comprehensive \"Three Systems Approach.\"</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach combined analytics, content, and deployment systems to improve appendectomy procedures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By incorporating real-time analytics and evidence-based content into daily clinical operations, the team was able to develop better care practices, increase clinician confidence, and improve performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cross-functional teams can help organizations understand inefficiencies from multiple perspectives and develop solutions that are practical rather than theoretical.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders should ask:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Which stakeholders need to work together to improve our current processes?</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use Diverse Expertise to Explore Innovation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cross-functional collaboration becomes even more important when organizations introduce emerging technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mayo Clinic partnered with Google to create an \"AI Factory,\" bringing together consultants, educators, clinical professionals, and legal experts to help overcome organizational silos and barriers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The model provides a structured environment where different groups can collaborate on AI initiatives while considering clinical, technical, ethical, and regulatory requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This type of collaboration can help healthcare organizations explore emerging technologies more safely and avoid duplicated efforts or poorly coordinated initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question for leaders is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Which stakeholders must be involved to pursue innovation safely and strategically?</strong></p><hr><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Step 3: Evaluate Organizational Readiness for Change</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Having the right technology does not mean an organization is ready to use it effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Readiness should be evaluated at two levels: the organization\'s ability to optimize existing capabilities and its preparedness to adopt new technologies.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measure How Effectively Existing Tools Are Being Used</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should first determine whether they are getting the full value from their current systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, work with Omada Health resulted in the development of 10 key performance indicators for evaluating readiness to leverage data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization introduced relatively straightforward, data-driven improvements, including alerts that prompted providers to follow up with patients within seven days of an initial encounter.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These changes contributed to a six-point increase in patient Net Promoter Scores over six months.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The example demonstrates that transformation does not always require a major technology implementation. Better use of existing information and workflows can also produce measurable improvements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations should regularly assess:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Whether existing tools are being fully utilized.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where processes are producing unnecessary delays.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Whether employees have sufficient training.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which data can support better decision-making.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where small changes could produce measurable improvements.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question to ask is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Are we prepared to get the maximum value from the capabilities we already possess?</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prepare for Responsible Technology Adoption</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When new technologies are required, organizational readiness becomes even more important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intermountain Healthcare established a Data Science and Artificial Intelligence Center of Excellence to create standards, establish objectives, and provide guidance for AI-related change.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization also developed an AI Playbook designed to support the responsible deployment and scaling of human-centered AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its framework addresses areas such as AI governance, validation, documentation, bias detection, data integrity, privacy, and AI literacy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Such preparation helps organizations understand the risks associated with emerging technologies before attempting to scale them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should establish:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and technology governance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Validation and testing procedures.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data-quality standards.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privacy and security controls.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ethical guidelines.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bias-monitoring processes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Employee training and AI literacy programs.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The key question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Are we organizationally prepared to adopt and scale new technology responsibly?</strong></p><hr><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Step 4: Allocate Resources According to Strategic Value</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transformation also requires disciplined financial decision-making.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations have limited budgets, and every technology investment competes with existing operational, clinical, and patient-care priorities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mayo Clinic Platform has used a technology budgeting framework built around several important considerations.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ask the Right Questions Before Investing</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should determine:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is the technology strategic? Does it address a genuine organizational priority?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Does it improve quality or safety? Could failing to adopt it create a disadvantage in patient care?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is there a regulatory or compliance requirement? Could the technology help the organization address an emerging mandate?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What is the potential impact? How many patients, clinicians, employees, or other stakeholders could benefit?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What is the financial impact? Can the investment reduce costs, increase revenue, or materially improve financial performance?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These questions help separate meaningful investments from technology purchased primarily because it is new or fashionable.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question to ask is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Does this investment create measurable strategic value, or are we adopting it simply because it is exciting?</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gradually Shift Resources Toward Transformation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resource allocation should also evolve as organizational efficiency improves.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">UW Health, for example, divides its technology spending into three broad areas:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Running the business.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Growing the business.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transforming the business.</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization initially dedicates a larger share of resources to maintaining core operations. As efficiency improves and operating costs decrease, more resources can be redirected toward growth and transformation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a sustainable path toward innovation without weakening the organization\'s core capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should therefore consider how operational savings can be reinvested into strategic transformation initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Are we dedicating sufficient resources to maintain and improve our core operations?</strong></p><hr><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Step 5: Create a Culture of Controlled Experimentation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experimentation is important for both operational improvement and technological innovation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should encourage employees to question inefficient processes, test new ideas, and learn from results without putting core operations at unnecessary risk.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experiment With Existing Processes</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Researchers at MD Anderson Cancer Center used patient flow analysis to identify workflow inefficiencies and develop tactics for improving patient movement through the organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach demonstrates how organizations can use experimentation to improve existing processes without immediately replacing them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders can support this culture by:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Giving teams permission to test small improvements.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Using real-time data to evaluate experiments.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establishing measurable success criteria.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creating safe boundaries around experiments.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sharing lessons across departments.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recognizing useful learning even when an experiment does not produce the expected result.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Where can teams test incremental improvements without disrupting essential operations?</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Create Safe Environments for Emerging Technologies</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experimentation is equally valuable when organizations explore new technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Researchers at NYU Langone Health developed NYUTron, a large language model-based system integrated into clinical workflows involving documentation and order entry.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The model demonstrated strong performance in predicting 30-day readmissions as well as other outcomes, including insurance denials and length of stay.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The experience illustrates the importance of combining technical expertise with clinical knowledge and agile experimentation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When introducing emerging technologies, organizations should begin with controlled pilots rather than immediate large-scale deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Successful pilots can then be evaluated against clearly defined objectives before being expanded.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>How can we safely test emerging technologies while measuring their real-world impact?</strong></p><hr><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Step 6: Build Continuous Feedback Loops</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transformation does not end when a process or technology is implemented.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations need mechanisms that continuously capture feedback from patients, clinicians, employees, and other stakeholders.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Feedback helps leaders understand whether a change is actually producing the expected results and where further improvements are required.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use Feedback to Improve Existing Operations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cleveland Clinic developed a Severity Scale to categorize, assess, and prioritize patient complaints.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The system provided leaders with more detailed information about where problems were occurring.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Analysis showed that approximately 25% of grievances were connected to communication breakdowns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This information enabled leadership teams to develop targeted patient-experience strategies, establish specific performance objectives, and allocate resources more effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can similarly use:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Patient surveys.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Employee feedback.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Focus groups.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Operational dashboards.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical performance data.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Complaint analysis.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Workflow metrics.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is to create a continuous improvement cycle rather than relying on occasional reviews.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question to ask is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>How are we using stakeholder feedback to continuously improve current operations?</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Apply Feedback to New Technologies</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Feedback becomes even more important when implementing emerging technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During the development and deployment of SepsisWatch, researchers at Duke Health used feedback from end users to continually improve the AI-based tool.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The process incorporated established design principles, subject-matter expertise, and professionals specializing in user experience and user interface design.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This helped evolve the system into a tool that clinicians wanted to use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The lesson is that successful healthcare technology must fit the people and workflows it is designed to support.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders should therefore create formal feedback mechanisms throughout the technology lifecycle.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Are we using stakeholder feedback effectively to adapt new technologies to real-world needs?</strong></p><hr><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Forward: Balancing Optimization and Transformation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare transformation does not require organizations to abandon everything they already know.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In fact, some of the most valuable improvements may come from better use of existing systems, workflows, data, and expertise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, healthcare organizations cannot afford to ignore technologies that can fundamentally improve how care is delivered.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The solution is organizational ambidexterity: <strong>optimize what works while deliberately exploring what could work better.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The six-step framework provides a practical approach:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Define clear objectives before choosing technology.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Build cross-functional teams that bring diverse expertise together.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Assess readiness for change across people, processes, data, and technology.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Allocate resources appropriately according to strategic and patient-centered value.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Foster controlled experimentation to improve existing processes and evaluate new solutions.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Integrate continuous feedback loops to refine both operations and technology.</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders who adopt this balanced approach can avoid two extremes: becoming so focused on tradition that they miss opportunities for progress, or pursuing innovation so aggressively that they create unnecessary cost and risk.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of healthcare will require both stability and experimentation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that can strengthen their existing foundations while intelligently exploring new capabilities will be better positioned to improve operational performance, support clinicians, enhance patient experiences, and navigate the continued evolution of healthcare technology.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">About the Authors</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Brian R. Spisak, PhD</strong> is the chief people and communication officer and senior partner at Csuite Growth Advisors, an organization focused on innovation and digital transformation in healthcare. He also serves as Program Director of AI and Leadership at Harvard University\'s National Preparedness Leadership Initiative and is a faculty member at the American College of Healthcare Executives. Spisak is the author of <em>Computational Leadership: Connecting Behavioral Science and Technology to Optimize Decision-Making and Increase Profits</em>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Tom Lawry</strong> is managing director of Second Century Tech LLC and a former Microsoft executive who held leadership roles including National Director for AI for Health and Life Sciences, Director of Worldwide Health, and Director of Organizational Performance for Microsoft\'s first health incubator. He has also served as a health system executive and board member and is the author of <em>Hacking Healthcare – How AI and the Intelligence Revolution will Reboot an Ailing System</em>.</p>', 'Healthcare Transformation: 6 Key Steps for 2025', 'Discover six practical steps healthcare leaders can use to balance existing capabilities with AI, digital innovation, and better patient outcomes.', 'healthcare transformation, healthcare technology, AI in healthcare, digital transformation in healthcare, healthcare innovation, digital health, healthcare AI, healthcare leadership, organizational transformation, patient care', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-18 11:48:36', 'published', 28283, 0, 14, NULL, '2024-06-18 11:48:36', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(331, 2, 2, 5, 'Know Thyself: An Analytics-Based Approach to Combating Living-off-the-Land Attacks', 'know-thyself-an-analytics-based-approach-to-combating-living-off-the-land-attacks', 'Learn how behavioral analytics and endpoint baselining can help healthcare organizations detect and stop living-off-the-land attacks.', '[\"Cybersecurity\",\"Living-off-the-Land Attacks\",\"LOL Attacks\",\"LOLBAS\",\"Ransomware\",\"Endpoint Security\",\"EDR\",\"Threat Detection\",\"Behavioral Analytics\",\"Healthcare Cybersecurity\",\"PowerShell\",\"Windows Security\"]', 'banner_image-1786364838504-382224670.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Know Thyself: An Analytics-Based Approach to Combating Living-off-the-Land Attacks</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Aristotle’s well-known idea that knowing yourself is the foundation of wisdom is just as relevant to cybersecurity as it is to everyday life. For modern healthcare organizations, understanding their own technology environments can be one of the most effective ways to recognize and prevent cyber threats.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations continue to face ransomware and other sophisticated cyberattacks. Although attacker dwell times have generally declined, threat actors can still remain inside a network for extended periods. During this time, they may study internal systems, move between devices, steal sensitive information, and establish broad access before deploying ransomware or another destructive payload.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This raises an important security question: <strong>Do organizations understand what normal activity actually looks like across their own IT environments?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If security teams cannot confidently identify normal behavior, detecting abnormal behavior becomes significantly more difficult.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many security programs primarily concentrate on identifying known malicious activity. While this remains important, organizations should also invest in understanding <strong>known-good behavior</strong>. Establishing a reliable baseline of normal activity can provide critical context when suspicious activity appears.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Growing Challenge of Living-off-the-Land Attacks</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding what behavior is expected-and where that behavior should occur-has become increasingly important as attackers adopt more <strong>living-off-the-land (LOL)</strong> techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Living-off-the-land attacks involve abusing legitimate tools and applications that are already available within an operating system or commonly deployed throughout an organization. Instead of introducing obviously malicious software, attackers can use trusted utilities to perform malicious activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach can make detection and prevention more challenging. Security products may be reluctant to completely block legitimate operating-system tools because doing so could disrupt legitimate business operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, attackers may abuse the Windows <code>bcdedit</code> command, which is legitimately designed to configure boot settings and can be used for troubleshooting. An attacker could potentially manipulate such functionality to interfere with endpoint security controls or prepare a system for further compromise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other commonly abused utilities include <strong>PowerShell, cscript, wscript, certutil</strong>, and many others. The <strong>LOLBAS (Living Off The Land Binaries and Scripts)</strong> project provides extensive information about legitimate Windows binaries and scripts that can potentially be abused by attackers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With the large number of available LOL techniques, attempting to block everything may seem unrealistic. However, organizations can significantly reduce the effectiveness of these techniques by applying straightforward data analytics and establishing behavioral baselines.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Start With Endpoint Execution Data</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can begin by using an <strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)</strong> platform or another endpoint security solution to identify and record the execution of selected LOL binaries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of immediately blocking every suspicious utility, security teams can collect execution information over time. Useful data points may include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How frequently the binary is executed</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which users execute it</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which machines execute it</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where the binary or associated script is launched from</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What parent process initiated the execution</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which command-line arguments were used</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Whether the activity occurs during expected business processes</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Over time, this information creates a behavioral picture of the organization\'s environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once enough data has been collected, LOL binaries and related behaviors can generally be grouped into several useful categories.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Widely Used Binaries</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some legitimate binaries are used extensively throughout an organization. Microsoft Office applications are a good example. Applications such as PowerPoint may be executed by a large number of employees every day.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attempting to block such an application entirely would obviously create operational problems. Generating an alert every time it runs would also produce an enormous amount of noise, making the resulting alerts difficult for security teams to use effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, security teams can add additional context to their detection logic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, blocking PowerPoint itself would be impractical. However, detecting a scenario in which PowerPoint launches PowerShell may provide a much more meaningful security signal, particularly when that execution pattern is inconsistent with normal organizational behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Adding information such as the parent process, command-line arguments, user, machine, or execution location can transform a noisy detection into a useful security control.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Binaries That Are Never Used</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another valuable discovery may be that certain LOL binaries are not used anywhere within the organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some Windows utilities have legitimate functionality but may no longer have a practical business purpose. The deprecated Windows <code>AT</code> command is one example.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If an organization collects execution data for several months and discovers that a particular utility has never been legitimately executed, that information creates an opportunity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Such binaries may become strong candidates for:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blocking</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Alerting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Additional monitoring</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Application control policies</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The underlying principle is simple: <strong>if a tool has no legitimate role in the environment, unexpected execution deserves greater scrutiny.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Binaries Used by Specific Users or Machines</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some utilities may be legitimate, but only for a specific group of employees or devices.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an employee in the finance department might legitimately use an FTP client to exchange billing information. Similarly, members of the IT team may require an SSH client to connect to servers and network infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In those cases, completely blocking FTP or SSH tools across the entire organization could interfere with legitimate work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the same activity occurring on an unrelated endpoint can be much more suspicious.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An FTP client launched from a nursing workstation, for example, could warrant investigation because the behavior may be inconsistent with the normal role of that machine. Similarly, an SSH client running from an endpoint that does not normally administer infrastructure could indicate lateral movement or unauthorized access.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates an opportunity to build <strong>context-aware security policies</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can permit a particular behavior for approved users or machines while blocking or alerting on the same behavior elsewhere.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is an important security principle: <strong>security controls do not always need to be universal to be effective.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Making a large portion of the environment resistant to a particular attack technique can substantially reduce an attacker\'s available paths without disrupting legitimate business operations.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Binaries Associated With Approved Locations</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Execution location can provide another valuable layer of context.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many organizations rely on scripts to automate routine activities such as configuring workstations, mapping printers, or performing administrative tasks. As organizations grow, these scripts can become an important part of normal IT operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A useful security practice is to organize legitimate scripts into clearly defined and controlled locations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an organization might store approved login scripts in a secured network share named <strong>LoginScripts</strong> rather than distributing them across arbitrary directories.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once the organization understands this normal behavior, it can establish more precise controls around script interpreters.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consider <code>wscript</code> as an example. If legitimate user login scripts are consistently executed from the approved <code>LoginScripts</code> location, the organization could develop policies that allow <code>wscript</code> to execute scripts from that trusted location while generating an alert or blocking execution from unexpected locations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The same principle can be applied to PowerShell and other scripting technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This does not mean every execution outside the approved location is automatically malicious. Researchers, developers, data analysts, and IT professionals may have legitimate reasons to execute scripts from other directories.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, restricting script execution across the majority of endpoints while explicitly allowing approved exceptions can substantially reduce the attack surface.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Definition of Normal</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These four categories are not exhaustive. A detailed analysis of an organization\'s environment may reveal additional patterns that can be incorporated into its security baselines.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The important step is to begin collecting and analyzing behavioral data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than asking only:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>\"Is this tool malicious?\"</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security teams should also ask:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>\"Is this behavior normal for this user, device, location, and business process?\"</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That shift can significantly improve the value of security monitoring.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A binary that is harmless on one workstation may be highly suspicious on another. A PowerShell execution launched by an administrator as part of a documented maintenance process may be expected, while the same execution chain originating from an unexpected application on a standard workstation may require immediate investigation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Context transforms raw telemetry into actionable intelligence.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Analytics Into Security Controls</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once normal behavior has been established, organizations can use those baselines to create more precise detection and prevention mechanisms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These controls can include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blocking unused or unnecessary binaries</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitoring unusual parent-child process relationships</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Restricting tools to approved users or systems</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Limiting script interpreters to trusted execution locations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Alerting on unusual command-line parameters</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitoring unexpected execution paths</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creating exceptions for legitimate business processes</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuously reviewing and updating behavioral baselines</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach also helps avoid the operational problems associated with overly aggressive security controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than blocking a legitimate technology everywhere, organizations can identify where it is actually needed and restrict its use everywhere else.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding the Environment to Reduce Risk</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Living-off-the-land techniques are effective partly because they allow attackers to hide their actions among legitimate system activity. The tools themselves may not be malicious, but the way they are used can reveal malicious intent.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That is why understanding the organization\'s normal operating environment is so important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When security teams know which applications normally execute, who runs them, where they are launched from, what processes start them, and which systems are expected to use them, deviations become much easier to identify.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not necessarily to eliminate every LOL technique. Instead, organizations should make it increasingly difficult for attackers to use legitimate tools in unexpected ways.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Behavioral analytics, endpoint telemetry, and thoughtful baselining can help accomplish this without unnecessarily disrupting legitimate operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ultimately, cybersecurity resilience begins with understanding the environment being protected.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By developing a clear definition of <strong>normal</strong>, organizations can more effectively recognize abnormal behavior, strengthen their defenses against living-off-the-land techniques, and respond to threats before they develop into widespread compromises.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In cybersecurity, knowing yourself is not merely a philosophical principle. It can become a practical defensive strategy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>About the Author:</strong> Christopher Frenz is the Information Security Officer and AVP of IT Security at Mount Sinai South Nassau.</p>', 'Know Thyself: Analytics Against Living-off-the-Land Attacks', 'Learn how behavioral analytics and endpoint baselining help healthcare organizations detect, restrict, and prevent living-off-the-land attacks.', 'living-off-the-land attacks, LOL attacks, LOLBAS, cybersecurity analytics, behavioral analytics, endpoint security, EDR, ransomware, healthcare cybersecurity, PowerShell security, Windows security, threat detection, cyber threat prevention, endpoint baselining, security monitoring', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-21 15:31:07', 'published', 148194, 1, 9, NULL, '2024-06-21 15:31:07', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(332, 2, 2, 5, '10 Tips to Avoid Planting AI Time Bombs in Your Organization', '10-tips-to-avoid-planting-ai-time-bombs-in-your-organization', 'Discover 10 practical strategies for deploying AI safely, ethically and sustainably while avoiding costly risks, bias and failures in healthcare.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Healthcare AI\",\"AI Governance\",\"Responsible AI\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Generative AI\",\"AI Risk Management\",\"Data Governance\",\"Ethical AI\",\"AI Transformation\",\"Healthcare Innovation\",\"Large Language Models\",\"LLMs\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Patient-Centric AI\"]', 'banner_image-1786364977639-536763986.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">10 Tips to Avoid Planting AI Time Bombs in Your Organization</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is rapidly transforming healthcare, but successful AI adoption requires much more than selecting the latest technology. Healthcare leaders must understand the organizational, ethical, regulatory and operational realities that come with AI transformation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal should be to move beyond the hype and fear of missing out and develop a mature approach to AI-one that brings leaders and teams together around clear, measurable goals for improving outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This post-hype perspective was the focus of a recent healthcare leadership discussion. The response demonstrated how much interest there is in understanding what responsible AI transformation actually looks like in practice.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Several AI initiatives have already revealed what can happen when organizations move too quickly. These examples provide important lessons for healthcare executives looking to adopt AI safely and sustainably.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Not to Do</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Across healthcare and other industries, rushed AI deployments are already creating significant problems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One well-known example involved Air Canada\'s customer-facing chatbot, which incorrectly promised a passenger a discounted flight. When the company later argued that the chatbot was effectively responsible for the inaccurate information, a Canadian tribunal rejected that defense and required the airline to honor the commitment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The incident demonstrates an important principle: organizations cannot simply transfer responsibility to an AI system when something goes wrong. Businesses remain accountable for the technologies they deploy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another example involved the National Eating Disorders Association and its planned use of Tessa, a chatbot intended to support people seeking information about eating disorders. Shortly before its planned launch, the system was found to be generating potentially harmful recommendations, including restrictive calorie intake, frequent weighing and rigid weight-loss targets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although Tessa was never put into operation, the incident demonstrated how quickly an AI initiative can become dangerous when appropriate testing, oversight and safeguards are not established.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research published in JAMA Network Open has also highlighted concerns surrounding biased algorithms and their potential contribution to racial and ethnic disparities in healthcare. Such systems can influence access to services, eligibility for interventions and the allocation of healthcare resources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The concern becomes even greater when problematic algorithms remain embedded in operational systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In simple terms, AI \"time bombs\" have already gone off. Without proactive governance and risk management, more organizations are likely to encounter similar failures.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What to Do: 10 Tips for Safer AI Transformation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders can reduce these risks by establishing a structured approach to AI adoption. The following ten principles can help organizations build safer, more sustainable AI initiatives.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Prioritize Transparency and Explainability</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should favor AI systems that provide understandable reasoning and transparent outputs. Leaders and users need to know how an AI system reaches conclusions, particularly when those conclusions influence important healthcare decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Implement Strong Data Governance</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-quality AI depends on high-quality data. Organizations should establish strong data governance practices covering data accuracy, diversity, labeling, privacy, security and appropriate usage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Poor or incomplete data can result in unreliable outputs and may reinforce existing disparities.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Engage Ethical and Regulatory Experts Early</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ethical and regulatory considerations should be addressed before an AI solution reaches deployment. Involving appropriate experts early can help organizations identify potential problems, meet applicable requirements and avoid expensive changes later.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most importantly, early oversight helps protect patients and other stakeholders.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Encourage Interdisciplinary Collaboration</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI transformation should not be owned exclusively by technology teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinicians, data scientists, IT professionals, executives, compliance teams, ethicists and other stakeholders should work together throughout the process. This interdisciplinary approach helps ensure that AI solutions are practical, responsible and aligned with patient needs.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. Design for Scalability and Interoperability</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI solutions should work effectively with existing healthcare technology environments. Systems that cannot integrate with current infrastructure can create additional complexity and operational costs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders should also consider whether an AI solution can scale across departments, facilities or healthcare organizations as adoption expands.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">6. Invest in Continuous Education and Training</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI adoption changes how employees work and make decisions. Continuous training is therefore essential.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare professionals should understand how to use AI tools, interpret their outputs, recognize limitations and apply human judgment when AI recommendations may be incomplete or incorrect.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">7. Maintain a Patient-Centric Approach</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology should ultimately improve the patient experience and quality of care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI initiatives should support patient engagement, personalized care and better outcomes without unintentionally increasing existing health disparities. Patient needs should remain central to technology decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">8. Monitor Performance and Impact Continuously</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI systems should not be treated as \"set it and forget it\" technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations need mechanisms for continuously evaluating performance and collecting feedback from employees, clinicians and patients. Monitoring allows teams to identify unexpected outcomes and improve systems over time.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">9. Establish Clear Accountability</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations must clearly define who is responsible for decisions made with AI assistance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can support decision-making, but accountability cannot simply be delegated to the technology. Clear governance structures help ensure that people remain responsible for evaluating AI outputs and making appropriate decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">10. Build an Ethical AI Culture</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responsible AI requires more than policies and technical controls. Organizations need a culture where employees can openly discuss AI risks, ethical concerns and unintended consequences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leadership should encourage responsible experimentation while ensuring that decisions consider the interests and welfare of patients, employees and other stakeholders.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These principles can serve as the foundation for AI policies, procedures and governance frameworks. Addressing these issues before deployment can save organizations time and money while reducing the possibility of serious harm.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Other Healthcare Organizations Are Doing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Successful AI transformation requires several organizational capabilities to work together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders need to move beyond conversations focused exclusively on individual tools such as ChatGPT and begin addressing the broader foundations of mature AI adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Two areas are particularly important: developing a holistic approach to AI deployment and building a strong, data-driven organizational culture.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creating a Blueprint for Large Language Model Deployment</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One healthcare system developed a structured blueprint for safely implementing large language models (LLMs).</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The framework considers multiple dimensions of AI deployment, including economic, privacy and operational implications. It also provides leaders with specific questions to evaluate the risks and benefits associated with each area.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The purpose was to give members of the C-suite a shared framework for discussing AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Different executives may naturally prioritize different objectives. One leader may focus on speed, while another prioritizes safety. Someone else may emphasize quality, cost or privacy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A common framework makes these trade-offs visible and gives leaders a shared language for discussing opportunities and risks.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Data-Driven Culture</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another healthcare organization focused on developing a stronger data-driven culture.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization established ten key performance indicators designed to evaluate whether its leaders, teams and processes were contributing to an AI-ready culture of care. A survey was also developed around these indicators to establish a baseline and identify areas requiring improvement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization placed particular emphasis on understanding clinicians\' data requirements and delivering useful, high-quality information when it was needed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The result was a rapid improvement in several important organizational indicators, including employee engagement and patient satisfaction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This example demonstrates an important lesson: AI transformation does not begin with the technology itself.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It begins with fundamentals such as data, processes, people and leadership.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that strengthen these foundations can create immediate improvements while simultaneously preparing themselves for more advanced AI capabilities.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Comes Next?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of healthcare AI requires a <strong>\"leadership first, technology last\"</strong> mindset.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Executives should begin by understanding the needs of their people, the challenges within their processes and the opportunities available to improve outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A systematic, evidence-based approach can help organizations understand how they operate and identify where change is most valuable. High-quality data can then provide the insights required to guide those changes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, data and technology alone are not enough.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Effective leaders combine evidence and data with their experience and judgment to develop solutions appropriate for their organization\'s unique circumstances.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This philosophy aligns with the American Medical Association\'s use of the term <strong>\"augmented intelligence\"</strong> to describe AI. The concept emphasizes technology\'s role in strengthening human intelligence rather than simply replacing it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human judgment, experience, creativity and emotional intelligence should remain central to important healthcare decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that preserve these human qualities while thoughtfully adopting AI will be better positioned to build a mature and sustainable AI-powered future.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conclusion</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can create tremendous opportunities for healthcare organizations, but rushing into transformation without adequate preparation can produce costly and potentially harmful consequences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most successful organizations will not necessarily be those that adopt the most AI tools. They will be those that build the strongest foundations for using AI responsibly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By prioritizing transparency, data governance, collaboration, training, interoperability, patient needs, continuous monitoring and accountability, healthcare leaders can reduce AI risks while maximizing the technology\'s potential.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of healthcare AI should therefore begin with leadership, people and data-not technology hype.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>About the Author</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brian R. Spisak, PhD, is an independent consultant specializing in digital transformation in healthcare. He is also a research associate at the National Preparedness Leadership Initiative at Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, a faculty member at the American College of Healthcare Executives and the author of <em>Computational Leadership</em>.</p>', '10 Tips to Avoid AI Time Bombs in Healthcare', 'Learn 10 practical strategies for safer healthcare AI adoption, from data governance and transparency to accountability, training and ethical oversight.', 'healthcare AI, artificial intelligence in healthcare, AI risk management, responsible AI, healthcare AI governance, ethical AI, AI transformation, healthcare technology, AI safety, healthcare data governance, generative AI healthcare, AI adoption, LLM healthcare, patient-centric AI, digital healthcare transformation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-25 13:14:38', 'published', 126121, 0, 8, NULL, '2024-06-25 13:14:38', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(333, 2, 2, 5, 'Seeing Through the Security Illusion', 'seeing-through-the-security-illusion', 'Security tools alone cannot stop modern attacks. Healthcare organizations need built-in security, hardened systems, and evidence-based defense.', '[\"Cybersecurity\",\"Healthcare Cybersecurity\",\"Ransomware\",\"Data Breaches\",\"Network Security\",\"Zero Trust\",\"Defense in Depth\",\"Application Security\",\"Endpoint Security\",\"Security Tools\",\"Threat Detection\",\"PowerShell\",\"Active Directory\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Security Strategy\"]', 'banner_image-1786365156533-6333873.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Seeing Through the Security Illusion</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Over the past several years, the security industry has witnessed a troubling contradiction. Organizations are investing more heavily than ever in cybersecurity products and technologies, yet data breaches and ransomware incidents continue to occur at an alarming rate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security budgets have expanded, new endpoint protection platforms have been deployed, monitoring capabilities have improved, and organizations have added layer after layer of security technology. Despite all of this investment, attackers continue to compromise environments and disrupt critical operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For security leaders, this should prompt an important question: <strong>Why are increased investments in security technology not consistently producing better security outcomes?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The problem is not that security tools have no value. They are an essential component of a modern security program. The problem is relying on those tools alone and assuming that deploying them automatically creates a secure environment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security Must Be Designed In</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Application security has taught the industry an important lesson for years: security should be built into an application rather than added after development is complete.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An application designed with secure coding practices, input validation, proper authentication, output encoding and other security controls will generally be much more resilient than an insecure application simply placed behind a firewall or another security product.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There is extensive evidence supporting this approach within application security. The same principle should apply across the broader enterprise, particularly within healthcare delivery organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If security cannot simply be bolted onto an application after the fact, why should we assume that deploying security products across a healthcare network will automatically make the entire environment secure?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The answer is that it will not.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security technologies provide important capabilities, but they are only one part of a broader security architecture. Organizations need to deliberately design their networks, systems and configurations so that security controls work together and compensate for one another.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Legitimate Tools Can Become Attack Weapons</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One example is the increasing use of legitimate administrative and IT tools by threat actors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers frequently use \"living off the land\" techniques, relying on software and utilities that already exist within the target environment. Tools such as ADFind and ADExplorer can have legitimate administrative purposes, particularly for Active Directory management and troubleshooting.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the same functionality can be extremely valuable to an attacker conducting reconnaissance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a difficult problem for endpoint security products. Security vendors cannot simply block every legitimate administrative utility because doing so could interfere with normal business operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consequently, these tools may not be blocked by default.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That does not necessarily represent a weakness in the security product. Rather, it demonstrates why default security configurations cannot be expected to prevent every possible phase of an attack.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization itself has to provide additional security controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, ADFind may be entirely appropriate on systems used by an Active Directory administrator. But there is little reason for a physician, nurse or many other healthcare employees to have access to the same utility.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That difference creates an opportunity to strengthen security.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can restrict the execution of administrative tools to systems and users that legitimately require them. If ADFind is attempted on a workstation belonging to an employee who has no operational reason to use it, that activity can become a meaningful security signal.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This type of network and system hardening can significantly strengthen the protection provided by endpoint security products.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Default Configurations Are Not Enough</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A similar principle applies when organizations evaluate the actual effectiveness of their security controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security products are capable of detecting and stopping many attack techniques, but no detection mechanism is perfect. A security strategy that assumes every malicious action will be identified by an endpoint product, firewall, SIEM or other technology is inherently fragile.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PowerShell provides a useful example.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Older versions of PowerShell, including PowerShell 2, provide considerably less security visibility than modern versions. They lack several of the logging and security integration capabilities available in newer releases, including integration with technologies such as AMSI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers can potentially attempt to downgrade their execution environment to take advantage of these differences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If an endpoint security product does not reliably identify every attack that uses such techniques, the organization needs another control to compensate for that weakness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One straightforward approach is system hardening.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If PowerShell 2 is not required for business operations, it should be disabled. More broadly, unnecessary services, protocols, software components and administrative capabilities should be removed or restricted wherever possible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is not to expect one security product to solve every problem. The goal is to reduce the number of opportunities available to an attacker in the first place.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers Test Our Defenses Too</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security professionals regularly use tools such as VirusTotal to investigate suspicious files and determine how different security products respond to them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers understand the value of this type of testing as well.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Threat actors can test their malware against multiple security technologies before deploying it. By doing so, they can identify which products detect their payloads and which configurations may allow their activity to remain hidden.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is therefore unrealistic to assume that attackers are unaware of the capabilities and limitations of the security products protecting their targets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In some situations, attackers may understand the practical effectiveness of security controls better than the organizations deploying them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This highlights a major weakness in the way security is sometimes managed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security decisions are still frequently based on assumptions such as \"this product should detect it\" or \"we already have protection against that.\"</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Those assumptions need to be replaced with evidence.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measure Security Control Effectiveness</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security should increasingly be treated as an evidence-based discipline.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of asking whether a security product is installed, organizations should ask whether the control actually works against the threats they are concerned about.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That means testing controls, measuring detection and prevention capabilities, identifying gaps and determining what additional protections are necessary.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, if testing demonstrates that an endpoint platform consistently detects one attack technique but performs poorly against another, that weakness should not simply be documented and forgotten.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization should introduce compensating controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Network segmentation, application allowlisting, identity restrictions, system hardening, privileged access controls and other defensive measures can reduce the attacker\'s ability to exploit the weakness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is the foundation of a true defense-in-depth strategy.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Security Into Healthcare Networks</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The need for this approach is especially important for healthcare delivery organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare environments contain highly interconnected systems, sensitive information and critical clinical technologies. A successful ransomware attack can therefore have consequences that extend far beyond data loss or financial damage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When critical systems become unavailable, patient care can also be affected.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations should consequently consider security when designing their networks rather than treating it as something that can be added later.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zero-trust principles, network segmentation, strong identity controls, least-privilege access and carefully designed administrative boundaries can help limit the impact of a compromise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systems deployed into those environments should also be hardened from the beginning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unnecessary software and services should be removed or disabled. Administrative tools should be available only where they are required. Legacy protocols and technologies should be eliminated when possible. Security configurations should be validated through testing rather than simply assumed to be effective.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Defense in Depth Is Essential</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">No individual security technology is capable of stopping every attack.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Endpoint security has limitations. Network security controls have limitations. Identity systems have limitations. Detection technologies have limitations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That is precisely why defense in depth matters.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If one control fails, another should reduce the attacker\'s ability to continue. If an endpoint product misses an attack, network segmentation may restrict movement. If an attacker obtains credentials, least-privilege access can limit what those credentials can do. If malware bypasses a detection mechanism, application restrictions and system hardening can provide another barrier.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not to create a perfect security product.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is to create an environment in which bypassing one control does not automatically give an attacker unrestricted access to the organization.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Beyond the Security Illusion</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The cybersecurity industry has spent years increasing investment in security technologies, but continued breaches and ransomware attacks demonstrate that technology alone is not enough.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security leaders need to move beyond the assumption that purchasing and deploying another security product automatically improves security.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tools remain important. Organizations should continue investing in technologies that provide meaningful detection, prevention, monitoring and response capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But those technologies need to be surrounded by thoughtful architecture, hardened configurations, access restrictions, segmentation and compensating controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most importantly, organizations need to measure whether their security controls actually work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A mature security program should be able to identify where its defenses are effective, where they are weak and what additional controls are required to close those gaps.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare delivery organizations, this is more than a cybersecurity maturity issue. The consequences of ransomware and other cyberattacks can directly affect patient safety and the delivery of healthcare.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The industry therefore needs to move past the illusion that buying security tools is equivalent to achieving security.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security needs to be <strong>designed in</strong>, not simply bolted on afterward.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The cost of failing to make that shift is too high-particularly when the safety and well-being of patients can be affected.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>About the Author:</strong> Christopher Frenz is Information Security Officer and AVP of IT Security at Mount Sinai South Nassau.</p>', 'Seeing Through the Security Illusion | Cybersecurity', 'Discover why security tools alone are not enough and how healthcare organizations can improve defense with hardened systems and layered security.', 'cybersecurity, healthcare cybersecurity, ransomware, data breaches, security tools, zero trust, defense in depth, network security, endpoint security, system hardening, PowerShell security, Active Directory security, healthcare IT security, security strategy, threat detection', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-26 17:57:09', 'published', 6023, 0, 9, NULL, '2024-06-26 17:57:09', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
