(334, 2, 2, 5, 'As AI Evolves, Stronger Security Coordination Becomes Essential', 'as-ai-evolves-stronger-security-coordination-becomes-essential', 'As AI capabilities advance, governments and industries are strengthening cybersecurity coordination to address emerging AI and quantum security risks.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI Security\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"AI Governance\",\"Healthcare Cybersecurity\",\"Frontier AI\",\"Quantum Computing\",\"Post-Quantum Cryptography\",\"AI Risk Management\",\"Data Security\",\"Critical Infrastructure\",\"Cyber Resilience\",\"Anthropic\",\"Project Glasswing\",\"OECD\",\"NIST\"]', 'banner_image-1786367427499-276990628.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is advancing at an extraordinary pace, but the growing capabilities of frontier AI systems are also introducing new cybersecurity challenges. As AI becomes more capable of identifying vulnerabilities, generating code and supporting sophisticated technical operations, organizations and governments are increasingly focused on how these capabilities can be secured and responsibly managed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recent developments highlight the growing need for coordinated action across the public and private sectors. New initiatives involving AI companies and governments, evolving U.S. policies around AI security, and international efforts focused on quantum technology all point toward the same challenge: security strategies must evolve alongside technological capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Three developments particularly illustrate this shift.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Project Glasswing expands its cybersecurity testing network</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Anthropic\'s Project Glasswing, an AI-focused cybersecurity initiative developed in collaboration with government and industry organizations, has expanded its testing program to include 150 additional organizations from critical sectors, including healthcare.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations were selected because compromises affecting their software or codebases could have significant consequences. The expanded group includes organizations working across areas such as power, water, healthcare, communications and hardware.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Anthropic, the participating organizations span 15 countries and include companies, nonprofits, technology providers and government-related organizations whose software may support large numbers of other institutions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For many of these organizations, a successful cyberattack could potentially affect millions of people and create broader implications for national and international security.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective of Project Glasswing is to help organizations reconsider traditional cybersecurity assumptions as AI changes the way vulnerabilities are discovered, analyzed and potentially exploited.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The initiative is particularly relevant to healthcare, where hospitals, health IT vendors and other organizations manage sensitive information and operate systems that are increasingly connected to cloud platforms and AI technologies.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI model vulnerabilities create new security questions</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The expansion of Project Glasswing came as Anthropic prepared to provide participating organizations with access to its newer AI capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, concerns surrounding model security and export controls subsequently created additional complications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">U.S. Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick issued directives concerning Anthropic\'s Mythos 5 and Fable 5 models, limiting their availability to locations outside the United States and to certain foreign persons within the country.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The issue was connected to a jailbreak discovered in Fable 5 by cybersecurity researcher Katie Moussouris, founder and CEO of Luta Security. The discovery demonstrated how seemingly limited vulnerabilities in advanced AI systems can generate significant policy and security discussions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Researchers had asked Moussouris to investigate software vulnerabilities in the model. Her findings raised questions about whether certain model capabilities could be bypassed through carefully constructed prompts or other techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, reporting indicated that the identified jailbreak did not expose the most powerful capabilities associated with the underlying Mythos model.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology leaders subsequently called for the export restrictions affecting the models to be reconsidered. Their argument was that researchers and security professionals need access to advanced AI capabilities to identify vulnerabilities and develop fixes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moussouris also argued that ordinary security testing and code remediation should not automatically result in restrictions that prevent legitimate developers from working with advanced models.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The episode illustrates a broader challenge facing the AI industry: security teams need increasingly capable AI tools to identify vulnerabilities, while those same capabilities can potentially be misused by malicious actors.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybersecurity assumptions are changing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional cybersecurity strategies were largely developed around conventional software vulnerabilities, human attackers and established attack techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advanced AI changes that equation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI systems can analyze large amounts of source code, identify potential weaknesses, generate exploit-related material and automate technical tasks at a speed that would be difficult for individual security researchers to match.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a need for organizations to reassess assumptions around vulnerability management, software security, threat detection and incident response.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations face particularly significant challenges because AI systems may interact with electronic protected health information and other highly sensitive data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A compromised AI system, cloud environment or supporting software component could therefore create consequences beyond the AI application itself.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">U.S. AI security policy emphasizes coordination</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The U.S. administration\'s latest executive order on AI innovation and security also reflects the growing focus on coordinated cybersecurity efforts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The order emphasizes collaboration between government agencies and private-sector organizations to modernize information systems, strengthen defenses against external threats, protect intellectual property and develop advanced AI-enabled capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybersecurity experts have generally viewed the security focus as an important development, although they have also emphasized that the executive order should not be interpreted as a replacement for industry-specific compliance requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare organizations, this distinction is particularly important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Federal evaluation of AI models does not automatically constitute HIPAA compliance certification. Similarly, government review of an AI model before its release does not eliminate the responsibility of healthcare organizations to perform their own security assessments and due diligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The policy instead represents an attempt to identify and address potential AI-related threats before highly capable systems become broadly available to adversaries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The executive order also establishes an AI cybersecurity clearinghouse intended to improve coordination around security remediation across critical infrastructure sectors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This could be especially important for organizations that have fewer resources available for cybersecurity, including rural hospitals, smaller financial institutions and small utilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many of these organizations continue to rely on software containing long-standing vulnerabilities that could potentially be exploited to steal information, disrupt essential services or deploy ransomware.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI could become a cybersecurity defense tool</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">While advanced AI introduces new risks, it can also become an important component of defensive cybersecurity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Powerful AI models can potentially help security teams identify known software vulnerabilities, analyze large codebases, automate portions of security testing and prioritize weaknesses based on their potential impact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a balancing challenge for policymakers and technology companies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most capable AI systems need appropriate safeguards because they can potentially be used for offensive purposes. At the same time, security researchers and defenders require access to advanced AI capabilities to understand vulnerabilities and develop effective countermeasures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Finding the right balance between access, innovation and security is therefore becoming a central component of AI governance.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quantum computing adds another layer of cybersecurity risk</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The cybersecurity conversation is also expanding beyond conventional AI threats.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advances in quantum computing could eventually challenge many of the cryptographic techniques currently used to protect sensitive information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development has responded by developing recommendations intended to encourage responsible quantum technology development while addressing security, resilience, interoperability and international cooperation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The OECD\'s recommendations emphasize the importance of anticipating both opportunities and risks associated with quantum technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They also encourage countries to develop strong quantum ecosystems, build multidisciplinary talent, strengthen technology supply chains and promote international collaboration.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These recommendations are particularly relevant to cybersecurity because quantum computing could eventually undermine cryptographic systems that organizations currently depend on.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare needs to prepare for post-quantum security</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations have a particularly strong reason to begin preparing for quantum-related cybersecurity risks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Medical records, genomic information, clinical research and other healthcare datasets can remain sensitive for many years. Attackers could potentially collect encrypted information today and attempt to decrypt it in the future once sufficiently capable quantum computers become available.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is commonly described as a \"harvest now, decrypt later\" strategy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare organizations, preparing for this possibility means identifying information that will remain sensitive over the long term and determining which systems rely on cryptographic technologies that may eventually require replacement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The National Institute of Standards and Technology has already finalized post-quantum cryptography standards designed to provide protection against future quantum-enabled attacks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations therefore have an opportunity to begin evaluating their cryptographic inventories, dependencies and migration requirements before quantum computing reaches a level capable of threatening existing systems.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and quantum security require long-term planning</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI security and quantum security may appear to be separate issues, but both demonstrate the same underlying problem: cybersecurity strategies must evolve before emerging technologies create large-scale vulnerabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare organizations, this means security teams need to consider not only today\'s threats but also how emerging technologies could change the threat landscape over the next several years.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical AI deployments should incorporate strong controls around data access, model security, identity management and infrastructure protection. Sensitive information should be appropriately de-identified where possible, while AI workloads should operate within secure environments with carefully controlled access.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, organizations should strengthen cloud security because compromised cloud accounts have become a major concern for healthcare providers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The combination of AI-enabled attacks, cloud vulnerabilities and future quantum capabilities creates a more complicated security environment for organizations that already operate highly sensitive infrastructure.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The next phase of AI security</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The development of increasingly capable AI models makes security coordination more important than ever.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Government agencies, AI developers, cybersecurity companies, healthcare organizations and researchers will need to work together to identify vulnerabilities, evaluate emerging capabilities and establish practical safeguards.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Initiatives such as Project Glasswing demonstrate how AI developers can collaborate with organizations operating critical infrastructure. Government AI security policies indicate growing recognition that frontier models can have national-security implications. Meanwhile, international quantum recommendations demonstrate the need to prepare for technological risks that may emerge years before they become widespread.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">None of these measures provides a complete solution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations will still need their own security controls, risk assessments, compliance programs and incident-response capabilities. But stronger coordination between technology developers, governments and security teams can help reduce the gap between emerging technological capabilities and the defenses required to protect them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI continues to evolve, cybersecurity can no longer be treated as a separate consideration added after innovation. <strong>Security coordination needs to develop alongside the technology itself.</strong></p>', 'AI Security and Cybersecurity Coordination in the AI Era', 'Explore how governments, AI companies and industries are strengthening security coordination to address AI vulnerabilities, cyber risks and quantum threats.', 'AI security, artificial intelligence cybersecurity, AI vulnerabilities, AI governance, frontier AI security, healthcare cybersecurity, quantum cybersecurity, post-quantum cryptography, Project Glasswing, Anthropic AI, cybersecurity coordination, AI risk management, critical infrastructure security', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-06-28 12:40:40', 'published', 171751, 0, 9, NULL, '2024-06-28 12:40:40', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(337, 2, 1, 14, 'Leapfrog Expands ASC Public Reporting Program to Boost Healthcare Transparency', 'leapfrog-expands-asc-public-reporting-program-to-boost-healthcare-transparency', 'Leapfrog has expanded its ASC public reporting program, adding more facilities and safety data to help patients compare surgical care options.', '[\"Healthcare\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Ambulatory Surgery Centers\",\"ASC\",\"Patient Safety\",\"Healthcare Quality\",\"Healthcare Transparency\",\"Medical Facilities\",\"Hospital Ratings\",\"Healthcare Data\",\"CMS\",\"Healthcare Reporting\",\"Digital Healthcare\",\"Patient Care\",\"Medical Technology\"]', 'banner_image-1786373727607-497692003.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Leapfrog Group has expanded its Ambulatory Surgery Center (ASC) Public Reporting Program as part of its latest effort to improve transparency around healthcare quality and patient safety.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The national nonprofit announced the expanded program alongside an update to its 2026 ratings. More than 3,900 ambulatory surgery centers are now included in the 2026 ratings, along with more than 2,400 hospitals. Leapfrog said the participation represents the highest level recorded during its 26-year history.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization said the expanded program is designed to make safety and quality information more accessible to patients and the public.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“Transparency is now the norm in American healthcare,” said Leah Binder, president and CEO of The Leapfrog Group. She added that participating hospitals and ASCs are demonstrating leadership by making information about healthcare quality and safety available to the public.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Growing Participation From Ambulatory Surgery Centers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hundreds of ASCs voluntarily participated in this year\'s reporting program. Facilities could participate by providing verification information, completing the Leapfrog ASC Survey 2.0, or using both methods.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The increased participation comes as ambulatory surgery centers continue to play a major role in the U.S. healthcare system. According to Leapfrog, ASCs account for the majority of surgeries performed in the country, making access to reliable safety and quality information increasingly important for patients.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Missy Danforth, senior vice president of healthcare ratings at The Leapfrog Group, said patients have historically had limited information available when comparing the safety and quality of different ASCs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The expanded reporting program is intended to give patients more information when evaluating surgical facilities while also encouraging ASCs to demonstrate their commitment to safe and high-quality care.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">New Elements Added to ASC Reporting</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leapfrog originally launched its ASC Public Reporting Program in 2019. The latest expansion introduces three additional elements to the reporting process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reporting information from the U.S. Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS)</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accreditation standards</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data collected through the ASC Survey 2.0</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The additional data points are intended to provide a broader view of facility performance and give patients more information about the quality and safety practices of participating ASCs.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Public Results Updated Monthly</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leapfrog said publicly reported results will be updated every month to reflect the most recent available information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hospitals and ASCs can submit information through November 30, which serves as the late-submission deadline for the 2026 survey and ASC reporting program.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization expects continued participation from healthcare facilities as it expands the amount of quality and safety information available to the public.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transparency Remains a Challenge</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite the record level of participation, hundreds of hospitals are still not graded by Leapfrog because they did not voluntarily submit quality and safety information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization also continues to deal with issues involving some Tenet hospitals. A judge\'s order resulted in Leapfrog withholding grades for certain Tenet facilities that challenged the organization\'s grading process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In response to the broader transparency issue, Leapfrog has introduced a “full transparency badge” for facilities that participate in its surveys. The badge is intended to recognize healthcare organizations that provide quality and safety information for public review.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanding Access to Healthcare Quality Information</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The expanded ASC Public Reporting Program represents another step toward increasing transparency across the U.S. healthcare system.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By publishing information from more ASCs and incorporating additional safety and quality data, Leapfrog aims to help patients make more informed decisions about where they receive surgical care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The initiative also places greater emphasis on healthcare organizations voluntarily sharing performance information and demonstrating accountability for patient safety and quality of care.</p>', 'Leapfrog Expands ASC Public Reporting Program', 'Leapfrog expands its ASC public reporting program with more facilities and safety data, giving patients greater transparency into surgical care quality.', 'Leapfrog ASC reporting, ASC public reporting program, ambulatory surgery centers, ASC safety, healthcare transparency, healthcare quality, patient safety, hospital ratings, CMS reporting, ASC Survey 2.0, healthcare data, healthcare reporting', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-02 14:23:11', 'published', 188723, 0, 4, NULL, '2024-07-02 14:23:11', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(338, 2, 1, 14, 'Ensemble Health Partners Secures Strategic Growth Investment from Thoreau', 'ensemble-health-partners-secures-strategic-growth-investment-from-thoreau', 'Ensemble Health Partners secures strategic backing from Thoreau to expand healthcare revenue cycle management technology, AI, and services.', '[\"Ensemble Health Partners\",\"Thoreau\",\"Healthcare\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Revenue Cycle Management\",\"RCM\",\"Healthcare AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Healthcare Investment\",\"Private Equity\",\"Cohere\",\"Large Language Model\",\"Healthcare Financial Workflows\",\"Patient Revenue\"]', 'banner_image-1786374020197-861552993.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ensemble Health Partners, a healthcare revenue cycle management (RCM) company, has secured a strategic growth investment from Thoreau, a healthcare-focused investment platform established by private equity executive Matt Holt.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The investment brings Thoreau into Ensemble\'s ownership structure alongside existing investors Berkshire Partners, Warburg Pincus, and Bon Secours Mercy Health. The financial terms of the transaction were not disclosed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ensemble provides end-to-end revenue cycle management services to healthcare organizations. Over its 12-year history, the company has expanded its operations to support more than 200 hospitals representing over $55 billion in net patient revenue.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its RCM platform covers a broad range of healthcare financial processes, including patient access, medical coding, claims management, denial prevention, accounts receivable follow-up, and patient financial engagement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Ensemble, its technology platform combines experienced revenue cycle professionals with data-driven intelligence and artificial intelligence capabilities to help healthcare providers improve financial performance while reducing administrative complexity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Investment to Support Technology and Workforce Expansion</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new strategic investment is expected to provide Ensemble with additional capital and expertise as the company continues expanding its healthcare revenue cycle business.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ensemble said the funding will help accelerate investments in its technology platform and workforce while enabling the company to expand the services it provides to healthcare organizations and their communities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“There is tremendous opportunity to reduce the friction in healthcare for providers, patients, and everyone involved, and we want to remain at the forefront of the innovation that makes that possible,” said Judson Ivy, founder and CEO of Ensemble Health Partners.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ivy added that Thoreau\'s experience across healthcare administration, technology, and services will provide Ensemble with additional resources and expertise as it develops new capabilities for its clients.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Thoreau Brings Healthcare Investment Expertise</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The investment follows reports that Thoreau Group had been exploring a potential transaction involving Ensemble Health Partners.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bloomberg reported earlier that Thoreau Group was in discussions to acquire Ensemble in a transaction that could value the company at approximately $12 billion. Matt Holt, who previously worked at private equity firm New Mountain Capital, established the Thoreau Group venture after departing the firm late last year.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The latest transaction remains subject to customary closing requirements and applicable regulatory and other approvals.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ensemble Expands Healthcare AI Strategy</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The investment comes as healthcare revenue cycle companies increasingly adopt artificial intelligence to automate financial and administrative workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ensemble has also been working with enterprise AI company Cohere to develop an RCM-native large language model designed specifically for healthcare revenue cycle operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The model is being developed using Ensemble\'s operational knowledge and real-world revenue cycle use cases. The companies intend to use the technology to support AI-powered agents across multiple stages of the revenue cycle, ranging from patient intake to account resolution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The collaboration represents part of Ensemble\'s broader strategy to combine healthcare domain expertise, operational data, and generative AI to improve revenue cycle workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As healthcare providers continue facing financial and administrative pressures, investments in RCM technology and AI could become increasingly important for organizations seeking to improve collections, reduce denials, streamline administrative processes, and enhance the patient financial experience.</p>', 'Ensemble Health Partners Secures Thoreau Growth Investment', 'Ensemble Health Partners secures strategic investment from Thoreau to expand healthcare RCM, AI technology, and revenue cycle management services.', 'Ensemble Health Partners, Thoreau investment, healthcare revenue cycle management, healthcare RCM, healthcare AI, RCM technology, Ensemble Health Partners investment, Thoreau Group, healthcare technology investment, artificial intelligence healthcare, Cohere, healthcare LLM, revenue cycle automation, patient financial experience, healthcare financial workflows', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-05 10:06:42', 'published', 219834, 0, 3, NULL, '2024-07-05 10:06:42', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(339, 2, 9, 14, 'Beyond Digital: Payer-Provider Impact', 'beyond-digital-payer-provider-impact', 'See how payers and providers can reduce fragmentation, streamline workflows, build trust, and create measurable healthcare impact.', '[\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Payer Provider Collaboration\",\"Healthcare Interoperability\",\"Digital Health\",\"Clinical Data\",\"Prior Authorization\",\"Healthcare AI\",\"Revenue Cycle Management\",\"Healthcare Administration\",\"Health Data Exchange\",\"TEFCA\",\"QHIN\",\"Healthcare Innovation\",\"K2 Collaborative Summit\",\"Payer Provider Summit\",\"Healthcare Transformation\",\"Healthcare Policy\"]', 'banner_image-1786374387329-45416611.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations continue to invest heavily in digital initiatives, automation, interoperability, and artificial intelligence. Yet a critical question remains: <strong>Are these investments actually reducing friction where care is delivered?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the sixth annual <strong>K2 Collaborative Payer/Provider Summit</strong>, held in May 2026, 189 leaders representing payer, healthcare provider, and technology organizations came together around a shared challenge: <strong>systemic fragmentation across the healthcare ecosystem</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The issue extends well beyond disconnected technology platforms. Fragmentation affects clinical data, administrative workflows, prior authorization, denials, artificial intelligence initiatives, and the way payers and providers measure value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Today, clinical information can remain distributed across EHR systems, health information exchanges, laboratories, proprietary data extracts, and manual chart-retrieval processes. Prior authorization and denial management can also vary significantly between payers, forcing providers to maintain payer-specific processes and workarounds.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, AI investments are often driven by different financial priorities on the payer and provider sides. Without shared objectives and appropriate governance, these competing priorities can increase distrust rather than create collaboration.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These challenges are occurring while healthcare organizations face simultaneous pressure from declining margins, workforce shortages, increasing pharmaceutical and medical expenses, quality requirements, consumer expectations, and expanding federal regulations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When payer and provider organizations respond with disconnected strategies, new technology can unintentionally introduce additional administrative work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Systemic fragmentation has therefore become more than an operational inconvenience-it is a strategic risk.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The K2 Collaborative Summit 2026 report examines how healthcare organizations can establish a measurable operating model that reduces unnecessary burden, strengthens collaboration, and builds trust throughout the healthcare value chain.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Is Working in 2026: Collaboration Models That Reduce Burden and Build Trust</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Through presentations, discussions, and collaborative working sessions, summit participants identified several approaches that can help payers and providers reduce administrative friction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Three particularly important models include:</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Streamline Shared Workflows</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should identify workflows that create administrative work for both sides and look for opportunities to automate or simplify them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A major part of this effort involves bringing payer intelligence directly into the provider\'s existing workflow. Instead of requiring clinicians and staff to move between multiple payer portals and disconnected systems, relevant information should be available within the tools they already use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not simply to introduce another technology layer. The goal is to make the existing workflow easier, faster, and more actionable.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Start Small, Demonstrate Value, Then Scale</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large-scale transformation does not always need to begin with a system-wide rollout.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Summit participants emphasized the value of starting with focused use cases, proving measurable results, and using those early experiences to develop a repeatable playbook.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Smaller initiatives allow organizations to:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Validate operational performance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify workflow problems early.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Collect feedback from frontline teams.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improve processes before broader implementation.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Detect and correct data-quality issues.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establish measurable ROI before scaling.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once a model demonstrates value, organizations can expand it with greater confidence and a clearer understanding of what works.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Connect Incentives to Near-Term Actions</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Long-term quality metrics such as HEDIS can be important, but they do not always influence day-to-day behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare staff already manage multiple payer portals, quality-gap lists, outreach activities, documentation requirements, and manual follow-up tasks. Incentives that are too distant from daily activities may therefore have limited impact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">More effective approaches can connect compensation, shared savings, or smaller incentives to clearly defined clinical actions that can be measured and understood by both parties.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These models make the connection between an action and its outcome more visible, helping teams understand why a particular workflow matters.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The K2 Summit report provides a broader set of collaboration strategies and the strategic context behind them.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Payer and Provider Executives Should Prioritize Over the Next 12 Months</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A central objective of every KLAS summit is to translate peer discussions into practical priorities that organizations can act on during the following year.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For 2026-2027, summit participants identified several priorities for healthcare executives, payer executives, technology partners, and other stakeholders.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Priorities for Healthcare Executives: Establish a Clinical Data Operating Foundation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations should create a centralized and consistent approach to clinical data, documentation, care gaps, and evidence exchange.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is to avoid rebuilding payer collaboration separately for every practice, contract, or individual workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should also assign clear operational ownership for data quality and integrate payer-related activities directly into EHR workflows whenever possible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Frontline adoption should be treated as a core design requirement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Even a strong executive strategy can fail if implementation results in more clicks, duplicate documentation, repeated data entry, or additional cleanup work after patient visits.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Priorities for Payer Executives: Make Rules Computable, Timely, and Actionable</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payers can play an important role in reducing fragmentation by making benefit, network, policy, and authorization requirements available in formats that can be consumed directly inside provider workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payers should also work to reduce unnecessary payer-specific exceptions that increase administrative effort for provider organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where better data can improve quality, risk management, utilization, or other shared outcomes, payers should consider supporting common data feeds and infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Transparency is equally important. Providers need timely and understandable information about care gaps, authorization status, denial reasons, and the expected value of different activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When providers can trust the information they receive and act on it quickly, collaboration becomes more effective.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Figure 2: Executive Action Areas Identified at the K2 Summit</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The summit also highlighted governance approaches that can help payer and provider organizations manage collaborative initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One potential model is a <strong>payer-provider governance council</strong> responsible for areas such as attribution, data quality, shared metrics, and workflow priorities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Participants also emphasized the importance of selecting use cases where both sides can achieve meaningful value rather than creating initiatives that primarily benefit one organization.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology Partners as Strategic Enablers</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology companies have an increasingly important role to play in improving collaboration across the healthcare ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology partners bring domain expertise, technical capabilities, implementation experience, and insight into successful approaches used by other organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than functioning only as software suppliers, these organizations can act as strategic advisors to payers and providers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their contributions can include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting regulatory and compliance initiatives.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Helping organizations adopt industry best practices.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sharing peer-driven implementation insights.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying opportunities for workflow optimization.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving interoperability and data exchange.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting measurable business and clinical outcomes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Helping organizations demonstrate tangible ROI.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The strongest technology partnerships should ultimately contribute to shared outcomes rather than adding another disconnected system to an already fragmented environment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy Priorities That Could Accelerate Operational Impact</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Government agencies, regulators, and policymakers can also play an important role in reducing fragmentation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The K2 Summit discussions identified four major policy areas where government action could help move healthcare technology requirements away from technical compliance and toward measurable operational improvement.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Move From Compliance to Measurable Operational Value</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Federal healthcare leaders, including CMS and ASTP/ONC, can continue strengthening and clarifying interoperability initiatives such as TEFCA and QHIN requirements while advancing standardized APIs and interoperability expectations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, success should not be measured solely by whether technology is technically available.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">More meaningful measures could include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Less time spent retrieving medical records.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Faster quality reporting.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Shorter prior authorization cycles.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Better coordination of care.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved reliability of data exchange.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Greater usability for frontline healthcare teams.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Requirements should place greater emphasis on reliability, conformance testing, attribution standards, and real-world usability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Congress can also support this transition through oversight and funding mechanisms that connect digital infrastructure investments to administrative simplification and improved patient access.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Reduce Variation in Prior Authorization and Documentation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Federal and state policymakers can establish clearer expectations around prior authorization response times, criteria transparency, digital submission, evidence requirements, and status tracking.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, reforms should avoid replacing one fragmented process with another set of payer-specific exceptions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Narrow gold-card programs or highly customized requirements can still create operational challenges when providers must manage different processes across multiple payers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The broader objective should be to <strong>reduce the number of workflows providers must manage</strong>, rather than simply making existing fragmented processes faster.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Establish Guardrails for AI in Payment, Access, and Utilization Management</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is becoming increasingly important across healthcare payment, utilization management, clinical operations, and administrative workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Government policy can help prevent an uncontrolled AI arms race by establishing expectations around transparency, human oversight, auditability, and bias monitoring.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-impact AI applications should be evaluated for inappropriate denial patterns and other unintended consequences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy should encourage AI applications that:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clarify complex rules.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reduce administrative workload.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improve navigation through healthcare processes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Support clinicians and administrative teams.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increase transparency.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, opaque automation that simply transfers cost or administrative work from one side of the healthcare ecosystem to another should receive greater scrutiny.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A practical governance framework could give healthcare executives greater confidence to adopt AI while protecting patients, members, and clinicians.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Support Smaller Providers, Safety-Net Organizations, and Regional Payers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many of the collaboration models discussed at the summit require significant investments in data infrastructure, analytics capabilities, workflow redesign, and technology coordination.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Smaller healthcare organizations may not have the financial or technical resources required to implement these capabilities independently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Government support could therefore include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Grants and targeted funding.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technical assistance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Multipayer collaboration programs.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Safe-harbor guidance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Shared infrastructure initiatives.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Support for proven interoperability and workflow models.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These efforts are particularly important for Medicaid organizations, rural providers, behavioral health organizations, federally qualified health centers, and post-acute care organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fragmentation can have an especially significant impact on patients receiving care in these settings, making scalable and affordable solutions essential.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Looking Ahead to the K2 Payer/Provider Summit 2027</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The work toward better payer-provider collaboration will continue.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">KLAS is already planning the next K2 Collaborative Summit, with an opportunity to expand the 2027 program through a dedicated payer forum and a CEO-focused provider track.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The proposed format includes a half-day payer forum and simultaneous provider CEO discussions on <strong>May 10, 2027, from 12:00 p.m. to 4:00 p.m. MST</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The discussions are expected to focus on important workflow, collaboration, and regulatory challenges affecting the healthcare ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Additional best-practice guidelines and governance templates are also expected to emerge from continued peer collaboration.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Fragmentation Into Measurable Progress</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations do not need another collection of disconnected digital initiatives. They need operating models that connect technology investments to measurable improvements in the way healthcare is delivered.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The K2 Collaborative Payer/Provider Summit demonstrates that meaningful progress requires cooperation among payers, providers, technology companies, and policymakers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The path forward includes simplifying shared workflows, making payer rules more usable, improving clinical-data foundations, establishing stronger governance, aligning incentives, and creating responsible frameworks for AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ultimately, the goal is straightforward: <strong>less administrative friction, greater transparency, stronger trust, and measurable value for organizations and the people they serve.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By moving beyond digital transformation as a technology objective and treating it as a collaborative operating challenge, payer and provider organizations can turn fragmentation from a strategic risk into an opportunity for sustainable improvement.</p>', 'Payer-Provider Collaboration & Impact', 'Explore how payers and providers can reduce fragmentation, streamline workflows, improve interoperability, and achieve measurable healthcare impact.', 'payer provider collaboration, payer provider fragmentation, healthcare interoperability, healthcare technology, healthcare IT, clinical data management, prior authorization, healthcare AI, digital health, payer provider workflow, healthcare data exchange, TEFCA, QHIN, healthcare administration, healthcare transformation, K2 Collaborative Summit 2026, payer provider summit, healthcare policy, healthcare innovation, administrative burden', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-08 16:49:13', 'published', 31594, 0, 10, NULL, '2024-07-08 16:49:13', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(340, 2, 2, 14, 'Where Payers Are Investing: What Healthcare Vendors Need to Know', 'where-payers-are-investing-what-healthcare-vendors-need-to-know', 'Health plans continue investing in technology, but rising cost pressure and demand for measurable value are reshaping payer technology decisions.', '[\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Health Insurance\",\"Payer Technology\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Risk Adjustment\",\"Interoperability\",\"Care Management\",\"Payment Integrity\",\"Quality Management\",\"CMS\",\"FHIR\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Healthcare Analytics\",\"Health Plans\",\"Healthcare Vendors\",\"Digital Health\"]', 'banner_image-1786374603256-324203444.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health plans continue to invest in technology, but the path to winning those investments is becoming more demanding. Payers are evaluating vendors more carefully, asking tougher questions around cost, integration, measurable outcomes, and long-term value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Across payer technology decisions recorded from June 1, 2024, through June 30, 2026, risk adjustment, interoperability, care management, payment integrity, and quality emerged as some of the most active areas. The underlying theme across these categories is straightforward: payers want technology that delivers practical business value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Solutions that can protect revenue, reduce administrative workloads, improve data quality, connect fragmented processes, and demonstrate financial impact are increasingly positioned to stand out. Vendors that combine strong functionality with transparent pricing and credible implementation support have a better opportunity to gain payer confidence.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Points to Know</h3><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Risk adjustment and CMS interoperability remain highly active areas. Many payer decisions are influenced by regulatory requirements, government mandates, and the industry\'s movement toward more prospective workflows.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cost pressure is influencing technology decisions across payer segments. A significant share of decisions references budgets, pricing, financial constraints, or cost considerations.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Interoperability and care management continue to attract investment. Buyers increasingly expect these solutions to contribute to broader modernization and platform strategies rather than operate as isolated systems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI is gaining attention, but practical applications remain the priority. Only seven decisions specifically referenced AI, ML, NLP, or agents, with most applications focused on concrete workflow improvements.</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Risk Adjustment Remains Active as Care Management and Quality Gain Momentum</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Risk adjustment continues to represent one of the most frequently observed areas in payer technology decisions. Health plans are looking for better chart retrieval, more accurate coding assistance, stronger reporting capabilities, improved data quality, and technology that supports both prospective and retrospective processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payers are also examining the services surrounding these solutions. Vendor performance, operational efficiency, and the amount of manual effort required from internal teams can significantly influence purchasing decisions. Solutions that reduce the need for error identification, manual reconciliation, and workarounds can provide a stronger value proposition.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Care management is also undergoing modernization. Health plans are looking to replace aging platforms, eliminate disconnected processes, and create stronger connections between utilization management, case management, appeals, and quality operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quality measurement and reporting are showing increased momentum as organizations seek to move beyond seasonal and static processes. Payers are increasingly interested in year-round gap closure, improved supplemental data collection, stronger provider collaboration, and workflows connected directly with EHR environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Across risk adjustment and quality, the objective is increasingly the same: transform data into actionable information faster and enable teams to respond before problems become larger operational or financial issues.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cost, Integration, and Interoperability Are Raising Vendor Expectations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Functionality remains important, but cost has become an equally significant consideration in payer technology decisions. A substantial portion of decisions references pricing, budgets, financial pressure, or overall cost.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This does not necessarily mean payers are selecting the cheapest technology. Instead, buyers are looking for vendors that can clearly explain the economics of their solutions. Licensing structures, implementation costs, usage-based charges, and additional modules are all coming under greater scrutiny.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A more expensive platform can still succeed when vendors can demonstrate measurable value. Reducing administrative work, improving accuracy, increasing staff productivity, strengthening financial performance, or supporting enterprise-wide modernization can help justify a higher investment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Interoperability is another area where expectations are expanding. CMS requirements and FHIR-related mandates continue to influence payer technology strategies, but compliance alone is no longer enough. Health plans increasingly want interoperability investments to support broader operational goals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That includes connecting prior authorization, care management, provider collaboration, data exchange, and other workflows while helping organizations simplify their technology environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A similar pattern is visible in care management. Payers want fewer disconnected applications and more configurable workflows that can support multiple lines of business without creating additional complexity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Is Emerging, but Practical Business Value Comes First</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is appearing more frequently in payer technology discussions, particularly in areas such as natural language processing, machine learning, automation, coding assistance, analytics, and internal productivity tools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, AI is not yet the primary purchasing driver across these segments. Only seven decisions explicitly referenced AI, ML, NLP, or agents, and those references were generally connected to specific operational use cases.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This suggests that payers are approaching AI with a practical mindset. Rather than investing in AI simply because it is a prominent technology trend, health plans appear more interested in applications that can demonstrate a direct operational benefit.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The strongest opportunities may therefore come from AI solutions that improve accuracy, reduce manual review, automate repetitive activities, accelerate decision-making, or help existing teams manage larger workloads without requiring proportional increases in staffing.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Vendors Should Take Away</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The payer technology market continues to offer significant opportunities, but buyers are becoming more disciplined. Regulatory requirements may create demand, but compliance alone is unlikely to differentiate a vendor.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vendors should focus on demonstrating how their solutions fit into broader payer workflows and technology strategies. Clear implementation plans, transparent pricing, measurable outcomes, interoperability, and practical automation can all strengthen the business case.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ultimately, payer organizations are looking beyond technology capabilities. They want partners that understand their operational challenges and can demonstrate how technology will produce measurable improvements. Vendors that can connect product functionality to financial, operational, and strategic outcomes will be better positioned as payer technology investments continue to evolve.</p>', 'Where Payers Are Investing: Key Healthcare Technology Trends', 'Explore where health plans are investing in technology, from risk adjustment and interoperability to care management, quality, and practical AI.', 'payer technology investments, healthcare technology, health plan technology, payer technology trends, risk adjustment, healthcare interoperability, care management, payment integrity, quality management, CMS interoperability, FHIR, AI in healthcare, healthcare IT, healthcare vendors', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-10 11:32:44', 'published', 14775, 0, 5, NULL, '2024-07-10 11:32:44', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(341, 2, 2, 14, 'Epic AI: What’s Working, What’s Not, and What Customers Need Next', 'epic-ai-whats-working-whats-not-and-what-customers-need-next', 'Epic customers report measurable AI gains, but adoption, workflow fit, pricing, and change management remain critical to long-term value.', '[\"Epic AI\",\"Healthcare AI\",\"EHR\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Clinical AI\",\"AI Adoption\",\"Healthcare Automation\",\"KLAS\",\"Digital Health\"]', 'banner_image-1786374949083-91406017.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic AI: What’s Working, What’s Not, and What Customers Need Next</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare AI is rapidly moving from experimentation into everyday clinical and administrative workflows, and organizations using Epic are seeing that transition firsthand. In a recent KLAS study, leaders from 30 unique healthcare organizations shared their experiences with Epic’s AI capabilities, including which tools are delivering meaningful results, where expectations are falling short, how Epic is supporting adoption, and whether its pricing model is sustainable.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The overall feedback is positive, but not without important qualifications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic customers generally appreciate the company’s fast pace of AI development and the growing range of capabilities available within the EHR. The strongest results tend to appear in workflows where the problem is clearly defined and improvements can be measured. However, the interviews also demonstrate that deploying an AI capability does not automatically translate into business or clinical value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Successful adoption depends on more than technology. Organizations need to align AI with existing workflows, educate users, validate outputs, monitor performance, and establish measurable success criteria.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic’s AI portfolio is generating measurable benefits across clinical and administrative workflows, although results differ significantly by use case and organization.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some of the same AI tools are described as both highly impactful and minimally impactful, highlighting the importance of workflow alignment and adoption.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customers generally view Epic’s AI development pace and vendor support favorably, but many want more assistance with training, change management, governance, and adoption.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI pricing is considered reasonable by many organizations today, but long-term confidence will depend on predictable costs, utilization visibility, and demonstrable ROI.</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customers Are Finding Real Value in Epic AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When healthcare leaders were asked which Epic AI capabilities had made the greatest impact, <strong>In Basket Drafts</strong> and <strong>Outpatient Insights</strong> were among the tools mentioned most frequently. Other capabilities, including <strong>Denials Appeals, Coding Assistant, Inpatient Insights, and AI Charting</strong>, also received positive feedback.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The range of tools receiving positive responses indicates that AI can address a wide variety of repetitive and time-consuming activities across healthcare organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most compelling success stories tend to involve workflows where results can be quantified. One healthcare director provided a clear example of the operational impact:</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“We’ve had over 880 generated summaries for 11,000 notes, saved 18,000 hours with some revenue cycle automation, and drafted 3,400 appeals through AI, which has resulted in a 17% reduction in the time we would typically spend creating those.”</p></blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">-Director, February 2026</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every organization has detailed measurements available yet. Still, the conversation among healthcare leaders is changing. Instead of asking whether AI is interesting or innovative, organizations are increasingly asking a more practical question:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Where is AI creating measurable clinical, financial, or operational improvement?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That shift toward measurable outcomes is likely to become increasingly important as healthcare organizations expand their AI investments.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">When AI Falls Short, Workflow Fit Often Matters</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The KLAS feedback also reveals an important contradiction: some Epic AI capabilities are identified as highly impactful by certain organizations while being considered less impactful by others.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>In Basket Drafts</strong> and <strong>Outpatient Insights</strong> provide particularly clear examples.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For some organizations, In Basket Drafts reduce the amount of manual effort clinicians spend responding to patient messages. In other settings, clinicians report that reviewing and editing AI-generated responses can take too much time, particularly when patient questions involve complicated or highly individualized circumstances.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Outpatient Insights presents a similar challenge. Summaries can help clinicians quickly understand patient information, but some specialists may find generalized summaries less useful when their workflows require highly focused, specialty-specific information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These differences suggest that AI performance cannot be evaluated solely by looking at the underlying technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Configuration, specialty requirements, workflow design, user expectations, and training can all influence whether a tool succeeds.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For Epic customers, the practical lesson is clear: <strong>AI should not always be treated as a plug-and-play feature.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations may need to validate outputs, adjust workflows, provide role-specific training, communicate appropriate use cases, and continuously measure performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A poor initial experience can also have long-term consequences. When clinicians lose confidence in an AI tool early in the adoption process, they may become less willing to revisit the technology even after future product improvements address the original shortcomings.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customers Want Epic to Move Quickly-But Need Help Adapting</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most organizations interviewed by KLAS believe Epic is moving at an appropriate pace with its AI development, although keeping up with the pace can be challenging.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders recognize that AI is advancing rapidly throughout the industry. Many would rather have new capabilities available for evaluation than wait several years for a slower development cycle.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One CMIO summarized this sentiment by saying:</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“It’s fast. I’d say it’s about right though, because the industry is moving at a certain pace.”</p></blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge is that rapid product development does not necessarily produce rapid organizational adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customers say they would benefit from greater visibility into how AI tools are being used, clearer recommendations for validation and monitoring, and more assistance in helping clinicians adjust to AI-enabled workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic\'s overall support receives positive feedback from many customers. However, the experiences are not completely consistent across every AI capability. Some organizations report delays or bottlenecks when working with specific Epic teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In some cases, healthcare organizations are effectively creating their own playbooks for AI governance, training, change management, and measuring value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates an opportunity for Epic to expand its role beyond delivering AI functionality and provide more structured guidance around successful implementation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Pricing Looks Reasonable-For Now</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pricing is another area where customer sentiment is generally positive but cautious.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most interviewed organizations currently consider Epic\'s AI pricing model appropriate. This is particularly relevant given the breadth of AI functionality Epic is making available through its platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, many organizations are still in the early stages of adoption. Some are benefiting from introductory pricing structures or lower utilization levels, making it difficult to determine how costs will look once AI becomes deeply embedded across the enterprise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One director described the uncertainty this way:</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“The first year is much cheaper than year two, so we have to see the value. Today, yes. Ask me next year.”</p></blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">-Director, February 2026</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As adoption grows, customers want greater visibility into usage, costs, and business outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They also want to understand how AI spending will change as utilization scales across departments and user groups. Clearer forecasting and stronger evidence of financial or operational return could become increasingly important in determining whether customers continue to view the pricing model as sustainable.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Pricing Is a Broader Healthcare Industry Issue</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The concerns surrounding Epic\'s AI pricing are not unique to Epic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Broader KLAS research on healthcare organizations purchasing AI from other vendors indicates that providers are increasingly scrutinizing how AI solutions are monetized. Concerns frequently arise when pricing is difficult to predict, becomes expensive at scale, or appears disconnected from the value the technology generates.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Per-user pricing can create resistance because it may discourage organizations from expanding AI access throughout the enterprise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By contrast, enterprise licensing and predictable flat-fee structures can make budgeting easier and remove some of the uncertainty associated with expanding adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customers are generally more comfortable paying for AI when they can clearly connect the cost to measurable outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic\'s suite approach can make it easier for healthcare organizations to experiment with multiple AI capabilities without managing numerous separate vendors. However, as usage increases, customers will expect a stronger relationship between what they pay, which tools they actually use, and the value those tools produce.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic Has an Advantage, but Integration Alone Is Not Enough</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic enters the AI market with an important advantage: the EHR is already deeply embedded in the daily workflows of its customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For many healthcare organizations, an Epic-first AI strategy is attractive because integrated capabilities can reduce the complexity of introducing another technology vendor into clinical and administrative environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, an <strong>Epic-first strategy does not necessarily mean an Epic-only strategy</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations will continue evaluating third-party AI solutions when those products provide more specialized capabilities, move faster in particular areas, or offer stronger implementation support, ROI evidence, and adoption resources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This means Epic\'s competitive advantage will depend not only on having AI capabilities inside the EHR but also on helping customers turn those capabilities into measurable outcomes.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Healthcare Organizations Need Next</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The next phase of healthcare AI adoption is likely to focus less on the number of tools available and more on the consistency of the results those tools deliver.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic customers appear willing to move quickly. They recognize the potential of AI and generally appreciate having new capabilities available within an environment they already use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But speed needs to be matched by execution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations need better guidance around:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI governance and oversight</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Workflow redesign and optimization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinician training and education</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Output validation and monitoring</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Usage and utilization measurement</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial forecasting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ROI measurement</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Change management and long-term adoption</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These elements can determine whether an AI implementation becomes a sustainable part of the organization\'s operations or remains an underused feature.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Looking Ahead</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic has a strong foundation for expanding healthcare AI adoption because its EHR already sits at the center of many clinical and administrative workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The KLAS feedback suggests that customers are generally optimistic about Epic\'s AI direction. They value the company\'s development speed, appreciate the integrated approach, and are already seeing meaningful benefits from several capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, customers are becoming more sophisticated in how they evaluate AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question is no longer simply whether Epic can deliver more AI tools. The bigger question is whether organizations can consistently move from <strong>implementation to adoption and from adoption to measurable outcomes</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As healthcare AI matures, Epic\'s customers are likely to judge progress by lasting clinical, operational, and financial value rather than the number of features released.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations appear ready to move forward. What they need now is clearer evidence, stronger adoption support, predictable economics, and practical guidance that helps turn AI capabilities into sustained results.</p>', 'Epic AI: What’s Working, What’s Not & What Customers Need', 'Explore what Epic customers say about AI impact, adoption, pricing, workflow fit, support, and the changes needed to deliver lasting value.', 'Epic AI, Epic healthcare AI, healthcare AI, Epic EHR, AI in healthcare, healthcare artificial intelligence, clinical AI, AI adoption, healthcare automation, KLAS Epic AI', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-12 15:15:15', 'published', 157275, 0, 9, NULL, '2024-07-12 15:15:15', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(342, 2, 9, 14, 'End-to-End Revenue Cycle Outsourcing 2025: What Healthcare Leaders Need to Know', 'end-to-end-revenue-cycle-outsourcing-2025-what-healthcare-leaders-need-to-know', 'Explore how end-to-end revenue cycle outsourcing is reshaping healthcare finance through technology, partnerships, scalability, and stronger financial performance.', '[\"Revenue Cycle Outsourcing\",\"Healthcare RCM\",\"Revenue Cycle Management\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"Healthcare Finance\",\"RCM Outsourcing\",\"Healthcare Outsourcing\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Revenue Cycle Automation\",\"Healthcare Operations\",\"Financial Performance\",\"Ensemble\",\"Guidehouse\",\"Optum\",\"R1 RCM\",\"Healthcare Leadership\",\"Digital Transformation\"]', 'banner_image-1786375272514-210085299.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations are under increasing pressure to maintain strong financial performance while continuing to deliver high-quality patient care. Rising operating costs, workforce shortages, complex payer requirements, and growing administrative demands are putting significant pressure on revenue cycle management (RCM) teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As these challenges continue, more health systems are evaluating <strong>end-to-end revenue cycle outsourcing (RCO)</strong> as a way to access specialized expertise, advanced technology, scalable resources, and proven operational capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The report <strong>“End-to-End Revenue Cycle Outsourcing 2025”</strong> examines how healthcare organizations are approaching outsourcing, which firms are delivering strong results, and what separates successful outsourcing relationships from those that struggle.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vendor Landscape at a Glance</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The report evaluates the current market landscape and provides insights into major revenue cycle outsourcing firms, including <strong>Ensemble, Guidehouse, Optum, and R1 RCM</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Several trends emerge from the analysis:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Higher-performing firms are recognized for building strong provider relationships, maintaining effective governance, and delivering measurable improvements in areas such as collections and denial management.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lower-performing organizations are making progress but continue to face challenges related to execution, consistency, operational performance, or patient experience.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and automation remain major areas of investment across the market, although their long-term impact on financial performance is still evolving.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provider organizations are increasingly evaluating vendors based on both measurable financial outcomes and the overall quality of the partnership.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The complete report provides deeper comparisons, performance insights, and feedback from healthcare organizations working with these firms.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">End-to-End RCO Market Momentum Continues to Build</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The healthcare revenue cycle outsourcing market has gained considerable momentum since 2023. The number of organizations entering new end-to-end outsourcing arrangements has nearly doubled, increasing from an estimated <strong>8 new contracts in the previous report to 14 in the latest assessment</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Three primary factors are driving this growth.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Greater Access to Technology</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advanced automation, analytics, and artificial intelligence capabilities can require significant investment and specialized expertise. For many healthcare providers, partnering with an experienced outsourcing firm provides access to technologies that may be difficult to build and maintain internally.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Performance and Cost Pressures</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Internal revenue cycle teams are increasingly expected to improve collections, reduce denials, control costs, and maintain operational consistency. Outsourcing can provide additional expertise and resources when internal teams do not have the capacity to address these challenges quickly.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Scalability</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations experiencing growth, mergers, acquisitions, or operational expansion can quickly place additional demands on internal revenue cycle teams. External partners can provide the workforce and infrastructure required to scale operations without creating equivalent pressure on internal resources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these factors indicate that end-to-end outsourcing is moving beyond an experimental strategy. It is becoming an established approach for healthcare organizations seeking immediate performance improvements while developing greater long-term financial resilience.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Partnerships That Deliver Results</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations reporting the strongest outsourcing experiences tend to view their relationship with the outsourcing firm as a <strong>strategic partnership rather than a simple vendor arrangement</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Successful relationships are built around clearly defined objectives, effective governance, transparent reporting, and frequent communication. Both sides need to understand how performance will be measured and how responsibilities will be shared.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Proactive problem-solving is another important factor. High-performing firms work with healthcare leaders to identify potential issues before they become significant operational problems. They also collaborate on strategy, develop solutions jointly, and create incentives that support shared objectives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cultural alignment can be equally important. When provider employees and outsourcing teams work together as a unified organization, communication becomes easier and operational decisions can be made more effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional performance metrics remain critical. These include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cash flow</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accounts receivable days</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Denial rates</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Collections performance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cost to collect</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Revenue cycle productivity</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, healthcare leaders also emphasize less tangible indicators of success. Proactive communication, cultural alignment, collaboration, accountability, and issue resolution can significantly influence the long-term health of the relationship.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most sustainable outsourcing partnerships therefore balance <strong>measurable financial performance with trust, transparency, and strong working relationships</strong>.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Firms Structure Their Engagements</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The structure of an outsourcing engagement can have a major influence on its success.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that recently transitioned to a new outsourcing partner described several approaches used by firms to maintain operational continuity. One of the most common strategies is <strong>rebadging</strong>, where existing revenue cycle employees transition to the outsourcing organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rebadging can help preserve institutional knowledge and reduce disruption, but the transition needs to be carefully managed. Successful organizations often prepare employees well in advance, retain selected internal leaders for oversight, and establish clear responsibilities before the transition begins.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance is another critical component.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-performing partnerships typically establish regular communication at multiple organizational levels. These interactions can include daily operational meetings, management reviews, performance discussions, and executive-level meetings.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In many engagements, the outsourcing firm takes responsibility for driving innovation, implementing best practices, and managing day-to-day revenue cycle operations, while healthcare leadership maintains authority over strategic and sensitive organizational decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The combination of <strong>rebadging, governance, accountability, and shared strategic planning</strong> provides an important foundation for long-term outsourcing success.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Laying the Groundwork for a Successful Partnership</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Successful revenue cycle outsourcing begins long before the contract is finalized. Healthcare leaders involved in recent outsourcing transitions identified several practices that helped create a smoother implementation.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Evaluate the Current Revenue Cycle State</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should conduct a detailed assessment of their existing revenue cycle operations before selecting an outsourcing partner.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This includes reviewing cost-to-collect metrics, documenting workflows, identifying performance gaps, evaluating existing technology, and assessing current vendor relationships.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A comprehensive baseline helps organizations determine where outsourcing can create the greatest value while identifying internal processes that may need improvement.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Involve Cross-Functional Teams Early</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Revenue cycle outsourcing affects more than the finance department.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations should involve leaders from <strong>HR, IT, operations, finance, and other relevant functions</strong> early in the planning process. Cross-functional participation can create more realistic implementation timelines, improve communication, reduce transition risks, and build organizational support.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Establish Clear Scope, Terms, and Fees</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contracts should clearly define the services included in the engagement, performance baselines, measurement methodologies, responsibilities, and fee structures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders should ensure that performance calculations are based on reliable internal data and that the complete scope of work is documented before implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear contractual expectations can reduce misunderstandings, operational gaps, and disputes over responsibility later in the relationship.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Prepare Employees for the Transition</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">People management is especially important when rebadging is involved.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should communicate openly with employees about the transition and provide clarity around roles, benefits, retention programs, timelines, and expectations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Early involvement from HR can help address workforce concerns and support cultural alignment between the healthcare organization and the outsourcing partner.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. Build a True Strategic Partnership</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Outsourcing success depends on more than selecting a capable firm. Both organizations need to establish trust, transparency, shared accountability, and effective leadership structures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations may benefit from retaining a small number of experienced internal leaders to provide oversight and institutional guidance. At the same time, the outsourcing firm should provide strong operational leadership and take ownership of execution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Leaders should also recognize that unexpected challenges-including technology integrations, data issues, workflow changes, and implementation delays-can occur. A partnership-oriented model makes it easier to address these issues collaboratively.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Looking Ahead</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Revenue cycle outsourcing is evolving beyond a solution for filling staffing or resource gaps. Healthcare organizations are increasingly using outsourcing to access specialized expertise, advanced technology, scalable capabilities, and operational experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, outsourcing does not automatically guarantee better financial results. The strongest outcomes depend on selecting the right partner, establishing clear expectations, maintaining effective oversight, and creating a relationship built around shared goals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and automation will continue to influence the market, but healthcare leaders should maintain realistic expectations regarding what these technologies can deliver today. Technology can improve efficiency and support decision-making, but successful revenue cycle transformation still depends heavily on people, processes, governance, and collaboration.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Going forward, outsourcing firms will need to connect technological innovation directly to the operational priorities of their healthcare clients while preserving the human elements that make partnerships successful.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare leaders evaluating their next revenue cycle strategy, the <strong>End-to-End Revenue Cycle Outsourcing 2025</strong> report provides deeper insight into vendor performance, market trends, successful engagement models, and the challenges organizations should consider before entering an outsourcing partnership.</p>', 'End-to-End Revenue Cycle Outsourcing 2025 | Healthcare Guide', 'Explore 2025 healthcare revenue cycle outsourcing trends, vendor performance, AI adoption, partnership strategies, and best practices for successful RCO.', 'revenue cycle outsourcing, healthcare revenue cycle outsourcing, RCO 2025, end-to-end revenue cycle outsourcing, healthcare RCM, revenue cycle management, healthcare outsourcing, RCM outsourcing, healthcare financial performance, revenue cycle technology, AI in revenue cycle management, healthcare revenue cycle vendors, Ensemble, Guidehouse, Optum, R1 RCM', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-15 13:58:46', 'published', 40715, 0, 8, NULL, '2024-07-15 13:58:46', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(343, 2, 2, 14, 'A Framework for Advancing Responsible AI in Healthcare', 'a-framework-for-advancing-responsible-ai-in-healthcare', 'GE HealthCare outlines a Responsible AI framework focused on governance, validation, privacy, security, and standards across the healthcare AI lifecycle.', '[\"Responsible AI\",\"Healthcare AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare\",\"GE HealthCare\",\"AI Governance\",\"Healthcare Technology\",\"AI Safety\",\"AI Validation\",\"AI Privacy\",\"AI Security\",\"Clinical AI\",\"Healthcare Innovation\",\"AI Risk Management\",\"Responsible Technology\",\"Digital Health\"]', 'banner_image-1786375674925-427567429.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At GE HealthCare, we believe artificial intelligence should ultimately serve people, assist clinicians, uphold human dignity, and function with appropriate human oversight. This principle is reflected in the way we design, develop, deploy, and manage our AI-powered healthcare solutions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our approach to Responsible AI covers the entire AI lifecycle, beginning with early-stage design and development and continuing through deployment, monitoring, evaluation, and ongoing improvement. Our Responsible AI Principles provide a foundation for innovation while supporting governance, structured validation, privacy and security protections, and alignment with recognized standards and assurance practices.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Trust Through More Than 125 Years of Healthcare Innovation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">GE HealthCare has more than 125 years of experience developing medical technologies, patient care solutions, and pharmaceutical services. Today, its installed base includes more than 4 million units worldwide across technologies such as X-ray, PET, Ultrasound, and ECG. Collectively, these solutions support more than 1 billion patients each year and generate data and insights that contribute to clinical care delivery.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The trust developed through this long history is fundamental to how we approach healthcare technology. We believe healthcare solutions should be assessed on more than technical performance alone. Technologies should be designed to operate reliably in real-world clinical and operational environments, support appropriate use, and address expectations around privacy, security, accountability, and risk management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI becomes increasingly capable and embedded in healthcare workflows, these expectations become even more important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">To support this, GE HealthCare has developed an operating model intended to guide the responsible development and deployment of AI-enabled technologies. The model is built around a governance framework supported by three primary dimensions:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quality and validation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privacy and security</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recognitions and certifications</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these areas provide a foundation for applying Responsible AI principles throughout the AI lifecycle.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance Framework</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responsible AI begins with effective governance and clearly defined accountability. AI systems require structured oversight because their potential impact depends heavily on the environment in which they are developed, implemented, and used.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is particularly significant in healthcare, where the operating context can involve different clinical workflows, patient populations, data environments, regulatory requirements, and levels of clinician or user involvement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our governance framework brings together perspectives from multiple disciplines to support responsible AI development, assessment, escalation, and deployment. These perspectives may include product development, clinical practice, quality, regulatory affairs, privacy, cybersecurity, legal, and risk management teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These cross-functional groups help establish important governance mechanisms, including ownership responsibilities, escalation procedures, lifecycle review checkpoints, and alignment between AI systems and their intended applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A structured governance approach can also help organizations maintain consistency as AI use cases expand. Teams need a common process for identifying potential risks, documenting decisions, evaluating controls, and determining whether additional safeguards are necessary.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responsible AI governance therefore considers more than what an AI system is technically capable of doing. It also examines how the technology should be used, who it is designed to support, what risks may arise, and which safeguards are needed to promote appropriate use.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quality and Validation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rigorous quality and validation practices represent another important component of the Responsible AI framework. In healthcare, AI systems should be assessed under conditions that reflect their intended environments. Strong performance in a development or testing environment by itself may not be enough to establish reliability in real-world use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Structured validation can help evaluate whether AI systems operate within defined expectations across different users, environments, and patient populations. The objective is to develop AI solutions capable of producing accurate, dependable, and high-quality results through appropriate testing, evaluation, and verification activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Validation methods should also correspond to the intended purpose of the technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an AI solution designed to assist with medical image interpretation may require different evaluation criteria, safeguards, and evidence than an AI application intended to improve documentation or administrative workflow efficiency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clinical and operational context helps determine potential risks, user requirements, performance expectations, and the evidence necessary to support responsible deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding both the strengths and limitations of an AI system is essential. Quality and validation processes can help identify where a system performs reliably, where limitations may exist, and how users should interpret and apply its outputs. These findings should be documented clearly so users can make informed decisions about appropriate AI use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Explainability and interpretability are also important considerations. Providing meaningful information about system behavior can help users better understand AI-generated outputs and recommendations, supporting confidence, informed decision-making, and appropriate human oversight.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privacy and Security</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privacy and security are foundational elements of responsible AI development. Modern AI systems can involve sophisticated data flows, connected technologies, cloud or digital infrastructure, and multiple points of interaction. Maintaining trust requires safeguards to be considered from the beginning of the development process rather than added after deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our approach incorporates privacy-conscious design, security and resilience controls, and risk-based operational practices. These measures are intended to help protect data, systems, and users across healthcare environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privacy-focused development supports responsible data use while helping protect individual autonomy, identity, and dignity. This includes evaluating what information is collected and used, how it is processed, who can access it, and how privacy protections are maintained throughout the AI lifecycle.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security and resilience are equally critical. AI systems require controls designed to support confidentiality, integrity, and availability, including protection against unauthorized access, unexpected disruptions, adversarial activity, and changing operating conditions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI becomes more deeply integrated into healthcare workflows, security considerations must extend across the complete technology lifecycle. This includes the AI model, data pipeline, application layer, user interface, deployment environment, and operational processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A responsible security strategy therefore evaluates the specific risks associated with each use case and applies appropriate controls across the relevant technology layers.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recognitions and Certifications</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">External standards, certifications, recognitions, and assurance activities provide another supporting element within our governance framework. These mechanisms can offer structure and transparency around organizational approaches to quality, privacy, cybersecurity, risk management, and governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Within Responsible AI, such recognitions should be viewed as components of a broader operating model rather than standalone measures of responsibility. They can help demonstrate that appropriate frameworks and controls are being applied, processes are documented, and organizational practices are subject to established forms of review.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Relevant certifications and frameworks may address areas including quality management, information security, privacy, risk management, software development, and AI governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their effectiveness ultimately depends on their scope, relevance to the organization and use case, and how well the associated practices are incorporated into everyday development and deployment activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Certifications and external recognitions can help demonstrate organizational maturity, but they do not eliminate the need for continuous governance, validation, monitoring, risk assessment, and improvement.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bringing Responsible AI Together</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When combined, governance, quality and validation, privacy and security, and external recognitions provide a practical foundation for advancing Responsible AI in healthcare.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The governance framework establishes ownership, oversight, escalation processes, and alignment with intended use. Quality and validation practices help determine whether AI systems perform reliably, consistently, and appropriately within their intended environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privacy and security safeguards help protect sensitive data, systems, users, and the trust that healthcare organizations and patients place in technology. Recognitions and certifications can provide additional evidence of alignment with relevant standards, frameworks, and assurance practices.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This lifecycle-based approach is designed to translate Responsible AI principles into practical processes across the development and deployment journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responsible AI is not a one-time activity. As AI technologies, healthcare environments, regulations, risks, and user expectations continue to evolve, responsible development requires ongoing evaluation and improvement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For GE HealthCare, this approach builds on more than 125 years of healthcare innovation and long-standing relationships with clinicians, healthcare organizations, patients, and other stakeholders.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining innovation with structured governance, rigorous validation, privacy and security protections, and continuous oversight, healthcare organizations can work toward AI systems that are not only capable, but also trustworthy, responsible, and aligned with the needs of the people they are designed to serve.</p>', 'Responsible AI in Healthcare: GE HealthCare Framework', 'Explore GE HealthCare’s Responsible AI framework covering governance, validation, privacy, security, certifications, and lifecycle oversight in healthcare.', 'Responsible AI in healthcare, healthcare AI, GE HealthCare AI, responsible artificial intelligence, AI governance, healthcare technology, AI validation, AI privacy and security, clinical AI, AI risk management, healthcare innovation, responsible AI framework', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-17 17:41:17', 'published', 58433, 0, 7, NULL, '2024-07-17 17:41:17', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(344, 2, 2, 2, 'Health Systems Building AI Agents Must Balance Trust, Governance and Token Budgets', 'health-systems-building-ai-agents-must-balance-trust-governance-and-token-budgets', 'Health systems deploying AI agents are balancing clinical oversight, governance, employee trust and token costs while measuring AI-driven workflow ROI.', '[\"Healthcare AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI Agents\",\"Agentic AI\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Epic EHR\",\"AI Governance\",\"LLM\",\"Token Budgeting\",\"Clinical AI\",\"Health Systems\",\"AI Adoption\",\"Digital Health\",\"AI Workflow Automation\"]', 'banner_image-1786376093443-324213678.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health Systems Building AI Agents Must Balance Trust, Governance and Token Budgets</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations are increasingly exploring autonomous AI agents to streamline clinical and administrative workflows, but deploying these technologies at scale requires more than simply building capable AI tools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As discussed in the first part of this series, Advocate Health and ECU Health have been using Epic\'s EHR-integrated Agent Factory platform to develop and deploy agentic AI applications. Their experiences demonstrate how health systems can use no-code AI development while still addressing important questions around clinical oversight, governance, trust and cost.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Andy Crowder, chief data and AI officer at Advocate Health, and Jacob Parrish, vice president of clinical operations at ECU Health, have highlighted two issues that are becoming increasingly important as healthcare organizations expand their use of AI agents: maintaining long-term confidence in AI-generated outputs and understanding the financial implications of large language model usage.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establishing Strict Architectural Rules for AI Agents</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At Advocate Health, AI agents are developed through collaborative \"immersion events\" held approximately every six weeks. Frontline physicians, nurses and pharmacists work alongside internal engineering teams and Epic engineers to design and refine workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach ensures that clinicians who understand the day-to-day realities of healthcare delivery have a direct role in shaping how AI is used.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A major principle behind Advocate\'s AI Studio is maintaining a human-in-the-loop model. AI agents are not permitted to independently perform high-impact clinical actions without appropriate human review and approval.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Crowder, AI should not independently order treatments, make diagnoses or take other consequential clinical actions without oversight from the clinician responsible for the workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This type of architecture places accountability with healthcare professionals while allowing AI to assist with repetitive and information-intensive tasks.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real-Time Monitoring and Exception Reporting</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance does not end when an AI agent is deployed. Healthcare organizations also need mechanisms for continuously evaluating whether agents are producing reliable results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate has implemented monitoring capabilities designed to protect data and identify inappropriate agent behavior. Clinicians can also report exceptions when an AI recommendation or output does not appear to be correct.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a clinician may indicate that an AI agent recommended a particular action but that the recommendation was not followed and provide an explanation. These exceptions can then be reviewed by technology and clinical teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The process provides a feedback loop that allows organizations to identify problematic behavior and make changes quickly. If an agent consistently performs poorly, it can be disabled while teams investigate and improve the underlying workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach makes monitoring an ongoing operational responsibility rather than a one-time deployment activity.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Long-Term AI Skills Across the Organization</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate is also preparing AI literacy training for its workforce of approximately 162,000 employees.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, enterprise AI adoption requires more than employee education. Health systems also need to determine how they will continuously monitor, evaluate and govern AI applications as the number of agents grows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Crowder expects AI error management to become an increasingly important IT responsibility. While Epic provides monitoring and observability capabilities, large health systems may ultimately rely on multiple vendors to manage their AI environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate is therefore exploring additional technologies and proof-of-concept projects to strengthen its internal capabilities around AI monitoring and management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Even when humans remain involved in the workflow, some situations may require an additional technology layer that identifies exceptions and determines when further intervention is necessary.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Depending on the organization, this capability may be described as AI monitoring, agent orchestration or AI operations. Regardless of terminology, health systems will need architectural capabilities that allow them to manage increasingly complex collections of AI agents.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health Builds on Existing AI Governance</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health approached Agent Factory from a somewhat different starting point.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization had already implemented governance processes for evaluating AI technologies outside of the Epic environment. As a result, introducing Agent Factory did not require creating an entirely new governance structure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Parrish noted that the partnership with Epic also simplified some of the implementation process because the relevant healthcare data was already contained within the Epic environment and protected by existing security and privacy controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This highlights an important consideration for healthcare organizations adopting AI: existing governance, security and data-management frameworks can significantly influence how quickly new AI capabilities can be introduced.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than creating completely separate processes for every new AI tool, organizations can benefit from establishing enterprise-wide principles that can be applied across different AI platforms and use cases.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding the Cost of AI Agents</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust and governance are only part of the challenge. Healthcare organizations must also understand how much it costs to operate AI agents.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large language models typically charge based on token consumption. While technology teams may already understand the concept of tokens, budgeting for fractional token usage can be unfamiliar territory for healthcare organizations beginning to deploy LLM-powered applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organizations running AI pilots, this creates a potential budgeting risk.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A pilot may appear inexpensive at the beginning, but significant usage across employees and workflows could result in substantially higher costs than initially anticipated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health had to work through questions surrounding token consumption and determine how those costs could translate into its technology budget.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The fundamental question is whether an organization is potentially facing a relatively small AI expense or a much larger operational commitment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Agent Factory is designed to be model-agnostic, but customers are responsible for the token-related costs associated with the underlying models used through the platform. This means health systems must carefully evaluate which AI agents justify their ongoing operating costs.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connecting AI Projects to Business and Clinical Value</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The emergence of token-based AI spending also changes how healthcare organizations should evaluate technology projects.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Jeremy Harper, chief information officer at Owl Health Works and a board member of Indiana HIMSS, has argued that healthcare organizations need to connect AI project lifecycles to measurable operational realities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every process is an appropriate candidate for a large language model.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should identify workflows where AI can meaningfully accelerate work, reduce manual effort or improve outcomes. At the same time, they should recognize situations where AI may provide only limited value compared with its cost and complexity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This type of screening can help healthcare IT teams prioritize AI initiatives based on measurable return on investment rather than simply pursuing every available AI capability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach is particularly important as token consumption becomes part of the operating cost of AI-enabled workflows.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Staying Focused After Initial AI Success</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once ECU Health\'s teams gained a better understanding of token budgeting, another challenge emerged: managing expectations around what AI agents could do next.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Employees using the initial agents naturally began wondering what other tasks the technology could perform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That enthusiasm can be valuable, but it can also cause organizations to expand AI projects too quickly before they fully understand their effectiveness, cost and operational impact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health has therefore taken an intentional approach to expanding its initial collection of agents.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than immediately adding numerous capabilities, leadership has focused on improving the specific workflows for which the initial agents were created.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is straightforward: use feedback from employees to make existing workflows easier and more effective before expanding into additional use cases.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Feedback Becomes a Critical AI Improvement Mechanism</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">User feedback has become an important part of ECU Health\'s AI development process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Employees working with the agents can quickly indicate whether a generated result was useful or whether it needs improvement. Because the organization can collect this feedback in real time, technology teams can identify patterns and make adjustments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The feedback can also be connected with operational metrics and dashboards, creating a more comprehensive view of how the agents are performing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This combination of direct user feedback and measurable performance data allows AI teams to continuously refine workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare organizations, this iterative model can be especially valuable because AI performance cannot be evaluated solely through technical metrics. The real test is whether the technology helps clinicians and staff complete their work more effectively without introducing unacceptable risks.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Path Toward Responsible Agentic AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The experiences of Advocate Health and ECU Health demonstrate that successful healthcare AI deployment involves much more than selecting an AI model or building an autonomous agent.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems need a framework that combines human oversight, governance, monitoring, employee education, cost management and continuous improvement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human-in-the-loop controls can help preserve clinical accountability. Real-time monitoring and exception reporting can identify problematic outputs. Enterprise governance can establish consistent standards for AI adoption, while token budgeting can help organizations understand the financial implications of scaling LLM-powered workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, healthcare leaders must remain disciplined about selecting use cases that provide measurable value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As health systems move from individual AI experiments toward larger collections of autonomous agents, these capabilities will become increasingly important. The organizations that succeed will likely be those that treat AI agents not simply as new software features, but as enterprise technologies requiring ongoing governance, financial management and operational oversight.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ultimate goal is not to deploy the largest possible number of AI agents. It is to build trustworthy AI systems that make healthcare workflows more efficient while delivering measurable value and maintaining the confidence of the people who rely on them.</p>', 'Healthcare AI Agents: Trust, Governance and Token Costs', 'Health systems are balancing AI trust, clinical oversight, governance and token costs as they deploy agentic AI to improve healthcare workflows.', 'healthcare AI agents, agentic AI, healthcare artificial intelligence, AI governance, AI in healthcare, Epic Agent Factory, healthcare IT, LLM token costs, AI token budgeting, clinical AI, health system AI, AI workflow automation, AI governance healthcare, autonomous AI agents', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-19 12:24:48', 'published', 176012, 0, 8, NULL, '2024-07-19 12:24:48', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(345, 2, 1, 14, 'What Health Systems Need to Prepare Before Launching AI Agents', 'what-health-systems-need-to-prepare-before-launching-ai-agents', 'Health systems adopting AI agents must address governance, security, data quality and workforce readiness before deploying autonomous workflows.', '[\"Healthcare AI\",\"AI Agents\",\"Agentic AI\",\"Healthcare IT\",\"Epic Systems\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Health Systems\",\"Clinical AI\",\"Healthcare Automation\",\"EHR\",\"Digital Health\",\"AI Governance\"]', 'banner_image-1786376693936-425789512.webp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Health Systems Need to Prepare Before Launching AI Agents</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations are moving beyond traditional AI assistants and beginning to explore autonomous AI agents that can perform tasks, coordinate workflows and make recommendations within clinical environments. However, having the financial resources to adopt these technologies does not necessarily mean a health system is prepared to deploy them successfully.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations considering agentic AI must first address several foundational issues, including compliance, cybersecurity, data governance, validation, workflow design and internal AI expertise. They also need to understand how changes in underlying data can affect the reliability of autonomous systems over time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare leaders and AI experts have cautioned that organizations adopting agentic AI simply because they fear missing the next major technology trend could encounter significant challenges if their infrastructure and teams are not ready.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to industry experts, successful AI-agent deployments depend heavily on whether an organization has already established strong data governance, developed the necessary technical capabilities and redesigned workflows to accommodate autonomous systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Two health systems using Epic\'s Agent Factory demonstrate how organizations with very different levels of technical resources can approach agentic AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate Health, a large multi-state healthcare organization operating major Epic environments, entered the initiative with an established artificial intelligence and machine learning capability. It has since deployed autonomous workflows supporting inpatient pharmacy operations and infusion preparation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health, a regional academic health network serving eastern North Carolina, took a different path. With a smaller technology organization and fewer resources to build sophisticated AI systems internally, the health system worked with Epic to create and deploy two AI-based workflows supporting patient transfers and discharge planning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Both organizations began using their agents in May and provide examples of how healthcare providers can apply autonomous AI to operational challenges while maintaining appropriate controls.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preparing for Agentic AI in Healthcare</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deploying an autonomous AI agent is considerably different from introducing a conventional software application.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations need to establish governance frameworks, determine how agents will access and use clinical information, validate outputs and create processes for monitoring performance after deployment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI expertise is another important consideration. Health systems without sufficient internal knowledge may struggle to maintain, evaluate or improve autonomous systems after implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data quality also presents a long-term challenge. Changes in patient populations, workflows, documentation practices or other source data can create data drift, potentially affecting the accuracy of an AI agent.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For this reason, organizations should treat AI deployment as an ongoing operational capability rather than a one-time technology implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Health systems that have already invested in data governance, technical teams and workflow optimization are better positioned to take advantage of autonomous AI.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Eliminating Additional Integration Layers</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epic introduced Agent Factory as a platform designed to allow healthcare organizations to create, customize and coordinate autonomous AI agents within the Epic electronic health record environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform provides a visual, no-code environment where organizations can work with preconfigured agent templates or create customized workflows. These agents can then be aligned with an organization\'s internal policies, clinical procedures and operational requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the platform\'s major advantages is its ability to work directly with existing Epic workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of requiring organizations to connect separate applications through additional APIs, middleware or external interfaces, Agent Factory can coordinate tools and information already available within the Epic environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach can reduce the number of additional systems clinicians need to interact with during their daily work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare organizations, this type of embedded integration can be particularly important because clinicians are often reluctant to switch between multiple applications to complete a single task.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security and monitoring are also important components of the platform. Organizations can use built-in capabilities to control access and monitor, trace and audit the actions taken by autonomous agents.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another advantage is direct access to Epic\'s existing data environment. Organizations do not necessarily need to create a separate data abstraction process or repository before building certain workflows.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health Uses AI for Patient Transfers and Discharge Planning</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health provides an example of how a smaller healthcare organization can use an enterprise AI platform without building every component internally.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The North Carolina-based health system worked with Epic developers to create two Agent Factory workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The first supports the organization\'s Transfer Center by validating locations and generating concise patient case summaries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The second supports discharge planning activities associated with progression-of-care rounds.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Transfer Center workflow analyzes selected information from the health system\'s EHR environment and creates short, structured summaries for staff responsible for coordinating patient transfers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than requiring incoming staff members to manually review extensive patient records, the agent extracts relevant information and provides a focused summary that follows defined clinical capabilities and transfer requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The workflow was designed with a deliberately narrow scope. Instead of asking an AI model to interpret an entire medical record and generate an unrestricted clinical assessment, the system works with a specific set of EHR information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That controlled approach has helped the organization maintain a high level of confidence in the results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health reported that the workflow had not produced a hallucination during its initial use and reduced chart-review work by approximately 20 hours per week. The organization estimated that the initial productivity improvement represented roughly half of a full-time equivalent position.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The health system has also intentionally limited the amount of information available to its AI workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, ECU Health has chosen not to allow the system to analyze patient phone conversations at this stage. The organization believes that maintaining a controlled data scope is appropriate while it continues to gain experience with autonomous AI.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-Assisted Discharge Planning</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The second ECU Health workflow focuses on progression-of-care rounds involving case managers and clinical teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The health system operates nine hospitals, and the AI-supported workflow is available to teams participating in discharge planning activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During these rounds, multidisciplinary teams review patient progress, identify barriers to discharge and determine what actions are required to move patients toward the next stage of care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Previously, case managers could spend significant time reviewing charts and compiling information before these discussions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The AI workflow generates a high-level summary of the patient\'s stay, allowing case managers to review important information before the meeting and concentrate on decisions and follow-up activities during the discussion.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The implementation also demonstrated why continuous feedback is essential for autonomous AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">On the first day, many users rated the generated summaries negatively because they were too lengthy and resembled traditional medical-record documentation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The technology team responded by adjusting the prompt constraints and reducing the output to the most relevant information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The change produced a significant improvement. By the second day, approximately 75% of case managers were providing positive feedback.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although the financial return from this workflow is more difficult to quantify, the health system reported that it improved the quality and efficiency of discharge discussions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of spending the beginning of each meeting reading reports and manually preparing notes, case managers can review the AI-generated summary before the meeting and arrive prepared to discuss specific actions.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate Health Applies Agents to Pharmacy Operations</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate Health represents a different scale of AI adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The multi-state healthcare organization already had an established internal AI and machine learning capability when it began working with Epic on Agent Factory.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of its initial applications focuses on inpatient medication-order verification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pharmacists review medication orders throughout the day to determine whether medications are appropriate for the patient\'s current condition and treatment plan.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The AI workflow acts as an investigative assistant by gathering relevant information from the patient\'s medical history, existing medications, current clinical activities and progress documentation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of manually searching through multiple sections of the patient\'s record, pharmacists receive a consolidated view of the information needed to support medication verification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not to replace the pharmacist\'s clinical judgment. Rather, the agent reduces the amount of time spent gathering background information so pharmacists can focus their attention on the verification process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach demonstrates an important model for healthcare AI: autonomous systems can be used to reduce administrative and information-retrieval workloads while keeping qualified professionals involved in critical decisions.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting Infusion and Oncology Workflows</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advocate Health is also using autonomous AI to support infusion-related workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Infusion services can create substantial administrative demands for clinical teams, particularly in areas such as patient preparation, treatment coordination and outreach.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI agents are being used to automate portions of these processes, helping clinical staff prepare patients and organize information before treatment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One workflow uses real-time clinical information to help accelerate the preparation of treatments, including therapies that depend on current laboratory results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organization is also using an agent through the MyChart patient portal to communicate with patients before appointments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Patients can receive instructions about preparation requirements, including whether they need to fast or adjust their usual medications before treatment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By beginning this preparation process weeks before an appointment, autonomous workflows can help identify requirements earlier and reduce last-minute administrative work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For patients undergoing complex treatments such as chemotherapy, this type of preparation can also reduce unnecessary friction on the day of treatment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Trust Through Continuous Feedback</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The experiences of ECU Health and Advocate Health highlight an important lesson for healthcare organizations exploring agentic AI: successful deployment requires more than simply activating an AI system.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations need clear governance, defined workflows, appropriate data boundaries and mechanisms for collecting feedback.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">User feedback can be particularly valuable during the early stages of deployment. An AI agent may technically produce a correct response but still fail to meet operational requirements if the information is too long, difficult to interpret or poorly aligned with how clinicians work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ECU Health discharge-planning experience illustrates this point. A relatively small adjustment to the prompt and output requirements significantly improved user acceptance within a short period.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare organizations should therefore establish processes that allow teams to evaluate AI outputs and make controlled adjustments after deployment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Token Management Becomes a New Operational Skill</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Agentic AI also introduces new technical considerations for healthcare IT teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional software applications generally operate according to predefined computational processes. AI agents, however, rely on model interactions, context and token consumption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations therefore need to understand how token budgets affect system performance, operating costs and the amount of information an agent can process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teams implementing autonomous workflows must balance the need for sufficient context with the requirement to keep outputs focused and efficient.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This makes AI operations a combination of technology management, workflow engineering and clinical governance.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Start Small and Expand Carefully</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The examples from ECU Health and Advocate Health suggest that health systems do not need to automate entire clinical processes immediately.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Narrowly defined workflows can provide a more controlled starting point.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ECU Health\'s transfer workflow, for example, focuses on a specific data set and produces a tightly structured output. Advocate Health\'s pharmacy workflow similarly supports a clearly defined part of the medication verification process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These use cases are easier to monitor and validate than an autonomous system given unrestricted access to an entire patient record and broad clinical decision-making responsibilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Starting with focused workflows can also help healthcare organizations build internal expertise before moving toward more complex applications.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Road Ahead for Autonomous Healthcare AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Agentic AI is creating new possibilities for healthcare organizations to automate information-intensive and administrative processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the technology also introduces new responsibilities around governance, security, validation, monitoring and workforce readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large health systems with established AI teams may be able to develop and manage more sophisticated autonomous workflows internally. Smaller organizations can potentially use platforms such as Epic\'s Agent Factory to access similar capabilities while relying more heavily on platform-level tools and implementation partnerships.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regardless of organizational size, the fundamental requirements remain similar.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare providers need reliable data, well-defined workflows, strong governance and teams capable of monitoring AI performance over time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The early implementations at ECU Health and Advocate Health demonstrate that autonomous agents can generate measurable operational improvements when they are designed around specific organizational needs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The next stage of healthcare AI adoption will therefore depend less on simply having access to autonomous technology and more on whether health systems are prepared to govern, monitor and continuously improve it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Note:</strong> This article focuses on healthcare organizations\' readiness and early use cases for autonomous AI agents. A follow-up discussion can examine architectural approaches for monitoring autonomous agents, managing token usage and addressing the operational challenges that emerge as these systems scale.</p>', 'Health Systems: What They Need to Launch AI Agents', 'Discover how health systems are preparing for AI agents through governance, data security, workflow integration, validation and clinical feedback.', 'healthcare AI, AI agents in healthcare, agentic AI, healthcare technology, health systems AI, Epic Agent Factory, autonomous AI, clinical AI, healthcare automation, AI governance, EHR AI, healthcare IT, digital health, AI in hospitals, healthcare innovation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-23 14:07:19', 'published', 242755, 0, 11, NULL, '2024-07-23 14:07:19', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(346, 2, 2, 2, 'From “Hello, World!” to AI: What Skills Actually Prepare Students for the Future?', 'from-hello-world-to-ai-what-skills-actually-prepare-students-for-the-future', 'As AI reshapes education, computational thinking, critical reasoning and problem-solving may matter more than mastering today’s AI tools.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI in Education\",\"EdTech\",\"Computational Thinking\",\"Computer Science Education\",\"Generative AI\",\"Education Technology\",\"AI Literacy\",\"Future of Work\",\"Digital Learning\",\"Critical Thinking\",\"K-12 Education\"]', 'banner_image-1786378167851-821909753.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From “Hello, World!” to AI: What Skills Actually Prepare Students for the Future?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For more than a decade, schools have been encouraged to prepare students for a technology-driven future. One of the most visible responses was the push to teach coding and computer science.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The message was straightforward: as technology became increasingly important to the workplace, students needed to learn how to build and work with it. Schools introduced programming courses, nonprofit organizations expanded access to computer science education, and countless initiatives promoted coding as an essential skill for the next generation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The movement helped bring computer science into classrooms that previously had little or no exposure to it. But it also revealed an important challenge: learning a particular technology does not necessarily prepare students for every technological change that follows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That question has become increasingly relevant with the rapid adoption of generative AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Schools are again facing pressure to respond quickly to an emerging technology. Teachers are being asked to explore AI tools, understand their capabilities and determine whether these systems belong in everyday instruction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But there is a fundamental difference between introducing students to an AI tool and preparing them to understand technologies that have not yet been invented.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">After two years of research and conversations with teachers who are interested in experimenting with AI, a more complicated picture has emerged. Even educators with backgrounds in engineering and computer science often find it difficult to identify a clear, broadly applicable instructional purpose for generative AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This raises a larger question:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What should students learn today if the technologies they will use tomorrow are impossible to predict?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increasingly, the answer appears to be less about mastering individual tools and more about developing the computational ideas and reasoning skills that allow students to understand, evaluate and adapt to technology.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Problem With Teaching the Tool</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Much of the conversation around AI education has focused on helping students become effective users of generative AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prompt engineering is one example. Students and educators are increasingly encouraged to learn how to formulate better instructions, refine prompts and interact more effectively with AI systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These skills can be useful. However, they may not remain equally valuable as AI interfaces and capabilities continue to evolve.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology can change considerably faster than educational curricula.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A technique that works particularly well with one generation of AI systems may become unnecessary when a new model, interface or workflow emerges. Building an entire educational strategy around a specific tool therefore creates a risk: students may learn how to operate technology without developing the deeper reasoning required to understand it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The history of computer science education provides a useful comparison.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During the early expansion of coding education, schools and organizations promoted programming as an important pathway into the technology workforce. These initiatives increased exposure to computer science and gave many students their first opportunity to write code.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, increased access did not automatically translate into predictable technology career pathways for every student.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One lesson from that period is particularly relevant today: <strong>technology-specific knowledge can become outdated, while underlying ways of thinking can remain useful.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The same principle should be considered when designing AI education.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If the goal is to prepare students for long-term technological change, education should not focus exclusively on how to operate today\'s AI systems.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A More Durable Skill: Computational Thinking</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One potential foundation is computational thinking.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Computational thinking involves a collection of problem-solving approaches commonly associated with computer science. Rather than being limited to writing software, these approaches can be applied to many different academic and professional disciplines.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students practicing computational thinking may learn to:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Break complicated problems into smaller and more manageable parts.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify patterns and relationships within information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Develop logical, step-by-step processes for solving problems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Compare different approaches to a problem.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evaluate the results produced by automated systems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify limitations and potential errors in computational processes.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These capabilities are useful well beyond programming.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Engineering, science, business, public policy and many other fields increasingly depend on systems that process information and generate recommendations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Computational thinking can also help students understand what happens behind the interface of technologies such as AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of simply accepting an AI-generated answer, students can ask:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>How was this result produced?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What information may have influenced it?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What assumptions are built into the system?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Could the output be incorrect or incomplete?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That shift is significant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students are no longer simply learning to use technology. They are learning how to question and evaluate it.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Using AI as Something to Study</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers do not necessarily need to make AI the center of every classroom activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In fact, some of the most valuable AI-related lessons may involve studying the technology rather than depending on it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, students can examine incorrect chatbot responses and investigate why an answer may be unreliable. They can compare how different inputs affect outputs, explore the importance of training data or discuss how algorithms influence the information people receive.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These activities turn AI into an object of investigation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A student might examine two AI-generated responses to the same question and determine which claims require additional evidence. Another student might explore how changing the available information affects a system\'s output.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Such exercises encourage students to think critically about automated systems instead of treating them as unquestionable sources of information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach also connects naturally with skills educators have already been trying to develop, including critical thinking, media literacy, evidence evaluation and problem-solving.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Teachers Are Already Experimenting With</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research with educators suggests that many teachers are already moving toward this type of approach, even when they do not explicitly describe their work as computational thinking.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some teachers ask students to identify mistakes in AI-generated responses. Others use examples of algorithms and data to connect emerging technologies with familiar real-world processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers are also experimenting with course-specific chatbots, AI literacy activities and small-scale classroom applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These experiments demonstrate something important: educators are not necessarily looking for AI to replace existing learning activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, many are trying to understand where the technology can provide educational value without taking over the intellectual work students need to perform themselves.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That distinction may become increasingly important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If an AI system generates the answer, students still need opportunities to determine whether the answer is correct. If AI produces an argument, students should still evaluate the evidence behind it. If a system recommends a solution, students should understand the reasoning and limitations behind that recommendation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective should be to strengthen human reasoning rather than outsource it.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Practical Lessons for Educators</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The uncertainty surrounding generative AI does not mean schools should ignore it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, educators can focus on learning activities that remain useful regardless of which AI products become dominant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One approach is to use AI outputs as material for analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students can evaluate responses, identify inaccuracies, compare competing answers and determine what additional evidence is required.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI lessons can also introduce broader concepts such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data quality</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Algorithmic bias</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Information reliability</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automated decision-making</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Model limitations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evidence evaluation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital literacy</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Computational reasoning</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These topics give students a foundation for understanding AI without tying their education to a particular platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Assignments can similarly emphasize structured reasoning. Rather than asking students simply to generate an answer with AI, educators can ask them to explain their reasoning, verify information and defend their conclusions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This keeps the thinking process at the center of learning.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What This Means for EdTech Companies</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge also presents an opportunity for educational technology developers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many AI products entering education began as general-purpose systems and were later adapted for classroom environments. This can leave teachers responsible for determining whether a product actually addresses a meaningful instructional problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A stronger model may involve educators much earlier in the product-development process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers understand classroom constraints, student behavior, curriculum requirements and the practical realities of instruction. Their input can help technology companies distinguish between features that appear impressive and tools that genuinely solve educational problems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Educators involved in current AI experimentation are already identifying potential use cases, developing classroom activities and creating specialized tools for their courses.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These experiments can function almost like early-stage product development.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Closer collaboration among teachers, school districts, researchers, product managers and edtech companies could therefore help identify where AI can provide measurable educational value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of starting with the question, <strong>“How can we put AI into the classroom?”</strong>, developers could begin with a more useful question:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>“Which learning problem needs to be solved, and can AI actually help solve it?”</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That distinction could influence the next generation of educational technology.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preparing Students for Technologies That Do Not Exist Yet</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The central challenge in technology education is not predicting which tool will dominate the future.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is developing students who can adapt when the technology changes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Coding remains valuable. AI literacy is increasingly important. Understanding digital systems will continue to matter.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But none of these skills should exist in isolation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students also need the ability to decompose problems, recognize patterns, reason systematically, evaluate evidence and question automated outputs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These capabilities can provide a foundation for adapting to technologies that students may encounter years from now.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal, therefore, should not be to turn every student into an AI user or programmer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It should be to help students become capable thinkers who can understand technology, challenge its outputs and determine when it should-or should not-be used.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Next Stage of AI in Education</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The arrival of generative AI has created significant excitement around the future of education, but widespread adoption should not be treated as inevitable or automatically beneficial.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The more important task is determining the circumstances in which AI genuinely improves teaching and learning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That requires continued experimentation, research and thoughtful governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As schools gain more experience with AI systems, educators and institutions will need frameworks for deciding when these technologies are appropriate, how their risks should be managed and how student learning can remain the primary objective.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Future research can help identify which classroom applications produce meaningful benefits and which simply add another layer of technology without improving educational outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question that educators are asking today may therefore be more valuable than any particular AI tool.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What can I actually use this for?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That question reflects a healthy approach to emerging technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Schools do not need to adopt every new tool simply because it exists. Teachers can experiment, evaluate results and retain the approaches that genuinely help students learn.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of education may not depend on teaching students to master today\'s AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It may depend on giving them the reasoning skills they need to understand whatever comes next.</p>', 'AI in Education: Skills Students Need for the Future', 'Discover why computational thinking, critical reasoning and problem-solving may prepare students better for AI and future technologies than tool-specific skills.', 'AI in education, computational thinking, AI literacy, generative AI in education, education technology, EdTech, future of education, computer science education, critical thinking, AI skills, student skills, digital literacy', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-24 10:50:50', 'published', 165769, 0, 10, NULL, '2024-07-24 10:50:50', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(347, 2, 2, 2, 'The AI Use Case Question Teachers Are Still Asking', 'the-ai-use-case-question-teachers-are-still-asking', 'Teachers are using AI to save time, but its real value in classroom learning remains uncertain, especially for core subjects like math.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI in Education\",\"Generative AI\",\"EdTech\",\"Education Technology\",\"Teachers\",\"Classroom AI\",\"AI Literacy\",\"ChatGPT\",\"K-12 Education\",\"Machine Learning\",\"Digital Learning\"]', 'banner_image-1786378778184-853577858.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The AI Use Case Question Teachers Are Still Asking</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A fourth-grade teacher posed a straightforward question that captures one of the biggest challenges surrounding artificial intelligence in education:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>“What can I actually use this for in math?”</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a simple question, but it reflects a much larger issue facing schools today.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Over the past few years, educators have been encouraged to respond to the rapid growth of generative AI tools such as ChatGPT. The conversation has often been dominated by predictions. Supporters have described AI as a technology capable of transforming teaching and learning, while critics have warned about misinformation, academic integrity, student dependence and the erosion of essential learning skills.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Yet the reality inside many classrooms has been considerably more measured.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers have not universally embraced AI, but neither have they broadly rejected it. Instead, many educators appear to be taking a practical approach: <strong>Does this technology solve a problem that actually matters to me or my students?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That question may be more important than the broader debate about whether AI will transform education.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In fall 2024, researchers from EdSurge held discussions with 17 teachers from different parts of the world to better understand how educators were experiencing generative AI. The participants taught students from third grade through 12th grade, and several had created or delivered lessons that either used AI or taught students about the technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their experiences reveal an interesting pattern. Teachers are finding clear value in AI for certain administrative and productivity tasks. However, when it comes to using generative AI as a central part of classroom instruction, the benefits are much less obvious.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For many educators, the dominant response is not enthusiasm or opposition.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is <strong>indifference</strong>.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Technology Moving Faster Than Schools Can Understand</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The speed at which generative AI entered education has created an unusual challenge for schools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditionally, schools introduce new technologies through a relatively structured process. Educators receive training, administrators establish policies, teachers experiment with the tools and schools evaluate whether the technology improves outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generative AI has followed a very different path.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumer-facing tools became widely accessible to students and teachers before many schools had established clear policies, instructional strategies or professional-development programs around them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As a result, educators have been expected to understand a rapidly changing technology while simultaneously figuring out how it should fit into their classrooms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One high school computer science teacher from Georgia described her concern about the way AI is being presented to students, parents and educators. The technology can appear almost mysterious when people do not understand what happens behind the interface.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A high school library media specialist from New York expressed a similar concern about the difficulty of keeping pace with the rapid development of AI tools and their potential influence on education.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These concerns point to a fundamental problem: <strong>the technology is advancing faster than schools can establish a shared understanding of how it should be used.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where Teachers Are Already Finding Value</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">While the instructional value of AI remains uncertain, teachers are finding practical benefits in another area: their own workload.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An engineering and computer science teacher from New Jersey described using generative AI as part of the lesson-planning process. The teacher noted that educators are often required to produce large amounts of administrative material, much of which may receive little attention after it is submitted.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can help generate those materials quickly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other educators described similar experimentation with lesson planning, newsletters and other routine communication tasks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This represents one of the clearest early applications of generative AI in education.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers have extensive responsibilities beyond classroom instruction. Their work can include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lesson planning</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Grading and feedback</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Parent communication</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Newsletter creation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Administrative documentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Classroom materials</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Extracurricular responsibilities</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Curriculum preparation</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When generative AI can reduce the time required for repetitive writing and drafting tasks, its usefulness is relatively easy to recognize.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is consistent with a broader pattern across professional industries. Generative AI is particularly effective at helping people draft, summarize, reorganize and transform information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For teachers dealing with significant administrative workloads, those capabilities can provide immediate value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The important distinction, however, is that <strong>saving a teacher time is not the same as improving student learning.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That difference becomes critical when AI moves from the teacher\'s desk into the classroom.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Classroom Use Case Is Still Difficult to Define</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The question changes when educators consider giving AI tools directly to students.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of asking:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>“Can AI make my work easier?”</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">teachers must ask:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>“What learning problem does AI solve for my students?”</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That question does not always have an obvious answer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some teachers are experimenting with narrowly defined applications. For example, one science teacher from Guam described allowing students to use ChatGPT as part of a writing revision process. Students first create their own work and then use AI to help produce a revised version.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The teacher deliberately places boundaries around the activity, particularly when students are conducting research.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other educators use AI as an object of study rather than simply as an instructional assistant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A high school special education teacher from New York described an activity in which students were shown how an AI system could produce incorrect results after being deliberately trained with poor information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective was not to convince students that AI was intelligent or authoritative.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It was to help them understand that the quality of an AI system depends heavily on the information and processes behind it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These approaches suggest a different philosophy for classroom AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than allowing technology to replace thinking, teachers can use it to create opportunities for students to <strong>question, evaluate, revise and investigate information.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Literacy May Be the More Practical Starting Point</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For many educators, the most compelling classroom application of AI may not be using AI to complete traditional schoolwork at all.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, it may be teaching students <strong>how AI works and how to evaluate its output.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students are already surrounded by algorithmic systems. Recommendation engines, social media feeds, search systems and automated decision-making technologies influence what information they see and how they interact with digital platforms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generative AI adds another layer to this environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That makes AI literacy an increasingly relevant educational skill.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">International organizations including UNESCO and the OECD have emphasized the importance of helping students understand AI systems and their implications. Rather than simply teaching students how to operate AI tools, schools can help them understand how those systems generate information, where they can fail and how people should evaluate their outputs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One elementary teacher from New York described AI literacy as beginning with two essential skills: understanding how to construct useful prompts and learning how to fact-check AI-generated information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another middle school teacher used a familiar classroom activity to explain machine learning concepts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students were asked to think about how they would create the best peanut butter and jelly sandwich. The ingredients represented the data, the process represented the algorithm and the resulting sandwich represented the output.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The exercise provided students with a simple way to understand an important concept:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>The quality of an AI system\'s output depends on the information and process used to create it.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach positions AI as a subject of critical inquiry rather than an unquestioned source of answers.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hallucinations Make Trust a Central Issue</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the strongest concerns among educators is the reliability of generative AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers have repeatedly encountered situations where AI systems produce information that sounds convincing but is incorrect or entirely fabricated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For educators, this creates an additional challenge.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A student who already understands a subject may be able to identify an inaccurate AI response. A student who does not understand the subject may have difficulty recognizing the problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That creates an important distinction between <strong>using AI effectively</strong> and simply <strong>using AI frequently</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A high school French teacher described this challenge by noting that AI can be useful when the person using it already understands the subject well. Without sufficient background knowledge, it becomes much harder to determine whether an AI-generated answer is accurate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This concern extends beyond factual errors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers are also thinking about bias.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI systems learn patterns from data, and those patterns can reflect limitations or biases present in the data used to train them. Educators are therefore increasingly interested in helping students understand how algorithmic systems can produce different outcomes for different groups of people.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A high school computer science teacher in New Jersey connected this issue to real-world examples involving hiring systems, incarceration data and facial-recognition technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this context, AI becomes more than a classroom productivity tool.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It becomes a way for students to explore questions about <strong>data, fairness, bias, reliability and technological decision-making.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Difference Between Assistance and Replacement</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The experiences shared by educators point toward an important distinction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can assist students without necessarily replacing the intellectual work students need to perform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an AI system might help a student identify weaknesses in a draft. A teacher might use AI to generate an initial lesson outline. Students might analyze an AI-generated response and determine whether its claims are supported by evidence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These activities can preserve the student\'s role as the person doing the thinking.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The situation changes when AI performs the central cognitive task instead.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If a student asks AI to solve a math problem, write an essay, conduct research or generate an explanation without engaging with the underlying process, the technology may reduce the opportunity for meaningful learning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Schools are designed in part to give students opportunities to practice difficult cognitive skills.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Those skills include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Critical thinking</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reading comprehension</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Writing</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Problem solving</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reasoning</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evidence evaluation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Independent judgment</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If a technology eliminates the need to practice these skills, educators have legitimate reasons to question whether the technology is supporting the educational objective.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Rise of Pragmatic Indifference</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Taken together, the teachers\' experiences reveal a perspective that is often missing from the public conversation about AI in education.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers are not necessarily rejecting generative AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But they are also not rebuilding their classrooms around it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, many educators appear to be adopting a position of <strong>pragmatic indifference</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They use AI when the benefit is obvious.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They avoid it when the educational value is uncertain.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They experiment when there is a clearly defined purpose.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">And they establish boundaries when AI risks replacing important learning activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a teacher may use AI to create a first draft of an administrative document while refusing to let students rely on AI for research.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another teacher may use AI to support writing revision while still requiring students to produce their initial work independently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These choices are not necessarily signs of resistance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They can instead be understood as examples of professional judgment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers are evaluating the technology based on whether it helps them accomplish meaningful goals.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Question That Still Matters</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The fourth-grade teacher\'s original question remains surprisingly important:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What can I actually use AI for in fourth-grade math?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">After years of intense discussion about artificial intelligence, educators are still looking for clear answers to questions like this.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge is not simply determining what AI can do.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern AI systems can generate explanations, write text, solve problems, summarize information and create learning materials.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The harder question is whether those capabilities improve the learning experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That requires educators to look beyond technological novelty and ask what students actually need.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If AI helps teachers reduce administrative work, there may be a strong practical case for using it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If AI helps students reflect on their writing, question information or understand how algorithmic systems work, it may have meaningful instructional value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But if AI simply completes the cognitive work that students are supposed to practice themselves, its educational value becomes much harder to justify.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This may explain why teachers have not embraced classroom AI as quickly as some technology advocates predicted.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They are waiting for a use case that makes educational sense.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">And perhaps that is exactly the question schools should continue asking:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Not “What can AI do?” but “What should students and teachers use AI to do?”</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The answer will likely determine whether generative AI becomes a meaningful educational technology-or simply another productivity tool that happens to exist inside schools.</p>', 'AI in Education: The Use Case Question Teachers Still Ask', 'Teachers are adopting generative AI for productivity, but its role in classroom learning remains unclear. Explore AI literacy, trust and teaching.', 'AI in education, generative AI in education, AI classroom use, teachers and AI, AI literacy, ChatGPT in education, education technology, EdTech, classroom AI, AI teaching tools', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-26 16:33:21', 'published', 124562, 0, 11, NULL, '2024-07-26 16:33:21', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL),
(348, 2, 2, 3, 'Prohibition Didn’t Stop Alcohol Use. Will It Work With AI?', 'prohibition-didnt-stop-alcohol-use-will-it-work-with-ai', 'Schools are banning AI tools, but educators argue that harm reduction, critical thinking and responsible use may work better than prohibition.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Generative AI\",\"AI in Education\",\"EdTech\",\"ChatGPT\",\"K-12 Education\",\"AI Ethics\",\"AI Policy\",\"Digital Learning\",\"Education Technology\",\"Responsible AI\",\"Harm Reduction\"]', 'banner_image-1786378986861-268429925.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prohibition Didn’t Stop Alcohol Use. Will It Work With AI?</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During one of our focus group sessions, a middle school media and library specialist from New York expressed a concern that captured a broader sentiment among educators:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“We don’t need another policy about what not to do with AI. We need a philosophy that helps teachers think critically about these tools.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That perspective emerged repeatedly during the EdSurge Research project, <strong>“Teaching Tech: Navigating Learning and AI in the Industrial Revolution.”</strong> The educators involved in the study represented schools and communities ranging from Georgia’s peach-growing regions to Guam’s white-sand beaches.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite differences in geography, school environments and teaching responsibilities, many participants shared a common view: when it comes to artificial intelligence in education, guidance and responsible-use frameworks may be more effective than outright bans.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Learning From Educators</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In fall 2024, EdSurge Research spoke with 17 educators from different parts of the world who teach students from third through 12th grade. The discussions focused on their experiences with and perceptions of generative AI, particularly conversational tools such as ChatGPT.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their responses revealed a complicated picture.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers recognized the potential of AI to support learning, lesson planning and creativity. At the same time, they were concerned about academic integrity, overreliance on technology, inaccurate information and students using AI without understanding its limitations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">To explore these perspectives further, some participants were asked to create lesson plans with the assistance of AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That exercise immediately revealed a challenge.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Several educators discovered that their schools had blocked popular AI chatbot websites on school-managed devices. Their work was temporarily disrupted, but participants quickly found alternative ways to access the tools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students, researchers observed, are likely to do the same.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As schools across the United States introduce restrictions on ChatGPT and other generative AI platforms-and as some states implement broader restrictions on student cellphone use-educators may be repeating an old pattern: assuming that restricting access will eliminate usage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our exploratory research suggests otherwise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When access is prohibited in one environment but remains available elsewhere, students may simply move their AI use outside the controlled environment. The technology remains accessible, but the guidance surrounding its use may disappear.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than focusing exclusively on prohibition, educators may benefit from a <strong>harm reduction approach</strong> to student AI use.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Is Harm Reduction?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most adults have experienced the limits of simply telling a child “no.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tell a three-year-old not to touch something, and curiosity may make that object even more attractive. The same basic dynamic can continue as children become teenagers, although the consequences become more complicated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Harm reduction takes a different approach.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of assuming that a potentially risky activity can be eliminated completely, harm reduction acknowledges that the activity exists and focuses on helping people engage with it more safely and responsibly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Applied to education, this means accepting that many students will encounter and use generative AI whether schools permit it or not.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than simply blocking AI tools on school devices and hoping students never use them for homework, schools can provide students with the knowledge and skills required to use those tools thoughtfully.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One computer science and engineering teacher from New Jersey summarized the challenge this way:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“AI can do the task, but can students explain why it matters?”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That question gets to the heart of AI literacy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective should not necessarily be to prevent every interaction between students and AI. Instead, schools can help students understand <strong>when AI is appropriate, how it should be used, where it can fail and why human judgment still matters.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Harm reduction has roots in public health, where it has been used to reduce the negative consequences associated with risky behaviors and substances. While no approach is perfect, its underlying principle is valuable for education: acknowledging reality can create better opportunities for prevention and responsible decision-making.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In K-12 education, that means balancing legitimate concerns about AI with the reality that students are already encountering these technologies.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Beyond Moral Panic and Blind Optimism</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI discussions in education often fall into two extremes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One side sees generative AI primarily as a threat to academic integrity, student learning and traditional teaching methods. The other sees AI as an almost unlimited opportunity to transform education.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neither perspective fully captures what educators are experiencing in classrooms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A harm reduction model occupies the middle ground.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It does not suggest that schools should allow unrestricted AI use. Nor does it argue that every AI interaction is dangerous. Instead, it encourages developmentally appropriate exposure, clear expectations and opportunities for students to develop critical thinking skills.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach will not eliminate every AI-generated essay or prevent every student from misusing a chatbot.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But it may help schools address the reality of AI use rather than attempting to make that reality invisible.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cautiously Curious</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Across the focus group sessions, educators described AI in schools as both exciting and disruptive.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They were curious about what the technology could accomplish, but they were also concerned about its consequences. Many wanted their students to explore AI responsibly while maintaining academic standards and professional boundaries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A high school special education teacher from New York City described the challenge:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“My students ask if they’re cheating when they use AI. I tell them - if you’re learning with it, not from it, that’s a good start.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The distinction is important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Using AI as a learning partner is fundamentally different from asking AI to complete an assignment and submitting the result without understanding it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The educator’s response also demonstrates how teachers can redirect student behavior rather than simply prohibit it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An elementary technology teacher from Texas raised another important question:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“We talk a lot about academic integrity, but no one’s defining what integrity looks like in the age of AI.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That uncertainty is becoming increasingly common.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">School districts are beginning to create AI policies and recommendations, but many teachers still lack practical guidance about issues such as disclosure, acceptable AI assistance and how much AI involvement should be permitted in different assignments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As a result, some educators are developing classroom-level frameworks of their own.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They may ask students to disclose when AI was used, explain how they used it or reflect on whether the technology contributed to their learning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These practices make AI use more visible instead of forcing it underground.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this sense, harm reduction is not about lowering ethical expectations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is about preparing students to meet a technological reality with stronger judgment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Three Principles of AI Harm Reduction in Schools</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Our analysis of educator experiences, combined with existing research, points toward three important areas for implementing harm reduction in K-12 education:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systems</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pedagogy</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Community</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Each addresses a different part of the educational ecosystem.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Systems: Embedded or Optional?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI is already integrated into many of the digital products schools and teachers use every day.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An engineering teacher at a virtual school in Georgia explained:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“If the tools are already in what we use every day, pretending they aren’t doesn’t make us safer.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The first step, therefore, is transparency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Schools can examine the technologies already embedded in their systems and understand where AI is being used. This includes reviewing software contracts, asking vendors to disclose AI functionality and communicating clearly with educators, students and families about how AI-powered tools operate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The same principle should apply to classroom activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If teachers use AI to brainstorm lesson ideas, create activities or refine instructional materials, that use can become an opportunity to model responsible behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of hiding AI involvement, educators can demonstrate how they evaluate AI-generated information, identify weaknesses and make final decisions themselves.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This helps students understand that using AI does not eliminate human responsibility.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Pedagogy: Building Capacity Through Co-Learning</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technology adoption often assumes that teachers will automatically understand how to use a new platform simply because the platform is available.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That assumption is particularly problematic with generative AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A literacy coach from Illinois explained:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“We can’t just give teachers a new platform and expect them to know what’s ethical. That has to be a learning process.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI literacy should therefore become an ongoing process rather than a one-time training session.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teachers and students can experiment with AI through small-scale classroom activities, pilot programs and collaborative discussions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They can compare AI-generated answers with human-generated responses, identify factual errors, evaluate sources and discuss when AI assistance strengthens learning versus when it replaces learning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a culture of <strong>co-learning</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The teacher does not need to present themselves as an AI expert with every answer. Instead, educators and students can investigate the technology together while maintaining appropriate boundaries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not to replace teachers with AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is to help teachers understand how AI can function as one tool within a broader instructional strategy.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Community: Create Context-Specific Guardrails</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There is no single AI policy that will work equally well for every classroom.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The needs of a kindergarten classroom are very different from those of an advanced high school computer science course.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Likewise, expectations may differ depending on student age, subject area, assignment type and community values.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For that reason, AI guardrails should be adaptable.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">District leaders can involve teachers, parents, students and other community stakeholders in defining responsible AI practices. Rather than creating policies entirely at the administrative level, schools can develop expectations collaboratively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can include questions such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When should students disclose AI use?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which assignments permit AI assistance?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When should AI tools be prohibited?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How should students verify AI-generated information?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What kinds of AI use support learning?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What constitutes inappropriate dependence on AI?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Context-specific rules can be more meaningful than blanket restrictions because they reflect the actual needs of the students and teachers affected by them.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Theory to Practice</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These three principles-systems, pedagogy and community-can help translate harm reduction from an abstract concept into a practical framework for schools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach does not eliminate the need for rules.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, it changes the purpose of those rules.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than asking only, “How do we stop students from using AI?” schools can also ask:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>“How do we prepare students to use AI responsibly?”</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That shift is significant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blocking a chatbot may prevent a student from accessing it on a school-issued laptop. But if the same student can access the technology from a personal phone, tablet or home computer, the restriction may only move the behavior somewhere educators have less visibility.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The student is still using AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The difference is that they may be doing so without guidance.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Could AI Harm Reduction Look Like?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A harm reduction approach could include practical measures such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teaching students how generative AI systems work.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establishing clear classroom rules for acceptable AI assistance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Requiring students to disclose meaningful AI involvement.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Teaching students to fact-check AI-generated information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Discussing hallucinations, bias and limitations in AI systems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Designing assignments that emphasize reasoning and personal understanding.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Giving students opportunities to critique AI-generated work.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Training teachers to evaluate AI tools critically.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reviewing AI functionality within existing school technology.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creating age-appropriate AI policies with input from the school community.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These strategies recognize that responsible AI use requires more than access restrictions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Students need an ethical framework for navigating the technology.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preparing Students for an AI-Driven Future</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A young student interacting with an AI chatbot may experience something very different from interacting with a traditional search engine.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Chatbots can respond instantly, maintain conversational context and provide seemingly supportive answers. For students with developing critical-thinking skills, that experience can be powerful-and potentially misleading.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The solution is not necessarily to eliminate the interaction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, schools can help students understand why an AI system responds the way it does, what its limitations are and when its answers should be questioned.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is particularly important as AI becomes increasingly embedded in everyday technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge facing educators is therefore larger than ChatGPT.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Schools are preparing students for a world in which AI will increasingly influence communication, employment, information discovery, creativity and decision-making.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prohibition alone cannot prepare students for that world.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Candor, Care and Curiosity</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The educators who participated in this research did not suggest abandoning academic integrity or allowing unrestricted AI use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, their experiences point toward a more nuanced approach.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI use among students is likely to continue regardless of whether every school permits it. The question is whether students will encounter these technologies with meaningful guidance or without it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A harm reduction framework provides one possible path.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It combines transparency with boundaries, curiosity with caution and technological experimentation with human judgment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is not to make AI risk-free.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is to help students and educators become better equipped to recognize risks, make informed choices and use technology in ways that support genuine learning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As schools navigate this latest industrial revolution, the most productive response may not be to simply say “no.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It may be to teach students <strong>how, when and why to say yes responsibly.</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Disclosure Statement</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Parts of this article were developed with the assistance of generative AI to help organize qualitative information and refine language. The analysis, interpretation and final editorial decisions remain the responsibility of the editorial and research team. AI was used as a supporting research and writing tool rather than as an author or independent decision-maker.</p>', 'AI in Schools: Why Harm Reduction May Beat AI Bans', 'Schools are banning AI tools, but educators argue that harm reduction, critical thinking and responsible AI use may be more effective.', 'AI in schools, generative AI in education, AI education policy, ChatGPT in schools, AI harm reduction, responsible AI, AI ethics in education, K-12 AI, EdTech, AI literacy', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2024-07-30 11:16:52', 'published', 120502, 0, 12, NULL, '2024-07-30 11:16:52', '2026-10-05 19:46:48', 1, NULL);
