(898, 6, 1, 5, 'Iran Cyberattacks on Israel Triple in a Year, Intensifying the Cybersecurity Threat', 'iran-cyberattacks-on-israel-triple-in-a-year-intensifying-the-cybersecurity-threat', 'Iran-linked cyberattacks targeting Israel reportedly tripled in a year, raising concerns over critical infrastructure, businesses, and cyber warfare.', '[\"Iran cyberattacks\",\"Israel cyberattacks\",\"Iran Israel cyber conflict\",\"cybersecurity\",\"cyber warfare\",\"cyber threats\",\"critical infrastructure security\",\"cyberattacks\",\"Middle East cybersecurity\",\"government cybersecurity\",\"business cybersecurity\",\"cyber defense\",\"geopolitical cyber threats\",\"cybersecurity news\"]', 'banner_image-1790110929704-816853845.png', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Iran Cyberattacks on Israel Triple in a Year, Intensifying the Cybersecurity Threat</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cyberattacks targeting Israel have increased sharply over the past year, highlighting the growing role of cyberspace in the broader conflict between Iran and Israel.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Yossi Karadi, Director General of Israel\'s National Cyber Directorate, authorities recorded approximately 1,600 hostile cyber incidents during Israeli military operations in June 2025. By June 2026, that figure had risen to nearly 4,800 incidents-roughly three times the level recorded a year earlier.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The increase underscores how cyber operations have become an important component of regional tensions, operating alongside conventional military activity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cyberattacks Expand Beyond Government Targets</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">While missile strikes and military operations have dominated international headlines, cyber incidents have increasingly affected a broader range of organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Karadi said that attacks have targeted government systems, essential services, businesses, law firms, accounting firms, and other private-sector organizations. Israeli authorities have stated that critical infrastructure has so far remained protected despite continued attempts to compromise systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Karadi also warned that some of the groups involved have significant technical capabilities and should be taken seriously.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to an interview reported in German-language media, Karadi emphasized that cybersecurity operations do not necessarily stop when conventional military activity decreases. In cyberspace, hostile activity can continue even when there is no active exchange of fire.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses Face Increasing Cybersecurity Risks</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The reported increase in cyber incidents also highlights the vulnerability of organizations outside the government and defense sectors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Karadi said that several companies with weaker cybersecurity protections had their computer systems wiped after attackers gained access to their networks. Such incidents demonstrate how cyberattacks can cause significant operational disruption without directly targeting military infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For businesses, the threat extends beyond data theft. Attackers can potentially disrupt internal systems, compromise sensitive information, interrupt services, and damage critical business operations.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cyber Warfare Becomes a Persistent Regional Concern</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Iran has denied responsibility for cyberattacks against other countries, while Iranian authorities have also reported that their own systems have been targeted by hackers. As a result, determining the origin and responsibility of individual cyber incidents can be challenging.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Nevertheless, the continued increase in reported attacks illustrates the expanding role of cyber operations in geopolitical conflicts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unlike conventional military attacks, cyber operations can be conducted remotely and may continue across borders regardless of whether military operations are active. This makes cybersecurity an increasingly important consideration for governments, infrastructure operators, and private companies.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What the Increase Means for Organizations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The reported rise in cyber incidents serves as a reminder that cybersecurity is no longer limited to protecting government networks or military systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations operating critical services, financial systems, legal services, healthcare infrastructure, and business networks can all become potential targets. Strong access controls, network monitoring, incident response capabilities, employee awareness, vulnerability management, and regularly tested backups can therefore play an important role in reducing operational risk.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The developments surrounding Iran and Israel also demonstrate how cyber operations can become a persistent element of geopolitical conflicts. Even when conventional military activity declines, malicious cyber activity may continue in the background.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As cyberattacks become increasingly integrated into modern conflicts, governments and businesses face the challenge of defending systems against threats that may not produce visible physical damage but can still disrupt essential services and economic activity.</p>', 'Iran Cyberattacks on Israel Triple | Cybersecurity News', 'Iran cyberattacks targeting Israel reportedly tripled in a year, raising concerns about critical infrastructure, businesses, and cyber warfare.', 'Iran cyberattacks, Israel cyberattacks, Iran Israel cyber conflict, cybersecurity news, cyber warfare, cyber threats, critical infrastructure cybersecurity, cyber defense, Middle East cyberattacks, Iran cyber threat', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2026-10-05 17:45:00', 'published', 5291635, 0, 3, NULL, '2026-10-05 17:45:00', '2026-10-05 19:46:47', 1, NULL),
(899, 6, 2, 19, 'How Accounting Automation Helps E-Commerce Finance Teams Accelerate Growth', 'how-accounting-automation-helps-e-commerce-finance-teams-accelerate-growth', 'Accounting automation helps e-commerce finance teams reduce manual work, improve accuracy, and gain real-time visibility to support faster growth.', '[\"Accounting Automation\",\"Finance Automation\",\"E-Commerce\",\"Financial Technology\",\"FinTech\",\"Lease Accounting\",\"ASC 842\",\"Accounting Software\",\"Finance Teams\",\"Business Automation\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Financial Operations\",\"E-Commerce Growth\",\"Financial Data\",\"Automation Technology\"]', 'banner_image-1790260219210-895922406.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">E-commerce businesses are increasingly turning to automation to improve fraud prevention, customer experiences, and operational efficiency. However, finance departments within many of these companies continue to depend on spreadsheets and other manual processes for critical accounting activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This gap can create unnecessary delays, increase the possibility of errors, and make it harder for finance teams to access reliable financial information when they need it most.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Commerce protection platform <strong>Signifyd</strong> experienced this challenge firsthand. The company implemented <strong>NetLease by Netgain</strong>, an accounting automation solution, to modernize its lease accounting processes and reduce its dependence on complex spreadsheets.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Beyond Manual Lease Accounting</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge became particularly visible after an accounting manager left Signifyd. The company was relying on a complicated spreadsheet to manage its <strong>ASC 842 lease accounting</strong> requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maintaining the spreadsheet required considerable manual effort and created opportunities for data inaccuracies. Finance employees were spending as much as <strong>eight hours each month</strong> managing lease accounting activities rather than concentrating on broader financial and strategic initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ASC 842 is a lease accounting standard that requires organizations to recognize most leases on their balance sheets. The standard is designed to provide greater transparency by making lease-related assets and obligations more visible in financial reporting.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For a growing e-commerce organization, manually managing these requirements can become increasingly difficult as operations, transactions, and financial data expand.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation Reduces the Finance Team\'s Workload</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By moving away from its spreadsheet-based approach and implementing automated lease accounting, Signifyd was able to simplify its financial processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company reported a <strong>50% reduction in the time required for lease accounting</strong> while also improving data accuracy. Removing the dependency on a complex spreadsheet gave the finance team more time to focus on activities that could directly support the company\'s broader business objectives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The change also illustrates how accounting automation can become more than a back-office efficiency tool. When routine financial processes are automated, finance professionals can spend more time providing insights and supporting strategic decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Risks of Manual Financial Processes</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to <strong>Chris Miller, SVP of Product Strategy at Netgain</strong>, finance teams that continue to depend heavily on manual processes can face several operational challenges.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Manual data entry increases the risk of mistakes and can result in discrepancies that require additional time to identify and correct. At the same time, slow transaction processing can delay access to important financial information and potentially affect the speed of business decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Limited visibility is another concern. When financial information is spread across spreadsheets and manually maintained systems, obtaining timely and reliable insights can become more difficult.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For rapidly growing e-commerce companies, these challenges can become more significant as transaction volumes and operational complexity increase.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation Supports Scalable E-Commerce Operations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automating accounting activities such as invoicing, reconciliation, and lease accounting can help finance teams reduce repetitive work while improving the consistency of financial data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For companies operating in the fast-moving e-commerce market, this can provide finance teams with more timely information and reduce the administrative burden associated with maintaining manual financial processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Miller noted that e-commerce companies can spend significant amounts of time each month handling these activities. Automated accounting workflows can help reduce errors, accelerate transaction processing, and provide finance professionals with more immediate access to financial insights.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Accounting Administration to Strategic Support</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Signifyd\'s experience demonstrates how replacing a manual accounting workflow with automation can change the way finance teams allocate their time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By reducing lease accounting work by half and improving data accuracy, the company removed some of the operational friction associated with its previous spreadsheet-based process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As e-commerce organizations continue to scale, finance teams will increasingly need systems that can handle routine accounting requirements efficiently while providing timely financial information. Automation can help create that foundation, allowing finance professionals to spend less time maintaining spreadsheets and more time contributing to business growth and strategic initiatives.</p>', 'Accounting Automation for E-Commerce Finance Teams | TGS Tech Info', 'Discover how accounting automation helps e-commerce finance teams reduce manual work, improve accuracy, and gain real-time financial visibility.', 'accounting automation, e-commerce finance, finance automation, ASC 842, lease accounting, accounting software, financial automation, e-commerce growth, finance teams, FinTech, financial operations, accounting technology', '2022-05-16 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-16 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:30:32', '2026-09-24 16:29:55', 1, NULL),
(900, 6, 2, 5, 'Cybercrime: The World’s Third-Largest Economic Force', 'cybercrime-the-worlds-third-largest-economic-force', 'Cybercrime has evolved into a global economic threat, driven by ransomware, AI, data breaches, supply-chain risks, and increasingly organized attackers.', '[\"Cybersecurity\",\"Cybercrime\",\"Ransomware\",\"Data Breaches\",\"AI Security\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Network Security\",\"Cloud Security\",\"Supply Chain Security\",\"Social Engineering\",\"Deepfake Attacks\",\"Phishing\",\"Malware\",\"Cyber Threats\",\"Information Security\"]', 'banner_image-1790260341597-587901915.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercrime: The World’s Third-Largest Economic Force</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Over the past four decades, cybercriminals have evolved from individuals launching simple computer worms into sophisticated, well-funded organizations targeting some of the world\'s most valuable industries. Today, cybercrime represents a major threat to almost every organization connected to the internet and continues to generate enormous economic losses worldwide.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Early Worms to Organized Cybercrime</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The modern era of cyberattacks can be traced back to the 1988 Morris worm. Before the World Wide Web became mainstream, a relatively small program launched from a computer at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) spread rapidly across the emerging internet.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The worm infected an estimated 6,000 of the approximately 60,000 computers connected to the internet at that time. Although calculating its precise financial impact was difficult, estimates ranged from approximately $100,000 to several million dollars.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Since then, cybercrime has become increasingly sophisticated. Attacks have grown larger, more targeted, and more closely connected to geopolitical and economic developments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In 1999, a teenager breached systems associated with the U.S. Department of Defense and NASA, installing backdoor access that enabled the theft of software valued at approximately $1.7 million. More than two decades later, the 2021 Colonial Pipeline ransomware incident demonstrated how a cyberattack could disrupt critical infrastructure. The company temporarily shut down pipeline operations and ultimately paid a ransom of approximately $4.4 million in Bitcoin.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In 2023, attackers exploited a zero-day vulnerability in MOVEit Transfer software, impacting thousands of organizations and exposing data associated with millions of individuals.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Economic Scale of Cybercrime</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The economic impact of cybercrime has reached a scale comparable with the world\'s largest national economies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The United States recorded a GDP of approximately $25.44 trillion at the end of 2022, while China\'s GDP was approximately $17.96 trillion. By comparison, estimates published in 2021 placed the global economic damage associated with cybercrime at around $6 trillion.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This enormous figure illustrates why cybercrime is increasingly viewed not simply as an IT problem but as a major business and economic risk.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some industry forecasts have projected substantial continued growth in cybercrime-related costs. Estimates from cybersecurity outlook research have placed potential annual global cybercrime costs at nearly $24 trillion by 2027, compared with approximately $8.4 trillion in 2022.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The impact is also visible at the national level. Research from Germany\'s Bitkom has estimated that cybercrime-related damages have reached hundreds of billions of euros. Organizations commonly report threats such as phishing, password attacks, malware, ransomware, and SQL injection among their significant security concerns.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Major Data Breaches</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large-scale data breaches demonstrate how extensively cyber incidents can affect organizations and individuals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Among major breaches reported in 2023 were incidents involving organizations such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">DarkBeam - United Kingdom - approximately 3.8 billion records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real Estate Wealth Network - United States - approximately 1.52 billion records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) - India - approximately 815 million records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Kid Security - Kazakhstan - approximately 300 million records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Twitter/X - United States - approximately 220 million records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">TuneFab - Hong Kong - approximately 151 million records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dori Media Group - Israel - approximately 100 TB of data</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tigo - Hong Kong - approximately 100 million records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">SAP SE Bulgaria - Bulgaria - approximately 95.6 million records</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Luxottica Group - Italy - approximately 70 million records</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The scale of these incidents highlights a critical reality: organizations are responsible not only for protecting their own systems but also for understanding how data moves through partners, vendors, cloud platforms, and other third-party environments.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">New Technologies Are Expanding the Threat Landscape</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence and machine learning are becoming increasingly important in cybersecurity. At the same time, these technologies can provide threat actors with new ways to automate and improve malicious activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing adoption of Internet of Things (IoT) devices and Industry 4.0 technologies is creating additional points of exposure. Cloud environments and Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications have also become attractive targets because they frequently contain large volumes of valuable business and customer information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers are increasingly exploring ways to use automation, generative AI, and other emerging technologies to improve phishing campaigns, social engineering, reconnaissance, and other attack activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercrime groups are also becoming more structured. Instead of operating as isolated individuals, many groups work across borders and divide responsibilities among specialized participants.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the World Economic Forum\'s <em>Global Risks Report 2020</em>, organized cybercrime entities were increasingly collaborating, while the report cited estimates that the likelihood of detection and prosecution in the United States could be as low as 0.05%.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ransomware-as-a-Service Lowers the Barrier to Entry</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminal organizations increasingly specialize in particular industries and attack techniques. This specialization allows attackers to develop more targeted campaigns against specific business environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One important development has been the rise of ransomware-as-a-service (RaaS). This model allows individuals with limited technical expertise to gain access to ransomware tools and infrastructure developed by more experienced cybercriminal groups.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The dark web can also provide an environment for anonymous communication, underground marketplaces, credential trading, and coordination between cybercriminals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these developments have created an ecosystem in which sophisticated cyberattack capabilities can be distributed among multiple participants.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Physical and Analog Security Still Matter</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybersecurity is not limited to software, networks, and cloud infrastructure. Physical security remains an important component of an organization\'s overall security strategy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unauthorized access to data centers, offices, server rooms, and other restricted locations can expose sensitive systems and information. Employees or contractors with inappropriate access may also unintentionally or deliberately contribute to social engineering incidents.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations must also consider the secure disposal of confidential documents, storage devices, computers, and other hardware. Physical tampering can introduce malicious components or code into otherwise trusted systems.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Software Supply Chain Is a Critical Security Consideration</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern businesses depend heavily on third-party software providers, cloud platforms, contractors, technology vendors, and service providers. As a result, weaknesses in a supplier\'s security environment can create risks for its customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A strong cybersecurity strategy therefore requires organizations to examine not only their own systems but also the security practices of important third-party partners.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supply-chain attacks can be particularly damaging because compromised software or services may provide attackers with access to multiple organizations at once.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfakes and Social Engineering</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Social engineering remains one of the most effective ways for attackers to obtain credentials, permissions, and sensitive information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The emergence of generative AI and deepfake technologies adds another layer to this challenge. Attackers can potentially use manipulated audio, video, images, and highly personalized messages to make fraudulent communications appear more credible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations therefore need to combine technical security controls with employee awareness and verification procedures.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Stronger Cybercrime Defenses</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The economic scale of cybercrime demonstrates why cybersecurity can no longer be treated solely as an IT department responsibility.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations of every size can become targets, from small businesses to large financial institutions and critical infrastructure providers. Attackers may operate across borders, use specialized teams, exploit third-party systems, and continuously adapt their techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence and machine learning offer significant opportunities for improving security, automation, threat detection, and organizational productivity. However, the same technologies can also be used by threat actors to increase the speed and scale of malicious activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reducing cyber risk requires a broad and continuous approach. Organizations should understand their digital and physical attack surfaces, strengthen identity and access controls, secure third-party relationships, protect sensitive information, train employees, maintain incident-response plans, and use modern cybersecurity technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybersecurity must increasingly become a fundamental business capability alongside infrastructure, cloud services, and other essential operational systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As cybercrime continues to evolve, organizations that understand their attack vectors and prepare for emerging threats will be better positioned to protect their systems, data, customers, employees, and business operations.</p>', 'Cybercrime: The World’s Third-Largest Economic Force', 'Explore the growing economic impact of cybercrime, from ransomware and data breaches to AI-driven attacks, supply-chain risks, and social engineering.', 'cybercrime, cybersecurity, cyber attacks, ransomware, data breaches, AI cybersecurity, cyber threats, network security, cloud security, supply chain attacks, phishing, malware, social engineering, deepfake attacks, information security', '2022-05-16 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-16 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:32:21', '2026-09-24 16:29:54', 1, NULL),
(901, 6, 1, 5, 'Black Friday Shopping Boom Triggers Sharp Rise in Holiday-Themed Cyberattacks', 'black-friday-shopping-boom-triggers-sharp-rise-in-holiday-themed-cyberattacks', 'Black Friday shopping drove a sharp rise in phishing and brand impersonation attacks, putting consumers and retailers at greater cyber risk.', '[\"Black Friday\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"Phishing\",\"Cyberattacks\",\"Holiday Shopping\",\"Online Shopping Security\",\"Brand Impersonation\",\"Cybercrime\",\"Generative AI\",\"AI Cybersecurity\",\"Email Security\",\"Consumer Security\",\"Retail Cybersecurity\",\"Dark Web\",\"Online Fraud\",\"Threat Intelligence\"]', 'banner_image-1790260454880-798097183.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Black Friday Shopping Boom Triggers Sharp Rise in Holiday-Themed Cyberattacks</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The holiday shopping season is creating new opportunities for cybercriminals, with phishing campaigns and brand impersonation attacks increasing sharply as consumers and retailers prepare for Black Friday and Christmas shopping.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to an analysis of customer data released by cybersecurity company Darktrace, holiday-themed phishing activity increased significantly throughout November. Christmas-related phishing attempts rose by <strong>327%</strong> between the first and final weeks of the month, while attacks using Black Friday themes increased by <strong>692%</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The United States experienced an even more significant increase during peak shopping periods. Phishing campaigns impersonating well-known retailers, including Walmart, Target and Best Buy, reportedly increased by more than <strong>2,000%</strong>.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminals Shift Focus Toward Consumers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Darktrace researchers observed that attackers increasingly turned their attention from businesses toward consumers as holiday shopping activity intensified.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Globally, impersonation of major consumer brands increased by <strong>92%</strong> during the analyzed periods, while attacks involving brands primarily associated with workplace environments declined by <strong>9%</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Nathaniel Jones, Darktrace\'s vice president of threat research, explained that the combination of artificial intelligence, automation and cybercrime-as-a-service marketplaces is helping attackers operate at greater speed and scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generative AI has also reduced the technical barriers for creating convincing phishing campaigns. Attackers can use AI-assisted tools to produce realistic messages, websites and other content designed to deceive potential victims.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Jeff Wolverton, CEO of PiviT Strategy, said the growing accessibility of generative AI is making phishing and malware campaigns more accessible to attackers, increasing the risks consumers face while shopping online.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Thread Hijacking Creates Additional Risk</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One increasingly sophisticated technique highlighted by Darktrace researchers is <strong>email thread hijacking</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this type of attack, criminals first gain access to a user\'s email account and monitor existing conversations. They can then insert malicious messages into legitimate email threads, making their communications appear to come from a trusted participant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers may use these compromised conversations to distribute malicious links, request confidential information, obtain credentials or redirect payments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Because the message is embedded within an existing conversation, recipients may be less suspicious than they would be when receiving an unexpected email from an unknown sender.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Makes Fake Online Stores Easier to Create</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybersecurity experts are also observing an increase in fraudulent online stores during the holiday shopping period.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Erich Kron, a security awareness advocate at KnowBe4, noted that improvements in readily available tools and artificial intelligence can make it easier for criminals to create convincing fake shopping websites.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers can reproduce elements of legitimate websites, including product images, logos, descriptions and other branding elements. AI can also help generate product descriptions and fabricated reviews designed to make fraudulent stores appear authentic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Criminals can additionally register domains that resemble legitimate brands or appear to be associated with trusted retailers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although fraudulent websites may be removed relatively quickly, the low cost and speed of creating replacement sites allows attackers to continue their campaigns.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Holiday Travel and Delivery Scams</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The holiday season provides criminals with numerous themes that can be used to create believable phishing messages.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mika Aalto, co-founder and CEO of Hoxhunt, pointed to increased travel, gift purchases and other seasonal activities as factors that give attackers more opportunities to manipulate consumers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Package delivery scams are a common example. A victim may receive a message claiming that a shipment has been delayed or requires additional information. Links in these messages can direct users to fake websites designed to steal login credentials or payment information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Travel-related scams can use similar tactics. A fraudulent notification may claim that a flight has been canceled or that an itinerary requires immediate confirmation. The urgency of such messages can encourage recipients to click without carefully checking the source.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile Shopping Increases the Challenge</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Threat actors also use seasonal promotions, discounts and coupons to target consumers on mobile devices.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Selena Larson, a senior threat researcher at Proofpoint, said attackers frequently use shopping deals and other timely offers to encourage users to interact with malicious content. End-of-year themes, including bonuses and salary increases, can also be used as lures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile shoppers face additional challenges because smaller screens can make it harder to identify suspicious URLs and verify the authenticity of a website.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Krishna Vishnubhotla, vice president of product strategy at Zimperium, advised consumers to verify that they are visiting an official website before entering payment or personal information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers may also use multiple redirects to move victims between websites and make it more difficult for them to determine where they have ultimately landed.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminals Also Promote Seasonal \"Discounts\"</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Holiday campaigns are not limited to attacks against consumers. Cybercriminal marketplaces can also take advantage of the seasonal shopping mindset.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Stephen Kowski, field CTO at SlashNext, said the increase in retail-brand impersonation demonstrates how threat actors are adapting their campaigns around consumer shopping habits.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern phishing campaigns can extend beyond corporate email systems and target personal email accounts, social media platforms and other communication channels that employees use while shopping online.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations therefore face the challenge of protecting users across multiple digital environments rather than relying exclusively on traditional corporate email security.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Darktrace\'s Jones also noted that cybercriminal marketplaces can use the holiday period to promote their own offerings. Compromised usernames, passwords and other stolen information may be advertised on dark-web marketplaces using bulk or seasonal pricing.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brands Continue Fighting Impersonation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Companies are taking several measures to reduce the impact of brand impersonation campaigns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These efforts include verifying official social media accounts, requesting the removal of fraudulent applications from app stores, and submitting takedown requests for websites and domains that imitate legitimate brands.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, brand impersonation remains difficult to eliminate because attackers can quickly create new domains and fraudulent websites after previous ones are removed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security experts recommend that companies actively communicate with customers when scams involving their brands are discovered. Public warnings can help consumers recognize fraudulent websites, messages and promotional offers.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Consumers Can Stay Safer During Holiday Shopping</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumers can reduce their exposure to holiday-themed cyberattacks by taking several precautions:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Visit retailers by manually entering their official website address rather than clicking unexpected promotional links.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Check the domain carefully before entering login, payment or personal information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Be cautious of unusually large discounts or offers that create a sense of urgency.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Avoid entering sensitive information into websites reached through unsolicited messages.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Verify delivery, travel and payment notifications directly through the relevant company\'s official application or website.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use multi-factor authentication on important accounts.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Avoid reusing passwords across shopping, email and financial accounts.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Treat unexpected messages requesting payment changes, credentials or sensitive information with caution.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Keep operating systems, browsers and security software updated.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Report suspicious websites, emails and social media accounts to the relevant platform or company.</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Growing Holiday Cybersecurity Challenge</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The rise in holiday-themed phishing and brand impersonation demonstrates how cybercriminals adapt their tactics to major consumer events.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Black Friday, Christmas shopping, package deliveries, travel and seasonal promotions all provide attackers with familiar themes that can make fraudulent messages appear more believable.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As artificial intelligence and automation make it easier to produce convincing digital content, consumers and organizations need to verify online communications carefully before clicking links, sharing information or completing transactions.</p>', 'Black Friday Cyberattacks Surge as Holiday Shopping Peaks', 'Black Friday shopping drives a surge in phishing, fake stores and brand impersonation attacks targeting consumers and retailers.', 'Black Friday cyberattacks, holiday phishing, cybersecurity, phishing attacks, brand impersonation, online shopping security, cybercrime, fake shopping websites, retail cybersecurity, generative AI', '2022-05-16 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-16 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:34:15', '2026-09-24 16:29:53', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(902, 6, 1, 5, 'How Deepfake Attacks Are Challenging Mobile Commerce Security and Biometric Authentication', 'how-deepfake-attacks-are-challenging-mobile-commerce-security-and-biometric-authentication', 'Deepfake attacks are challenging mobile commerce security by bypassing biometric authentication and increasing risks of account takeover and payment fraud.', '[\"Deepfake Fraud\",\"Mobile Security\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"Mobile Commerce\",\"Biometric Authentication\",\"AI Fraud\",\"Face ID\",\"Voice Authentication\",\"Account Takeover\",\"Payment Fraud\",\"Identity Theft\",\"AI Security\",\"Fraud Prevention\",\"Liveness Detection\",\"Anti-Spoofing\"]', 'banner_image-1790260571551-302452188.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Deepfake Attacks Are Challenging Mobile Commerce Security and Biometric Authentication</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology is becoming a growing security concern for mobile commerce as cybercriminals use artificial intelligence to create convincing fake faces, voices, videos, and identities. These techniques can undermine biometric authentication, facilitate account takeovers, and expose consumers and businesses to payment fraud.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile shopping has become a major part of digital commerce. With 187.5 million people in the United States reportedly shopping through mobile devices last year, mobile transactions now represent roughly half of e-commerce sales. The rapid growth of mobile purchasing has also created a larger target for cybercriminals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric authentication has traditionally been viewed as one of the most convenient ways to protect mobile applications. Technologies such as Apple\'s Face ID, Android\'s face-recognition systems, and voice authentication allow consumers to access banking, retail, payment, and other services without repeatedly entering passwords.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, advances in generative AI are changing the threat landscape.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminals can now create increasingly realistic synthetic images, videos, and voices that are designed to imitate legitimate users. These capabilities are creating new challenges for organizations that rely heavily on biometric verification as a security layer.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric Authentication Alone May Not Be Enough</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric authentication can provide a convenient layer of protection, but it should not necessarily be treated as an isolated security solution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI-generated fraud becomes more sophisticated, attackers can combine stolen personal information with synthetic media to impersonate legitimate users. Facial recognition, voice authentication, and identity-verification systems can become targets for these attacks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Appdome, a mobile security company focused on protecting applications from evolving threats, attackers are increasingly experimenting with deepfake-based biometric bypass techniques. Such attacks can be used to take over accounts, access sensitive information, and initiate fraudulent transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The financial consequences can be significant. Appdome has reported incidents involving compromised banking applications where organizations experienced financial losses reaching $10,000 per hour.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, financial institutions, payment providers, and mobile application developers, the threat extends beyond direct financial losses. Successful fraud can also result in customer complaints, regulatory concerns, operational costs, and reduced confidence in digital services.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brian Reed, SVP at Appdome, has argued that organizations need to move away from fragmented security strategies that rely on multiple disconnected fraud and security products.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, he advocates an AI-native, platform-based approach that can continuously identify and respond to threats within mobile applications.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Mobile Commerce Is Becoming a Target</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile commerce relies heavily on convenience. Consumers expect to open an application, verify their identity quickly, and complete a transaction with minimal friction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric authentication supports this experience by replacing traditional passwords and other manual verification methods.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That convenience also makes biometric systems attractive targets for attackers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminals can use deepfake technology in several ways. Synthetic facial images or videos may be used in attempts to circumvent facial-recognition systems. Stolen photographs can potentially be transformed into synthetic identities for fraudulent identity-verification processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Voice cloning creates another potential attack path. An attacker can generate an artificial voice that resembles a legitimate customer and attempt to use it during phone-based authentication or other voice-verification processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake techniques can also be combined with stolen credentials, personal information, and social-engineering campaigns. Rather than relying on one attack method, criminals can combine multiple techniques to create more convincing impersonation attempts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a difficult challenge for mobile businesses because traditional authentication controls may not be designed to identify every form of synthetic media.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfakes Are Changing the Identity-Fraud Landscape</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the biggest challenges is the speed at which AI-generated content is evolving.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Earlier forms of manipulated media were often easier to identify because of obvious visual or audio inconsistencies. Modern generative AI systems can create much more convincing representations, reducing some of the visual and auditory clues that previously helped identify manipulated content.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This has important implications for identity verification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A fraudulent user may attempt to present synthetic facial imagery to a verification system, while another attacker may use cloned audio during a voice-based authentication process. In other cases, attackers can attempt to interfere with how a mobile application receives or processes biometric information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Appdome, attack techniques can include virtual-camera manipulation, where pre-recorded or generated video is presented to a facial-recognition system, as well as image-buffer manipulation designed to interfere with biometric processing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These techniques demonstrate why authentication cannot be considered independently from the broader mobile application security environment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Liveness Detection and Anti-Spoofing Become More Important</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional facial recognition generally attempts to determine whether a person matches an enrolled biometric profile. Deepfake attacks introduce another question: Is the biometric input coming from a genuine person in a genuine environment?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is where liveness detection and anti-spoofing technologies become increasingly important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Liveness detection attempts to distinguish a real person from an artificial representation. Depending on the implementation, systems may analyze movement, facial behavior, environmental signals, or other characteristics to determine whether the biometric input represents a live user.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Anti-spoofing controls can add another layer by identifying suspicious attempts to manipulate biometric systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For mobile commerce companies, these technologies can complement biometric authentication rather than replacing it entirely.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-Native Mobile Defense</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional cybersecurity models often depend on multiple individual security products. One system may handle fraud detection, another may monitor application integrity, and another may protect authentication.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge is ensuring that these controls work together quickly enough to respond to an attack.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An AI-native mobile security model attempts to bring threat detection and response closer to the application itself.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Appdome, autonomous mobile defense can continuously monitor application environments, identify suspicious activity, and respond to emerging attack patterns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is designed to provide protection during the application\'s lifecycle rather than relying exclusively on security controls outside the application.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For mobile commerce providers, the objective is to identify suspicious behavior before it can become an account takeover, unauthorized transaction, or larger security incident.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Existing Compliance Frameworks Face New Challenges</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The rapid development of generative AI is also creating challenges for existing security and compliance frameworks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations already operate under requirements involving customer identity verification, payment security, privacy, and fraud prevention. However, many established security practices were created before highly sophisticated generative AI and deepfake attacks became widely accessible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Know Your Customer (KYC) procedures and payment-security standards such as PCI DSS remain important components of financial and digital security. However, organizations may need additional technical controls to address attack methods involving synthetic identities, manipulated biometrics, and AI-generated content.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The emergence of deepfake fraud therefore creates a need for businesses to continuously review whether their existing authentication and fraud-prevention processes address current attack techniques.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Appdome Expands Its Deepfake Protection Capabilities</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Appdome announced an expansion of its Account Takeover Protection capabilities with new dynamic defense plugins designed to detect deepfake attacks across Android and iOS applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company said the additions are designed to help protect Apple Face ID, Google facial-recognition technology, and third-party facial and voice-recognition services from AI-generated manipulation and other deepfake techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Eric Newcomer, principal analyst and CTO at Intellyx, has highlighted the continuing challenge faced by organizations that depend on biometric authentication.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As attackers discover new methods for circumventing biometric controls, mobile application providers need mechanisms capable of identifying and stopping suspicious activity within the application environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Appdome\'s approach focuses on providing mobile applications with detection and control mechanisms that can respond to these attacks without relying exclusively on external security systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tom Tovar, CEO of Appdome, has also emphasized the difficulty developers and biometric technology providers face in detecting AI-generated deepfakes and techniques designed to bypass facial-recognition systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The broader security challenge remains significant: organizations cannot prevent people from creating deepfake content, but they can work to prevent manipulated biometric data from being successfully used against their applications.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Mobile Commerce Businesses Can Respond</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile commerce providers can take several steps to strengthen defenses against deepfake-enabled fraud.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Add AI-Based Deepfake Detection</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can incorporate AI-powered detection capabilities into mobile applications to identify suspicious synthetic media and biometric manipulation attempts.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Strengthen Real-Time Biometric Verification</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric authentication can be supported by real-time verification and liveness checks that make it more difficult for attackers to use pre-recorded or artificially generated content.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Monitor Emerging Attack Techniques</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology continues to evolve. Security teams should continuously monitor new attack methods and update detection mechanisms as new techniques emerge.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Use Multiple Security Signals</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should avoid relying on a single authentication signal. Device integrity, behavioral signals, identity information, transaction patterns, biometric verification, and application-security controls can be combined to create a broader security picture.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. Protect the Application Environment</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security controls implemented directly within mobile applications can help detect suspicious activity closer to the point where an attack occurs.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">6. Continuously Test Fraud Defenses</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should regularly test authentication and fraud-prevention systems against emerging attack techniques to identify weaknesses before criminals exploit them.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Future of Mobile Commerce Security</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology is creating a new challenge for mobile commerce because it targets one of the industry\'s most important priorities: convenient and trusted identity verification.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumers increasingly expect mobile banking, shopping, and payment applications to provide fast authentication without creating unnecessary friction. Businesses, meanwhile, need to maintain that convenience while preventing unauthorized access and fraudulent transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing availability of AI-generated faces, voices, and videos means that biometric authentication should increasingly be considered part of a broader security strategy rather than a standalone defense.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile businesses can strengthen their security posture by combining biometric verification with liveness detection, anti-spoofing technology, application protection, behavioral analysis, continuous threat monitoring, and AI-driven fraud detection.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As deepfake capabilities continue to evolve, the organizations that continuously adapt their mobile security controls will be better positioned to protect customer identities, payment information, applications, and digital transactions from increasingly sophisticated forms of AI-enabled fraud.</p>', 'Deepfake Attacks Threaten Mobile Commerce Security', 'Learn how deepfake fraud threatens mobile commerce, biometric authentication, account security, payment transactions, and consumer trust.', 'deepfake attacks, deepfake fraud, mobile commerce security, biometric authentication, AI fraud, mobile security, Face ID security, voice authentication, account takeover, payment fraud, identity theft, deepfake detection, liveness detection, anti-spoofing, cybersecurity', '2022-05-17 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-17 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 9, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:36:11', '2026-09-24 16:29:52', 1, NULL),
(903, 6, 1, 5, 'AI-Powered Fraud Is Hiding Inside Legitimate E-Commerce Transactions', 'ai-powered-fraud-is-hiding-inside-legitimate-e-commerce-transactions', 'AI-powered fraud is making legitimate e-commerce transactions harder to distinguish from attacks, driving a shift toward identity-based fraud detection.', '[\"AI Fraud\",\"E-Commerce Fraud\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"Fraud Prevention\",\"Account Takeover\",\"Identity Fraud\",\"Online Fraud\",\"Synthetic Identity\",\"AI Security\",\"Social Engineering\",\"Phishing\",\"Fraud Detection\",\"Digital Identity\",\"Payment Fraud\",\"E-Commerce Security\"]', 'banner_image-1790260777969-394648662.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">E-commerce fraud is entering a more difficult phase for merchants and consumers alike. Instead of relying on obviously suspicious transactions, attackers are increasingly using legitimate accounts, stolen credentials, synthetic identities, and familiar purchasing patterns to hide malicious activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This shift means a fraudulent transaction may look completely normal on the surface. The account may be valid, the payment information may appear legitimate, and the customer\'s behavior may resemble an ordinary purchase.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Dany Naigeboren, senior director of risk at fraud prevention company Forter, fraud is increasingly moving away from the traditional point-of-sale attack and toward the identities and accounts behind digital transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The result is a new challenge for online businesses: fraud is not necessarily about breaking into a system. In many cases, attackers are learning how to operate within systems that already trust the user.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Is Lowering the Barrier for Online Fraud</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is making sophisticated fraud techniques more accessible to a wider range of attackers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Naigeboren said that individuals with relatively limited technical experience can now learn how to conduct online fraud in a short period of time. Techniques that previously required specialized knowledge and significant resources can increasingly be supported by readily available AI tools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is particularly concerning for e-commerce businesses because fraudulent activity does not always produce obvious technical warning signs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Account takeovers, for example, allow criminals to use genuine customer accounts after obtaining legitimate credentials. From the merchant\'s perspective, the resulting purchase can resemble normal customer behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can also help attackers create more convincing phishing messages, impersonate legitimate users, automate repetitive activity, and conduct social engineering at greater scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As these capabilities become easier to access, the distinction between sophisticated fraud operations and smaller individual attacks is becoming less clear.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fraud Is Moving Beyond the Checkout</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional fraud prevention often focuses heavily on the transaction itself. However, many modern attacks begin before a customer reaches checkout.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers can target login credentials, customer accounts, promotions, loyalty programs, returns, and other stages of the customer journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Account takeover is one example. A criminal who obtains access to a legitimate account may be able to make purchases or exploit loyalty benefits without creating a new fraudulent identity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Promotional abuse and policy exploitation can create another challenge. A customer may appear legitimate while deliberately manipulating discounts, refunds, chargebacks, or other merchant policies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This makes intent increasingly important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A transaction that looks normal when examined independently may become suspicious when viewed alongside a customer\'s previous logins, devices, sessions, purchases, returns, and other interactions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumers Are Becoming a Bigger Target</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Naigeboren has also pointed to increasing activity involving account takeovers, phishing, social engineering, and impersonation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than directly attacking a retailer\'s infrastructure, criminals can sometimes achieve their objective by compromising customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an attacker could obtain a customer\'s credentials through phishing and then use the existing account to make an apparently routine purchase. Because the account itself is legitimate, conventional transaction-level controls may have difficulty identifying the activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted phishing can make this problem more difficult by helping attackers produce convincing communications at scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automated tools can also increase the volume of attacks without requiring the same level of manual effort that earlier fraud campaigns demanded.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, this creates a broader security challenge: protecting the transaction also requires understanding the identity and behavior behind the transaction.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Using AI to Fight AI-Enabled Fraud</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fraud prevention providers are increasingly turning to artificial intelligence and broader behavioral analysis to respond to these evolving threats.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Forter\'s approach focuses on identity and the behavior associated with an individual interaction. The company\'s platform is designed to analyze signals across the customer journey and make real-time decisions about whether activity appears legitimate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its network includes transaction data from more than $1 trillion in transactions, which the company uses to support fraud detection and risk assessment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not simply to determine whether a single purchase looks suspicious. Instead, identity-related signals and historical behavior can provide additional context for evaluating activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach can also support hybrid commerce models, including buy-online, pick-up-in-store transactions, where digital identity signals can become relevant to physical interactions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Identity-Level Detection Matters</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the biggest challenges in modern fraud prevention is that individual transactions rarely tell the entire story.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A single purchase might look perfectly normal. However, a broader view could reveal unusual activity involving the same account, device, session, login history, promotional activity, or return behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Forter argues that modern fraud prevention therefore needs to connect signals across multiple customer touchpoints.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These touchpoints can include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Account logins</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Checkout activity</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment transactions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Promotions and loyalty programs</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Returns and refunds</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Chargebacks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Devices and sessions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Account creation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Online-to-offline interactions</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connecting these signals can provide merchants with a more comprehensive view of customer activity than evaluating each event independently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This identity-centric approach is particularly relevant as attackers increasingly attempt to blend malicious activity into normal customer workflows.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Friendly Fraud Adds Another Layer of Complexity</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every form of e-commerce abuse involves an obviously malicious attacker.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Retailers also face so-called friendly fraud, in which legitimate customers dispute transactions, misuse promotional programs, exploit return policies, or otherwise manipulate merchant processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These cases can be difficult to distinguish from genuine customer problems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a customer may claim not to recognize a transaction, request a refund, or dispute a charge. Determining whether the claim is legitimate or part of an abuse pattern requires more context than the transaction alone may provide.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Post-transaction analysis of returns and chargebacks can therefore be an important part of fraud prevention.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Next Challenge: AI Agents</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The evolution of AI introduces another question for e-commerce platforms: who or what is actually performing a transaction?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI agents become capable of browsing websites, selecting products, adding items to carts, and potentially completing purchases, businesses may encounter interactions that look similar to conventional human activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a new identity problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fraud prevention systems may need to determine whether activity originates from a human customer, an authorized automated agent, or an unauthorized system controlled by an attacker.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ability to connect an agent\'s actions with a verifiable underlying human identity could become increasingly important as autonomous digital interactions become more common.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fraud Prevention Will Require Continuous Adaptation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The changing nature of e-commerce fraud highlights the limitations of relying exclusively on static rules.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers continuously adapt their methods, while AI can accelerate the development and scaling of new techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Naigeboren argues that there is no single solution capable of addressing every form of fraud. Instead, businesses need broader visibility across identities, interactions, and the customer journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-driven and continuously learning systems can help organizations identify patterns that may not be apparent when individual events are examined in isolation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For e-commerce businesses, the challenge is therefore moving from simply asking whether a transaction is suspicious to understanding <strong>who is behind the interaction, how that identity behaves across the customer journey, and whether the activity is consistent with legitimate intent.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI becomes more deeply integrated into both fraud attacks and fraud prevention, identity-based analysis is likely to remain an important part of protecting digital commerce.</p>', 'AI-Powered Fraud Hides Inside Legitimate E-Commerce Transactions', 'AI is making e-commerce fraud harder to detect as attackers exploit legitimate accounts, identities and behavior across the customer journey.', 'AI fraud, e-commerce fraud, AI-powered fraud, fraud prevention, account takeover, identity fraud, synthetic identity fraud, cybersecurity, online fraud, fraud detection, AI security, e-commerce security, phishing, social engineering, payment fraud, digital identity', '2022-05-17 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-17 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:39:38', '2026-09-24 16:29:50', 1, NULL),
(904, 6, 1, 5, 'Unprotected Machine Identities Emerge as a Growing Enterprise Cybersecurity Risk', 'unprotected-machine-identities-emerge-as-a-growing-enterprise-cybersecurity-risk', 'Organizations are struggling to secure machine identities as identity-related breaches rise and gaps in privileged access protection remain.', '[\"Cybersecurity\",\"Identity Security\",\"Machine Identity\",\"Privileged Access Management\",\"PAM\",\"Enterprise Security\",\"Cloud Security\",\"Zero Trust\",\"Access Management\",\"MFA\",\"Service Accounts\",\"Application Security\",\"Privileged Accounts\",\"IT Security\",\"Cyber Risk\"]', 'banner_image-1790261027263-108933526.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations are facing a growing cybersecurity challenge as machine identities and privileged accounts become increasingly difficult to secure. A recent report from privileged access management (PAM) provider Delinea highlights significant gaps in how enterprises protect both human and non-human identities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research, based on responses from 2,100 security decision-makers worldwide, found that <strong>84% of organizations experienced an identity-related security breach during the previous 18 months</strong>. The findings point to a widening gap between the importance organizations place on identity security and the resources and controls actually deployed to protect it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As enterprises expand their use of cloud platforms, applications, automated services, and interconnected systems, the number of digital identities continues to grow. This creates additional opportunities for cybercriminals to compromise privileged accounts and move through corporate environments.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identity Security Remains a Major Priority</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research shows that identity security has become an important focus for security teams. However, organizations continue to face challenges when attempting to turn that priority into effective security programs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the research:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">60% of IT security decision-makers said various concerns prevent them from fully executing their security strategies.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">63% believe identity security is not sufficiently understood by executive leadership.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">75% believe their organizations could fall short of protecting privileged identities because they do not receive adequate support.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, organizations recognize the business importance of identity security. Around <strong>90% of respondents</strong> said their organizations understand its importance to achieving business objectives, while <strong>87%</strong> identified identity security as one of their most important security priorities for the coming year.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge is turning that recognition into sustained investment, appropriate controls, and executive support.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Executive Support and Security Budgets Remain Challenges</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security professionals continue to face difficulties obtaining the budget and executive alignment required to strengthen identity protections.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Delinea\'s research indicates that <strong>63% of respondents said their boards do not fully understand identity security and its role in business operations</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Joseph Carson, chief security scientist and advisory CISO at Delinea, said that although business leaders recognize the importance of identity security, security teams may not receive the financial and organizational support necessary to deploy critical security controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Without adequate protection, privileged identities can become attractive targets for attackers seeking access to sensitive systems and information.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privileged Access Policies Are Still Incomplete</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations also have gaps in their privileged access management policies and processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research found that only <strong>44% of surveyed organizations have implemented ongoing security policies and processes for privileged access management</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These controls can include password rotation and approval processes, time- or context-based access restrictions, and monitoring of privileged-user behavior through recording and auditing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another concern is the continued use of privileged accounts without multifactor authentication. More than half, or <strong>52% of respondents</strong>, said privileged users can access sensitive systems and data without MFA requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These gaps can increase the potential impact of a compromised privileged account.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Machine Identities Create Another Security Challenge</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Privileged identities are not limited to employees and administrators. Modern enterprise environments also depend on numerous <strong>non-human identities</strong>, including service accounts, application accounts, software processes, code, and other machine identities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These identities can automatically connect to systems, exchange information, and access sensitive resources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the research found that only <strong>44% of organizations manage and secure their machine identities</strong>. This leaves a significant portion of machine and application identities potentially exposed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing number of non-human identities makes this issue particularly important. Organizations may have thousands of automated identities operating across cloud platforms, applications, databases, APIs, and internal infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers can potentially exploit these identities to move through an environment while attempting to avoid detection.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Machine Identity Protection Matters</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Machine identities can have extensive access privileges because they are often required to perform automated business processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A compromised service account or application identity could potentially provide access to systems and information beyond the initial point of compromise. If appropriate controls are not in place, attackers may use these identities for lateral movement or to reach higher-value systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security teams therefore need to consider machine identities as part of their overall privileged access strategy rather than focusing exclusively on employee accounts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This includes identifying where privileged machine identities exist, determining what resources they can access, monitoring their activity, and applying appropriate authentication and access controls.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Security Gap Continues to Expand</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The findings highlight a broader challenge for enterprise security teams: organizations may understand the importance of protecting privileged identities while still leaving significant gaps in implementation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An attacker does not necessarily need to compromise multiple accounts. A single privileged identity can potentially provide a pathway to sensitive systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that secure human administrator accounts but overlook application, service, and machine identities may therefore leave another avenue available to attackers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The increasing adoption of cloud services and distributed applications makes this challenge more complex. Identities can exist across multiple environments, making centralized visibility and consistent security controls more difficult.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cloud Environments Add Complexity</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cloud adoption has significantly expanded the number and variety of identities organizations need to manage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Delinea\'s findings, enterprises continue to face difficulties protecting identities across increasingly distributed environments. Traditional security technologies may not provide sufficient visibility into every privileged identity operating across cloud and hybrid infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simple password-management approaches may also be insufficient when organizations need to understand the full lifecycle and behavior of privileged identities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A stronger approach requires organizations to identify the different types of privileged identities within their environments and apply security controls appropriate to each identity type.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a More Complete Identity Security Strategy</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research suggests that protecting privileged identities requires more than securing traditional administrator accounts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can strengthen their approach by focusing on several areas:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Discovering human and machine privileged identities across their environments.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Applying MFA to privileged access wherever appropriate.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establishing password rotation and approval processes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitoring and auditing privileged activity.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Limiting access based on time, context, and business requirements.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reviewing service and application accounts regularly.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving communication between security teams and executive leadership.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Allocating appropriate resources to identity security initiatives.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As enterprises continue adopting cloud applications, automation, APIs, and machine-to-machine communication, machine identities are likely to remain an important component of enterprise cybersecurity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Delinea research demonstrates that identity security is widely recognized as a priority, but significant gaps remain between that recognition and the controls organizations have implemented. Addressing those gaps requires visibility across both human and non-human identities, combined with appropriate privileged access controls and sustained organizational support.</p>', 'Unprotected Machine Identities: A Growing Cybersecurity Risk', 'Machine identities are becoming a major enterprise security risk as organizations struggle with privileged access, identity breaches, and cloud security gaps.', 'machine identities, machine identity security, identity security, privileged access management, PAM, cybersecurity, enterprise cybersecurity, privileged identities, cloud security, service accounts, application identities, MFA, access management, cyber risk', '2022-05-18 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-18 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 6, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:43:47', '2026-09-24 16:29:45', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(905, 6, 2, 3, 'How AI Is Transforming Business Data Into Reliable, Actionable Strategies', 'how-ai-is-transforming-business-data-into-reliable-actionable-strategies', 'AI is reshaping how businesses transform structured and unstructured data into reliable insights, faster decisions, automation, and growth.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Generative AI\",\"Business Strategy\",\"AI Analytics\",\"Large Language Models\",\"LLM\",\"Data Transformation\",\"Business Intelligence\",\"AI Automation\",\"Predictive Analytics\",\"Enterprise AI\",\"Cloud Computing\",\"Data-Driven Decision Making\"]', 'banner_image-1790261203624-251651947.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How AI Is Transforming Business Data Into Reliable, Actionable Strategies</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is often described as a relatively new technology in the business world. However, AI itself has existed for decades. What has changed significantly is the scale at which organizations can now use AI to process information, understand complex data, automate workflows, and turn raw information into actionable business strategies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Daniel Ziv, VP of Experience Management and Analytics, GTM Strategy at workforce management company Verint, artificial intelligence should not be viewed as a single technology. Instead, it represents a collection of capabilities designed to perform different tasks depending on the business problem being addressed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the most important developments within AI is the evolution of large language models (LLMs). Although LLM research has been progressing for many years, recent improvements in natural language understanding and generation have made these technologies much more accessible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The public availability of generative AI tools has also helped organizations and individuals recognize how quickly AI can interpret language, generate content, analyze information, and support business processes.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI\'s Growing Role in Business</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The combination of artificial intelligence and cloud computing is creating new opportunities for organizations to automate processes and analyze information at a much greater scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Historically, companies deploying AI systems often had to purchase hardware, install software, configure infrastructure, and train employees before seeing meaningful results. These projects could require months or even years of preparation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cloud computing has changed that equation by providing access to substantial computing resources without requiring organizations to build all the infrastructure themselves. Businesses can now process significantly larger datasets and deploy AI capabilities more quickly and cost-effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generative AI is also becoming increasingly capable of understanding natural language. This is particularly important because businesses generate enormous amounts of information in different formats.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Structured data, such as sales figures, transaction records, customer IDs, and financial information, has traditionally been easier for computers to process. Organizations have used structured data for analytics and predictive modeling for many years.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unstructured and semi-structured information presents a more complicated challenge. Customer emails, documents, conversations, audio recordings, videos, social media interactions, and other forms of content contain valuable information but are not always organized in a format that traditional systems can easily analyze.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advances in generative AI are making it easier to extract meaning from these sources and incorporate them into broader business analysis.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning General AI Into Business-Specific Intelligence</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the biggest challenges for organizations is ensuring that AI-generated insights are relevant to their specific business environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">General-purpose LLMs are trained using enormous amounts of information, but that does not automatically mean they understand a particular company\'s customers, processes, products, or behavioral patterns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This distinction is critical.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An AI model may understand language and provide a reasonable response, but it may not have enough business-specific context to make a highly accurate recommendation for a particular organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is where proprietary and behavioral data become important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Companies can combine the language capabilities of generative AI with their own customer interaction data, operational information, historical records, and business rules. This allows AI systems to move beyond general knowledge and provide insights that are more closely aligned with specific business objectives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ongoing development of enterprise AI is therefore not simply about creating increasingly powerful models. It is also about providing those models with the right information, context, and safeguards.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that successfully combine AI capabilities with high-quality business data can potentially automate analysis and identify patterns that would otherwise require substantial manual effort.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Path From Data to Predictive Insights</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data transformation is another important part of this process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">SoundCommerce, for example, uses a different approach to data management by providing a no-code environment designed to make data transformation more accessible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before data can support business decisions, it must go through several stages.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">First, information needs to be extracted from different source systems. This may include customer relationship management platforms, e-commerce systems, point-of-sale systems, marketplaces, mobile applications, and other business platforms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The data then needs to be validated to ensure that it meets appropriate quality standards.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">After validation, organizations must determine what the information actually means and how it should be used to solve a particular business problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This stage is particularly important because poor-quality or incorrectly interpreted data can ultimately lead to poor business decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data from multiple sources may also use different formats and structures. For example, a retail organization could receive order information from a physical store, an e-commerce website, a marketplace such as Amazon, and its own mobile application.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although each source may represent an order differently, businesses often need to bring all of those records into a common structure so they can be analyzed together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can assist with this mapping and transformation process by helping organizations identify relationships between different data fields and formats.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Natural Language Is Becoming a Data Management Tool</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One notable development is the ability to describe data requirements using natural language rather than traditional programming instructions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of requiring every business user to understand complex software engineering concepts, AI-assisted data tools can allow users to explain what they want the system to accomplish.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a business user could describe which customer information should be combined, how different fields should be interpreted, and what type of output is required.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The AI system can then help translate those instructions into the technical transformations required to prepare the data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This does not eliminate the need for technical expertise. Rather, it changes how people interact with technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ability to communicate requirements clearly becomes increasingly important because the quality of an AI-generated transformation depends heavily on how accurately the user describes the desired outcome.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this environment, business professionals may not necessarily need to become software engineers, but they do need to become effective at communicating requirements to AI systems.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Enterprise AI Is Becoming More Specialized</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generative AI capabilities are being developed across the technology industry by companies such as Microsoft, Google, Amazon Web Services, and Snowflake.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These organizations are developing AI services, models, data platforms, and tools that businesses can incorporate into their own technology environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, companies are developing proprietary AI applications for specialized business problems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One example is customer lifetime value forecasting. AI can analyze historical purchasing behavior and other customer information to estimate potential future value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Specialized applications like this demonstrate why enterprise AI is increasingly moving toward domain-specific use cases rather than relying exclusively on general-purpose AI models.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The combination of foundation models, proprietary business data, and specialized algorithms can allow organizations to build systems designed around specific operational and commercial objectives.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Does Not Replace the Need for Clear Business Questions</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite rapid technological progress, AI does not eliminate the importance of defining the right business problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The value of an AI implementation depends significantly on how clearly an organization can identify the use case it wants to address.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For smaller or less technically mature businesses, narrowly defined applications may be easier to implement and evaluate than large-scale AI transformation projects.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A company might begin with a specific problem such as improving customer segmentation, predicting demand, identifying purchasing patterns, automating data classification, or analyzing customer interactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once the organization understands the value generated by the initial application, it can potentially expand AI into additional business processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach also makes it easier to measure whether the technology is delivering meaningful results.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Business Questions to Automated Answers</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many of the questions businesses are asking AI to answer are not actually new.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Companies have been trying for decades to understand customer behavior, determine where to invest advertising budgets, forecast demand, improve customer relationships, and identify opportunities for growth.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What has changed is the ability to automate parts of this analytical process at a much larger scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can process enormous volumes of information, identify patterns, summarize complex datasets, generate recommendations, and support decision-making in significantly less time than many traditional manual approaches.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, reliable AI-driven strategies depend on more than the AI model itself. Organizations also need high-quality data, clearly defined objectives, appropriate validation processes, business-specific context, and effective human oversight.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI continues to evolve, the most important transformation may not be the technology\'s ability to answer entirely new questions. Instead, it may be the ability to answer familiar business questions faster, at greater scale, and with increasingly sophisticated analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organizations, the next stage of AI adoption will therefore involve connecting advanced models with trusted business data and turning those capabilities into practical, measurable outcomes.</p>', 'AI for Business: Turning Data Into Actionable Strategies', 'Discover how AI, generative AI, and data transformation help businesses turn structured and unstructured data into actionable insights and decisions.', 'AI for business, artificial intelligence, generative AI, business strategy, actionable insights, AI analytics, data transformation, enterprise AI, LLM, predictive analytics, AI automation, business intelligence, cloud AI, data-driven decision making', '2022-05-19 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-19 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 8, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:46:43', '2026-09-24 16:29:44', 1, NULL),
(906, 6, 2, 1, 'How MACH Architecture Is Reshaping Modern Technology Stacks', 'how-mach-architecture-is-reshaping-modern-technology-stacks', 'MACH architecture helps businesses build flexible, scalable tech stacks that support faster innovation, seamless integration, and digital growth.', '[\"MACH Architecture\",\"MACH\",\"Composable Architecture\",\"Microservices\",\"API-First\",\"Cloud-Native SaaS\",\"Headless Architecture\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Technology Strategy\",\"E-Commerce Technology\",\"Composable Commerce\",\"Enterprise Technology\",\"Digital Experience\",\"Cloud Computing\",\"Modern Tech Stack\"]', 'banner_image-1790261350022-792075910.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>How MACH Architecture Is Reshaping Modern Technology Stacks</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern businesses are under constant pressure to improve digital experiences, respond quickly to changing customer expectations, and adopt new technologies without repeatedly rebuilding their entire technology infrastructure. For many organizations, traditional monolithic platforms can make that difficult.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is where <strong>MACH architecture</strong> has gained attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH stands for <strong>Microservices, API-first, Cloud-native SaaS, and Headless architecture</strong>. Together, these principles provide a framework for creating technology ecosystems that are modular, scalable, interoperable, and easier to evolve.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than depending on one large platform to handle every business requirement, MACH allows organizations to assemble their technology stack from independent components. Each component can be updated, replaced, or scaled without requiring a complete re-platforming initiative.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding the MACH Approach</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH is built around four fundamental principles:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Microservices: Applications are divided into smaller, independent services that can be developed and deployed separately.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">API-first: APIs provide a consistent way for different applications and services to communicate with one another.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cloud-native SaaS: Software is delivered through scalable cloud-based services rather than relying heavily on traditional on-premises infrastructure.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Headless architecture: The front-end experience is separated from the underlying back-end systems, allowing organizations to deliver content and commerce across multiple channels.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these principles create a more composable technology environment in which businesses can select the tools that best support individual requirements.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Beyond Monolithic Technology</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional monolithic platforms generally combine multiple capabilities into a single system. While this approach can simplify initial implementation, changing one part of the system can sometimes require changes across the wider platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH takes a different approach.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With independently managed services, businesses can introduce new capabilities without replacing their entire technology foundation. A company could, for example, replace a search service, introduce a new customer experience platform, or integrate a different commerce solution without rebuilding every other component.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This flexibility can be particularly valuable for organizations operating in rapidly changing digital markets.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Composability Matters</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the central ideas behind MACH is <strong>composability</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A composable technology environment allows organizations to assemble their digital ecosystem from smaller, specialized services. Instead of accepting the limitations of a single platform, technology teams can select solutions based on business requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can provide several advantages.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Greater agility:</strong> Teams can introduce or modify individual services without making changes throughout the entire technology stack.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Continuous innovation:</strong> New technologies can be tested and integrated incrementally, reducing the need for large-scale transformation projects.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Scalability:</strong> Individual services can be scaled according to demand instead of scaling an entire application unnecessarily.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Technology flexibility:</strong> Organizations can replace individual components when better solutions become available.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Freedom to Experiment</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital transformation is rarely a one-time project. Customer expectations, technology capabilities, and business requirements continue to change.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH architecture supports an iterative approach to technology development. Teams can experiment with different services, integrations, and customer experiences without necessarily disrupting the entire digital ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can make experimentation less dependent on major platform migrations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For technology leaders, the benefit is not simply technical flexibility. A modular architecture can also support a culture in which teams are encouraged to test ideas, measure outcomes, and continuously improve digital experiences.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving Interoperability</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another important characteristic of MACH is interoperability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">API-driven systems make it easier for applications and services to exchange information. This can help organizations connect commerce platforms, customer data systems, content management tools, analytics solutions, marketing technologies, and other business applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of creating isolated technology environments, organizations can build interconnected ecosystems in which different services work together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For businesses operating across multiple channels, this can help create more consistent digital experiences.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH and E-Commerce</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">E-commerce has been one of the most visible areas for MACH adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Online businesses frequently need to respond to changes in customer behavior, introduce new shopping experiences, expand into additional channels, and integrate emerging technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A MACH-based architecture can provide the flexibility required for these changes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a business could use a headless commerce platform for its back end while delivering experiences through websites, mobile applications, marketplaces, in-store systems, or other digital touchpoints.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This separation between the back end and presentation layer allows development teams to create experiences for different channels without completely redesigning the underlying commerce infrastructure.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH Beyond Retail</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although MACH principles have strong connections to digital commerce, their applications are not limited to retail.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations in <strong>financial services, healthcare, manufacturing, travel, entertainment, and technology</strong> can also use modular architecture to address complex digital requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a healthcare organization could connect patient-facing applications with independent scheduling, communication, analytics, and content services. A travel company could similarly combine booking, customer experience, personalization, and marketing technologies into a connected ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The specific architecture will vary by organization, but the underlying objective remains the same: create technology systems that can evolve without excessive dependency on a single platform.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measuring the Business Value</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Adopting MACH architecture involves more than evaluating software costs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations also need to consider factors such as development efficiency, speed of implementation, opportunity costs, scalability, maintenance requirements, and the ability to respond to changing market conditions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An incremental approach can allow businesses to prioritize measurable outcomes instead of attempting to transform their entire technology environment simultaneously.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The potential value therefore extends beyond infrastructure. Faster experimentation, improved integration, and greater technology flexibility can influence how organizations deliver digital products and services.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Challenges to Consider</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">MACH architecture is not automatically the right answer for every organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A modular environment can introduce additional complexity because businesses may need to manage multiple vendors, integrations, APIs, services, and development processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations also need appropriate technical expertise, governance practices, security controls, and monitoring capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Without effective architecture management, a collection of independent technologies can become difficult to maintain.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For this reason, businesses considering MACH should evaluate their existing infrastructure, organizational capabilities, business objectives, and long-term technology strategy before beginning a transformation.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Future of Composable Technology</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing interest in MACH reflects a broader shift toward composable technology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses increasingly want technology environments that can adapt as their requirements change. Instead of committing to a single platform for every function, organizations can combine specialized solutions and evolve them over time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach can help technology become a more flexible business asset rather than a constraint on digital strategy.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As cloud services, APIs, AI, automation, and digital experience technologies continue to develop, modular architectures may become increasingly important for organizations seeking to modernize their technology ecosystems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ultimately, MACH is not simply a collection of technical principles. It represents a way of thinking about technology as an adaptable ecosystem-one where individual components can evolve while the broader business continues moving forward.</p>', 'How MACH Architecture Is Reshaping Modern Technology Stacks', 'Explore how MACH architecture helps businesses build scalable, flexible technology stacks through microservices, APIs, cloud SaaS, and headless systems.', 'MACH architecture, MACH technology, composable architecture, microservices architecture, API-first architecture, cloud-native SaaS, headless architecture, composable commerce, digital transformation, modern technology stack, enterprise technology, e-commerce technology', '2022-05-19 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-19 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 6, NULL, '2026-09-24 14:49:10', '2026-09-24 16:29:42', 1, NULL),
(907, 6, 2, 1, 'Why One-Size-Fits-All ERP Systems Struggle to Meet Mid-Market Retail Needs', 'why-one-size-fits-all-erp-systems-struggle-to-meet-mid-market-retail-needs', 'Legacy ERP systems often overwhelm mid-market retailers with high costs and complexity, while cloud-native automation offers faster, flexible operations.', '[\"ERP\",\"Enterprise Resource Planning\",\"Retail Technology\",\"Cloud ERP\",\"Cloud-Native ERP\",\"SME Technology\",\"Mid-Market Retail\",\"Retail Automation\",\"Business Automation\",\"AI ERP\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"E-Commerce\",\"Inventory Management\",\"CRM\",\"Supply Chain Management\"]', 'banner_image-1790262336852-381420932.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Traditional ERP Models Fall Short for Mid-Market Retailers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The one-size-fits-all approach used by many legacy enterprise resource planning (ERP) systems continues to create challenges for mid-market retailers. With tight margins and rapidly changing customer expectations, retailers have little room for software that is slow, rigid, or difficult to manage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many traditional ERP platforms were originally designed for large enterprises. As a result, small and medium-sized businesses can end up working with systems that are overly complicated, expensive to implement, and difficult to adapt to changing operational requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For smaller retailers, managing multiple information and communication technology (ICT) suppliers, contracts, software licenses, and disconnected applications can become a significant operational burden. At the same time, businesses must maintain accurate data and respond quickly to new market and technology challenges.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Morgan Browne, founder and CEO of Enterpryze, argues that the ERP industry has historically overlooked the practical requirements of small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). He believes automation-focused systems designed specifically for SMEs can provide a simpler path to growth without adding unnecessary technological complexity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ERP software can bring inventory, sales, finance, and other operational information together within a centralized platform. For retailers, this can improve visibility, reduce repetitive work, control costs, and support more informed decisions. Centralized operational data can also strengthen customer relationship management (CRM) by giving teams better access to current information about customers, orders, and inventory.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Browne told the E-Commerce Times that the slow movement of the ERP industry toward SMEs is influenced by a combination of established business models and older technology architectures.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large-Scale ERP Architecture Creates Unnecessary Complexity</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Browne, three important characteristics of an effective SME-focused ERP platform are ease of use, rapid deployment, and a cloud-native architecture that minimizes traditional service and maintenance requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unlike conventional on-premises ERP systems, cloud-native platforms can provide regular updates without requiring businesses to perform extensive upgrades or tolerate lengthy disruptions. This approach can reduce maintenance requirements while allowing companies to access improvements continuously.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The transition can be compared to the broader shift from purchasing and maintaining physical media toward subscribing to continuously updated digital services. Instead of repeatedly investing in infrastructure and upgrades, businesses can use cloud services that evolve as the underlying technology improves.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For businesses that have outgrown basic accounting software or are managing separate systems for CRM, inventory, finance, and operations, ERP needs to provide a unified view of business activity. Real-time visibility can help management monitor inventory, cash flow, sales, and customer interactions more effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional ERP providers have generally focused on large organizations with substantial technology budgets, extended implementation periods, and customized deployments. SMEs operate differently. They often require faster implementation, predictable costs, limited consulting requirements, and software that can function without a large internal IT department.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Meeting those expectations requires greater standardization, automation, and reduced implementation effort.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Legacy ERP Still Creates Problems for SMEs</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many established ERP platforms were initially developed for on-premises environments, extensive customization, and single-tenant deployments. These characteristics can make implementation complicated and updates expensive.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When such platforms are adapted for SMEs, vendors may simply remove advanced features instead of fundamentally redesigning the user experience and deployment model. This can result in a smaller version of an enterprise ERP that still retains much of its original complexity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving a legacy ERP system to the cloud does not automatically make it cloud-native. In some cases, the underlying architecture remains largely unchanged, with the existing system simply hosted in a cloud environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A truly cloud-native approach can instead be designed around scalability, automation, continuous updates, and simplified deployment from the beginning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Platforms such as Enterpryze promote this approach through technologies including multitenant architecture, API-first integrations, continuous updates, and automation built into the platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than taking a large enterprise system and reducing its functionality for smaller companies, cloud-native ERP platforms can redesign financial and operational workflows around the needs of SMEs.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fragmented ERP Ecosystems Increase the Technology Burden</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A fragmented technology environment can create another significant problem for smaller businesses.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When a single ERP system is too expensive or complicated to implement, SMEs may adopt separate applications for accounting, inventory management, CRM, reporting, and other functions. Spreadsheets and manual processes are then often used to connect the gaps between these systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Browne describes this situation as fragmentation by default.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The resulting complexity includes obvious expenses such as multiple software subscriptions, integration costs, consulting fees, and support contracts. However, fragmented systems can also create less visible operational costs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data may become inconsistent between applications, forcing employees to spend time comparing and reconciling information. Management teams may also hesitate to rely on reports when different systems contain conflicting figures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can slow decision-making and make it harder to maintain accurate visibility into inventory, cash flow, sales, and customer activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every additional application can also introduce another vendor relationship, license, login, integration, and support requirement. Instead of simplifying operations, the technology ecosystem can gradually become another source of administrative work.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">When Traditional ERP Becomes a Bottleneck</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consider a mid-sized e-commerce retailer experiencing a sudden supply chain disruption that delays shipments for several weeks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company may need to quickly change purchasing plans, identify alternative suppliers, modify reorder points, and adjust inventory strategies. In a heavily customized legacy ERP environment, making these changes may require assistance from the software provider.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If the vendor\'s operating model depends heavily on consulting and support services, the retailer may have to submit a request, wait for the services team to prioritize it, and then wait for the required changes to be implemented and deployed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During a supply chain disruption, such delays can create additional financial pressure. Inventory decisions may become outdated while the business waits for technology changes, while consulting costs can increase at a time when cash flow is already under pressure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A cloud-native and highly automated ERP model can reduce this dependency by allowing authorized business users to make operational changes more quickly and deploy updates without waiting for lengthy vendor intervention.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Automation Is Changing ERP</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation changes the role an ERP system can play in an organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A traditional management system primarily records business activity. It stores orders, invoices, inventory movements, financial transactions, and other information, then presents that information through reports and dashboards.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An automation-oriented ERP system can go further by using rules, data, and increasingly AI-driven capabilities to initiate actions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For an e-commerce business, for example, the system could automatically recommend or generate purchase orders based on demand patterns, inventory levels, and sales velocity. Bank transactions can be matched and reconciled automatically, while invoices can be scanned, validated, and entered with less manual effort.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The system can also identify unusual activity, such as duplicate invoices, unexpected pricing changes, or abnormal spending patterns, and flag those issues for review.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing adoption of generative AI has further changed expectations around business software. Users increasingly expect enterprise applications to understand context, provide relevant insights, and reduce repetitive administrative work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-enabled ERP platforms can incorporate capabilities such as demand forecasting, inventory optimization, automated workflows, and predictive cash-flow analysis.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Future of ERP for Mid-Market Retailers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For mid-market retailers, the ERP discussion is increasingly moving beyond simply choosing between on-premises and cloud software.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The larger question is whether the platform can provide the simplicity, automation, integration, and real-time visibility required by a growing business.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A modern ERP platform designed around SME requirements can help bring financial, operational, inventory, and customer information together while reducing dependence on disconnected applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As retailers face changing supply chains, increasing customer expectations, tighter margins, and growing technology demands, flexible and automated ERP architectures can become an important part of their digital operations strategy.</p>', 'Why One-Size-Fits-All ERP Fails Mid-Market Retailers', 'Discover why legacy ERP systems struggle with mid-market retailers and how cloud-native automation can improve flexibility, visibility, and efficiency.', 'ERP for mid-market retailers, SME ERP, cloud-native ERP, retail ERP software, ERP automation, cloud ERP, retail technology, AI ERP, business automation, inventory management, e-commerce ERP', '2022-05-20 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-20 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-09-24 15:05:37', '2026-09-24 16:29:41', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(908, 6, 1, 19, 'Retailers Turn to Cryptocurrency Payments as Consumer Demand Gains Momentum', 'retailers-turn-to-cryptocurrency-payments-as-consumer-demand-gains-momentum', 'Growing crypto ownership and consumer demand are encouraging retailers to explore cryptocurrency payments, despite volatility and regulatory concerns.', '[\"Cryptocurrency\",\"Crypto Payments\",\"Retail Technology\",\"FinTech\",\"Digital Payments\",\"Blockchain\",\"E-commerce\",\"Retail Payments\",\"Cryptocurrency Adoption\",\"Payment Gateway\",\"Crypto-to-Fiat\",\"Digital Currency\",\"Retail Innovation\",\"Consumer Payments\",\"Financial Technology\"]', 'banner_image-1790265941015-372445559.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Retailers Turn to Cryptocurrency Payments as Consumer Demand Gains Momentum</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cryptocurrency has long been associated with volatile markets, speculative investments, and high-profile blockchain ventures. However, digital currencies are increasingly attracting attention for another purpose: everyday payments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As cryptocurrency ownership expands, consumers are showing greater interest in using digital assets to purchase products and services. According to the 2025 State of Crypto Holders Report from the National Cryptocurrency Association, approximately 21% of American adults, representing around 55 million people, own some form of cryptocurrency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The report also indicates that crypto ownership is increasingly translating into real-world spending. Around 39% of cryptocurrency holders said they had used digital currencies to purchase goods or services, with the majority of those users making purchases at least once a year.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumer Interest Is Driving Payment Adoption</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumer interest could play an important role in encouraging retailers to support cryptocurrency transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Capital One survey published in late 2024 found that 65% of consumers would like merchants to offer cryptocurrency as a payment option. Among those respondents, 80% expressed interest in using crypto to purchase everyday retail products.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The survey also found that 55% of cryptocurrency-owning consumers would prefer an e-commerce retailer that accepts cryptocurrency over a comparable retailer that does not.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers operating in highly competitive markets, these preferences could create another opportunity to differentiate their payment experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">\"Cryptocurrencies are here to stay,\" said Shane Rodgers, an advisor to PDX GlobalPay, which developed the PDX Beam crypto payment platform. He argued that retailers adopting cost-effective crypto payment infrastructure could benefit from entering the market early.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rodgers also suggested that flexible payment options could help retailers attract new customers while strengthening relationships with existing buyers.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Retailers See Potential for New Customers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Retail adoption of cryptocurrency payments remains relatively limited, but interest among businesses is growing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Capital One reported that 75% of merchants planned to begin accepting cryptocurrency within the following 24 months, while its research also showed that a significant portion of businesses remained reluctant to adopt digital currency payments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cryptocurrency currently represents only a small share of retail payment activity. However, the customers who use it can represent an interesting opportunity for merchants.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the Capital One research, approximately 40% of customers paying with cryptocurrency were new customers for a retailer. The research also indicated that crypto-paying customers had purchase amounts roughly twice as large as customers using credit cards.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These figures suggest that cryptocurrency could potentially provide retailers with access to a digitally oriented customer segment while increasing transaction values.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Integration and Volatility Remain Challenges</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Despite the potential benefits, cryptocurrency adoption is not without obstacles.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One major challenge is integrating crypto payments with existing retail infrastructure. Chantelle Borrelli, head of communications at DMG Blockchain Solutions, said retailers can be concerned about implementation complexity, cryptocurrency volatility, regulatory uncertainty, and the technical expertise required to manage digital assets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Volatility can be particularly problematic for businesses that want predictable transaction values. Refunds and payment disputes can also present additional complications because blockchain transactions are generally designed to be difficult to reverse once confirmed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blockchain technology records transactions on a distributed ledger maintained across a network. Cryptographic mechanisms help protect the integrity of transaction records, making unauthorized alterations extremely difficult after transactions have been confirmed.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, cryptocurrency payments can provide potential advantages, including faster settlement, alternative payment infrastructure, and access to consumers who are comfortable using digital assets.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Making Cryptocurrency Payments Easier for Merchants</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment infrastructure will be an important factor in determining whether cryptocurrency moves further into mainstream retail.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rodgers argued that crypto payment platforms should reduce reliance on traditional banking and card-processing infrastructure where possible. Traditional payment systems can involve transaction fees that are particularly significant for businesses operating with narrow margins.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An alternative blockchain-based payment rail can potentially provide another route for processing transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another consideration is interoperability. Retailers may be reluctant to adopt a payment solution that works only with a specific point-of-sale system. An open payment infrastructure that can work across different POS environments may provide merchants with greater flexibility as their technology stacks evolve.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses may also prefer payment models that do not require them to maintain cryptocurrency balances. Holding digital assets can expose merchants to price fluctuations and additional operational considerations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For this reason, crypto-to-fiat conversion is emerging as one approach to reducing the direct exposure of merchants to cryptocurrency price movements.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Crypto-to-Fiat Conversion Could Reduce Merchant Risk</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some cryptocurrency payment platforms are designed to convert digital currencies into local currency during the transaction process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PDX Beam, for example, is positioned as a crypto payment gateway that can work with different POS environments while converting cryptocurrency into fiat currency for merchants.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Rodgers, this approach allows merchants to receive traditional currency without directly holding cryptocurrency. The payment platform handles the conversion process and associated crypto-related risks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Such systems could make cryptocurrency payments more familiar to retailers because the merchant\'s settlement process can resemble a conventional cash or payment transaction rather than requiring the business to manage digital assets directly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lower transaction costs and faster settlement could also make crypto payment solutions more attractive to businesses, particularly smaller merchants that are sensitive to payment-processing expenses and settlement times.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Alternative Payment Method to Mainstream Option</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of cryptocurrency in retail will depend on several factors, including consumer adoption, regulatory developments, payment infrastructure, transaction costs, and merchant demand.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, cryptocurrency is no longer only an investment-related technology. It is increasingly being evaluated as another potential payment channel that could provide access to digitally engaged consumers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Borrelli expects digital currencies to become increasingly visible in the global payments landscape over the next decade, potentially moving from a niche payment method toward a more familiar option for consumers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Whether that transition happens at scale will depend on how effectively the industry addresses volatility, regulation, security, integration, refunds, and merchant settlement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, the growing interest in cryptocurrency payments presents an evolving technology opportunity-one that could become increasingly relevant as consumers expect more choice in how they pay for goods and services.</p>', 'Retailers Adopt Crypto Payments as Consumer Demand Grows', 'Explore how growing crypto ownership and consumer demand are driving retailers to consider cryptocurrency payments and blockchain-based payment solutions.', 'cryptocurrency payments, crypto payments, retail cryptocurrency, crypto adoption, digital payments, blockchain payments, retail technology, fintech, crypto payment gateway, cryptocurrency retail, crypto-to-fiat, digital currency payments, e-commerce crypto payments', '2022-05-23 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-23 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 6, NULL, '2026-09-24 16:05:46', '2026-09-24 16:29:38', 1, NULL),
(909, 6, 1, 5, 'Netenrich Launches AI/ML Platform to Transform Cloud Security Operations', 'netenrich-launches-aiml-platform-to-transform-cloud-security-operations', 'Netenrich launches an AI/ML-powered platform that correlates cloud, security, and operations data to prioritize risks and accelerate responses.', '[\"AI\",\"Machine Learning\",\"Cloud Security\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"Security Operations\",\"Security Analytics\",\"MSSP\",\"Managed Security Services\",\"Google Chronicle\",\"Threat Intelligence\",\"Risk Management\",\"Cloud Computing\",\"Automation\",\"Netenrich\",\"Digital Operations\"]', 'banner_image-1790266122693-309279642.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Netenrich Launches AI/ML Platform to Transform Cloud Security Operations</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Netenrich has introduced an artificial intelligence and machine learning-powered approach to security operations through its Resolution Intelligence platform, aiming to address limitations associated with traditional security operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The cloud-based security and operations analytics company announced the platform as a new approach to protecting digital infrastructure. Resolution Intelligence analyzes real-time security information to identify high-impact business resources that are vulnerable, exposed to critical risks, or require immediate action.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bringing Security and Operations Data Together</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resolution Intelligence is designed to aggregate and correlate security and operational information across networks, cloud environments, and applications. By bringing these data sources together, the platform can identify critical issues and risky behaviors and prioritize them according to their potential business impact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and machine learning capabilities analyze incoming information to determine which actions should receive immediate attention. Risk scores and contextual intelligence help security and operations teams focus on issues that require the most urgent response.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform follows a cloud-native architecture designed to take advantage of distributed cloud computing. It also integrates with Google Chronicle, allowing security data to be brought into Netenrich\'s ActOn Data Lake alongside other operational data for analysis.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Platform Designed for MSSPs and Service Providers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Netenrich, Resolution Intelligence is designed specifically to support managed security service providers (MSSPs) and other service-provider environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform combines correlation, rules management, threat analysis, and data analytics to provide prioritized insights and situational awareness. Security telemetry from across an organization\'s infrastructure can be combined with operational information within a common framework.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resolution Intelligence can ingest security data from Google Chronicle along with operational data, then correlate and rank issues based on risk and business context.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is particularly relevant for MSSPs, MSPs, and organizations operating at service-provider scale, where managing multiple customer environments and maintaining operational efficiency can be challenging.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and Automation for Security Operations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the platform\'s key capabilities is its use of AI and machine learning to automate parts of security analysis and customer management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resolution Intelligence provides managed service providers with a multi-level, multi-tenant environment that can support multiple customers from a unified interface. Automation can help simplify customer workflows while enabling providers to scale their services.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform provides visibility into the security and operational status of critical assets across an entire customer base, individual customer environments, or defined customer groups.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its unified interface brings security and digital operations together, allowing teams to manage rules, perform threat analytics, and track operational conditions across customer environments.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving From Reactive to Proactive Security</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Netenrich positions Resolution Intelligence as a platform designed to move security operations beyond simply responding to large volumes of alerts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining real-time analytics, AI, automation, and risk prioritization, the platform is intended to help organizations identify potential problems earlier, investigate threats more efficiently, and reduce the impact of security incidents.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional security operations can require analysts to process large numbers of alerts, including false positives and lower-priority events. Automated analysis and risk-based prioritization can help reduce the amount of manual investigation required for routine tasks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform also brings previously separated security, operations, and business teams into a more unified operational framework, enabling greater collaboration around digital infrastructure and security.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real-Time Monitoring and Automation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real-time notifications and automation are important elements of the platform\'s approach to detecting operational and security problems before they result in larger disruptions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bradd Busick, CIO at MultiCare Health, said the organization\'s technology infrastructure is no longer viewed solely through individual point solutions. He noted that the approach helps the organization better manage disruptions while maintaining its focus on patient care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For service providers, the integration with Google Chronicle is intended to simplify the use of security analytics in multi-tenant environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Jonas Kelley, head of Americas MSSP Partnerships at Google Cloud, said the platform can help MSSPs operationalize Chronicle across multiple tenants while allowing providers to incorporate their own intellectual property into their services.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advanced Security Analytics</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resolution Intelligence also incorporates security analytics capabilities for threat hunting, investigation, and threat intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security analysts can use threat intelligence within the platform and apply machine learning and AI techniques to identify patterns and generate prioritized insights.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining security telemetry, operational data, contextual information, and automated analysis, Netenrich aims to give organizations a more comprehensive view of their digital security environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform\'s overall approach is focused on identifying high-priority risks, automating repetitive security tasks, improving response times, and helping service providers manage security operations at scale.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and ML are used to analyze security and operational data.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Resolution Intelligence correlates information across networks, clouds, and applications.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform prioritizes risks based on business impact and contextual intelligence.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Google Chronicle integration brings additional security data into the platform.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Multi-tenant capabilities are designed for MSSPs and other service providers.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation can reduce manual security analysis and repetitive operational tasks.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security analytics supports threat hunting, investigation, and threat intelligence.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"></p>', 'Netenrich AI/ML Platform for Cloud Security Operations', 'Netenrich\'s Resolution Intelligence uses AI, ML, and security analytics to prioritize cloud risks, automate security operations, and improve threat response.', 'Netenrich, Resolution Intelligence, AI security, machine learning security, cloud security, cybersecurity, security operations, security analytics, MSSP, Google Chronicle, threat intelligence, cloud security platform, AI/ML cybersecurity, risk management', '2022-05-23 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-23 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 5, NULL, '2026-09-24 16:08:43', '2026-09-24 16:29:37', 1, NULL),
(910, 6, 2, 5, 'Why Web-Based Businesses Need to Automate Content Security Policies', 'why-web-based-businesses-need-to-automate-content-security-policies', 'Automated Content Security Policies help web businesses detect unsafe scripts, manage third-party risks, and strengthen client-side security.', '[\"Content Security Policy\",\"CSP\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"Web Security\",\"Client-Side Security\",\"Application Security\",\"Website Security\",\"Third-Party Scripts\",\"XSS\",\"JavaScript Injection\",\"Web Application Security\",\"Security Automation\",\"Enterprise Cybersecurity\",\"Digital Security\",\"Threat Detection\"]', 'banner_image-1790266286140-319063672.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For decades, cybersecurity strategies have largely focused on protecting a company\'s servers, backend infrastructure, applications, and databases. While securing these systems remains essential, businesses that depend heavily on websites and customer-facing applications now face another critical challenge: protecting the client side.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organizations that collect customer information through websites and web applications, browsers and front-end environments have become increasingly attractive targets for cybercriminals. Chief Security Officers (CSOs), Chief Information Security Officers (CISOs), and security teams therefore need to look beyond traditional backend defenses and understand what is happening across the client-facing layer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One important component of this strategy is the Content Security Policy (CSP). However, simply implementing a CSP once and leaving it unchanged is no longer sufficient. Modern websites continuously evolve, integrate third-party services, add marketing technologies, and deploy new scripts. As a result, CSP management needs continuous monitoring, testing, and automation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security Requires Continuous Monitoring</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A website\'s security posture should never be treated as a \"set it and forget it\" activity. Websites receive a constant stream of visitors, while their underlying code and integrations can change frequently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">New plugins may be introduced. Analytics and advertising tools may be added. Marketing trackers can be modified. Development teams may deploy new features or update existing scripts. Each of these changes can potentially introduce a new security concern.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consider the way aviation systems operate. A commercial aircraft does not rely solely on a pilot manually checking every component throughout a flight. Sensors, monitoring systems, and automated alerts continuously provide information about the aircraft\'s condition and highlight issues that require attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Web security can benefit from the same principle. Automated monitoring can continuously observe a website\'s client-side behavior and provide security teams with actionable information when something changes or violates established security policies.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Case for CSP Automation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern cybersecurity already makes extensive use of automation, artificial intelligence, machine learning, automated detection, and continuous monitoring. These technologies help security teams manage increasingly complex environments without requiring humans to manually inspect every individual event.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Client-side security presents a similar challenge.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Large enterprise websites can rely on thousands of scripts originating from internal systems as well as third-, fourth-, and even fifth-party providers. These scripts may support analytics, advertising, payment processing, customer engagement, personalization, authentication, and other business functions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Because these external resources can change over time, organizations cannot simply assume that a previously trusted script will always remain safe.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation provides a practical way to maintain visibility across this complex environment. Instead of requiring security teams to manually inspect every script and policy change, automated CSP management can continuously evaluate website behavior and identify activity that requires investigation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The continuing exposure of customer information and the increasing complexity of modern web applications have made this visibility particularly important. Security teams need to understand what is running on their websites, what resources those scripts can access, and whether their behavior matches established security expectations.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Can a Content Security Policy Identify?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Content Security Policy can help organizations control which sources a browser is permitted to load and interact with. This can provide an additional layer of defense against several client-side attack scenarios.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unsafe or unauthorized scripts are among the most important concerns. Malicious or compromised scripts can contribute to attacks such as cross-site scripting (XSS), JavaScript injection, and payment-page or point-of-sale skimming attacks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The risk becomes more complicated when third-party scripts are involved. A business may intentionally integrate a legitimate analytics platform, advertising service, customer-support widget, payment component, or marketing tracker. However, if that external resource changes unexpectedly, introduces an unsafe dependency, or attempts to access resources beyond its intended scope, the organization needs a way to identify the behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A properly managed CSP can help security teams establish boundaries around what resources are permitted to load and where those resources can interact.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Third-Party Scripts Require Attention</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern websites rarely operate using only internally developed code. Businesses frequently depend on a large ecosystem of external technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing teams may add tracking platforms. Product teams may integrate customer engagement tools. Developers may introduce new libraries. E-commerce teams may add payment services. Analytics teams may deploy additional measurement tools.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Each integration increases the complexity of the client-side environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Manually reviewing every script on a large website is difficult to sustain. As the number of pages, scripts, domains, and integrations increases, the workload can quickly exceed what security teams can consistently manage.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation can help by continuously observing these resources and highlighting unexpected behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, if a script attempts to communicate with an unauthorized domain or access a resource outside its intended scope, an automated monitoring system can flag the activity for investigation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">From Detection to Remediation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Effective CSP management is not limited to identifying violations. Security teams also need processes for investigating, responding to, and reducing those violations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An automated CSP workflow can support activities such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Initial website scanning</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Script and resource discovery</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">CSP policy generation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy testing and validation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy enforcement</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Violation detection and reporting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuous monitoring</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Policy refinement and tuning</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These capabilities can significantly reduce the manual effort associated with managing client-side security.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of spending months manually analyzing website behavior and constructing policies, organizations can use automation to accelerate discovery, testing, and policy development while still maintaining appropriate human oversight.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Continuous Crawling and Real-World Website Behavior</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the advantages of an automated approach is the ability to continuously evaluate a website in a way that resembles real user activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automated crawling and browser-based testing can help security teams understand which scripts are executed, what resources they interact with, which external services are contacted, and whether those activities align with established policies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This continuous visibility is particularly useful for organizations whose websites change frequently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A policy that was appropriate several months ago may no longer provide adequate coverage after new integrations, plugins, tracking technologies, or application features are introduced. Continuous monitoring helps security teams identify these changes rather than relying entirely on periodic manual reviews.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Stronger Client-Side Security Posture</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The client side has become an important part of an organization\'s overall cybersecurity strategy. Protecting servers and backend systems remains critical, but organizations must also understand the technologies operating within users\' browsers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automated CSP management can help bridge this gap by providing continuous visibility, policy enforcement, violation detection, and ongoing policy optimization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For IT and security teams, automation can reduce repetitive manual work while allowing them to focus on higher-value security activities. For businesses, stronger client-side controls can help reduce exposure to unauthorized scripts and third-party risks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most importantly, security should be treated as an ongoing process rather than a one-time configuration. As websites evolve, their security policies need to evolve with them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining Content Security Policies with continuous monitoring and automation, web-based businesses can create a more adaptable client-side security strategy-helping protect their applications, customer information, and digital experiences in an increasingly complex threat environment.</p>', 'Automated Content Security Policy for Web Security', 'Learn how automated Content Security Policies help web businesses monitor scripts, manage third-party risks, detect violations, and strengthen client-side security.', 'content security policy, CSP automation, automated CSP, web security, client-side security, website security, cybersecurity, third-party scripts, CSP violations, XSS protection, JavaScript security, web application security', '2022-05-23 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-23 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 6, NULL, '2026-09-24 16:11:26', '2026-09-24 16:29:36', 1, NULL),
(911, 6, 2, 19, 'Digital Dollars Rise as Consumer Payment Preferences Reshape Retail', 'digital-dollars-rise-as-consumer-payment-preferences-reshape-retail', 'Consumer demand for digital wallets, BNPL, contactless payments, and flexible checkout options is transforming retail payment strategies worldwide.', '[\"Digital Payments\",\"Retail Payments\",\"Digital Wallets\",\"Buy Now Pay Later\",\"BNPL\",\"FinTech\",\"Payment Technology\",\"Contactless Payments\",\"E-commerce\",\"Retail Technology\",\"Embedded Finance\",\"Payment Security\",\"Consumer Behavior\",\"Checkout Optimization\",\"Payment Gateway\"]', 'banner_image-1790266455119-844817102.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Digital Payments Are Reshaping the Retail Experience</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The retail payments landscape is undergoing a significant transformation as consumers increasingly expect faster, simpler, and more flexible ways to pay. Payment options are no longer just a checkout feature; they are becoming an important factor in purchasing decisions and overall customer experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">From biometric payment cards and digital wallets to Buy Now, Pay Later (BNPL) services, shoppers are showing a growing preference for retailers that support the payment methods they already use. Research indicates that consumers may abandon an online purchase when their preferred payment option is unavailable, creating an additional challenge for merchants trying to improve conversion rates.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">BNPL has also expanded beyond specific retail categories and become a familiar payment option for a broad range of consumers. Its adoption has been particularly visible among younger shoppers who value flexibility and convenient financial services. At the same time, consumers increasingly move between physical stores, websites, mobile applications, and social platforms, making a consistent omnichannel experience more important for retailers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Liam Gerada, CEO and co-founder of Krepling, has highlighted how millennials and Gen Z consumers are increasingly comfortable obtaining financial services from non-traditional providers. These consumers also expect digital experiences to remain seamless regardless of the platform or channel they use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Krepling provides e-commerce businesses with tools to build, manage, and scale online stores without requiring extensive coding expertise.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Adapting Payment Experiences to Consumer Preferences</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growth of embedded finance and partnerships with independent software vendors (ISVs) is further changing how merchants deliver payment services. Through these partnerships, businesses can introduce payment capabilities across social media platforms, rewards programs, loyalty ecosystems, and other digital touchpoints.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment providers and retailers are therefore responding by investing in technologies that provide greater flexibility while maintaining security and convenience. These developments are pushing the payments industry toward more personalized and customer-focused experiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contactless payments have become one of the most visible developments in retail transactions. As digital wallets gained popularity for online purchases, smartphones equipped with contactless technology also made it easier for consumers to use the same devices for in-store payments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Jason Pavona, GM of North America Enterprise at Worldpay, pointed to the continued growth of digital wallets at physical points of sale. According to Worldpay data cited by Pavona, nearly one-third of U.S. point-of-sale payments are expected to be made through digital wallets by 2027.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">New Payment Solutions Target Retail Checkout Challenges</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment technology providers are developing new solutions to address checkout friction and help merchants improve conversion rates. One example is Krepling Pay, an e-commerce payment and channel management solution designed to give merchants greater visibility into payment costs while incorporating security measures for customer and business data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform is designed to simplify the checkout experience and help merchants address cart abandonment. With global online cart abandonment rates averaging around 70%, even small improvements in the payment process can represent a significant opportunity for e-commerce businesses.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumers frequently leave online checkouts because of unexpected shipping charges, complicated return policies, lengthy registration requirements, or an inconvenient payment process. Streamlining these steps can reduce friction and make it easier for customers to complete transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Krepling Pay combines payment functionality with centralized e-commerce workflow integrations and customer analytics. These capabilities can help merchants monitor purchasing patterns, understand customer behavior, and develop strategies around conversion and retention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Gerada, the solution was developed to address the changing expectations surrounding secure, personalized, and convenient checkout experiences. Features such as one-click transactions, localized currencies, language support, and customizable checkout layouts are intended to make the payment process easier for customers across different markets.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How One-Click Payment Technology Works</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Modern payment platforms are increasingly focused on reducing the number of steps required to complete an online purchase.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With a one-click checkout model, customers provide their payment and personal information during checkout, after which the platform can create a secure digital wallet containing encrypted information. This allows customers to complete subsequent purchases more quickly across supported browsers and devices.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Localized currency and language capabilities can also improve the experience for international shoppers. For global e-commerce businesses, these features can reduce confusion during checkout and create a more familiar purchasing environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security remains another critical component. Payment providers are increasingly implementing encryption, tokenization, authentication, access controls, and compliance measures to protect transaction and customer information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Krepling has positioned PCI compliance and 3D Secure support as part of its approach to protecting payment information while maintaining a streamlined checkout experience.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Retailers Face Growing Pressure to Modernize Payments</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As consumer expectations continue to evolve, retailers need to consider payment flexibility alongside pricing, delivery, customer service, and loyalty initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Worldpay\'s Pavona has emphasized the importance of adapting payment strategies as digital wallets become increasingly prominent in online commerce. Digital wallets can simplify checkout by allowing consumers to use stored payment credentials and shipping information rather than entering the same details repeatedly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This development also changes the relationship between retailers and customers. Payment experiences increasingly connect with broader discussions around customer ownership, loyalty programs, rewards, retention, and personalized service.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, payment technology is therefore becoming part of the overall customer relationship rather than simply the final step of a transaction.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment Technology Also Faces Fraud and Security Challenges</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The transformation of retail payments brings significant security challenges for fintech companies and payment providers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing use of digital technologies and artificial intelligence has created new opportunities for fraudsters while also increasing the need for stronger fraud-prevention systems. At the same time, customers are becoming more conscious of how businesses protect their personal and financial information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Donal McGuinness, CEO of payment link platform Prommt, the global retail sector lost approximately US$429 billion to payment fraud in 2023. He also cited research indicating that nearly half of businesses worldwide experienced fraudulent activity, cyberattacks, or data breaches during the preceding year.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These developments are increasing pressure on organizations to strengthen payment security and data protection. Encryption, tokenization, strict data governance, access controls, multi-factor authentication, and regulatory compliance are becoming important elements of modern payment infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For payment providers and retailers, the challenge is to balance stronger security controls with a checkout process that remains convenient for customers.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Millennials and Gen Z Influence Payment Priorities</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Younger consumers are playing an increasingly visible role in shaping retail payment expectations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research cited by McGuinness indicates that 51% of millennials and 48% of Gen Z consumers would abandon a purchase when their preferred payment method is unavailable. The same discussion highlights the interest among Gen Z consumers in automated payment experiences that can reduce checkout time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These consumers frequently move between physical stores, e-commerce websites, mobile applications, and virtual environments. As a result, they expect payment services to work consistently across devices and channels.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, this means payment flexibility can influence more than transaction completion. It can affect customer satisfaction, brand perception, repeat purchases, and long-term loyalty.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Future of Retail Payments</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The evolution of retail payments is being driven by several interconnected trends, including digital wallets, contactless transactions, BNPL, embedded finance, one-click checkout, personalization, and stronger security requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumers increasingly expect payments to be fast, secure, convenient, and available through the channels they already use. Retailers that fail to provide appropriate payment choices can potentially create unnecessary friction during the purchasing journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, payment providers must address growing concerns around fraud, privacy, compliance, and data security. The next phase of digital payments will therefore depend on how effectively businesses balance convenience with protection.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As consumer behavior continues to change, payment technology is becoming a central component of the modern retail experience rather than simply a mechanism for completing a transaction.</p>', 'Digital Payments Reshape Retail Consumer Payment Preferences', 'Discover how digital wallets, BNPL, contactless payments, and flexible checkout options are transforming retail payments and consumer behavior.', 'digital payments, retail payments, digital wallets, BNPL, buy now pay later, fintech, payment technology, contactless payments, ecommerce payments, embedded finance, payment gateway, payment security, consumer payment preferences, retail technology', '2022-05-24 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-24 00:00:00', 'published', 1, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-09-24 16:14:15', '2026-09-28 12:14:10', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(912, 6, 2, 5, 'How Deepfakes Are Threatening Mobile Commerce and Biometric Security', 'how-deepfakes-are-threatening-mobile-commerce-and-biometric-security', 'Deepfake attacks are exposing weaknesses in mobile biometrics, putting e-commerce accounts, payments, customer data, and trust at risk.', '[\"Deepfake Fraud\",\"Mobile Commerce Security\",\"Biometric Authentication\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"AI Fraud\",\"Mobile Security\",\"Identity Theft\",\"Account Takeover\",\"Face ID\",\"Voice Authentication\",\"Payment Fraud\",\"AI Security\"]', 'banner_image-1790266583480-341764201.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Deepfakes Are Threatening Mobile Commerce and Biometric Security</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology is becoming a growing cybersecurity concern for mobile commerce. AI-generated faces, voices, and videos can be used to imitate legitimate users and challenge the reliability of biometric authentication. As a result, retailers, financial institutions, payment providers, and consumers are facing increased exposure to account takeover, identity theft, payment fraud, and unauthorized transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile shopping has become an important part of the e-commerce ecosystem, with millions of consumers using smartphones to browse products, make purchases, access banking services, and manage digital accounts. Biometric technologies such as Apple Face ID, Android facial recognition, and voice authentication have helped make these experiences faster and more convenient.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the same convenience is attracting increasingly sophisticated cybercriminals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake attacks can manipulate or reproduce biometric characteristics and use them to circumvent identity-verification processes. Attackers can combine stolen personal information, AI-generated media, and automated fraud techniques to impersonate legitimate customers and target mobile applications.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Deepfake Fraud Is Becoming a Mobile Security Concern</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional mobile security strategies often rely on multiple layers of authentication and fraud controls. While these technologies remain important, the rapid development of generative AI is creating new attack techniques that security teams must continuously address.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminals can use AI-generated images and videos to create convincing representations of individuals. Voice-cloning technology can similarly reproduce a person\'s speech patterns and may be used during phone-based authentication or social-engineering attacks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These techniques can support several types of fraud, including:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Account takeover</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Synthetic identity creation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payment fraud</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unauthorized transactions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identity verification bypass</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Credential theft</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Financial-account compromise</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The challenge is particularly significant for mobile applications because authentication is expected to be both secure and frictionless. Users want quick access to banking, shopping, and payment applications, while businesses need to ensure that the person behind a transaction is actually the legitimate account holder.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric Authentication Alone May Not Be Sufficient</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric authentication has become a major component of mobile security. Facial recognition and fingerprint authentication can provide stronger protection than passwords alone while also improving the user experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, biometric authentication should not be treated as an isolated security control.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology can introduce manipulated visual or audio information into authentication workflows. Attackers may use synthetic media, virtual cameras, manipulated image buffers, or other techniques to attempt to deceive facial-recognition systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates an important distinction between authentication and continuous threat detection.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A system may successfully verify a biometric characteristic while still failing to identify that the interaction itself is malicious. For mobile commerce businesses, security therefore needs to consider not only whether a biometric match occurs, but also whether the surrounding activity demonstrates signs of fraud or manipulation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Attackers Can Target Mobile Commerce</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile commerce applications contain valuable information, including customer identities, payment information, account credentials, transaction histories, and personal data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attackers can attempt to exploit these applications through several stages.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Creating Synthetic Identities</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cybercriminals can combine publicly available information, stolen identity data, and AI-generated media to construct convincing synthetic identities. These identities can then be used during account creation or identity-verification processes.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Bypassing Facial Recognition</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-generated images and videos can potentially be used to imitate legitimate users. Attackers may attempt to introduce manipulated media into facial-recognition workflows to circumvent identity checks.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Using Voice Cloning</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Voice-generation technology can reproduce characteristics of a person\'s voice. When voice authentication is used as part of an account-access process, cloned voices may become another potential attack vector.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Taking Over Accounts</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Once attackers successfully bypass authentication or obtain account credentials, they can attempt to change account information, access stored payment details, or initiate unauthorized transactions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. Monetizing Stolen Access</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Compromised accounts can be used for fraudulent purchases, financial theft, resale of credentials, or additional attacks against the affected customer.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Growing Role of AI-Native Mobile Defense</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A fragmented security architecture can make it difficult for organizations to respond quickly when attackers introduce new techniques.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile businesses may use separate tools for authentication, fraud monitoring, application security, threat detection, and identity verification. While each component can provide value, organizations also need these defenses to work together effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An AI-native security approach can continuously analyze application activity, identify suspicious behavior, and adapt to changing attack patterns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of relying exclusively on static rules, modern mobile defense strategies can combine artificial intelligence, machine learning, threat intelligence, runtime protection, and behavioral signals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is to identify malicious activity as close to the point of attack as possible.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real-Time Protection Can Reduce the Attack Window</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake attacks can evolve quickly. By the time a new fraud technique is identified through conventional reporting and manual investigation, attackers may already be using variations of it against other applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Real-time, on-device protection can help reduce this window.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security controls integrated directly into mobile applications can monitor application behavior and detect suspicious activity during runtime. This approach can provide another layer of protection without requiring every security decision to be handled by external systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organizations handling payments and sensitive customer information, detecting an attack before fraudulent activity reaches backend systems can be particularly important.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Regulations Also Face New AI-Driven Challenges</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many existing security and compliance frameworks were developed before generative AI and modern deepfake technology became widely accessible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Requirements related to identity verification, payment security, and customer authentication continue to provide important safeguards. However, organizations must also consider how AI-generated attacks affect their existing security controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The emergence of deepfake fraud creates additional questions for mobile businesses:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Can existing identity-verification processes detect synthetic media?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How effectively can biometric systems identify spoofing attempts?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are fraud controls updated as new AI attack techniques emerge?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Can security teams respond to attacks in real time?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are mobile applications protected during runtime?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations may need to continuously reassess their security architecture rather than assuming that compliance with an existing framework automatically protects against emerging AI-powered attacks.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake Defense for Mobile Applications</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mobile security providers are increasingly developing specialized capabilities designed to detect deepfake and biometric-bypass attempts.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, Appdome has expanded its mobile application security capabilities with defenses targeting deepfake attacks against Android and iOS applications. These protections are designed to help secure biometric authentication technologies and identify attempts involving manipulated facial or voice data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake detection can address attack techniques such as manipulated video streams, virtual-camera injection, synthetic facial representations, and image-buffer manipulation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The broader objective is to prevent manipulated data from being used to compromise the application or continue an account-takeover attack.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As deepfake technology becomes more accessible, these capabilities are becoming an increasingly important part of the mobile security conversation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Mobile Commerce Businesses Can Respond</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations operating mobile commerce, financial, retail, and payment applications can take several steps to strengthen their defenses against AI-driven fraud.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Integrate AI-Based Deepfake Detection</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security teams can incorporate AI-powered detection capabilities into mobile applications to identify manipulated images, videos, voices, and other suspicious authentication signals.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strengthen Biometric Verification</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Biometric authentication should be combined with additional security signals, including liveness detection, device intelligence, behavioral analysis, and runtime application protection.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitor New Attack Techniques</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology is changing rapidly. Security teams should continuously monitor emerging attack methods and update detection mechanisms accordingly.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protect Applications at Runtime</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Application protection should continue after deployment. Runtime defenses can help identify suspicious activity while the application is actively being used.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Combine Security and Fraud Intelligence</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security and fraud teams can benefit from sharing threat intelligence and behavioral signals. Combining these capabilities can provide a broader view of account activity and potential attacks.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Minimize the Impact of Account Takeover</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should also prepare for the possibility that an attacker successfully obtains access. Transaction monitoring, risk-based authentication, account controls, and rapid incident-response processes can help limit potential damage.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protecting Trust in Mobile Commerce</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Deepfake technology is changing the threat landscape for mobile commerce. Facial recognition, voice authentication, and other biometric technologies remain valuable security mechanisms, but they cannot be viewed as the only line of defense.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI-generated attacks become more sophisticated, businesses need security strategies that can identify manipulation, detect suspicious behavior, and respond to threats in real time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For retailers, financial institutions, payment providers, and mobile application developers, the challenge is not simply protecting a login screen. It is protecting the entire digital transaction journey-from identity verification and account access to payment authorization and post-transaction monitoring.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of secure mobile commerce will therefore depend on combining biometric authentication with AI-driven threat detection, liveness verification, application protection, behavioral intelligence, and continuous security monitoring.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By moving toward adaptive and AI-native security strategies, organizations can better prepare their mobile applications for the next generation of AI-powered fraud while working to preserve customer confidence and protect digital transactions.</p>', 'Deepfake Threats to Mobile Commerce & Biometric Security', 'Discover how deepfake attacks bypass mobile biometrics, enable payment fraud, and threaten e-commerce security, identity, and customer trust.', 'deepfake fraud, mobile commerce security, biometric security, AI fraud, deepfake attacks, mobile cybersecurity, biometric authentication, Face ID security, voice authentication, payment fraud, account takeover, identity theft, mobile app security', '2022-05-24 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-24 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 8, NULL, '2026-09-24 16:16:23', '2026-09-24 16:29:33', 1, NULL),
(913, 6, 1, 3, 'How AI Agents Are Reshaping Commerce, Brand Visibility, and Market Power', 'how-ai-agents-are-reshaping-commerce-brand-visibility-and-market-power', 'AI agents are reshaping e-commerce by influencing product discovery, recommendations, trust, and brand visibility through agentic commerce.', '[\"AI Agents\",\"Agentic Commerce\",\"Generative AI\",\"E-Commerce\",\"AI Shopping\",\"Brand Visibility\",\"GEO\",\"Generative Engine Optimization\",\"Retail Technology\",\"Digital Commerce\",\"AI Marketing\",\"Machine-Readable Data\",\"Customer Experience\",\"AI Recommendations\",\"Commerce Automation\"]', 'banner_image-1790266724832-771475901.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How AI Agents Are Reshaping Commerce, Brand Visibility, and Market Power</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is moving beyond conversational interfaces and beginning to influence how consumers discover, compare, and purchase products. Across retail, payments, and technology, AI-powered checkout systems, automated product comparisons, and autonomous purchasing tools are helping create a new model of e-commerce known as <strong>agentic commerce</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PwC views agentic commerce as a significant shift in the way products are surfaced and evaluated. Instead of consumers manually browsing dozens of search results, AI agents can increasingly identify products, compare alternatives, evaluate relevant information, and help execute transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This shift is changing the role of brands. In traditional digital commerce, retailers and marketplaces controlled much of the customer discovery experience. In an agent-driven environment, the AI system making recommendations can become an additional gatekeeper between a product and the consumer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recent research and digital commerce trends highlighted by the National Retail Federation indicate that AI agents are progressing from experimentation toward practical applications. Emerging commerce protocols, including Google\'s Universal Commerce Protocol, are also helping establish the infrastructure required for agents to interact with commerce systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Eric Shea, principal for customer and commercial excellence at PwC US, told E-Commerce Times that the major change is the maturation of the underlying infrastructure. AI agents are increasingly connecting with real-time inventory, pricing, fulfillment, payment, identity, and product-data systems through APIs and standardized protocols.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Is Driving the Agentic Commerce Trend?</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Several developments are contributing to the growth of agentic commerce.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Interoperable commerce standards are helping AI agents move beyond answering questions and toward executing commerce-related activities. At the same time, retailers have spent years modernizing cloud infrastructure, customer data systems, and digital commerce platforms. Those investments are creating the technical foundation for software agents to interact with real-world commerce environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consumer expectations are changing as well. Shoppers increasingly want purchasing experiences that are personalized, conversational, and convenient. AI agents can potentially serve as an orchestration layer between a consumer\'s intent and the final transaction.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Shea, early behavioral changes are already becoming visible, including greater shopping intent within AI prompts and increasing referral activity from AI platforms to retail and brand websites.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As agents become capable of handling more of the journey from product discovery to checkout, customer loyalty may also take on a different meaning. Brands have traditionally competed for human attention, emotional connection, and recognition. In agentic commerce, they also need to establish confidence with the systems making recommendations.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The New Economics of Product Discovery</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional online search presents consumers with numerous products and links. AI agents can reduce that large selection into a much smaller shortlist.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Shea estimated that roughly 25% to 40% of shoppers already use AI-powered tools for some shopping activities. If these tools increasingly influence purchasing decisions, the economics of digital discovery could change significantly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For years, brands have invested heavily in impressions, rankings, clicks, and website traffic. Agentic commerce can compress that process into a smaller decision cycle in which an AI system evaluates available information and presents only a few options.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a different definition of discoverability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of simply competing to attract a consumer\'s click, brands increasingly need to provide the information and trust signals that allow an AI system to confidently recommend their products.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Machine Visibility Is Becoming Important</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In an agent-driven shopping environment, a product may have strong brand recognition but still face limited visibility if its information is incomplete, inconsistent, or difficult for machines to interpret.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brands may therefore need to focus more heavily on structured product information, accurate specifications, reliable pricing, inventory availability, delivery information, customer reviews, and fulfillment performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This does not eliminate traditional brand storytelling. Instead, brand narrative and machine-readable information may need to work together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations that previously optimized primarily for transactions and conversions may need to reconsider the quality and depth of the content surrounding their products. AI agents need contextual information to understand what a product offers, who it is designed for, and how it compares with alternatives.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Cost of Being Left Out</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-driven recommendation systems can create a much smaller visibility window for brands.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional search engines and marketplaces may expose consumers to dozens of products. An AI assistant may instead recommend only a handful of options. That means a brand that does not appear in the recommendation layer may receive little or no exposure during that particular purchasing journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Shea warned that brands that fail to become machine-readable, trustworthy, and operationally competitive could become effectively invisible in some AI-driven shopping experiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The potential impact extends beyond website traffic. Reduced visibility could influence conversion opportunities, customer acquisition, and long-term brand relevance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This dynamic could become more significant as AI systems become increasingly personalized and as shopping interfaces expand into voice assistants, AI-enabled wearables, and other ambient computing experiences.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Brands Can Become More Visible to AI Agents</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data quality is becoming one of the most important requirements for organizations preparing for agentic commerce.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI agents need accurate and structured information to evaluate products and make recommendations. Product specifications, pricing, inventory, delivery timelines, reviews, return policies, and fulfillment information all contribute to the decision-making process.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is where <strong>Generative Engine Optimization (GEO)</strong> and machine-readable commerce practices become increasingly relevant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional SEO focuses heavily on helping search engines discover, understand, and rank web content. AI-driven discovery requires an additional layer of optimization: ensuring that information is structured, trustworthy, consistent, and useful to machine reasoning systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A product with outdated pricing, inconsistent specifications, incomplete inventory information, or unreliable delivery estimates may become less attractive to an AI agent attempting to reduce the risk of a poor recommendation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As a result, organizations may need to treat operational data quality as part of their visibility strategy rather than as a purely technical concern.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust Is Becoming a Commerce Signal</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Agentic commerce also changes how brands build and maintain trust.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI systems can evaluate signals related to product accuracy, fulfillment performance, customer satisfaction, return experiences, review quality, sustainability claims, and pricing consistency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These signals extend beyond a company\'s own website. AI systems can encounter information across social networks, creator communities, review platforms, forums, publishers, and other third-party sources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For brands, maintaining consistent information across this broader digital ecosystem may therefore become increasingly important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The shift also connects marketing with operations. Inventory accuracy, reliable fulfillment, transparent pricing, simple returns, and consistent product quality can all contribute to the signals an AI system uses when determining whether a product is suitable for recommendation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In this environment, every operational touchpoint can potentially influence digital discoverability.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human Influence Still Matters</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing role of AI agents does not necessarily eliminate human influence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creators, online communities, reviews, social platforms, and cultural conversations continue to shape consumer preferences. However, AI agents may increasingly act as interpreters of that information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates a two-layer visibility challenge for brands.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Companies need to maintain emotional relevance with human audiences while also making their product information sufficiently clear and structured for machines.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Technical documentation, product attributes, verified reviews, structured content, and reliable data can therefore become increasingly important alongside traditional brand-building activities.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trust, Advertising, and AI Recommendations</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advertising presents another challenge for agentic commerce.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI systems become part of the purchasing journey, platforms may eventually incorporate sponsored recommendations or other commercial placements into AI-generated responses. However, transparency could become particularly important because consumers may expect AI assistants to prioritize relevance rather than advertising revenue.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PwC\'s perspective is that sponsored recommendations need to be clearly distinguishable from organic recommendations and should not undermine the quality of the overall experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If users believe an AI assistant is recommending a product primarily because it is paid to do so, confidence in the system could decline.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For this reason, future AI commerce platforms may need to balance monetization with recommendation transparency, governance, and consumer trust.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preparing for the Agentic Commerce Era</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Agentic commerce is creating a new layer between consumer intent and purchasing decisions. AI agents can increasingly discover products, evaluate information, compare alternatives, and participate in transactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For brands, this means visibility can no longer depend solely on traditional search rankings, advertising, or website traffic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations preparing for this environment should consider several areas:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maintain accurate and structured product information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Keep pricing and inventory data current.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strengthen product and technical documentation.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Build reliable trust signals through customer experiences.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitor reviews and third-party information.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improve fulfillment and post-purchase reliability.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Make digital content understandable to both humans and machines.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maintain consistency across owned and third-party channels.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Develop GEO and machine-readable commerce strategies.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clearly distinguish advertising from organic recommendations.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The emergence of agentic commerce does not simply represent another change in the e-commerce interface. It is changing how products can be discovered and evaluated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI agents increasingly participate in shopping journeys, the brands that receive attention may depend not only on what consumers recognize, but also on what machines can understand, verify, and confidently recommend.</p>', 'AI Agents Are Reshaping Commerce and Brand Visibility', 'Explore how AI agents are transforming e-commerce, product discovery, brand visibility, trust, recommendations, and the future of agentic commerce.', 'AI agents, agentic commerce, AI in e-commerce, AI shopping, brand visibility, generative AI, GEO, generative engine optimization, AI recommendations, retail technology, digital commerce, AI marketing, machine-readable data, autonomous shopping, commerce automation', '2022-05-24 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-24 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 8, NULL, '2026-09-24 16:18:44', '2026-09-24 16:29:32', 1, NULL),
(914, 6, 2, 10, '80% of Data & Analytics Governance Initiatives Could Fail by 2027', '80percent-of-data-and-analytics-governance-initiatives-could-fail-by-2027', 'Gartner predicts 80% of D&A governance initiatives could fail by 2027 without business-focused priorities, while GenAI may accelerate value.', '[\"Data Governance\",\"Data Analytics\",\"D&A Governance\",\"AI Governance\",\"Generative AI\",\"GenAI\",\"Master Data Management\",\"Data Management\",\"Gartner\",\"Chief Data Analytics Officer\",\"Data Strategy\",\"Enterprise Data\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"AI Strategy\",\"Data Quality\"]', 'banner_image-1790687611420-236480257.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Gartner: 80% of Data &amp; Analytics Governance Initiatives Could Fail by 2027</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By 2027, as many as 80% of data and analytics (D&amp;A) governance initiatives could fail because organisations lack a clear business crisis, priority, or measurable outcome, according to Gartner.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gartner says organisations need to move away from governance programs that operate primarily as reactive, data-focused functions. Instead, D&amp;A governance should be closely connected to business priorities, opportunities, risks, and measurable outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Saul Judah, VP Analyst at Gartner, said governance programs are more likely to deliver value when they help organisations respond to important business challenges. Recent disruptions, including the COVID-19 pandemic and rising energy costs, demonstrated how data and analytics leaders could help organisations respond quickly when governance and analytics capabilities were aligned with business needs.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Beyond Traditional Data Governance</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many organisations continue to use a centralised, one-size-fits-all approach to data and analytics governance. Gartner says this model is increasingly unsuitable for organisations pursuing digital business initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than applying the same governance rules and processes across every situation, organisations need governance models that can adapt to different business requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A modern D&amp;A governance framework should be able to:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Support clearly defined business outcomes</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Respond to changing opportunities and risks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Scale as organisational requirements evolve</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Encourage innovation while maintaining appropriate controls</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Apply different governance approaches depending on business needs</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connect data and analytics decisions with broader organisational priorities</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gartner recommends that chief data and analytics officers (CDAOs) reconsider traditional command-and-control governance models and focus more strongly on business value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Judah said CDAOs should shift away from a centre-out approach and develop governance that is more agile, scalable, and responsive to business opportunities and risks.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">GenAI Could Accelerate D&amp;A Governance</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing adoption of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is also creating an opportunity for organisations to modernise their data and analytics governance practices.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI governance involves establishing organisational accountability, decision rights, policies, risk controls, and investment processes for the responsible use of artificial intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As organisations introduce more AI and GenAI applications, governance programs will need to address both traditional data requirements and the new risks and responsibilities associated with AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gartner predicts that by 2027, the use of GenAI could accelerate time to value for data and analytics governance and master data management programs by 40%.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Using GenAI to Improve Governance</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organisations can incorporate GenAI-enabled capabilities into their existing governance programs to improve productivity and make governance processes easier to adopt.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential applications include automating or accelerating activities such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data cataloguing</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data classification</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Governance documentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Self-service access to information</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer-data enrichment</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data discovery and understanding</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Business-specific data management activities</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Anurag Raj, Senior Principal Analyst at Gartner, said organisations should establish strong governance over their core information before expanding their GenAI use cases.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">GenAI can potentially help governance teams achieve faster results by reducing manual work, improving accessibility, and supporting specific business requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, AI-enabled capabilities could help organisations enrich customer data to improve targeting and support more effective marketing and customer-engagement initiatives.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Business-Centric Governance Strategy</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of D&amp;A governance is likely to require a more flexible approach rather than a single governance model applied across an entire organisation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For CDAOs and data leaders, the focus is shifting from simply governing data assets toward creating governance frameworks that support business outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organisations that connect governance with measurable priorities, manage emerging AI risks, and use GenAI to improve governance processes can create a more adaptable foundation for data-driven and AI-enabled initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As digital transformation and GenAI adoption continue to expand, D&amp;A governance will increasingly need to balance <strong>control, innovation, risk management, and business value</strong>.</p>', 'Gartner: 80% of Data Governance Initiatives May Fail by 2027', 'Gartner predicts 80% of D&A governance initiatives may fail by 2027, while GenAI could accelerate governance and master data value by 40%.', 'data governance, data analytics governance, D&A governance, Gartner data governance, AI governance, GenAI, generative AI, master data management, data management, data strategy, enterprise data governance, AI strategy', '2022-05-23 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-23 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-09-29 13:13:44', '2026-09-29 19:51:56', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(915, 6, 2, 17, 'Only 10% of Businesses Are Experience-Orchestrated, Exposing a Major Personalisation Gap', 'only-10percent-of-businesses-are-experience-orchestrated-exposing-a-major-personalisation-gap', 'Only 10% of businesses have reached advanced experience orchestration, exposing gaps in personalisation, data trust, and customer insights.', '[\"Experience Orchestration\",\"Personalisation\",\"Customer Experience\",\"Hyper-Personalisation\",\"Customer Data\",\"Data Privacy\",\"Identity Management\",\"AI\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Customer Data Management\",\"Data Security\",\"B2B Marketing\",\"MarTech\",\"Customer Insights\"]', 'banner_image-1790687775336-879517354.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Only 10% of Businesses Are Experience-Orchestrated, Exposing a Major Personalisation Gap</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses are under growing pressure to deliver highly relevant and personalised customer experiences. As products and services become increasingly similar across competitors, organisations are looking for new ways to differentiate themselves through customer value, data, and digital experiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Affinidi, a data and identity management company, highlighted these challenges in its report, <strong>“Unlocking Customer Value as an Experience-Orchestrated (X-O) Business,”</strong> an IDC InfoBrief commissioned by Affinidi. The findings draw on the IDC Affinidi DNB 2024 Survey, which examined digital-native businesses across Singapore, India, Australia and New Zealand (ANZ), and North America.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research highlights a significant gap between the growing demand for hyper-personalised experiences and the ability of businesses to use customer data effectively.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hyper-Personalisation Is Becoming a Business Priority</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses are increasingly recognising that traditional approaches to personalisation are no longer enough. Customers now expect companies to understand their preferences and deliver relevant interactions across multiple touchpoints.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the survey, <strong>58% of businesses are looking to differentiate themselves by prioritising hyper-personalised products and services</strong>. This represents a move beyond basic, field-level personalisation toward more proactive experiences that can respond to customer needs in real time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses in mature markets, including North America and ANZ, are already placing greater emphasis on hyper-personalisation as competition increases.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In comparison, organisations in Singapore and India continue to place greater emphasis on profitable growth, with personalisation ranking lower among their immediate priorities. However, as personalisation becomes increasingly connected to customer acquisition, retention, and long-term value, businesses across Asia are expected to place greater emphasis on it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">IDC forecasts that by 2027, <strong>30% of Asian organisations will make structural and technological changes to deliver value outcomes</strong>, moving beyond simply providing experiences toward creating greater value parity for customers.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Personalisation and Privacy Challenge</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although businesses recognise the importance of customer data, many still struggle to convert that data into meaningful, personalised experiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Only <strong>33% of surveyed businesses said they can anticipate customer needs and deliver highly personalised, value-focused services</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data collection and management remain major obstacles. Around <strong>56% of businesses identified data collection and management as a key challenge</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, customers are becoming increasingly conscious of how organisations collect, store, and use their personal information. Security incidents and data breaches have increased concerns around sharing personal data, creating a difficult balance for businesses.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customers want experiences that are relevant to them, but they also want greater control over the information they provide. Organisations therefore need to demonstrate transparency, security, and responsible data practices before customers are likely to share additional information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research also found that <strong>59% of surveyed businesses continue to face customer security concerns during the registration process</strong>, despite the presence of data protection regulations such as GDPR in Europe, India\'s Digital Personal Data Protection framework, and Singapore\'s PDPA.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data as the Foundation of an Experience-Orchestrated Business</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses seeking to make experience a competitive differentiator need to move toward an <strong>Experience-Orchestrated (X-O) business model</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An X-O business connects its people, processes, applications, systems, and data to create meaningful value exchanges between internal and external stakeholders.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data is central to this transformation. Businesses need to collect information responsibly, establish customer trust, and then make that data accessible across systems so it can generate useful insights and support better decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Several key pillars contribute to an X-O business model:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connected systems and data: Organisations need connections across people, applications, processes, systems, and data. A unified customer view can provide a foundation for analytics, insights, and cross-functional collaboration.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Outcome-focused culture: Businesses need to develop a culture that embraces AI while focusing on measurable customer and business outcomes rather than simply increasing technology adoption.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trusted intelligence: Data becomes valuable when it is interpreted within the right context. Building trust is therefore essential when organisations use AI and customer intelligence.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data-driven action: Businesses must actively listen to customers, employees, partners, and suppliers while combining relevant information to create actionable insights.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organisations should assess their maturity across these areas, identify gaps in their existing customer experiences, and connect improvements to measurable business actions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Only 10% Have Reached the Most Advanced X-O Stage</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing adoption of AI is raising customer expectations even further. Businesses are now expected to respond quickly to changing preferences while providing relevant and seamless experiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the survey found that <strong>only 10% of businesses across India, Singapore, ANZ, and North America had reached the most advanced stage of their journey toward becoming an X-O business</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the primary barriers is the absence of a unified customer view. Customer information is frequently distributed across multiple applications and data repositories, making it difficult for businesses to develop a complete understanding of their customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key challenges include:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Data Fragmentation:</strong><br>Customer information often exists across disconnected systems and applications. This fragmentation makes it harder to create consistent customer profiles and deliver personalised experiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Changing Privacy Requirements:</strong><br>Businesses operating across multiple regions must navigate different data privacy requirements. Increasing regulatory complexity adds additional challenges to data collection, storage, processing, and sharing.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Scalability and Data Management:</strong><br>As organisations grow, maintaining accurate and secure customer data becomes increasingly difficult. Limited access to real-time information and concerns about data sharing can further restrict the ability to scale personalised experiences.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Holistic Identity Can Support X-O Transformation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Addressing these challenges requires businesses to rethink how they collect, manage, protect, and use customer identity and data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Holistic identity management can provide organisations with a privacy-focused approach to identity verification, onboarding, communication, consent management, and customer data management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Affinidi\'s Holistic Identity framework is positioned as an approach for connecting customer information across multiple platforms while supporting privacy and security requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Solutions focused on consent management, identity resolution, secure communication, and authentication can help organisations create more consistent customer experiences while giving individuals greater control over their personal information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By connecting previously siloed data sources, businesses can generate more complete customer insights and use those insights to improve personalisation, operational efficiency, and customer engagement.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Toward Customer Value</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research demonstrates that personalisation is increasingly becoming an important component of customer experience strategy. However, achieving meaningful personalisation requires more than collecting larger amounts of customer data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses need connected systems, trusted data, effective identity management, transparent privacy practices, and the ability to convert information into actionable insights.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As customer expectations continue to evolve alongside AI and digital technologies, organisations that pursue an experience-orchestrated approach will need to balance personalisation with privacy, security, and customer control.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The transition to X-O is therefore closely connected to how effectively businesses can turn fragmented customer information into trusted, actionable intelligence while creating experiences that deliver measurable value.</p>', 'Experience-Orchestrated Businesses: Personalisation Gap', 'Only 10% of businesses are advanced in experience orchestration. Explore personalisation, customer data, privacy, and identity management challenges.', 'experience orchestration, X-O business, hyper-personalisation, customer experience, customer data, data privacy, identity management, AI, digital transformation, customer personalisation, customer insights, MarTech', '2022-05-22 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-22 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 6, NULL, '2026-09-29 13:16:15', '2026-09-29 19:51:59', 1, NULL),
(916, 6, 2, 9, 'Zoho Analytics Expands Self-Service BI with AI, Machine Learning and Advanced Data Capabilities', 'zoho-analytics-expands-self-service-bi-with-ai-machine-learning-and-advanced-data-capabilities', 'Zoho Analytics launches an AI-powered self-service BI platform with predictive insights, ML tools, OpenAI integration, and 100+ new enhancements.', '[\"Zoho Analytics\",\"Business Intelligence\",\"Self-Service BI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Generative AI\",\"Machine Learning\",\"Data Analytics\",\"Predictive Analytics\",\"Zia\",\"OpenAI\",\"Data Management\",\"ETL\",\"AutoML\",\"Business Analytics\",\"BI Platform\"]', 'banner_image-1790687901041-145687910.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho Analytics Expands Self-Service BI with AI, Machine Learning and Advanced Data Capabilities</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho has introduced an enhanced version of <strong>Zoho Analytics</strong>, its self-service business intelligence and analytics platform, with more than 100 new enhancements designed to make data analysis more intelligent, automated, and accessible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The latest release brings expanded artificial intelligence and machine learning capabilities, including diagnostic insights, predictive analytics, automated reports, and AI-generated dashboards. The platform also introduces a dedicated machine learning model-building environment, OpenAI integration, more than 25 additional data connectors, and new extensions for third-party business intelligence platforms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Zoho, the latest release combines several years of investments in automation, low-code and no-code development, machine learning, third-party integrations, and its in-house AI engine, Zia.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“Zoho Analytics was launched in 2009 as Zoho Reports, long before technology had caught up to Zoho’s vision for business intelligence,” said Clarence Rozario, Director of Product Management, Zoho BI &amp; Analytics Platform. He explained that the company has continued investing in automation, low-code and no-code technologies, machine learning, integrations, and Zia to expand the platform\'s capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho said its BI platform, developed in India, is used by approximately 3 million users worldwide. India has become a significant market for Zoho Analytics, with the company reporting 67% growth in new revenue for the product and identifying India as its second-largest market. The growth has been particularly driven by mid-sized and large organizations in industries such as IT-enabled services, financial services, manufacturing, and retail.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Addressing Modern Data Challenges</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Businesses are dealing with rapidly increasing data volumes and formats while also facing greater requirements for data governance and more sophisticated analytical needs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">To address these challenges, Zoho Analytics has expanded its capabilities across four major areas:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data management</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data science and machine learning</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Platform extensibility</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These enhancements are designed to help organizations manage data more effectively, generate insights faster, and build customized analytics workflows.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanded Data Management Capabilities</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The updated Zoho Analytics introduces a broader data management environment aimed at helping organizations improve data quality, governance, and accessibility.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform already supports more than 500 data connectors and now adds Stream Analytics along with more than 25 additional connectors. Stream Analytics allows organizations to work with real-time data streams, helping users analyze information as it becomes available.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho Analytics also introduces enhanced ETL capabilities that allow business users to create and manage data pipelines directly within the platform. Through its visual pipeline builder, users can develop end-to-end data workflows without relying entirely on traditional development processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Developers can further customize these workflows using the platform\'s Python Code Studio. The environment supports custom transformations and machine learning models, allowing organizations to incorporate specialized processing and analytical logic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another addition is enhanced natural-language interaction through <strong>Ask Zia</strong>, Zoho\'s AI assistant. Users can interact with data using natural-language queries instead of relying exclusively on traditional analytical interfaces.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform also introduces automatic versioning and a new Sandbox environment to support safer data management and development workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho Analytics can additionally work with Zoho Flow to help organizations orchestrate data pipelines and workflows from a centralized environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A new <strong>Unified Metrics Layer</strong> provides a centralized way to define, standardize, monitor, control access to, and catalog business metrics. Zoho is also extending this capability into a Headless BI model, allowing data applications to consume standardized metrics in real time.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generative AI Integrated into Business Intelligence</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is a major component of the new Zoho Analytics release.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform now includes expanded Generative AI capabilities intended to help different types of users discover and understand business insights more quickly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the major additions is <strong>Diagnostic Analysis</strong>. Zoho\'s Zia Insights engine can automatically provide contextual diagnostic information, helping users investigate the factors behind changes in business metrics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ask Zia</strong> has also received significant enhancements. The natural-language analytics assistant supports multilingual queries and can now be used for additional actions, including creating customized data models.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ask Zia can also be accessed through collaboration environments such as Microsoft Teams, enabling users to interact with business data and receive contextual insights without leaving their communication environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another new capability is <strong>Auto Analysis</strong>, which can automatically generate metrics, reports, and dashboards using AI-based analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho Analytics has also added integration with OpenAI. The integration uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to provide more contextually relevant responses when working with business data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Through Bring Your Own Key (BYOK) support for OpenAI APIs, users can connect their own OpenAI credentials. The platform can also assist users in discovering public datasets and generating formulas and SQL queries.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these capabilities are designed to make analytics more conversational, automated, and accessible to a wider range of business users.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data Science and Machine Learning Studio</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The updated platform expands beyond traditional business intelligence by providing tools for organizations that want to build their own machine learning models.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new <strong>Data Science and Machine Learning (DSML) Studio</strong> includes an AutoML capability that allows users to create custom machine learning models with limited coding requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Users can perform activities such as feature engineering, hyperparameter tuning, model training, testing, comparison, deployment, and management within the analytics environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho has also introduced <strong>Code Studio</strong>, an integrated Python development environment. Developers can use it to create custom machine learning models or bring existing Python models and external libraries into Zoho Analytics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This gives organizations greater flexibility to combine standard BI capabilities with customized data science workflows.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Greater Platform Extensibility</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Zoho Analytics has also expanded its platform extensibility through capabilities such as a no-code data connector builder, an actions framework, BI fabric, and client-side software development kits.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new <strong>BI Fabric</strong> allows organizations to bring insights from multiple BI platforms into a centralized analytics portal. Platforms such as Power BI and Tableau can be incorporated into the portal, giving users a unified location for discovering and accessing analytics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can apply granular access controls to manage who can access different analytical resources.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform\'s actions framework also allows users to trigger workflows directly from analytics, including URL and webhook-based actions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Integration with <strong>Zoho Flow</strong> provides access to more than 500 application triggers, allowing analytics workflows to connect with other business applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The no-code data connector builder further expands integration options by allowing organizations to create custom connectors for applications that are not covered by existing connectors. Partners can also develop connectors for publication and distribution through Zoho Marketplace.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">More Than 100 Platform Enhancements</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Alongside the major AI, machine learning, data management, and extensibility improvements, Zoho Analytics includes more than 100 additional updates.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These include new visualization options, improvements to dashboard creation, enhanced auditing and administration controls, redesigned mobile applications, and Right-to-Left (RTL) language support.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With these changes, Zoho Analytics is expanding from a traditional self-service BI platform toward a broader environment that combines business intelligence, artificial intelligence, machine learning, data management, automation, and third-party integrations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The expanded capabilities are intended to support organizations that need to connect more data sources, automate analytical processes, build custom models, and provide business users with faster access to actionable insights.</p>', 'Zoho Analytics AI-Powered Self-Service BI', 'Explore Zoho Analytics\' latest AI-powered BI updates, including machine learning, predictive analytics, OpenAI integration, data connectors, and automation.', 'Zoho Analytics, AI-powered BI, self-service BI, business intelligence, artificial intelligence, machine learning, predictive analytics, OpenAI, Zia, data analytics, AutoML, ETL, data management, BI platform', '2022-05-23 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-23 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-09-29 13:18:21', '2026-09-29 19:51:54', 1, NULL),
(917, 6, 2, 10, 'Qlik Cloud Analytics Delivers 209% ROI, Forrester TEI Study Finds', 'qlik-cloud-analytics-delivers-209percent-roi-forrester-tei-study-finds', 'A Forrester TEI study commissioned by Qlik finds Qlik Cloud Analytics can deliver 209% ROI through faster analysis, automation, and cloud savings.', '[\"Qlik Cloud Analytics\",\"Qlik\",\"Cloud Analytics\",\"Business Intelligence\",\"BI\",\"Data Analytics\",\"Data Management\",\"Data Visualization\",\"AI Analytics\",\"Enterprise Analytics\",\"Cloud Computing\",\"Data Preparation\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"ROI\",\"Total Economic Impact\",\"Forrester Consulting\",\"Legacy Systems\",\"Data Automation\"]', 'banner_image-1790688070670-697548603.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Qlik Cloud Analytics Delivers 209% ROI, Forrester TEI Study Finds</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As enterprises continue to generate, collect, and analyze growing volumes of data, many organizations are still working with legacy and on-premises business intelligence environments. These systems can make it difficult to bring together information from different sources, reduce manual workloads, and turn data into timely business decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A recently published <strong>Total Economic Impact™ (TEI) study commissioned by Qlik and conducted by Forrester Consulting</strong> examined the potential economic benefits organizations can achieve by adopting Qlik Cloud Analytics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The study highlights how cloud-based analytics can help businesses address challenges associated with fragmented data, manual data preparation, and the ongoing cost of maintaining legacy analytics infrastructure.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Siloed Data Into Actionable Insights</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations often maintain data across multiple systems and departments, making it difficult for teams to obtain a complete view of business performance. Traditional BI environments can also require significant manual effort to prepare, organize, and analyze information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qlik Cloud Analytics provides a centralized cloud environment that can help organizations bring previously siloed data together. By making information more accessible, teams can spend less time locating and preparing data and more time using insights to support business decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform also incorporates AI capabilities to help organizations explore data and identify actionable insights as data volumes continue to increase.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Findings From the Study</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Forrester TEI study identified several potential financial and operational benefits associated with Qlik Cloud Analytics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>$620,000 in Additional Revenue</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The study identified approximately <strong>$620,000 in risk-adjusted additional revenue over three years</strong>. Improved access to data can help organizations make decisions more quickly and reduce the delays associated with manual analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By bringing previously isolated data into a more centralized analytics environment, businesses can identify information faster and use it to support operational and strategic decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>35% Less Time Spent on Data Analysis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations in the study achieved an estimated <strong>35% reduction in time spent on data analysis</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qlik Cloud Analytics combines visualization, data aggregation, and data organization capabilities to help analysts investigate information more efficiently. Its associative technology can also allow users to explore relationships within data without being limited to traditional SQL-based approaches.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can help data teams spend more time generating insights and less time performing repetitive analysis activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>50% Time Savings on Data Preparation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data preparation can consume a significant amount of an analyst\'s working time, particularly when information must be manually collected and processed from multiple systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the study, organizations achieved <strong>50% time savings on data preparation</strong>, representing an estimated <strong>$2.5 million in value</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automation of activities such as data aggregation, reporting, dashboard preparation, and financial reporting contributed to these efficiency gains.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>100% Legacy System Savings</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving analytics workloads to the cloud can also reduce dependence on older infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The study reported <strong>100% savings associated with legacy systems</strong>, contributing to approximately <strong>$2.1 million in total savings over three years</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By transitioning data analysis and processing activities to Qlik Cloud Analytics, organizations can progressively retire legacy systems and reduce expenses associated with maintaining older environments.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting Modern Data and AI Strategies</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growing adoption of AI is increasing the importance of having reliable, accessible, and well-organized data. Organizations looking to use AI effectively must be able to connect data from different sources and make that information available for analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qlik Cloud Analytics combines cloud analytics capabilities with AI-oriented functionality to help organizations work with expanding data volumes while supporting a range of analytical use cases.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform can support organizations across descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, and prescriptive analytics scenarios, helping teams move from understanding historical performance toward identifying potential actions and opportunities.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conclusion</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Forrester Consulting TEI study commissioned by Qlik illustrates the potential economic impact of moving analytics workloads to the cloud. The findings highlight improvements in data analysis, data preparation, decision-making, and legacy infrastructure costs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With reported benefits including <strong>209% ROI, $2.1 million in three-year savings, 50% less time spent on data preparation, and 35% faster data analysis</strong>, Qlik Cloud Analytics demonstrates how modern cloud analytics can help organizations reduce manual workloads and extract greater value from their data.</p>', 'Qlik Cloud Analytics: 209% ROI | Forrester TEI Study', 'Discover how Qlik Cloud Analytics delivered 209% ROI, with major savings in data preparation, analysis, and legacy infrastructure costs.', 'Qlik Cloud Analytics, Qlik ROI, 209% ROI, Forrester TEI study, Total Economic Impact, cloud analytics, business intelligence, data analytics, data preparation, enterprise analytics, AI analytics, cloud BI, data visualization, legacy system savings', '2022-05-23 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-23 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-09-29 13:21:11', '2026-09-29 19:51:55', 1, NULL),
(918, 6, 1, 17, 'Gong Surpasses $300M ARR as AI Revenue Platform Expands Beyond the Technology Sector', 'gong-surpasses-dollar300m-arr-as-ai-revenue-platform-expands-beyond-the-technology-sector', 'Gong surpasses $300M ARR as AI adoption accelerates, with customer growth expanding across financial services, healthcare, media and manufacturing.', '[\"Gong\",\"AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Revenue Intelligence\",\"Sales AI\",\"Generative AI\",\"SaaS\",\"ARR\",\"Sales Technology\",\"Revenue Operations\",\"Business Growth\",\"Enterprise AI\",\"Sales Automation\",\"MarTech\"]', 'banner_image-1790878174504-22113300.jpeg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gong, the AI-powered revenue intelligence company previously valued at more than $7 billion, has surpassed $300 million in annual recurring revenue (ARR), according to results from its fiscal 2024 year, which ended January 31, 2025.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company says its growth has been driven by rising demand for AI-powered solutions that help revenue teams improve productivity, understand customer interactions and make more informed business decisions. Since its founding in 2016, Gong has focused on applying artificial intelligence and data intelligence to revenue-related workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gong reported that usage of its AI capabilities increased by nearly 50% during the past 12 months. One of the company\'s widely used AI offerings, Ask Anything, allows revenue professionals to obtain insights from calls, deals and contacts. The generative AI solution experienced approximately 400% year-over-year growth, highlighting increased adoption of AI tools for analyzing revenue data and customer interactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company\'s newer products, Gong Engage and Gong Forecast, have also recorded significant growth. Both solutions are built on Gong\'s AI technology and Data Engine and are designed to help organizations improve sales engagement, forecasting and revenue management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gong also reported increasing adoption across its product portfolio. Approximately one in four customers now uses multiple Gong solutions, indicating broader adoption of the platform across revenue-related operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">\"Over the past year, we\'ve seen rising market adoption as revenue teams progressively rely on AI - not just for better insights, but for automating actions and workflows that drive greater productivity, business growth and forecasting accuracy,\" said Amit Bendov, CEO of Gong, in a company statement.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gong Expands Into New Industry Segments</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gong\'s customer growth is also extending beyond the technology industry. The company says organizations in media, financial services, healthcare and manufacturing are increasingly adopting AI-powered revenue solutions as businesses look for measurable improvements in sales performance and operational efficiency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The expansion reflects a broader use of AI across revenue organizations. Gong\'s technology is being adopted not only by sales teams but also by revenue operations, sales enablement, customer success, support and professional services teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Gong, customers have reported measurable business improvements after implementing its AI-powered solutions. SpotOn and Frontify, for example, have reported double-digit improvements in areas including revenue, win rates and deal sizes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other customer examples highlight the potential impact of AI-enabled revenue management. Canva reported a 60% increase in representative capacity along with 6% revenue growth, while Paycor reported a 141% increase in deal wins after using Gong\'s AI-powered pipeline management capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company also points to broader market demand for enterprise AI applications that deliver measurable business outcomes rather than focusing solely on foundational AI technologies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">\"Gong\'s momentum reflects the current market transformation,\" said Sonya Huang, General Partner at Sequoia, in a statement. \"Gong is reshaping the future of revenue with AI, transforming how companies manage customers and deliver results at every stage of their business.\"</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With ARR now exceeding $300 million and adoption expanding across industries and business functions, Gong is positioning its AI platform as a broader enterprise revenue technology solution. The company\'s continued product expansion reflects the growing demand for AI tools that can support sales execution, forecasting, customer engagement and other revenue-generating activities.</p>', 'Gong Surpasses $300M ARR as AI Revenue Growth Expands', 'Gong surpasses $300M ARR as AI adoption grows across sales, healthcare, finance, media and manufacturing, expanding its enterprise revenue platform.', 'Gong, Gong AI, $300M ARR, annual recurring revenue, AI revenue platform, revenue intelligence, sales AI, generative AI, Gong Engage, Gong Forecast, Ask Anything, enterprise AI, sales automation, revenue operations, MarTech', '2022-06-01 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-01 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 3, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:09:48', '2026-10-01 18:59:14', 1, NULL),
(919, 6, 2, 17, 'Marketing Data Governance Emerges as a Key Driver of AI Readiness and Revenue Growth', 'marketing-data-governance-emerges-as-a-key-driver-of-ai-readiness-and-revenue-growth', 'New research links stronger marketing data governance with faster lead delivery, higher sales acceptance, AI readiness and stronger revenue growth.', '[\"Marketing Data Governance\",\"AI Readiness\",\"Marketing Data\",\"Revenue Growth\",\"Lead Management\",\"Data Quality\",\"AI Governance\",\"Demand Generation\",\"CRM\",\"B2B Marketing\"]', 'banner_image-1790878345363-527746224.jpeg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing Data Governance Emerges as a Key Driver of AI Readiness and Revenue Growth</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">New research from Integrate and Demand Metric highlights a strong connection between marketing data governance, AI readiness and business growth. The findings show that high-growth organizations are more likely to have structured, governed and accessible marketing data, giving them a stronger foundation for adopting and managing AI.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-growth organizations are more likely to have AI-ready marketing data supported by standardized intake, automated validation and formal governance processes.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Stronger governance practices are associated with faster lead delivery and higher sales acceptance rates.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-growth organizations report substantially greater readiness for AI oversight, including frameworks covering bias, fairness and explainability.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automating data validation before CRM ingestion can help organizations improve data quality and reduce downstream cleanup.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research suggests that technology adoption alone does not determine growth; how organizations manage and operationalize their data plays an important role.</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-Growth Organizations Show Stronger AI Readiness</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>2026 State of the Marketing Data Governance Gap</strong> study from Integrate and Demand Metric identified a notable difference in data governance practices between high-growth and lower-growth organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the study, high-growth organizations are more than twice as likely to report that at least 75% of their marketing data is AI-ready. In the research, AI-ready data is defined as data that is clean, governed and accessible.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The study reports that <strong>24% of high-growth organizations</strong> say 75% or more of their marketing data meets this definition, compared with <strong>10% of lower-growth organizations</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This difference suggests that organizations investing in data quality and governance are also better positioned to support AI-driven marketing initiatives.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Are Organizations Preparing for AI Bias and Fairness?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI readiness extends beyond clean and accessible data. Organizations also need processes for managing issues such as bias, fairness and explainability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research found that high-growth organizations are nearly three times as likely to strongly agree that they have formal frameworks addressing AI bias, fairness and explainability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The figures show <strong>29% of high-growth organizations</strong> strongly agree that such frameworks are in place, compared with <strong>10% of lower-growth organizations</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This indicates that AI governance is becoming increasingly connected to broader marketing data management and accountability practices.</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“The high-growth companies in this report didn’t get there by buying more AI,” said Mehul Nagrani, CEO of Integrate, in a statement. “They built the data governance that makes AI worth buying.”</p></blockquote><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Does More Marketing Technology Automatically Drive Growth?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The study indicates that simply adopting more technology does not appear to distinguish high-growth organizations from lower-growth organizations. Instead, the way those technologies are governed and integrated into business processes appears to be an important difference.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, high-growth organizations are nearly four times as likely to strongly enforce standardized vendor intake requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research also found that <strong>45% of high-growth organizations</strong> report real-time or near-real-time lead delivery, compared with <strong>17% of lower-growth organizations</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sales acceptance shows another significant difference. <strong>31% of high-growth organizations</strong> report sales lead acceptance rates of 80% or higher, compared with <strong>9% of lower-growth organizations</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These findings highlight the importance of connecting marketing operations, data quality and sales processes rather than treating them as separate functions.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Data Governance Findings</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Additional findings from the study include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-growth organizations are nearly four times as likely to rate their overall data governance maturity as Advanced or Leading.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">79% of high-growth organizations automate lead validation before CRM ingestion, compared with 44% of lower-growth organizations.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-growth organizations are more than three times as likely to report sales lead acceptance rates of 80% or higher.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-growth organizations report stronger practices around standardized data intake and lead management.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Faster lead delivery and stronger validation processes are associated with higher levels of sales acceptance.</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Is the Marketing Data Governance Gap?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The study describes a <strong>Governance Gap</strong> between organizations that treat data governance as an upstream operational discipline and those that depend heavily on manual intervention and downstream data cleanup.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations with mature governance processes can establish data-quality controls earlier in the marketing and lead-management lifecycle.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can include:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Standardizing vendor and data intake requirements.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Automating validation before data enters CRM or marketing automation systems.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving lead delivery speed.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Establishing clear ownership for marketing data.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Creating formal accountability for AI-related data and governance.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitoring data quality continuously rather than correcting issues only after they affect downstream teams.</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Taking these steps can help marketing and revenue teams build more consistent processes around the data used for reporting, lead management and AI applications.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Data Governance Matters for Revenue Teams</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing data governance is increasingly connected to revenue operations. Poor-quality or inconsistent data can create delays, increase manual work and make it more difficult for sales teams to act on marketing-generated leads.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Integrate and Demand Metric research suggests that organizations reporting stronger governance practices also report faster lead delivery, higher sales acceptance and greater AI readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">John Follett, co-founder of Demand Metric, said the research places data governance more directly within the revenue conversation. He noted that high-growth organizations report stronger governance practices alongside higher sales lead acceptance and greater AI readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For marketing leaders, the broader takeaway is that data quality and accountability can be built into marketing processes from the beginning instead of relying primarily on downstream corrections.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Stronger Foundation for AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As organizations expand their use of AI across marketing and revenue operations, data governance becomes an important part of the underlying infrastructure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clean, accessible and properly governed data can provide a stronger foundation for AI applications, while formal governance frameworks can help organizations address accountability, fairness and explainability.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The findings from the 2026 study provide a useful view into how high-growth organizations approach marketing data governance and how those practices correspond with lead-management performance and AI readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Access the full 2026 State of the Marketing Data Governance Gap report for the complete research findings and recommendations.</strong></p>', 'Marketing Data Governance, AI Readiness & Revenue Growth', 'Explore how marketing data governance connects with AI readiness, faster lead delivery, sales acceptance and revenue growth in new research.', 'marketing data governance, AI readiness, marketing data, AI governance, revenue growth, lead management, data quality, demand generation, CRM data, sales acceptance', '2022-06-01 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-01 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 6, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:12:26', '2026-10-01 18:59:11', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(920, 6, 1, 17, 'Intentsify Launches QuantumDemand to Activate B2B Buying Groups at Scale', 'intentsify-launches-quantumdemand-to-activate-b2b-buying-groups-at-scale', 'Intentsify’s QuantumDemand helps B2B teams identify buying groups, unify intent signals, and measure account-level purchase readiness.', '[\"Intentsify\",\"QuantumDemand\",\"B2B Marketing\",\"Buying Groups\",\"Demand Generation\",\"Account-Based Marketing\",\"ABM\",\"Buyer Intent\",\"Marketing Technology\",\"MarTech\",\"Enterprise Marketing\",\"Revenue Marketing\",\"Marketing Qualified Accounts\",\"MQA\",\"AI Marketing\",\"Purchase Intent\"]', 'banner_image-1790878485326-673057539.jpeg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intentsify has launched QuantumDemand, an AI-powered solution designed to help enterprise revenue teams understand buying groups through a combination of buyer identity, intent, and engagement signals.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform shifts attention from individual campaign metrics toward buying group progression and account-level purchase readiness.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">QuantumDemand provides marketing and sales teams with a consolidated view of who is involved in a buying decision, what they are evaluating, how they are engaging, and where they are within the purchase journey.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its Quantum Index is designed to provide a shared measurement of buying group readiness and help teams identify accounts showing stronger signals of potential pipeline development.</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intentsify Introduces QuantumDemand for Enterprise Buying Group Activation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intentsify has introduced <strong>QuantumDemand</strong>, an AI-powered solution designed to give enterprise marketing and sales teams a broader understanding of buying groups and their purchase journeys.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new offering combines buyer identity, intent, and engagement signals to help organizations understand the different stakeholders involved in an enterprise purchase. Rather than evaluating engagement through individual campaigns or isolated marketing activities, QuantumDemand focuses on how interactions across a buying group contribute to account-level purchase progression.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Enterprise purchasing has become increasingly complex. Buying decisions often involve larger and more diverse committees, while evaluation cycles can extend across multiple stages. At the same time, AI is changing how organizations research vendors, compare solutions, and make purchasing decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Intentsify, many go-to-market platforms continue to focus on individual metrics such as clicks, impressions, campaigns, channels, and MQLs. QuantumDemand is intended to provide additional context around how multiple buyer interactions collectively influence a buying group\'s movement toward a purchase decision.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Is QuantumDemand?</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">QuantumDemand is designed to provide enterprise GTM teams with a unified view of buying activity. The platform brings together information about <strong>who is involved in a purchase, what solutions they are considering, how they are engaging, and how their buying group is developing</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marc Laplante, CEO and co-founder of Intentsify, said that traditional B2B marketing has historically concentrated on metrics such as leads, clicks, impressions, and MQLs because these signals are relatively easy to measure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, enterprise purchasing decisions typically involve multiple stakeholders rather than a single individual. QuantumDemand is positioned as a way to provide additional context around these group-level buying decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measuring Buying Group Readiness</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">B2B organizations have traditionally used fragmented intent signals to estimate whether an account is preparing to make a purchase. QuantumDemand aims to connect these signals to provide a more comprehensive picture of buying activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The solution focuses on <strong>buying context</strong> by combining information about buyer activity, solution-level interests, engagement patterns, and the development of buying groups.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of treating every engagement as an independent indication of intent, QuantumDemand is designed to identify patterns that indicate whether an account is progressing toward a potential purchase and which stakeholders are contributing to that movement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform continuously identifies, engages, and measures buying groups across an organization\'s target market. As different members of a purchasing committee interact with content and evaluate solutions, the system is designed to build an evolving picture of account-level purchase readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can use QuantumDemand to:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify solution-specific buying groups.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Engage multiple stakeholders across channels used by their target buyers.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measure buying group progression rather than relying only on individual campaign activity.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify and prioritize accounts showing stronger signals of potential pipeline development.</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quantum Index Provides a Shared View of Purchase Readiness</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A key component of QuantumDemand is the <strong>Quantum Index</strong>, which Intentsify describes as a proprietary measurement of buying group readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Quantum Index uses Intentsify\'s data foundation, including its <strong>Identity Graph</strong> for resolving buyer identities and its <strong>Knowledge Graph</strong> for connecting buyers, solutions, behaviors, and intent signals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is designed to help marketing and sales teams understand where buying groups are forming, how they are progressing, and which accounts are demonstrating signals associated with potential pipeline development.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform also shifts the focus from individual <strong>Marketing Qualified Leads (MQLs)</strong> toward <strong>Marketing Qualified Accounts (MQAs)</strong>, where account-level engagement from multiple members of a buying committee can provide additional context.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting Modern Revenue Teams</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intentsify positions QuantumDemand as an evolution of its existing data capabilities, bringing buyer identity, buying group behavior, and solution-specific intent together into a single contextual framework.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For enterprise revenue teams, this approach is intended to create a common view between marketing and sales by connecting buyer-level activity with broader account-level purchasing behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">QuantumDemand is <strong>available immediately to enterprise organizations</strong>, according to Intentsify.</p>', 'Intentsify Launches QuantumDemand for B2B Buying Groups', 'Intentsify launches QuantumDemand, an AI-powered platform that helps B2B teams identify buying groups and measure account-level purchase readiness.', 'Intentsify QuantumDemand, B2B buying groups, buying group activation, B2B intent data, buyer intent, demand generation, account based marketing, ABM, MarTech, AI marketing, enterprise marketing, marketing qualified accounts, MQA, purchase readiness, revenue marketing', '2022-06-02 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-02 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:14:45', '2026-10-01 18:59:09', 1, NULL),
(921, 6, 2, 17, 'AI in Marketo: How CMOs and RevOps Leaders Can Prepare for the Next Era of Marketing Automation', 'ai-in-marketo-how-cmos-and-revops-leaders-can-prepare-for-the-next-era-of-marketing-automation', 'Explore how AI in Marketo can improve campaign execution, data quality, QA, personalization, governance, and revenue-focused marketing operations.', '[\"AI in Marketo\",\"Marketo\",\"Adobe Marketo Engage\",\"Marketing Automation\",\"MarTech\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"CMO\",\"RevOps\",\"Marketing Operations\",\"Demand Generation\",\"Lead Management\",\"Data Quality\",\"Campaign Automation\",\"AI Marketing\"]', 'banner_image-1790878649023-711160453.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI in Marketo: How CMOs and RevOps Leaders Can Prepare for the Next Era of Marketing Automation</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence is rapidly becoming part of the systems marketing teams already use every day. Instead of operating as a separate technology layer, AI is increasingly being integrated into platforms that support campaign execution, audience management, content creation, lead qualification, data management, and performance measurement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For teams using Marketo, this evolution is becoming particularly relevant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI in Marketo is not simply about adding intelligent features to an established marketing automation platform. It represents a broader shift in how marketing organizations approach campaign operations, quality assurance, data governance, personalization, and the relationship between technology and human expertise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For CMOs and RevOps leaders, the larger opportunity is not simply to make campaigns faster.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is to create a marketing engine that can operate with greater consistency, scalability, and connection to revenue.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI in Marketo Goes Beyond Faster Campaign Execution</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consider a busy professional kitchen.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The kitchen has experienced chefs, documented recipes, organized workstations, quality standards, and established processes. Now imagine adding intelligent tools that can prepare ingredients, identify missing items, recommend improvements, and check whether a dish meets specific standards before it reaches the customer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Those tools can significantly improve efficiency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But they cannot compensate for an organization that has no consistent recipes, poorly labeled ingredients, unclear responsibilities, or different definitions of quality across teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing organizations face a similar situation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can accelerate marketing operations, but the results depend heavily on the foundation underneath it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Adobe has been expanding AI capabilities around Marketo, including AI-assisted functionality intended to support tasks such as program validation, Smart List creation, program and Smart Campaign creation, lead-list processes, and asset validation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These capabilities can reduce repetitive operational work. However, their value becomes much greater when marketing teams already have clear processes, naming conventions, data standards, QA procedures, and governance frameworks.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The teams that benefit most from AI are therefore not necessarily the teams that adopt every new AI capability first.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They are the teams that understand how their marketing operation works.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They know how programs should be structured. They understand what clean data looks like. They know which activities can safely be automated. They understand where human review is essential.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>AI can amplify operational maturity.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">For CMOs, AI Changes the Marketing Capacity Conversation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For many CMOs, the immediate appeal of AI is increased productivity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But productivity alone should not be the final objective.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A more important question is:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>What should the marketing organization do with the additional capacity created by AI?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If AI reduces the amount of time spent checking programs, preparing lists, creating content variations, reviewing assets, or assembling repetitive campaign components, marketers can potentially redirect that time toward higher-value activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That could include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Campaign strategy</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Audience planning</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Personalization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lifecycle optimization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experimentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer journey design</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Revenue analysis</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sales alignment</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Performance optimization</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The transition will not happen automatically.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Without a clear operating model, AI can simply become another collection of tools inside an already complicated MarTech environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That can increase activity without necessarily increasing business impact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">CMOs therefore need to establish the strategic purpose behind AI adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why are we using AI?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which processes should AI support?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where should human judgment remain mandatory?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What risks need to be controlled?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How will AI improve customer experiences?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How will AI contribute to revenue performance?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is not to make marketers faster at operating software.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The objective is to help the organization create better customer experiences and stronger revenue outcomes at scale.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Makes Marketing Governance More Important</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is easy to assume that automation and AI reduce the need for governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In practice, the opposite can be true.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The more automated a marketing operation becomes, the more important standards and controls become.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted validation can help identify issues before campaigns are launched. Asset validation can potentially help teams identify problems involving links, tokens, configurations, or other campaign elements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, AI still needs a definition of what constitutes an acceptable result.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an AI system may identify whether a program contains a particular element. But the organization still needs to establish whether that element is appropriate for the campaign.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Similarly:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can identify potential data-quality issues, but teams need data standards.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can generate content, but teams need brand and compliance guidelines.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can assist with campaign logic, but teams need lifecycle rules.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can review assets, but humans still need to evaluate context.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can suggest changes, but organizations need approval processes.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates an important shift in perspective.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>AI does not eliminate standards. It increases the value of having clear standards.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connecting Marketo AI to Business Outcomes</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI adoption should not exist as an isolated technology initiative.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For CMOs and RevOps leaders, AI should be connected to measurable business outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of asking only whether the organization is using AI, leadership should examine questions such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are campaigns being launched more efficiently?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are QA problems being identified earlier?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is data quality improving?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is personalization becoming easier to scale?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are marketing and sales handoffs becoming more consistent?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are operational bottlenecks decreasing?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are marketers spending more time on strategic activities?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is marketing contributing more effectively to pipeline?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These questions create a more useful framework for measuring AI adoption.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketo is fundamentally part of a larger marketing and revenue ecosystem. AI should therefore be evaluated based on how effectively it strengthens that ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is not AI adoption for its own sake.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is a more effective marketing operation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI in Marketo Is Also a Data Quality Opportunity</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data quality is one of the most important foundations of marketing automation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Poor data can affect nearly every stage of the customer journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It can influence:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Segmentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Personalization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lead scoring</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lead routing</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Campaign targeting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reporting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Attribution</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sales follow-up</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted lead management and import processes can help organizations identify inconsistencies, review field mappings, identify duplicates, and standardize records more efficiently.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But technology alone cannot solve a data governance problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If an organization has never agreed on which values are acceptable, which fields are mandatory, how duplicates should be handled, or how records should move through the lifecycle, AI cannot independently establish the organization\'s business rules.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is why <strong>AI readiness and data readiness are closely connected</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A poorly structured Marketo environment may use AI to discover problems faster.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A well-governed Marketo environment can potentially use AI to scale operations with greater consistency.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That distinction is important.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Is Changing Marketing Content Operations</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Campaign operations are only one part of the transformation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI is also influencing how marketing teams create and manage content.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted capabilities can support activities such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Email drafting</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Subject-line ideation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preheader suggestions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Message variations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Persona-based content</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Content adaptation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Localization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Image concepts</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Campaign copy variations</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can significantly shorten the distance between an initial idea and a usable marketing asset.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, faster content production does not automatically mean better content.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-generated content still needs:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Audience context</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brand direction</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strategic messaging</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Compliance review</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Editorial review</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human judgment</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The strongest operating model is not to ask AI to replace the marketing team\'s thinking.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead, AI can accelerate the early stages of the process while marketers focus on deciding what message is appropriate and why.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>AI can generate possibilities. People still determine which possibility makes sense.</strong></p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI Can Support More Responsive Buyer Experiences</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The impact of AI is also moving beyond internal marketing productivity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted conversational experiences can help organizations engage website visitors, answer questions, collect information, support meeting-booking workflows, and connect conversations with broader marketing processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This creates an opportunity to make buyer journeys more responsive.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a visitor might arrive on a website, interact with a conversational experience, request information, and eventually become part of a relevant marketing journey.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That interaction can provide useful context for future engagement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For CMOs, the strategic question becomes:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>How can AI make buyer experiences more relevant, timely, and connected?</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That question is more valuable than simply asking which AI feature the organization should test next.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">MCP and the Next Stage of Marketo AI</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another development worth watching is the use of Model Context Protocol (MCP) technology with marketing platforms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Adobe has introduced a Marketo Engage MCP Server designed to provide a bridge between AI assistants and Marketo functionality.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This type of architecture can allow AI systems to interact with marketing-platform operations through structured capabilities rather than relying only on manual navigation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The broader implication is significant.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI becomes increasingly connected to marketing systems, it may become capable of assisting with a wider range of operational tasks involving programs, campaigns, leads, emails, lists, forms, and other assets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, greater access also creates greater governance requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations should consider:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Access permissions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Approval workflows</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sandbox testing</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data protection</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Auditability</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Documentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Role-based access</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human oversight</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Change management</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The more powerful an AI system becomes, the more important it is to define what the system is allowed to do.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">7 Ways CMOs Can Prepare for AI in Marketo</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI adoption is not simply a platform upgrade.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is an operational and organizational change.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Here are seven practical ways marketing leaders can prepare.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Start With the Business Problem</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is easy to become focused on AI capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Natural-language campaign assistance, program validation, automated data processes, content generation, and conversational AI can all be interesting.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But technology should not be the starting point.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Start with the problem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are campaigns taking too long to launch?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is QA consuming too much time?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are list imports creating data problems?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is personalization difficult to scale?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are leads getting delayed during lifecycle transitions?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are marketing teams spending too much time on repetitive platform tasks?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify the friction first.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Then determine whether AI can address it.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. Establish a Clear Definition of Quality</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI becomes much more useful when teams have a shared understanding of what a successful outcome looks like.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For Marketo programs, this could include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Naming conventions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Folder structures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Required tokens</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Program templates</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">QA procedures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Approval requirements</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For lead management, standards could include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Required fields</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accepted values</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Duplicate-handling rules</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Field mapping standards</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data normalization rules</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For email campaigns, teams may need to define requirements around:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Links</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tokens</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Subject lines</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dynamic content</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">UTM parameters</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accessibility</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Images</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Personalization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Compliance language</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Audience segmentation</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clearer these standards are, the more effectively AI can support them.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Treat AI as a Teammate, Not an Autopilot</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can review, summarize, generate, recommend, and identify potential issues.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But critical marketing decisions should not automatically be delegated to an AI system.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI needs context, instructions, rules, and review.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A useful operating model is to treat AI as an additional team member that can provide a first pass or operational assistance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, AI can:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Draft content</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Review campaign configurations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify potential problems</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generate variations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Summarize information</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Suggest improvements</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human team members can then apply business context and make the final decision.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>AI can accelerate execution, while people retain accountability.</strong></p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Create a Safe Testing Environment</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing teams should have a controlled environment for experimenting with AI.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A sandbox can allow teams to test workflows without unnecessarily affecting production programs, customer-facing assets, or live data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Start with lower-risk use cases.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Review an existing program.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Test a small data import.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Generate subject-line alternatives.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Review an email asset.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Summarize conversational interactions.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Test campaign naming conventions.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evaluate AI-generated recommendations.</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Document the results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify what worked.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Record where human review was required.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The purpose of experimentation is not to prove that AI is perfect.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is to understand where AI provides practical value.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">5. Use AI to Strengthen QA</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Quality assurance is one of the areas where AI assistance can provide meaningful operational value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted validation may help identify repetitive issues before campaigns reach customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential examples include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Missing tokens</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Broken links</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inconsistent configurations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Incorrect settings</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Incomplete campaign setup</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But AI should complement QA rather than eliminate it.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human reviewers still need to evaluate strategic questions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Does the campaign target the correct audience?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Does the offer fit the buyer stage?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Is the campaign aligned with the customer journey?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Could the campaign affect lead scoring or routing?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Does the overall experience make sense?</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI can help identify mechanical issues while humans concentrate on context and judgment.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">6. Prepare Teams for Higher-Value Work</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI will change the nature of marketing operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some repetitive tasks will become faster.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some manual processes may become partially automated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some operational responsibilities may change significantly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing leaders should prepare teams for that transition rather than treating AI purely as a cost-reduction exercise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Future marketing capabilities may increasingly include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-assisted analysis</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prompt design</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data governance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Workflow architecture</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experimentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Systems thinking</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Documentation</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Change management</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cross-functional communication</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The value of marketing professionals will increasingly depend on their ability to understand systems, interpret AI-generated outputs, apply business context, and connect technology decisions to customer and revenue outcomes.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">7. Connect AI Adoption to Revenue Outcomes</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI should ultimately connect back to measurable business performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Productivity metrics are useful, but they are only part of the picture.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing leaders can also evaluate whether AI-assisted processes contribute to:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Faster campaign launches</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fewer QA issues</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Better data quality</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">More scalable personalization</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved marketing-to-sales handoffs</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Better conversion performance</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reduced operational bottlenecks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Stronger pipeline contribution</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The specific measurements will vary by organization.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What matters is that AI becomes connected to the marketing operating model rather than remaining an isolated experiment.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building an AI-Ready Marketo Foundation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The future of AI in Marketo will depend on more than the capabilities of the technology itself.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations will also need strong foundations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That includes:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clean and governed data</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Standardized campaign structures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Documented workflows</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Consistent naming conventions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strong QA procedures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Defined approval processes</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Appropriate access controls</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear ownership</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measurable business objectives</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These fundamentals become even more valuable as AI becomes more deeply integrated into marketing operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A mature marketing organization can use AI to extend its existing capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An immature organization may simply automate existing inconsistencies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">That is why preparation matters.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Preparing for the Next Era of AI in Marketo</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI in Marketo represents more than a collection of new platform capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is part of a broader transformation in how marketing teams plan, execute, validate, personalize, and measure customer engagement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For CMOs and RevOps leaders, the most important preparation may not be learning every new AI feature.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It may be strengthening the operational foundation that allows those capabilities to work effectively.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clean data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clear processes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Strong governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Defined quality standards.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Human oversight.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Revenue-focused measurement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When those elements are in place, AI can become more than an automation layer. It can become an operational accelerator that helps marketing teams work with greater consistency and scale.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The organizations that prepare now will be better positioned to evaluate new AI capabilities as they become available and determine where they fit into their marketing operating model.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>AI in Marketo is ultimately not just about doing marketing work faster. It is about building a smarter, more scalable, and better-governed marketing engine.</strong></p>', 'AI in Marketo: A Guide for CMOs & RevOps Leaders', 'Learn how AI in Marketo can transform campaign execution, data quality, QA, personalization and marketing operations for CMOs and RevOps leaders.', 'AI in Marketo, Marketo AI, Adobe Marketo Engage, AI marketing automation, Marketo automation, CMO marketing strategy, RevOps, MarTech, marketing operations, demand generation', '2022-06-02 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-02 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 14, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:17:29', '2026-10-01 18:59:08', 1, NULL),
(922, 6, 2, 18, 'eSkilled Launches AI-Powered Analytics and Reporting for Smarter RTO Decision-Making', 'eskilled-launches-ai-powered-analytics-and-reporting-for-smarter-rto-decision-making', 'eSkilled adds AI-powered analytics and reporting to help RTOs turn live SMS and LMS data into faster, smarter operational decisions with ease.', '[\"eSkilled\",\"RTO Software\",\"RTO Analytics\",\"AI Reporting\",\"Student Management System\",\"Learning Management System\",\"EdTech\",\"AI in Education\",\"Vocational Education\",\"Training Management\",\"Education Technology\",\"Business Intelligence\",\"Data Analytics\",\"AVETMISS\",\"RTO Compliance\"]', 'banner_image-1790878807939-490303369.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">eSkilled has introduced a new RTO Analytics &amp; AI Reporting capability within its Student and Learning Management System (SMS and LMS), giving Registered Training Organisations (RTOs) access to live operational insights, interactive dashboards and AI-powered reporting from a single platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new capability expands eSkilled’s all-in-one RTO software by combining student management, online learning, compliance management and advanced analytics. The solution is designed specifically for Australia’s vocational education and training sector, helping organisations use their existing operational data to make faster and more informed decisions.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Live RTO Data Into Actionable Insights</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">RTOs manage large amounts of information every day, including student enrolments, learner progress, attendance, trainer credentials, financial activity, compliance requirements and organisational performance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditionally, extracting and analysing this information can involve manual exports, spreadsheets or specialist reporting knowledge. These processes can make it difficult for teams to access current information quickly and can add unnecessary time to routine management and compliance reporting.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">eSkilled’s Analytics &amp; AI Reporting capability is designed to simplify this process by allowing authorised users to work with live data directly within the RTO software.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“RTOs already have the data they need to make informed decisions-it’s simply been too difficult to access,” said Scott Rogers, CEO of eSkilled. “We’ve built the RTO Analytics &amp; AI Reporting directly into eSkilled RTO software so every member of an RTO can ask questions, build reports and monitor performance using live operational data, without relying on spreadsheets or complex reporting tools.”</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-Powered Report Creation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new reporting functionality provides access to 29 reportable entities and more than 650 reportable fields. These cover areas such as students, enrolments, trainers, finance, attendance, quality management, risk management, continuous improvement and AVETMISS data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Users can create reports through a point-and-click interface or use natural-language prompts to describe the information they need. The AI can then identify relevant data, apply filters, create visualisations and configure the report.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, RTO staff can ask questions such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which trainers have credentials expiring in the next 90 days?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Show outstanding invoices that are more than 30 days old by program.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Which qualifications have the highest withdrawal rates this year?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of manually assembling data from different sources, users can generate structured reports based on their requirements, helping reduce the time spent preparing operational, management and compliance reports.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Chat With Live RTO Data</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Alongside AI-generated reporting, eSkilled has introduced AI Chat with Your Data. The feature allows authorised users to ask operational questions using natural language and receive responses based on live SMS information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This can help teams investigate areas such as attendance, completion rates, financial performance, compliance activities and quality management without needing to understand database structures or complex reporting logic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach makes organisational data more accessible to different teams across an RTO and allows users to obtain information in a conversational format.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dashboards for Different RTO Teams</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Analytics &amp; AI Reporting is designed to support different roles within training organisations, including CEOs, RTO managers, training coordinators, compliance officers, finance teams, trainers and administrative staff.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The capability includes live KPI dashboards, organisation-wide reporting and automated report scheduling. It also supports proactive AVETMISS data quality monitoring and trainer credential tracking, giving organisations greater visibility into important operational and compliance activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By bringing these capabilities into the existing eSkilled environment, RTOs can access reporting and analytics alongside their student management and learning management workflows.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Security and Permission Controls</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data security and access control are incorporated into the reporting and AI functionality. Reports and AI queries operate within existing user permissions, helping ensure that staff can only access information appropriate to their role.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to eSkilled, AI responses are generated using sanitised and permission-controlled data. Comprehensive audit logging also records reporting and AI-related activity, supporting organisational governance and regulatory compliance requirements.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Connected Platform for RTOs</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The introduction of Analytics &amp; AI Reporting adds advanced business intelligence capabilities to eSkilled’s broader RTO software ecosystem.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining student management, online learning delivery, compliance management and AI-powered analytics within one connected platform, eSkilled aims to give training organisations a more streamlined way to access operational information and use it for day-to-day decision-making.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For RTOs managing increasingly complex student, compliance and operational requirements, live analytics and natural-language reporting can provide a more accessible way to work with the data already available within their existing systems.</p>', 'eSkilled AI Analytics & Reporting for RTOs | Smart RTO Software', 'Discover eSkilled\'s AI-powered analytics and reporting for RTOs, enabling live data insights, interactive dashboards and natural-language reporting.', 'eSkilled, RTO analytics, AI reporting for RTOs, RTO software, student management system, learning management system, RTO reporting, AI in education, EdTech, vocational education, AVETMISS reporting, RTO compliance, business intelligence, education analytics', '2022-06-03 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-03 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:20:08', '2026-10-01 18:59:07', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(923, 6, 1, 17, 'Qualtrics Launches XM Data & AI Platform to Simulate, Predict, and Deliver Trusted Experience Outcomes', 'qualtrics-launches-xm-data-and-ai-platform-to-simulate-predict-and-deliver-trusted-experience-outcomes', 'Qualtrics introduces XM Data & AI to help organizations simulate experiences, predict customer behavior, and deliver trusted outcomes at scale.', '[\"Qualtrics\",\"XM Data & AI\",\"Experience Management\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI Platform\",\"Customer Experience\",\"CX\",\"Experience Management Platform\",\"Predictive AI\",\"Experience Agents\",\"Customer Lifetime Value\",\"MarTech\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Customer Data\",\"AI Simulation\"]', 'banner_image-1790878972652-89440114.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics Expands Experience Management With XM Data &amp; AI</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics has announced its new <strong>XM Data &amp; AI Platform</strong>, expanding its experience management capabilities with artificial intelligence designed to help organizations simulate scenarios, predict behavior, and deliver trusted outcomes in real time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Every organization faces a difference between what customers, employees, and other stakeholders expect and what they actually experience. Qualtrics refers to this difference as the <strong>Experience Gap</strong>. As expectations continue to rise across industries, businesses are increasingly looking for ways to identify and address these gaps before they affect customer loyalty, engagement, and long-term value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Qualtrics, poor customer experiences put nearly <strong>$3 trillion in sales at risk</strong>. The company says traditional experience technologies often provide insights after an interaction has already taken place, limiting an organization\'s ability to respond while the experience is still happening.</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“Experience has always been the strategy every leader believed in, but unfortunately the technology wasn’t available to execute at scale,” said Jason Maynard, CEO of Qualtrics.</p></blockquote><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Introducing the XM Data &amp; AI Platform</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics created the Experience Management category by helping organizations collect and understand feedback from customers, employees, products, and brands.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new <strong>XM Data &amp; AI Platform</strong> is designed to extend that approach into AI-driven experience management. The platform is expected to become available in <strong>2027</strong>, with Qualtrics providing a preview during its September 9, 2026 livestream.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company says its acquisition of <strong>Press Ganey Forsta for $6.75 billion in May 2026</strong> adds extensive healthcare experience data, governance capabilities, and regulatory expertise to its broader XM data environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Qualtrics, the combined platform brings experience data into a unified system and introduces three major capabilities:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simulation: Test potential outcomes before making business decisions.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prediction: Identify what an individual is likely to do while there is still time to respond.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trusted Outcomes: Deliver AI-driven actions within organizational rules and policies.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these capabilities are intended to move experience management beyond disconnected surveys, listening applications, and static data repositories.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experience Loops for Continuous Customer Value</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics is also introducing the concept of <strong>Experience Loops (X-loops)</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unlike traditional journey maps that generally represent a defined path with an endpoint, X-loops are designed to continuously follow the customer lifecycle-from acquisition and retention to expansion and long-term engagement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Within these loops are <strong>Experience Moments (X-moments)</strong> where organizations can identify opportunities to improve interactions and influence customer outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics says the platform\'s experience ontology provides the context required for AI systems to understand what a signal represents, who it belongs to, where it occurs in the customer lifecycle, and what outcome should potentially follow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This contextual layer is intended to help Experience Agents produce more relevant and controlled results rather than relying only on generalized AI models.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experience Data as the Foundation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The XM Data &amp; AI Platform builds on more than two decades of Qualtrics experience intelligence across customers, employees, and organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics says its platform includes continuously updated experience information across <strong>18,000 organizations</strong>, while Press Ganey contributes decades of healthcare experience data covering more than <strong>41,000 facilities</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company positions this combination of experience and industry-specific context as an important foundation for its AI capabilities.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simulation: Test Business Decisions Before Implementation</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the platform\'s key capabilities is <strong>Experience Simulation</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations can use simulation to explore how customers might respond to potential changes before those changes are introduced to the wider market.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, companies could evaluate:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How customers might react to a pricing change</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential responses to a new product or service</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where a policy could create customer friction</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How high-value customers might respond to a business decision</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential outcomes before investing significant resources</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics says the simulations use synthetic data and digital representations of customers, supported by its research and experience datasets.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prediction: Understand Potential Customer Behavior</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Simulation focuses on how groups or populations may respond, while <strong>Experience Prediction Models</strong> are designed to estimate how an individual may behave.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics says these models are smaller language models trained using its proprietary experience datasets and experience ontology.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is to help organizations anticipate how specific experience-management decisions could affect metrics such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer engagement</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer satisfaction</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Retention</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Churn</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer lifetime value</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is intended to give organizations more time to respond before an important customer interaction or decision occurs.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trusted Outcomes: Acting Within Organizational Rules</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The third component is <strong>Trusted Outcomes</strong>, which focuses on putting AI-generated decisions into action while maintaining organizational controls.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before an action reaches a customer or another individual, the platform checks the proposed action against established business rules and policies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experience Agents can then act within those boundaries and use the contextual information collected throughout previous interactions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics says customer data remains within the organization\'s environment and is not used to train shared models. This approach is designed to give businesses greater control over how their data is used and how AI-generated actions are delivered.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer Momentum and Experience Agents</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations are already exploring AI-driven experience management to respond to customer signals in real time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Steve Lilly, Director of Customer Experience at SiriusXM, said:</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“The organizations that win are the ones that can hear signals across every touchpoint and act on it in real time.”</p></blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics also highlighted TruGreen\'s deployment of Experience Agents across millions of customer interactions. According to the company, TruGreen reported <strong>$7 million in ROI from closed-loop feedback</strong>, a <strong>30% reduction in customer escalations</strong>, and <strong>$30 million in total ROI</strong> across digital optimization, retention, and churn prevention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These figures are company-reported results and may vary across organizations and implementations.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Two New Capabilities Available Today</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Alongside the broader XM Data &amp; AI Platform announcement, Qualtrics introduced two capabilities that are available immediately.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Experience Loop Diagnostic Service</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>X-Loop Diagnostic Service</strong> is designed to help organizations understand how their customer and employee experience systems operate across different touchpoints.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The service can identify missing data, disconnected systems, and relationships between experience moments and business outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Qualtrics, the engagement provides three primary outputs:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A relationship map representing how the experience currently operates.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A visual X-loop model identifying important experience moments and their business value.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A governance plan and activation roadmap outlining areas for improvement and prioritization.</p></li></ol><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The resulting information can also contribute to the experience ontology used within XM.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reputation Experience</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Qualtrics is also bringing <strong>Reputation Experience</strong> into its broader experience management suite.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The capability is designed to help organizations manage the information and feedback customers encounter publicly, including business locations, operating hours, contact information, ratings, reviews, and other local information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Companies can use existing survey responses to generate verified ratings that can be consumed by search engines and AI assistants.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The system also supports managing responses across platforms such as <strong>Google, Yelp, and Facebook</strong>, allowing businesses to monitor and respond to important customer feedback from a centralized experience-management environment.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanding the Role of AI in Experience Management</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With XM Data &amp; AI, Qualtrics is positioning experience management as a continuous process rather than a system focused primarily on collecting feedback and analyzing historical information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining experience data with simulation, prediction, and controlled AI actions, the company aims to help organizations understand potential outcomes before decisions are made, anticipate customer behavior, and respond during important experience moments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The XM Data &amp; AI Platform is expected to be available in <strong>2027</strong>, while the X-Loop Diagnostic Service and Reputation Experience capabilities are being introduced as available offerings.</p>', 'Qualtrics XM Data & AI Platform for Experience Management', 'Qualtrics introduces XM Data & AI to simulate experiences, predict behavior, and deliver trusted outcomes using AI and experience data.', 'Qualtrics XM Data & AI, Qualtrics AI Platform, Experience Management, XM Platform, Customer Experience, CX, Experience AI, Experience Agents, Predictive AI, AI Simulation, Customer Lifetime Value, MarTech, Customer Data, Digital Experience, Reputation Management', '2022-06-03 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-03 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:22:52', '2026-10-01 18:59:06', 1, NULL),
(924, 6, 1, 4, 'Mozilla Data Collective Secures $5M to Build a More Inclusive AI Data Ecosystem', 'mozilla-data-collective-secures-dollar5m-to-build-a-more-inclusive-ai-data-ecosystem', 'Mozilla Data Collective raises $5M to expand diverse AI datasets, strengthen data governance, and support fairer participation in the AI economy.', '[\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI Data\",\"Machine Learning\",\"AI Training Data\",\"Data Governance\",\"Data Sharing\",\"Multilingual AI\",\"NLP\",\"AI Startups\",\"Responsible AI\",\"Data Ecosystem\",\"Mozilla Data Collective\",\"AI Technology\",\"Generative AI\",\"Data Licensing\"]', 'banner_image-1790879164894-823836491.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective Secures $5M to Build a More Inclusive AI Data Ecosystem</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective has secured $5 million in funding from Mozilla to accelerate the development of a more diverse, transparent, and equitable data ecosystem for artificial intelligence. The investment comes as the data-sharing platform reports strong growth since becoming an independent company earlier in 2026.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company is focused on addressing a major challenge within the AI industry: ensuring that training datasets represent the languages, cultures, and communities that make up the global population. Mozilla Data Collective provides AI developers with access to curated datasets while creating mechanisms that allow the organisations and communities contributing those datasets to participate more fairly in the value generated by AI.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanding Access to Diverse AI Data</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI systems increasingly depend on large volumes of high-quality training data. However, many existing datasets do not adequately represent multilingual and multicultural populations. Mozilla Data Collective is attempting to address this gap by expanding access to datasets covering hundreds of languages and a broad range of artificial intelligence and natural language processing applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform currently includes datasets covering more than 450 languages, with approximately 350 organisations approved to contribute data. Before datasets become available, contributing organisations are vetted and the datasets undergo a review process focused on provenance, licensing, consent, and quality.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is designed to provide AI builders with more reliable information about where datasets originate and how they can be used.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Growing Adoption Across the AI Market</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective says its datasets are already being used by major AI laboratories, thousands of AI startups and scale-ups, and numerous unicorn companies. The organisation has also reported significant commercial momentum, with its annualised revenue run rate reaching nine times the milestone it had established for its current stage of development.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growth comes at a time when transparency and responsible data practices are becoming increasingly important for AI companies. Regulatory developments, including the European Union\'s AI Act, are increasing attention on areas such as training-data transparency, licensing, provenance, and responsible data governance.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organisations developing AI models, access to clearly documented and appropriately licensed datasets can therefore become an increasingly important part of their data strategy.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">New Dataset and Data-Access Initiatives</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective has continued expanding its platform with new capabilities intended to improve the relationship between data contributors and AI developers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of these initiatives is its Compensated Datasets program, which is designed to create additional mechanisms for contributors to receive value from datasets used in AI development.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company has also introduced tools that allow AI builders to discover datasets and request specific data based on their development requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another initiative, called Lost in Transcription, focuses on improving speech recognition for underserved language communities, particularly communities where multiple languages are commonly used together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These initiatives reflect the company\'s broader objective of making AI datasets more representative while improving transparency around how data is sourced and used.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective\'s Background</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective was initially launched in November 2025 as a social enterprise incubated by the Mozilla Foundation. In 2026, the organisation became a standalone, mission-locked British company.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The latest $5 million investment from Mozilla extends the relationship between the two organisations and is intended to support the next phase of Mozilla Data Collective\'s expansion.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Foundation Executive Director Nabiha Syed said the organisation was created around the idea of developing a different model for the AI data economy, with greater emphasis on human agency and value exchange.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Funding to Support Multilingual and Multimodal Data</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new investment is expected to support several areas of expansion. Mozilla Data Collective plans to increase its presence in multimodal cultural video datasets and expand large-scale text collections covering languages from Europe, Africa, and South Asia.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company also plans to introduce additional licensing models and more affordable subscription options for startups and scale-ups.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its R&amp;D Lab is expected to contribute new security and data-improvement technologies to the platform. These capabilities are intended to help organisations share large datasets with greater control while making complex archives easier for AI developers to work with.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Different Model for AI Data</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mozilla Data Collective\'s latest funding highlights the growing demand for diverse and responsibly sourced AI training data. As AI development expands across industries and regulatory scrutiny increases, organisations are paying greater attention to the quality, origin, licensing, and governance of the data used to develop AI systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining curated datasets, contributor participation, provenance information, and new commercial models, Mozilla Data Collective is positioning its platform around a data ecosystem intended to benefit both AI developers and data contributors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company plans to continue expanding its dataset portfolio and platform capabilities while exploring relationships with additional mission-aligned investors and organisations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For the broader AI industry, the development also reflects a growing shift toward data strategies that place greater emphasis on representation, transparency, consent, and sustainable value exchange.</p>', 'Mozilla Data Collective Raises $5M for AI Data Ecosystem', 'Mozilla Data Collective raises $5M to expand diverse AI datasets, data governance, multilingual resources, and fairer AI data practices.', 'Mozilla Data Collective, AI data ecosystem, AI training data, artificial intelligence, machine learning, data governance, multilingual AI, AI datasets, data sharing, responsible AI, NLP datasets, AI data licensing', '2022-06-06 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-06 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 5, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:26:05', '2026-10-01 18:59:00', 1, NULL),
(925, 6, 1, 17, '80% of Marketers Have Unused Survey Data, Highlighting a Major Content Creation Gap', '80percent-of-marketers-have-unused-survey-data-highlighting-a-major-content-creation-gap', 'A new survey finds 80% of marketers have unused survey data, revealing major challenges in analyzing research and turning insights into content.\r\n', '[\"Market Research\",\"Survey Data\",\"Marketing Technology\",\"MarTech\",\"B2B Marketing\",\"Content Marketing\",\"Data Visualization\",\"AI Marketing\",\"Marketing Analytics\",\"Data Storytelling\",\"AI Tools\",\"Marketing Research\"]', 'banner_image-1790879295319-534729990.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Companies are investing heavily in market research, surveys, panels, and third-party data, but a significant portion of that research never makes it into published marketing content. New survey findings from Flowerplot, an AI-powered data storytelling platform for marketing teams, highlight the gap between collecting research and turning that information into useful content.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to the survey, 37% of companies spend between $150,000 and $500,000 each year on market research, while another 10% spend more than $500,000. Despite this investment, many marketing teams struggle to transform research findings into reports, visualizations, articles, and other content assets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The research found that 37% of teams require one to two weeks or longer to extract useful insights from survey data and incorporate them into marketing content. The process can also involve four or five people, including data analysts, marketers, designers, and other content professionals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the most notable findings is that 80% of respondents said they had collected survey data with the intention of using it for marketing content but ultimately never published anything based on that research.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“Data analysts are underwater, and most marketers aren’t comfortable working directly with data,” said Katherine Boyarsky, cofounder of Flowerplot.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Closing the Gap Between Research and Content</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Flowerplot was created to address the operational challenges that marketing teams face when working with proprietary research. The platform combines data management, analysis, visualization, and content creation capabilities in a single workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform allows marketers to upload and manage survey information, explore results, identify potential insights, and create visualizations. Its AI agent, Dot, is designed to help users analyze their data and discover patterns that can potentially be developed into marketing stories.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Users can also create branded charts and data visualizations and then publish or export those assets for use across marketing campaigns and content formats.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Katherine Boyarsky and Gabriela Pinto, the company\'s cofounders, previously worked on Datalily, a Boston-based content marketing and research studio. Their experience includes producing research-driven reports such as the HubSpot State of Marketing Report, DoorDash Restaurant Trends Report, and Klaviyo AI Consumer Trends Report.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Their experience working on hundreds of research projects helped identify a recurring challenge: organizations may have valuable proprietary data, but turning that data into polished marketing content can require substantial time and coordination.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Making Data Visualization Easier for Marketing Teams</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Gabriela Pinto, who brings a design background to the company, highlighted another challenge faced by creative teams working with survey data. Traditional design tools and dedicated data visualization platforms often require marketers and designers to compromise between creative control and data functionality.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Flowerplot aims to reduce that friction by allowing creative and marketing teams to work more directly with research data. The platform is designed to reduce manual visualization work while helping teams maintain consistency and accuracy across their content.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of relying entirely on analysts to interpret and prepare research findings, marketers can use the platform to explore their own datasets and identify information that may be relevant to their campaigns and editorial strategies.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Early Customers and Market Adoption</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Flowerplot\'s early users include marketing teams from companies such as Rippling, Knak, Triple Whale, Wistia, and SuperOffice.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Veronica Borg, Content Marketing Specialist at SuperOffice, said the platform helped her team move from accessing the tool to identifying potentially useful insights more quickly.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The development of platforms such as Flowerplot reflects a broader shift in B2B marketing toward data-driven content. As companies continue investing in original research, the ability to efficiently analyze that information and convert it into engaging content can become an important part of the content production workflow.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For marketing teams sitting on unused survey results, research data can represent a significant source of potential insights. Tools that bring data analysis, visualization, and content creation closer together are designed to make that information easier to turn into publishable marketing assets.</p>', '80% of Marketers Have Unused Survey Data | B2B Marketing', 'A new survey finds 80% of marketers leave survey data unused, highlighting challenges in data analysis, visualization, and content creation.\r\n', 'survey data, marketing content, market research, MarTech, B2B marketing, data visualization, AI marketing, content marketing, marketing analytics, survey research, data storytelling', '2022-06-06 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-06 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:28:15', '2026-10-01 18:58:58', 1, NULL),
(926, 6, 1, 4, 'Evalueserve and Databricks Partner to Accelerate Enterprise Data and AI Transformation', 'evalueserve-and-databricks-partner-to-accelerate-enterprise-data-and-ai-transformation', 'Evalueserve and Databricks partner to modernize enterprise data, strengthen governance, and help businesses scale analytics and AI across workflows.', '[\"Evalueserve\",\"Databricks\",\"Data and AI\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"Data Engineering\",\"Data Governance\",\"Enterprise AI\",\"AI Transformation\",\"Analytics\",\"Agentic AI\",\"Lakehouse\",\"Business Intelligence\"]', 'banner_image-1790879433840-100163206.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evalueserve, a global domain-focused AI services company, has announced a new partnership with Databricks to help enterprises modernize their data environments, improve data governance, and establish trusted foundations for analytics and artificial intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The collaboration brings together the Databricks Data + AI Platform with Evalueserve’s expertise in data engineering, AI, analytics, and industry-specific business workflows. The companies aim to help organizations move beyond AI experimentation and build scalable, production-ready data and AI capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As enterprises expand their use of AI, fragmented information, inconsistent data quality, and complex governance requirements can create significant barriers to adoption. Through the partnership, Evalueserve and Databricks will work to address these challenges by combining modern data infrastructure with domain expertise and practical business applications.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Trusted Data Foundations for Enterprise AI</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Databricks Data + AI Platform provides organizations with a unified environment for working with enterprise data, analytics, and AI applications. Its lakehouse architecture uses open data formats and governance capabilities, allowing organizations to maintain greater control over their data while developing intelligent applications.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Evalueserve plans to develop a portfolio of data engineering accelerators and industry-specific solutions using Databricks technologies. These solutions are intended to shorten implementation timelines and connect modern data foundations with business-critical use cases.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The partnership will leverage capabilities across the Databricks Data + AI Platform, including business-context technologies such as Genie Ontology and governed enterprise AI capabilities through Unity Gateway, alongside data engineering, analytics, and governance functions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“Enterprise AI is only as strong as the data foundation behind it,” said Gururaj Bhat, EVP and Head of Data &amp; AI at Evalueserve. “Databricks gives us a powerful foundation to help clients bring together their data, strengthen governance and lineage, and make enterprise information ready for AI. By combining that foundation with Evalueserve’s domain expertise and workflow knowledge, we can build AI and agentic applications around the business problems that matter most.”</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Initial Solutions from the Partnership</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The companies have identified several initial solutions designed to help enterprises improve the way they manage and use data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Private Credit Intelligence Accelerator:</strong><br>This solution combines structured and unstructured credit information within a governed lakehouse environment. It is designed to support faster underwriting, earlier covenant monitoring, and more traceable investment and investor reporting.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Data Quality Framework:</strong><br>The reusable framework supports data validation, profiling, monitoring, remediation, scoring, and governance. It is designed to help organizations establish more consistent and reliable data across enterprise systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Automodel Generators:</strong><br>This metadata-driven accelerator automates the design and generation of enterprise data warehouse models. By reducing manual modeling activities and standardizing development practices, it can help teams create analytics-ready data environments more efficiently.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Combining Technology With Domain Expertise</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Nitin Narkhede, Chief Technology Officer at Evalueserve, said the partnership is focused on helping organizations develop data and AI architectures in which governance, business context, and intelligence work together.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“The opportunity with Databricks is to help enterprises build a data and AI architecture where governance, context, and intelligence are seamlessly integrated,” said Nitin Narkhede. “By combining these platform capabilities with Evalueserve’s domain expertise and engineering excellence, we can deliver repeatable solutions that make complex enterprise data more usable, trusted, and actionable, enabling organizations to accelerate AI adoption at scale.”</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The partnership forms part of Evalueserve’s broader strategy of combining leading technology platforms with more than 25 years of domain and workflow expertise.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than taking a technology-first approach, Evalueserve focuses on the specific business challenge and desired outcome. Its teams then bring together data engineering, AI, governance, analytics, and adoption capabilities to develop solutions aimed at delivering measurable business value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Through its collaboration with Databricks, Evalueserve intends to help enterprises create stronger data foundations, improve the reliability and accessibility of business information, and move AI initiatives toward scalable production use.</p>', 'Evalueserve and Databricks Partner for Enterprise AI', 'Evalueserve and Databricks partner to modernize enterprise data, strengthen governance, and accelerate analytics, AI, and agentic applications.', 'Evalueserve, Databricks, Data and AI, Enterprise AI, Artificial Intelligence, Data Engineering, Data Governance, AI Transformation, Enterprise Data, AI Applications, Agentic AI, Data Analytics, Machine Learning, Lakehouse, Business Intelligence', '2022-06-07 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-07 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:30:34', '2026-10-01 18:58:55', 1, NULL),
(927, 6, 1, 17, 'monitorQA Introduces AI Intelligence to Transform Audit Data Into Faster Operational Decisions', 'monitorqa-introduces-ai-intelligence-to-transform-audit-data-into-faster-operational-decisions', 'monitorQA launches AI Intelligence to help operations teams analyze audit data, uncover recurring failures and make faster decisions.', '[\"monitorQA\",\"AI Analytics\",\"Operational Intelligence\",\"Audit Management\",\"Compliance\",\"Audit Data\",\"Business Intelligence\",\"AI-Powered Analytics\",\"Operations Management\",\"Data Analytics\",\"Dashboards\",\"Query Builder\",\"Compliance Technology\",\"MarTech\"]', 'banner_image-1790879572629-435121477.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>monitorQA Launches AI Intelligence to Help Operations Teams Turn Audit Data Into Faster Decisions</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">monitorQA, an operational intelligence platform focused on making compliance verifiable and defensible, has introduced <strong>Intelligence</strong>, a new AI-powered analytics capability designed to help operations teams analyze audit data and turn findings into actionable decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional audit programs can generate significant amounts of operational information. However, extracting meaningful insights from that data often requires teams to export spreadsheets, create customized reports and manually investigate performance trends. monitorQA\'s Intelligence capability is designed to simplify this process by allowing users to interact directly with their audit data using natural-language questions.</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">“Every audit application collects data. Very few teams have time to turn that data into a decision,” said Peter Swaniker, Co-Founder and CEO of monitorQA. “Intelligence closes that gap. It gives every manager, not just the people who know how to build a report, a direct way to ask a question and get a straight answer back.”</p></blockquote><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Audit Data Into Actionable Answers</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With Intelligence, operations managers can ask questions about their audit programs without having to manually construct complex reports. Questions can include identifying underperforming locations, comparing pass rates or finding sites that may require additional attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform presents responses through different formats, including charts, tables and key performance indicators, together with written explanations that provide additional context.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Users can also continue the conversation by asking follow-up questions. This allows teams to move from an initial observation to a deeper investigation of the factors contributing to a particular result.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Four Core Capabilities of Intelligence</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">monitorQA\'s Intelligence capability is built around four primary functions:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>1. Ask</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Users can ask questions about their audit data using natural language. They can investigate areas such as location performance, pass rates and sites that may require attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>2. Explore</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Insights can be displayed as charts, tables or KPI values, accompanied by written explanations. Users can continue asking questions within the same conversation to understand the factors influencing a result.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>3. Build</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When an analysis provides useful information, users can convert it into a dashboard. Individual results can also be pinned and added to dashboards, allowing teams to build a collection of relevant operational insights over time.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>4. Summarize</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dashboards can generate AI-powered written summaries of the information they contain. Users can also ask questions about a dashboard directly, making it possible to investigate insights without leaving the dashboard view.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Query Builder for More Customized Analysis</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For teams that require greater control over how their information is analyzed, monitorQA also provides a <strong>Query Builder</strong>.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The tool allows users to select a data entity and measurement, group information by fields such as template or auditor, and apply filters to refine the results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Depending on the analysis, users can visualize results through multiple formats, including:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tables</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bar charts</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Line charts</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pie charts</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">KPI displays</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This provides teams with an alternative to natural-language analysis when they need to define specific reporting parameters themselves.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving From Insight to Action</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The introduction of Intelligence is intended to reduce the amount of manual work involved in extracting useful information from audit programs.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of spending significant time preparing spreadsheets and building reports, teams can use AI-assisted analysis to investigate recurring failures, compare operational performance and identify locations that may require attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another aspect of the capability is its connection to the underlying audit data. monitorQA indicates how much data supports an answer, helping users understand whether a finding is based on a smaller number of audits or a broader dataset.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This context can help teams interpret findings more appropriately and distinguish between isolated observations and patterns supported by larger volumes of operational data.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting More Informed Operational Decisions</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining natural-language analysis, customizable queries, dashboards and AI-generated summaries, monitorQA\'s Intelligence capability is designed to help operations teams move beyond simply documenting audit results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The approach focuses on identifying patterns, investigating the factors behind performance issues and prioritizing areas that may need further attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organizations managing large audit programs, the capability provides another way to connect compliance and operational data with day-to-day decision-making.</p>', 'monitorQA Launches AI Intelligence for Faster Audit Data Analysis', 'monitorQA introduces AI Intelligence to help operations teams analyze audit data, identify recurring failures and turn insights into faster decisions.', 'monitorQA, AI intelligence, audit data analytics, operational intelligence, audit management, compliance analytics, AI analytics, business intelligence, audit reporting, operations management, compliance technology, audit dashboards', '2022-06-07 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-07 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:32:53', '2026-10-01 18:58:53', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(928, 6, 2, 10, 'Datadobi Launches Data Access Governance to Help Enterprises Secure and Control Unstructured Data', 'datadobi-launches-data-access-governance-to-help-enterprises-secure-and-control-unstructured-data', 'Datadobi adds Data Access Governance to StorageMAP, helping enterprises discover, govern, and secure access to unstructured data at scale.', '[\"Datadobi\",\"Data Access Governance\",\"StorageMAP\",\"Unstructured Data\",\"Data Governance\",\"Data Security\",\"Cybersecurity\",\"Data Management\",\"AI Data Readiness\",\"Data Compliance\",\"Access Control\",\"Enterprise Storage\",\"IT Infrastructure\",\"Data Protection\"]', 'banner_image-1790879737049-565982559.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Datadobi has announced the general availability of Data Access Governance (DAG) within its StorageMAP platform, giving enterprises greater visibility into who can access their unstructured data and whether those permissions align with organizational security and governance policies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As enterprises manage growing volumes of unstructured information across fragmented storage environments, understanding where data resides and who can access it has become increasingly important. Datadobi’s new capability is designed to help organizations identify access risks, strengthen governance, and make more informed decisions about their data.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improving Visibility Across Unstructured Data</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">StorageMAP’s Data Access Governance capability provides administrators with visibility into access permissions across distributed unstructured data environments. By identifying which users and groups have access to specific datasets, organizations can uncover excessive or misaligned permissions and take steps to reduce unnecessary exposure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This visibility can be particularly valuable as enterprises address cybersecurity, regulatory compliance, artificial intelligence initiatives, and data management challenges. Organizations need to understand their data and associated access controls before they can effectively protect or operationalize it.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting Data Governance and Security</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">DAG expands the capabilities already available through StorageMAP, including data discovery, classification, risk assessment, lifecycle management, and AI Data Readiness.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these capabilities are intended to help organizations understand what data they have, determine its business value and risk, and apply appropriate policies across their storage environments. This approach can help enterprises improve data governance while supporting broader initiatives involving AI adoption, cyber resilience, compliance, and storage optimization.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Addressing Enterprise Access Risks</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Datadobi stated that the new capability was tested with a number of beta customers. During early access, organizations identified challenges in determining which important datasets were unnecessarily exposed and which users could access them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditionally, investigating these issues can require manual analysis across multiple systems. Data Access Governance is designed to provide a centralized view that can help teams identify overexposed information and validate whether existing access controls are consistent with organizational policies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For security teams, this visibility can help identify potential exposure before it contributes to a larger security incident. For data and compliance teams, it can also provide information needed to review access controls and support governance processes.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Supporting AI Data Readiness</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growth of enterprise AI initiatives has increased the importance of well-governed data. Organizations need to know what information is available, where it is stored, and who can access it before using that information in AI-related workflows.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By adding access governance to its existing data discovery and AI Data Readiness capabilities, StorageMAP aims to help organizations establish greater control over the underlying unstructured data used in their technology environments.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Helping Organizations Meet Compliance Requirements</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data Access Governance can also support organizations operating under data protection and industry-specific requirements. Regulations and standards such as GDPR, HIPAA, and PCI DSS place importance on appropriate handling and access to sensitive information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">DAG provides visibility that can help organizations review whether access permissions are appropriate, identify potential gaps, and document how access controls are being managed.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Turning Unstructured Data Into a Managed Asset</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Datadobi, Data Access Governance represents an important step toward bringing unstructured data under greater organizational control. Instead of treating unstructured information solely as a storage challenge, enterprises can use visibility and governance capabilities to better understand its risks and potential value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With the addition of DAG, StorageMAP brings together data discovery, classification, risk assessment, lifecycle management, AI Data Readiness, and access governance capabilities. These functions are designed to help enterprises make informed decisions about their unstructured data while supporting security, compliance, AI initiatives, and long-term data management strategies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Datadobi’s latest capability reflects the growing enterprise need for greater visibility and control as unstructured data continues to expand across increasingly complex storage environments.</p>', 'Datadobi Data Access Governance for Unstructured Data', 'Discover how Datadobi\'s StorageMAP Data Access Governance helps enterprises monitor access, reduce data exposure, improve compliance, and secure unstructured data.', 'Datadobi, StorageMAP, Data Access Governance, unstructured data governance, data access management, data security, enterprise data governance, access control, AI data readiness, cybersecurity, data compliance, enterprise storage', '2022-05-10 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-05-10 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:35:37', '2026-10-01 18:58:50', 1, NULL),
(929, 6, 2, 17, 'Economic Influence: Where Should Brands Invest When Marketing Budgets Are Rationalised?', 'economic-influence-where-should-brands-invest-when-marketing-budgets-are-rationalised', 'As budgets tighten, brands are rethinking marketing investments and turning to digital channels, influencers and measurable strategies for growth.', '[\"Economic Influence\",\"Marketing Budget\",\"Influencer Marketing\",\"Digital Marketing\",\"Marketing Strategy\",\"ROI\",\"Brand Awareness\",\"Social Media\",\"Customer Loyalty\",\"B2B Marketing\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Cost Effective Marketing\"]', 'banner_image-1790879874430-267139422.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Economic Influence: Where Should Brands Invest When Marketing Budgets Are Rationalised?</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Economic uncertainty has forced businesses across industries to reassess how they manage their marketing budgets. Whether organisations have reduced spending, redirected resources or changed their priorities, almost every brand has had to reconsider where its marketing investment can generate the greatest value.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Economic Influence and the Shift Toward Digital Marketing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As businesses faced changing restrictions and market conditions, brands were required to adapt quickly and rethink the way they operated and communicated with customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This economic pressure also encouraged companies to examine their finances more carefully and identify marketing channels that could deliver measurable results without unnecessary expenditure.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Although the period created significant challenges, it also accelerated digital transformation. Businesses became more cautious about spending while simultaneously recognising the importance of maintaining visibility and relevance in an increasingly digital-first environment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Throughout 2020 and into 2021, social media, online communities and digital creators demonstrated how effectively brands could continue communicating with audiences even when traditional business activities were disrupted.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">While traditional advertising experienced significant disruption, digital channels continued to provide businesses with opportunities to reach, engage and convert audiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Also Read:</strong> How To Get Sales Deals Across the Line When the C-Suite Is Involved</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measuring the Impact of Influencer Marketing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Influencer marketing emerged as an increasingly important part of the digital marketing landscape. Industry projections at the time indicated that the influencer marketing sector could reach approximately $15 billion by 2022, demonstrating the rapid growth of the channel.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research also indicated that a significant proportion of marketers planned to increase their influencer marketing budgets during 2021.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the reasons for this continued investment was the ability to connect brands with highly targeted audiences through trusted voices and established online communities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measuring influencer marketing can initially appear more complicated than measuring some traditional advertising activities. However, modern social platforms provide businesses with a wide range of performance indicators.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Depending on the campaign, marketers can monitor metrics such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Website visits</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Link clicks</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Purchases and conversions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Video views</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Likes and comments</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Shares</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Audience reach</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Engagement rates</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Follower growth</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lead generation</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This data enables businesses to evaluate campaign performance and determine whether their marketing investment is producing the intended results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Working with an experienced influencer marketing agency can also help brands identify creators whose audiences, communication style and expertise align with their products, services and campaign objectives.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Focusing on Return on Investment</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When marketing budgets are under pressure, return on investment becomes increasingly important.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Influencer marketing can support several different business objectives, including increasing brand awareness, generating leads, driving sales, expanding social media reach and building stronger relationships with potential customers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, brands should define their objectives before investing in a campaign. A campaign focused on awareness may require different influencers, content formats and performance indicators from a campaign designed specifically to generate conversions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An experienced agency can help businesses analyse audience data, identify relevant influencers and establish appropriate campaign metrics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach can help organisations avoid spending money simply for visibility and instead focus on activities that contribute to measurable business objectives.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building Customer Retention and Brand Loyalty</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Marketing campaigns can deliver benefits beyond immediate sales.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When customers repeatedly interact with useful, relatable and trustworthy content, they may develop a stronger connection with a brand. Over time, this can contribute to customer loyalty and retention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A good example is HelloFresh. While its service addresses a need that consumers could also satisfy through traditional grocery shopping, its marketing strategy focuses heavily on convenience, recipes, pre-measured ingredients and reducing food waste.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These additional elements help differentiate the brand and create a customer experience around the core product.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sustainability has also become an increasingly important consideration for many consumers. Businesses that communicate practical ways to reduce waste or operate more responsibly can use these messages to strengthen their relationship with environmentally conscious audiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The same shift can be seen in advertising. As consumers increasingly consume information through digital channels, brands have greater opportunities to communicate online rather than relying exclusively on printed materials.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Also Read:</strong> My 5 Predictions About the ABM Industry in 2021</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cost-Effective Marketing Strategies</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When economic conditions are uncertain, businesses naturally become more conscious about how they allocate their marketing budgets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than investing heavily in every available channel, marketers are increasingly focused on identifying platforms that can deliver measurable engagement and meaningful business outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growth of social media has demonstrated the potential value of digital creators and influencer-led campaigns. At the same time, many traditional advertising methods have become less attractive to brands looking for flexible and measurable marketing options.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There is no universal price for influencer marketing. Campaign costs vary according to factors such as audience size, industry, creator expertise, content format, campaign duration and expected deliverables.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some industry research has reported very high returns from influencer marketing, including estimates of hundreds of percent in return for each unit of currency invested. However, results can vary considerably between campaigns, making it important for businesses to evaluate performance using their own objectives and data.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The key consideration is not simply how much a campaign costs, but whether the investment contributes to the desired business outcome.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Power of Storytelling and Human-to-Human Interaction</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Influencer marketing is about more than placing a product in front of an audience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Successful creators spend years developing relationships with their communities. Their content often combines personal experiences, education, entertainment and recommendations, creating a communication style that can feel more personal than conventional advertising.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Social media influencers have effectively created a new form of digital storytelling where consumers can interact with people rather than simply receiving messages from a brand.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This human-to-human approach can help businesses create stronger emotional connections with their audiences.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Matt Hayes, Managing Director of Midlands-based brand agency Champions (UK) plc, has previously emphasised the importance of brand trust, emotional connection and relatability in marketing campaigns.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The underlying principle is straightforward: customers are not always looking only for a product or service. They may also want to understand the values behind a brand and feel that its communication is relevant to them.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When businesses successfully create that connection, they can move beyond competing solely on price.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">The Continued Growth of Social Media</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The pandemic significantly changed the way consumers interacted with digital platforms. Research at the time indicated that approximately 82% of users had increased their social media consumption since the beginning of the pandemic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This increase created new opportunities for brands but also introduced greater competition for consumer attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As more businesses move toward digital-first marketing, simply maintaining an online presence is no longer enough. Brands need to understand their target audiences, create relevant content and measure whether their activities are contributing to meaningful results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For businesses reviewing their marketing budgets, this means focusing on channels that align with their objectives rather than following every emerging trend.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Where Should Brands Invest?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Economic pressure does not necessarily mean that brands should stop marketing. Instead, it can provide an opportunity to examine which activities are generating value and which areas require adjustment.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brands reviewing their budgets should consider:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Audience relevance: Does the channel reach the people the business actually wants to engage?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Measurable results: Can campaign performance be tracked effectively?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Customer relationships: Does the activity help build trust and loyalty?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cost efficiency: Is the investment proportionate to the expected outcome?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Brand alignment: Does the marketing activity fit the company\'s positioning and values?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Long-term value: Can the campaign contribute beyond short-term visibility?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Influencer marketing, social media and other digital channels can provide valuable opportunities, particularly when campaigns are built around clear objectives and measurable outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ultimately, the most effective approach to budget rationalisation is not simply spending less. It is understanding where marketing investment can create the greatest business value.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As brands continue adapting to changing economic conditions and evolving consumer behaviour, a combination of digital-first thinking, measurable campaigns, relevant storytelling and strong customer relationships can help businesses remain visible and competitive.</p>', 'Economic Influence: Where Should Brands Invest?', 'Discover how brands can optimise marketing budgets through influencer marketing, digital channels, measurable campaigns and customer-focused strategies.', 'economic influence, marketing budget, influencer marketing, digital marketing, marketing ROI, social media marketing, brand awareness, customer loyalty, marketing strategy, digital advertising', '2022-06-08 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-08 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:37:54', '2026-10-01 18:58:48', 1, NULL),
(930, 6, 2, 17, 'Somantra Launches AEO & GEO Metrics to Measure AI Search Brand Mindshare, Consideration and Engagement', 'somantra-launches-aeo-and-geo-metrics-to-measure-ai-search-brand-mindshare-consideration-and-engagement', 'Somantra’s new AEO and GEO metrics measure brand visibility, recommendations, competitive positioning and engagement across AI search platforms.', '[\"Somantra\",\"AEO\",\"GEO\",\"AI Search\",\"Generative AI\",\"ChatGPT\",\"Google AI Overviews\",\"Claude\",\"Gemini\",\"Perplexity\",\"Brand Visibility\",\"Brand Mindshare\",\"Brand Consideration\",\"Brand Engagement\",\"MarTech\",\"AI Marketing\",\"Digital Marketing\"]', 'banner_image-1790880099163-986843605.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Somantra has announced the general availability of its <strong>AEO Metrics Suite</strong>, a set of three proprietary metrics designed to help marketing teams understand how brands are positioned across generative AI and AI-powered search platforms.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The suite introduces <strong>Brand Mindshare Score, Brand Consideration Score and Brand Engagement Score</strong>, moving beyond conventional AI-search visibility measurements such as brand mentions and citation counts. The metrics are designed to help organizations determine not only whether their brands appear in AI-generated responses, but also whether they are recommended, how they compare with competitors and how deeply AI assistants engage with their content during conversations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform is built on Somantra’s existing <strong>Answer Engine Optimization (AEO)</strong> and <strong>Generative Engine Optimization (GEO)</strong> conversation-mapping infrastructure. According to the company, the infrastructure tracks millions of AI-search conversations across brand categories and is intended to provide marketing teams with more detailed visibility into how consumers encounter brands while researching products and services.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Beyond AI Search Mentions</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Many organizations currently evaluate AI-search visibility by tracking how frequently their brand or website is mentioned or cited in AI-generated answers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">While these measurements can indicate visibility, they do not necessarily explain the context in which a brand appears. A brand that is briefly mentioned as one of several alternatives can receive the same visibility signal as a brand that is directly recommended to a potential customer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional citation counts can also miss changes caused by variations in search queries. A small change in wording may influence which brands an AI assistant recommends, while basic visibility reporting may not capture the difference.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Somantra\'s approach is designed to examine these variations and provide additional context around brand positioning within AI-generated answers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company said its perturbation testing across <strong>4,445 ChatGPT responses and 135 matched query pairs</strong> in the Australian insurance category showed that changing a single word in a query could influence which brand an AI assistant identified as the safest or best-value option.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Three Metrics for AI Brand Visibility</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Somantra\'s AEO Metrics Suite uses three complementary measurements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Brand Mindshare Score</strong> evaluates a brand\'s share of voice and share of citation across its broader query landscape. The measurement also considers query neighbourhoods and variations that may not be captured through conventional keyword tracking.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Brand Consideration Score</strong> focuses on how a brand is positioned against named competitors within AI-generated responses. The metric is intended to differentiate between a simple brand mention and a stronger recommendation or favourable positioning.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Brand Engagement Score</strong> evaluates how deeply an AI assistant interacts with information associated with a brand across multi-turn conversations. Instead of only recording whether a brand was mentioned once, the metric examines whether the assistant continues to engage with the brand\'s claims and information throughout the conversation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, the metrics are designed to provide marketing teams with a more detailed view of AI-search performance and how their brands are being presented during purchase-related conversations.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rentomojo Uses the Metrics for AEO and GEO Strategy</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Indian furniture and appliance rental platform <strong>Rentomojo</strong> is using Somantra\'s metrics as part of its AEO and GEO programme.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rentomojo operates across multiple cities in India and offers rental products across categories such as furniture, appliances, sofas, beds and wardrobes. According to Dhruv Wahal, AVP – Marketing &amp; Growth at Rentomojo, AI-search visibility can vary considerably between product categories.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Wahal said the company wanted to understand more than whether Rentomojo was being mentioned or cited in AI-generated responses. The objective was to identify the buyer-intent conversations in which the company was actually being recommended.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company uses Somantra\'s Mindshare and Consideration scores to examine brand positioning across different queries and in comparison with competing brands. Its Engagement Score provides additional information about the pages and content AI assistants use while developing responses across a conversation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These insights can then be used as an additional input when evaluating content and AI-search strategies.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How Somantra\'s Measurement Process Works</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The process begins with a brand providing information about its products and services. Somantra then identifies relevant customer personas and statistically significant competitors before developing a query set based on consumer search behaviour.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The query framework can include interactions across platforms such as <strong>ChatGPT and Google AI</strong>, covering both single-turn searches and multi-turn conversation journeys.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Responses are subsequently evaluated across dimensions such as citation, positioning and engagement depth. The resulting information is aggregated into the three proprietary scores at the brand and product-line levels.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Somantra says brands can refresh their measurements on a <strong>daily, weekly or monthly basis</strong> through its dashboard. The dashboard provides movement tracking, competitor comparisons and query-level information to help marketing teams monitor changes in AI-search visibility.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Free AEO Brand Audit</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The three metrics are also included in Somantra\'s free AEO brand audit. According to the company, the audit examines <strong>15,000 customer conversation paths across five ideal customer profiles</strong> customized to a brand\'s category.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The audit is intended to give organizations an initial view of how their brands appear across AI-powered search conversations and where opportunities may exist to improve their AEO and GEO strategies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As AI-powered search continues to influence how consumers research products and services, measurement frameworks such as these are becoming an additional consideration for marketers seeking to understand brand visibility, competitive positioning and content performance within generative AI environments.</p>', 'Somantra AEO & GEO Metrics for AI Search Brand Visibility', 'Discover how Somantra measures AI search brand visibility with Mindshare, Consideration and Engagement scores across ChatGPT, Google AI and more.', 'Somantra, AEO metrics, GEO metrics, AI search visibility, Answer Engine Optimization, Generative Engine Optimization, ChatGPT, Google AI Overviews, Claude, Gemini, Perplexity, brand mindshare, brand consideration, brand engagement, AI search marketing, MarTech', '2022-06-08 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-08 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 5, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:41:39', '2026-10-01 18:58:47', 1, NULL),
(931, 6, 1, 9, 'DocuWare Unveils Next-Generation Document Management Platform with Built-In AI', 'docuware-unveils-next-generation-document-management-platform-with-built-in-ai', 'DocuWare introduces a next-generation document management platform with AI-powered search, intelligent processing, and redesigned web and mobile tools.', '[\"DocuWare\",\"Document Management\",\"AI\",\"DocuWare Aura\",\"Intelligent Document Processing\",\"IDP\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"OCR\",\"Workflow Automation\",\"Document Automation\",\"Enterprise AI\",\"Digital Transformation\",\"Cloud Document Management\",\"Business Process Automation\",\"GenAI\"]', 'banner_image-1790880242360-823982206.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">DocuWare has announced the launch of its next-generation document management platform, bringing together a redesigned digital experience, an integrated AI assistant, and expanded intelligent document processing capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new DocuWare platform is designed to help organizations manage business documents more efficiently while turning information stored in documents into actionable business intelligence. The company says the platform combines three major technology developments: a redesigned web and mobile experience, DocuWare Aura, and enhanced DocuWare Intelligent Document Processing (IDP).</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform was first previewed during the 2026 DocuWorld Partner Conferences and is now available to new DocuWare Cloud customers.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Redesigned Web and Mobile Experience</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A major part of the new DocuWare release is its redesigned user interface, which provides a modern experience with simplified navigation and improved accessibility.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform has been developed according to Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 AA, helping make document management and workflow activities more accessible to users with different needs and levels of technical experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new mobile application complements the web experience by allowing users to access and share documents while away from their desks. Users can also manage assigned workflow tasks, receive task notifications, and import files and documents directly from mobile devices.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During the transition period, the new DocuWare experience will operate alongside the previous web client and mobile application, which are being referred to as classic DocuWare. This approach allows existing customers to transition to the new environment according to their own schedules.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">DocuWare Aura Brings AI Into Document Management</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new platform also introduces DocuWare Aura, an integrated AI assistant designed specifically for document search, analysis, and information retrieval.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than relying on publicly available information or unrelated external data, DocuWare Aura works directly with documents stored within an organization\'s DocuWare repositories. It also follows existing access permissions when retrieving information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Users can interact with their business documents using natural-language questions. Aura can help users find information, summarize documents, and compare information across file cabinets. Responses can also include references to the original documents, helping users trace information back to its source.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is intended to make organizational knowledge easier to access while helping employees use existing business information more effectively.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expanded Intelligent Document Processing</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">DocuWare has also expanded its Intelligent Document Processing capabilities. The technology automatically captures, classifies, and extracts information from documents to reduce manual data-entry work.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">One of the new capabilities is GenAI-based Zero Shot Extraction. It allows DocuWare IDP to extract information from document types that the system has not previously processed without requiring traditional document-specific training.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The updated IDP capabilities also include OCR support for scanned documents and images. OCR can convert text from these files into machine-readable information, with support for 20 languages.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another addition is Master Data Matching, which can compare information extracted from documents with master data stored in other business systems, including enterprise resource planning platforms. This can help transform extracted document information into structured business records.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cloud Availability and Future On-Premises Support</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new DocuWare experience is currently available to new DocuWare Cloud customers. An on-premises version is planned for a future release, giving organizations additional deployment options.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Existing DocuWare customers and partners can continue using their current clients and configurations alongside the new experience during the transition period. This allows organizations to adopt the new platform without requiring an immediate migration of their existing data or settings.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With its combination of AI-assisted document interaction, intelligent document processing, and redesigned web and mobile applications, the new DocuWare platform represents an evolution of document management toward more connected and automation-focused business workflows.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Accessibility</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform follows WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility standards. These standards define requirements intended to make web content more accessible and usable for people with disabilities, covering both Level A and Level AA success criteria.</p>', 'DocuWare Launches AI-Powered Document Management Platform', 'DocuWare launches a next-generation platform with AI assistant, intelligent document processing, OCR, and redesigned web and mobile experiences.', 'DocuWare, document management, AI document management, DocuWare Aura, intelligent document processing, IDP, OCR, workflow automation, enterprise AI, document automation, GenAI, digital transformation', '2022-06-08 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-08 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:44:02', '2026-10-01 18:58:46', 1, NULL),
(932, 6, 1, 17, 'Guideline Unveils WRAP Cloud, a New AI-Enabled Audience Analytics Platform', 'guideline-unveils-wrap-cloud-a-new-ai-enabled-audience-analytics-platform', 'Guideline launches WRAP Cloud, an AI-enabled SaaS platform bringing trusted TV and CTV audience analytics to publishers, agencies, and brands.', '[\"Guideline\",\"WRAP Cloud\",\"Audience Analytics\",\"TV Audience Measurement\",\"CTV\",\"Connected TV\",\"Media Intelligence\",\"Advertising Intelligence\",\"SaaS\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI\",\"MCP\",\"Model Context Protocol\",\"Media Planning\",\"MarTech\"]', 'banner_image-1790880367939-427467911.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Guideline has introduced WRAP Cloud, a modern SaaS-based version of its WRAP audience analytics platform, bringing more than three decades of television audience analysis into a browser-based environment.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The new platform is designed to help publishers, agencies, brands, programming teams, sales organizations, media planners, and business leaders access trusted TV and CTV audience insights without relying on dedicated machines or complex software deployments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">WRAP has long been used across the television industry to transform linear TV and CTV ratings into actionable audience intelligence. With WRAP Cloud, Guideline is moving that established methodology to a cloud-based platform while maintaining the reporting and analysis capabilities customers already rely on.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The platform is designed around the changing way media teams work today. Audience insights are increasingly needed across multiple departments, locations, markets, and work environments. WRAP Cloud provides browser-based access without requiring local software installation, virtual desktop environments, or manual management of data files.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Key Capabilities of WRAP Cloud</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With WRAP Cloud, organizations can access several capabilities designed to simplify audience analysis:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Browser-Based WRAP Access</strong><br>\r\nUsers can access the WRAP experience through supported web browsers without installing the software locally, making audience analysis easier to access across different working environments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Broader Access to Audience Insights</strong><br>\r\nNew users can be onboarded more quickly, allowing organizations to make audience intelligence available to a wider range of teams instead of limiting access to specialized research professionals.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Reduced Software Management</strong><br>\r\nThe cloud-based model eliminates much of the operational work associated with version updates, software installations, and manual data-file management. Teams can work from the same platform and release.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Continuous Platform Improvements</strong><br>\r\nNew reports, datasets, artificial intelligence capabilities, and Model Context Protocol (MCP) integrations can be introduced through the SaaS platform without requiring customers to manually deploy software upgrades.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">TV and CTV Audience Measurement</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The initial WRAP Cloud release brings the core WRAP experience into a SaaS environment. It includes local daily ratings, CTV audience measurement, commonly used reports, and multi-market audience analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Guideline stated that customers\' existing data licensing arrangements remain unchanged. The platform also includes enterprise-focused controls such as single sign-on, role-based access, and market-level permissions enforced through APIs. These controls are designed to ensure that users can access only the markets and information they are authorized to view.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI and MCP-Enabled Capabilities</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A major part of Guideline\'s cloud strategy is the integration of artificial intelligence and Model Context Protocol (MCP) capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By bringing AI capabilities and agentic workflows into the platform, Guideline aims to create a foundation where audience intelligence can become more accessible and easier to use across different business functions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The SaaS architecture also allows new capabilities to be delivered centrally, meaning customers do not need to manage individual software deployments whenever the platform receives an update.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Steve Silvers, Chief Product Officer at Guideline, said the company\'s goal is to make trusted audience analysis accessible to the people responsible for programming, sales, planning, and investment decisions, rather than limiting it to users working from dedicated systems.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">John Yang, Lead Product Manager for Guideline\'s WRAP product suite, similarly described the move as a transition in how WRAP is delivered rather than a change to its underlying methodology. According to Guideline, customers retain the analysis and reports they already use while gaining browser-based access and a cloud platform capable of supporting future reports, datasets, and AI functionality.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Cloud-Based Direction for Audience Intelligence</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">WRAP Cloud represents Guideline\'s broader move toward delivering advertising intelligence through modern SaaS infrastructure. By combining established audience measurement methodologies with cloud accessibility, enterprise controls, AI capabilities, and MCP integrations, the platform is positioned to support organizations that need audience intelligence across multiple teams and markets.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The launch also establishes a foundation for Guideline to deliver additional datasets, reporting capabilities, and AI-enabled functionality through a centralized platform as the product evolves.</p>', 'Guideline Launches WRAP Cloud for TV & CTV Audience Analytics', 'Guideline launches WRAP Cloud, an AI-enabled SaaS platform delivering TV and CTV audience analytics, multi-market insights, and modern media intelligence.', 'Guideline WRAP Cloud, WRAP Cloud, audience analytics platform, TV audience analytics, CTV audience measurement, TV ratings, media intelligence, advertising intelligence, SaaS audience analytics, AI media analytics, Model Context Protocol, MCP, media planning, MarTech', '2022-06-08 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-08 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 4, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:46:08', '2026-10-01 18:58:45', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(933, 6, 1, 17, 'Blackbaud Launches Platform for Good™ to Unite Social Impact Operations with AI', 'blackbaud-launches-platform-for-goodtm-to-unite-social-impact-operations-with-ai', 'Blackbaud launches Platform for Good™, uniting data, AI and workflows to help social impact organizations turn intelligence into action.', '[\"Blackbaud\",\"Platform for Good\",\"Artificial Intelligence\",\"AI\",\"Nonprofit Technology\",\"Social Impact\",\"Fundraising\",\"MarTech\",\"Marketing Technology\",\"Raiser’s Edge NXT\",\"Lantern AI\",\"Agents for Good\",\"Data Intelligence\",\"Nonprofit Marketing\",\"Financial Technology\",\"Higher Education\"]', 'banner_image-1790880654138-548511927.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud, a leading provider of AI-powered technology for social impact organizations, has introduced <strong>Platform for Good™</strong>, a unified operating system designed to connect social impact workflows, data, artificial intelligence and organizational processes through a shared technology foundation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The announcement was made during <strong>bbcon</strong>, Blackbaud’s annual customer conference. The new platform is designed to help organizations identify opportunities, improve operational capacity and turn information into actionable insights while continuing to use the Blackbaud products and workflows they already rely on.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to Blackbaud, the Platform for Good evolution does not require existing customers to make changes to their current systems. Instead, the company is connecting its ecosystem beneath the surface through a common architecture designed to make data and intelligence more useful across applications.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">A Connected Architecture for Social Impact</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Platform for Good is structured around three major layers that work together:</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>The Data Core:</strong><br>\r\nThe Data Core combines Blackbaud\'s sector-specific data resources, including the Blackbaud Philanthropic Dataset, research and industry information from the Blackbaud Institute, and Blackbaud Giving Search. Together, these resources provide a foundation for generating intelligence tailored to the social impact sector.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>The Intelligence Layer:</strong><br>\r\nThe Intelligence Layer uses Blackbaud\'s Social Impact Signal Graph to connect signals across the social impact ecosystem. It is designed to identify relationships, patterns and contextual information, helping organizations transform large amounts of data into more meaningful intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>The Action Layer:</strong><br>\r\nThe Action Layer connects intelligence with execution. Through AI-powered assistants, agents and workflows, organizations can use insights to support operational activities while maintaining human oversight and control.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud says the result is intended to be more than an individual software product. Platform for Good is designed as a connected operating environment supported by responsible AI practices, risk management capabilities and an ecosystem of partners, donors and developers.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lantern™ Brings Specialized AI to Fundraising</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As part of the Platform for Good announcement, Blackbaud introduced <strong>Lantern™</strong>, a domain-specific language model designed specifically for fundraising intelligence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Developed through a strategic collaboration with Databricks, Lantern draws on Blackbaud\'s extensive experience with fundraising workflows, decisions and outcomes. Unlike general-purpose AI models that may be optimized around consumer or commercial behavior, Lantern is designed to understand patterns associated with charitable giving.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The technology will operate within Blackbaud\'s Intelligence Layer and is intended to provide fundraising-related intelligence directly within the environments where customers already work.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Expansion of Agents for Good™</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud is also expanding its <strong>Agents for Good™</strong> portfolio with additional AI-powered capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company announced enhancements to its <strong>Development Agent</strong>, which is designed to help fundraising teams identify and engage donors who may not currently be assigned to a specific team member.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud also introduced the <strong>Data Health Agent</strong>, which functions as a continuous data-management assistant. It is designed to identify data-quality issues and support high-volume cleanup through workflows that allow users to review and approve proposed actions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company also previewed future agent-based experiences focused on areas including finance and education.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Raiser’s Edge NXT Enters a New AI-Powered Phase</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud also unveiled the next stage of <strong>Blackbaud Raiser’s Edge NXT®</strong>, its fundraising CRM platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The updated platform has been rebuilt as a native cloud and AI-powered environment with connected data, automation and embedded intelligence. New capabilities are designed to identify promising prospects and relevant giving signals for fundraising teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud also highlighted <strong>Strategy Assistant</strong>, which provides benchmarks and AI-generated recommendations within users\' existing workflows. The company says these capabilities draw on more than $1 trillion in giving history tracked through Raiser’s Edge NXT.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud Marketing Connects Campaigns and Fundraising Data</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Another announcement was <strong>Blackbaud Marketing™</strong>, a marketing technology solution designed specifically for nonprofit marketing and fundraising teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Embedded within Raiser’s Edge NXT, the solution is intended to help organizations plan campaigns, identify target audiences, coordinate engagement across multiple channels and evaluate campaign performance using connected constituent and fundraising information.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This approach is designed to bring marketing activities closer to the fundraising data and intelligence already available within the Blackbaud ecosystem.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">New AI-Powered Financial Workflows</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud also announced several developments aimed at improving financial operations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company introduced its vision for an <strong>Accounts Payable Agent</strong> within Financial Edge NXT®. The planned capability is designed to review invoice information, prepare payment runs while considering available cash and provide recommendations for human approval.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud also announced its next-generation <strong>Payment Assistant™</strong>, being developed through a strategic partnership with BILL.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In addition, <strong>Deposit Connect™</strong> is designed to bring gift, fee and disbursement information together across Raiser’s Edge NXT, Blackbaud Integrated Payments and Financial Edge NXT, helping organizations connect financial information across their workflows.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Connected Campus for Higher Education</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Blackbaud also introduced the <strong>Higher Education Connected Campus</strong> solution, designed to connect different parts of the student lifecycle, including advancement, finance, awarding and payments.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The solution is being developed through Blackbaud\'s partnership with Student First and is intended to provide higher education institutions with a broader view of their operations and student-related financial activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By connecting these functions, institutions can gain greater visibility into how financial resources support student outcomes and make decisions across different areas of the organization.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a More Connected Social Impact Technology Ecosystem</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Together, these announcements represent Blackbaud\'s broader strategy to connect <strong>data, specialized AI and operational workflows</strong> across the technology products used by social impact organizations.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than requiring customers to move to a completely separate system, Blackbaud says organizations will be able to adopt new Platform for Good capabilities as they become available while continuing to operate within the products and workflows they already use.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The company\'s latest developments indicate a growing focus on embedding AI directly into fundraising, marketing, financial management, data stewardship and higher education workflows. Through Platform for Good, Blackbaud aims to create a more connected technology environment where sector-specific data and intelligence can support organizations from insight through execution.</p>', 'Blackbaud Launches Platform for Good™ for Social Impact AI', 'Blackbaud launches Platform for Good™, connecting AI, data and workflows to help social impact organizations improve fundraising and operations.', 'Blackbaud Platform for Good, Blackbaud AI, nonprofit AI, social impact technology, fundraising technology, nonprofit technology, Lantern AI, Agents for Good, Raiser’s Edge NXT, Blackbaud Marketing, nonprofit marketing, AI fundraising, social impact AI', '2022-06-09 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2022-06-09 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 5, NULL, '2026-10-01 18:50:54', '2026-10-01 18:58:39', 1, NULL),
(934, 1, 2, 14, 'De-Risking Genetic Testing: Detecting Fraudulent Billing and Claims', 'de-risking-genetic-testing-detecting-fraudulent-billing-and-claims', 'Learn how payors can detect genetic testing fraud using claims analytics, provider patterns, medical necessity checks, and AI-powered anomaly detection.', '[\"Genetic Testing\",\"Healthcare Fraud\",\"Medical Billing Fraud\",\"Claims Fraud\",\"Fraud Detection\",\"Healthcare Analytics\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Claims Analytics\",\"Healthcare Payors\",\"Fraud Waste and Abuse\",\"FWA Detection\",\"Genetic Testing Fraud\",\"Medical Necessity\",\"Provider Analytics\",\"Healthcare AI\"]', 'banner_image-1791229810877-121075871.png', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">De-Risking Genetic Testing: Detecting Fraudulent Billing and Claims</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Genetic testing has emerged as a powerful advancement in modern healthcare. By analyzing an individual\'s DNA, these tests can provide valuable insights into inherited conditions, disease risks, treatment options, and medication responses. When appropriately prescribed and clinically relevant, genetic testing can support more personalized and informed patient care.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, the rapid growth of the genetic testing market has also created new challenges for healthcare payors. As the volume, complexity, and cost of genetic testing claims continue to increase, insurers must identify inappropriate billing, unnecessary testing, and potentially fraudulent claim activity before it results in significant financial losses.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding Genetic Testing</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Genetic testing generally involves analyzing a biological sample such as blood, saliva, or other tissue to identify changes or variations in a person\'s genetic material.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Depending on the type of test, results may help healthcare professionals:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identify or confirm a suspected genetic condition</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Determine whether an individual carries a hereditary condition</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Assess the likelihood of developing certain diseases</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Support treatment and medication decisions</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Determine appropriate medication dosage</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provide information that may be relevant to family health planning</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">While these capabilities can provide substantial clinical value, not every genetic test ordered or billed provides meaningful medical benefit.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Genetic Testing Fraud Is a Growing Concern</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The increasing availability of broad genetic testing panels has created opportunities for questionable billing practices. Some fraudulent or inappropriate schemes may involve expensive panel-based tests that have limited clinical relevance to the patient\'s condition.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In other cases, claims may involve services that were not medically necessary, were not properly ordered, or may not have been performed at all.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For healthcare payors, these activities can result in unnecessary claim payments and make it more difficult to distinguish legitimate genetic testing from suspicious billing activity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Common Genetic Testing Fraud Red Flags</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying suspicious claims early can help payors prevent improper payments and prioritize investigations. Several indicators may warrant additional review.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">1. Questionable Provider-Patient Relationships</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Claims may require closer examination when the ordering physician appears to have no established relationship with the patient.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Telemedicine and virtual healthcare arrangements can add complexity to this assessment. Payors should evaluate whether the ordering provider had an appropriate clinical relationship with the patient and whether the test was reasonably supported by the patient\'s medical circumstances.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">2. High-Volume Billing Patterns</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A sudden increase in genetic testing claims from an individual provider, laboratory, or connected provider network can be an important indicator.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Data analytics can help identify providers whose claim volumes, testing frequency, or billing amounts significantly exceed comparable providers.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">3. Medically Unnecessary Testing</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every genetic test is appropriate for every patient. Claims should be evaluated against available clinical, diagnosis, procedure, and patient-level information to identify testing patterns that may not align with medical necessity requirements.</p><h4 style=\"text-align: justify;\">4. Tests That May Not Have Been Performed</h4><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Claims involving services that cannot be supported by appropriate documentation or evidence of service delivery may represent another significant risk area.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cross-referencing claims data with provider, patient, laboratory, and service information can help identify inconsistencies that deserve further investigation.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Genetic Testing Code Categories to Monitor</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Genetic testing may appear across multiple CPT code categories. Depending on the testing methodology and service provided, relevant areas may include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">81105–81479: Molecular Pathology Procedures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">81410–81479: Genomic Sequencing Procedures and Other Molecular Multianalyte Assays</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">81490–81599: Multianalyte Assays with Algorithmic Analyses</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">0002M–0020M: Multianalyte Assays</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">0001U–0574U: Proprietary Laboratory Analyses</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Particular attention may be warranted for Tier 2 molecular pathology codes, including <strong>81403–81408</strong>, as well as the unlisted molecular pathology code <strong>81479</strong>, depending on the billing context.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payors may also benefit from reviewing panel testing codes such as <strong>81445–81456</strong> and analyzing whether services that should be reported separately are being appropriately billed rather than improperly unbundled.</p><blockquote><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Note:</strong> CPT code applicability and billing requirements can change over time. Payors should validate current coding guidance and applicable reimbursement policies before using specific codes for automated fraud rules.</p></blockquote><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Using Data Analytics to Detect Suspicious Claims</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Traditional manual claim reviews may not be sufficient when payors are dealing with large volumes of genetic testing claims.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advanced analytics can help identify patterns that may otherwise remain difficult to detect. For example, analytical models can compare:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provider claim volume</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Testing frequency</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Average reimbursement</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Patient-provider relationships</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Procedure combinations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Geographic patterns</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provider peer groups</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Changes in billing behavior</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unusual increases in testing activity</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potentially duplicated or overlapping services</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By combining these signals, payors can prioritize high-risk claims and providers for additional review.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How AI Can Strengthen Genetic Testing Fraud Detection</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Artificial intelligence and machine learning can provide an additional layer of protection by continuously analyzing claims data and identifying unusual behavior.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">AI-driven models can detect sudden billing spikes, unusual provider behavior, and patterns that differ significantly from historical or peer-group activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, an analytics platform can help identify:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Abnormal increases in genetic testing claims</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Providers billing significantly above their peer group</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unexpected combinations of procedures</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">New provider billing patterns</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Unusual patient-provider relationships</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">High-cost testing clusters</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potentially inappropriate utilization patterns</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These insights allow healthcare payors to move beyond reactive claim review and develop a more proactive approach to fraud, waste, and abuse detection.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">HCFSPlatform™ for Genetic Testing Claims Monitoring</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>HCFSPlatform™</strong> can support healthcare payors in identifying suspicious billing activity across genetic testing and other high-risk healthcare service areas.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Its analytics capabilities can help identify anomalous billing patterns across areas such as laboratory services, durable medical equipment (DME), telehealth, and other healthcare categories.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For genetic testing, payors can use analytical insights such as:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Peer Comparison Alerts to identify providers whose billing behavior differs significantly from comparable providers.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Individual Tier Alerts to highlight unusual provider activity based on defined billing thresholds.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Billing Spike Detection to identify sudden changes in claim volume or reimbursement.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Provider Pattern Analysis to detect emerging billing behavior that may require investigation.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Anomaly Detection to prioritize unusual claim activity for further review.</p></li></ul><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Building a Proactive Fraud Detection Strategy</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Genetic testing fraud detection should not rely on a single rule or indicator. A stronger strategy combines claims data, provider intelligence, utilization patterns, peer comparisons, and advanced analytics.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare payors can strengthen their approach by continuously monitoring provider behavior, reviewing unusual testing patterns, validating medical necessity, and using data-driven alerts to prioritize potentially problematic claims.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As genetic testing continues to expand, organizations that combine clinical oversight with advanced analytics will be better positioned to reduce improper payments while supporting appropriate access to legitimate genetic testing.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Conclusion</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Genetic testing can play an important role in personalized healthcare, but its growing adoption also creates new challenges for healthcare payors.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Detecting suspicious billing requires more than simply reviewing individual claims. By analyzing provider behavior, testing volumes, procedure combinations, patient-provider relationships, and peer-level billing patterns, payors can identify potential risks earlier.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advanced analytics and AI-powered anomaly detection can help transform genetic testing fraud management from a reactive process into a proactive strategy-helping payors reduce financial exposure, prioritize investigations, and protect the integrity of healthcare claims.</p>', 'Genetic Testing Fraud Detection | Healthcare Claims Analytics', 'Learn how healthcare payors can detect genetic testing fraud using claims analytics, AI anomaly detection, provider comparisons, and billing pattern analysis.', 'genetic testing fraud, genetic testing billing fraud, healthcare fraud detection, medical billing fraud, healthcare claims analytics, claims fraud detection, healthcare AI, AI fraud detection, genetic testing claims, healthcare payors, fraud waste abuse, FWA detection, provider analytics, medical necessity, healthcare data analytics', '2026-10-06 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2026-10-06 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 7, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:50:22', '2026-10-05 20:09:21', 1, NULL),
(935, 1, 2, 14, 'RPM & Revenue: Understanding the Remote Patient Monitoring Landscape', 'rpm-and-revenue-understanding-the-remote-patient-monitoring-landscape', 'Remote patient monitoring is transforming healthcare while creating new opportunities to identify billing risks and improve payment integrity.', '[\"Remote Patient Monitoring\",\"RPM\",\"Healthcare\",\"Healthcare Analytics\",\"Medicare\",\"Medical Billing\",\"Payment Integrity\",\"Fraud Detection\",\"AI in Healthcare\",\"Healthcare Compliance\"]', 'banner_image-1791229953901-345770523.png', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">RPM &amp; Revenue: Understanding the Remote Patient Monitoring Landscape</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Remote patient monitoring (RPM) has become an important part of modern healthcare delivery. By using connected digital devices, healthcare providers can collect and review patient health information without requiring patients to visit a medical facility for every routine measurement.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">RPM can support the management of both acute and chronic conditions by giving providers access to ongoing health data. Depending on the program, information such as blood pressure, blood glucose, oxygen saturation, weight, and other health indicators can be captured remotely and shared with care teams.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For patients, this approach can reduce unnecessary office visits and make ongoing monitoring more convenient. For providers and payers, however, the increasing volume of RPM activity also creates a need for stronger oversight of billing, utilization, and service patterns.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">RPM: A Rapidly Growing Healthcare Service</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The use of remote patient monitoring has expanded significantly over the past several years. Between 2019 and 2022, the number of Medicare beneficiaries receiving RPM services increased approximately tenfold, while RPM billing increased about twentyfold.[1]</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The rapid growth has also attracted increased regulatory attention. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General (HHS-OIG) released a consumer alert addressing remote patient monitoring and potential concerns surrounding its use and billing.[2]</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">RPM activity continued to develop through 2024, and the service remains an area with considerable potential for future growth. HHS-OIG also released an August 2025 Data Snapshot examining Medicare billing for remote patient monitoring, providing additional insight into the scale and development of RPM services.[3]</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As RPM becomes more common, healthcare organizations and payers need effective methods for distinguishing legitimate utilization from potentially inappropriate billing.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What Should Payers Watch For?</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The growth of RPM can create opportunities for improper billing when services are not appropriately documented, reported, or coordinated.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Understanding the different forms of RPM is an important first step. Remote monitoring generally includes two broad areas:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Remote Physiologic Monitoring: This involves collecting physiological information such as blood pressure, blood glucose, oxygen saturation, oxygen levels, and weight.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Remote Therapeutic Monitoring: This generally involves non-physiological information, which may include patient-reported data associated with therapeutic treatment or management.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">RPM billing also involves different procedure codes representing components of the monitoring service. These may include activities associated with patient education and device setup, device supply, and treatment management.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Because multiple services and providers can potentially be involved, payers need to examine utilization patterns rather than relying only on individual claims.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying Potential RPM Billing Risks</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">RPM can generate substantial amounts of claims data, making automated analysis particularly useful for identifying unusual activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential warning signs may include sudden increases in RPM billing, unusually high service volumes, overlapping services, multiple providers billing for the same patient, or utilization patterns that differ significantly from comparable providers.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A data-driven monitoring strategy can help payers identify these patterns earlier and prioritize claims or providers that require additional review.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some useful analytical approaches include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitoring sudden spikes in RPM utilization.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying unusual billing volumes by provider.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Detecting potentially overlapping services.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Comparing provider activity against peer or specialty benchmarks.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identifying multiple providers billing RPM services for the same patient.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reviewing outlier utilization patterns.</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monitoring changes in billing behavior over time.</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These methods can help transform large volumes of claims information into actionable intelligence for payment integrity teams.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How HCFS Can Help</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Addressing potential improper billing in RPM requires a combination of analytics, monitoring, and targeted investigation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The HCFSPlatform™ uses Artificial Intelligence (AI) and advanced analytics to help identify unusual billing activity and emerging utilization patterns. Its analytical capabilities can detect spikes, anomalies, targeted concerns, and outlier behavior that may warrant additional investigation.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Examples of RPM-focused alerts include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">[2432-20] – Spike in Remote Patient Monitoring (RPM)</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">[2566-01] – Potentially Inappropriate RPM Treatment Management Services Billed With E/M Visits</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">[2568-01] – Multiple Physicians Billing RPM Services for the Same Patient</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">[2567-20] – Outlier Remote Physiologic Monitoring Treatment Management Add-On Code Utilization</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These types of alerts can help payment integrity teams focus their attention on billing patterns that may require further review instead of manually examining every claim in the same manner.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">HCFS continues to expand its analytical capabilities and introduce additional alerts to help organizations identify evolving billing risks.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Moving Toward Smarter RPM Oversight</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Remote patient monitoring can provide meaningful benefits to patients and healthcare providers, particularly when continuous data is important for managing chronic or acute conditions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At the same time, rapid growth in RPM makes effective oversight increasingly important. Payers can benefit from combining claims data, advanced analytics, artificial intelligence, targeted analysis, and investigative workflows to identify potential areas of concern.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The goal is not simply to identify unusual claims. A stronger approach is to understand utilization patterns, detect emerging risks, prioritize investigations, and support informed payment integrity decisions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For organizations looking to strengthen their RPM oversight capabilities, an integrated analytics platform can provide the tools needed to monitor this rapidly developing area of healthcare.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">About HCFS</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">HCFS is focused on providing an efficient, user-friendly platform that brings Artificial Intelligence, pre-pay and post-pay analytics, querying, case management, and shared analytics together in one integrated solution.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The HCFS ecosystem also supports solutions such as AUDITPLUS for auditing, SVRS, record retrieval, and related payment integrity activities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Organizations interested in learning more about HCFSPlatform™, HCFS services, data mining, record reviews, or available auditing solutions can contact the HCFS team for additional information or a product demonstration.</p><p></p>', 'RPM & Revenue: Remote Patient Monitoring & Billing Risks', 'Explore remote patient monitoring, RPM billing risks, healthcare analytics, and AI-driven strategies for identifying unusual utilization patterns.', 'remote patient monitoring, RPM, RPM billing, remote patient monitoring billing, healthcare analytics, Medicare RPM, healthcare fraud, payment integrity, medical billing, healthcare AI, RPM compliance, remote physiologic monitoring, remote therapeutic monitoring, claims analytics', '2026-10-06 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2026-10-06 00:00:00', 'published', 0, 0, 5, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:52:34', '2026-10-05 20:09:22', 1, NULL),
(936, 6, 2, 14, 'Telehealth Billing Patterns: What SIU Teams Should Watch', 'telehealth-billing-patterns-what-siu-teams-should-watch', 'Discover telehealth billing patterns SIU teams should monitor across payers, codes, modifiers, and service locations to identify potential risks.', '[\"Telehealth\",\"Healthcare Fraud\",\"SIU\",\"Medical Billing\",\"Claims Analytics\",\"Healthcare Compliance\",\"Coding Errors\",\"Payer Analytics\",\"Medicare\",\"Medicaid\",\"Fraud Detection\"]', 'banner_image-1791230201121-229880831.jpg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Telehealth Billing Patterns: What SIU Teams Should Watch</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Telehealth billing continues to present challenges for healthcare payers as requirements can differ across procedure codes, modifiers, and places of service. Medicare, Medicaid, and commercial health plans may each have their own billing expectations, making a standardized approach across all payer lines potentially problematic.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For Special Investigations Unit (SIU) teams, these differences can provide valuable signals for identifying billing activity that may require education, clarification, or additional investigation.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">What SIU Teams Should Look For</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A useful starting point is identifying providers, facilities, or organizations whose telehealth billing activity appears inconsistent with applicable payer requirements.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Potential areas for review may include:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Repeated use of identical coding practices across different payer types</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Procedure and modifier combinations that do not appear consistent with the service provided</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recurring place-of-service discrepancies</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Telehealth claims containing unusual or inconsistent modifier patterns</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Billing behaviors that remain unchanged despite differences in payer policies</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Repeated use of legacy or outdated coding practices</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Not every inconsistency represents fraud or intentional misconduct. Some patterns may result from outdated billing workflows, system configuration issues, or a lack of understanding of changing payer policies.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">However, when similar patterns continue over time or remain unchanged following provider education and outreach, they may warrant a deeper review.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Look Beyond Individual Coding Errors</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Telehealth requirements continue to evolve, which makes it important for SIU teams to evaluate billing behavior within a broader context.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rather than focusing exclusively on individual coding errors, investigators can benefit from looking for recurring patterns across providers, organizations, payer lines, locations, and time periods.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For example, a single questionable claim may have a straightforward operational explanation. A repeated pattern involving the same provider, coding combination, modifier, and place of service may provide a much stronger reason for additional analysis.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This broader approach can help SIU teams distinguish isolated billing issues from behaviors that may indicate systematic workflow problems or potentially inappropriate billing practices.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why Pattern Recognition Matters</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Telehealth claims can generate significant volumes of data, making manual review of every transaction inefficient. The challenge for SIU teams is determining which activity deserves closer attention.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pattern-based analysis can help identify:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Providers with unusually high telehealth billing activity</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recurring coding and modifier combinations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Payer-specific billing anomalies</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Changes in billing behavior over time</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Outlier providers or organizations</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Repeated patterns that continue after education or clarification</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Combining these signals with provider-level and claims-level information can provide investigators with a more complete view of potential risk.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">HCFSPlatform™ Can Help Identify Unusual Billing Behavior</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Effective telehealth reviews require more than awareness of coding and payer policies. SIU teams also need the ability to analyze claims activity, identify outliers, and prioritize cases that may have the greatest potential impact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>HCFSPlatform™</strong> combines AI-driven capabilities, targeted alerts, claims analysis, and investigative workflows to help payers identify unusual billing trends and focus their resources on higher-priority activity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">By bringing pattern recognition and analytics into the review process, payers can better identify recurring behaviors that may otherwise be difficult to detect through isolated claim reviews.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Interested in learning more about the analytics used to identify these types of billing patterns? Contact <strong>info@hcfraudshield.com</strong> to learn more about the HCFSPlatform™.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For questions or comments regarding this content, contact <strong>SIU@hcfraudshield.com</strong>.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Additional HCFS Solutions</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">HCFS is focused on providing healthcare organizations with an efficient, user-friendly platform designed to support fraud, waste, abuse, and payment-integrity initiatives.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The HCFSPlatform™ brings together capabilities including <strong>Artificial Intelligence, Pre-Pay, Post-Pay, Querying, Case Management, and Shared Analytics</strong> within an integrated platform.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">HCFS also offers solutions and services such as <strong>AUDITPLUS</strong> for auditing, <strong>SVRS</strong>, record retrieval, data mining, record reviews, and other specialized healthcare analytics and review services.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">To explore the platform and available solutions, contact the HCFS team to request a demonstration.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">References</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">[1] HHS Office of Inspector General – Compliance Guidance<br>https://oig.hhs.gov/compliance/ma-icpg/</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">[2] Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services – Fraud and Abuse<br>https://www.cms.gov/fraud</p>', 'Telehealth Billing Patterns SIU Teams Should Monitor', 'Learn how SIU teams can identify telehealth billing patterns, coding inconsistencies, payer risks, and recurring claims behavior requiring review.', 'telehealth billing, telehealth coding, SIU healthcare, healthcare fraud detection, medical billing fraud, claims analytics, payer fraud, telehealth compliance, healthcare SIU, billing inconsistencies', '2026-10-06 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2026-10-06 00:00:00', 'published', 3, 3, 4, NULL, '2026-10-05 19:56:41', '2026-10-07 13:43:17', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO `contents` (`id`, `user_id`, `content_type_id`, `category_id`, `title`, `slug`, `short_description`, `tags`, `banner_image`, `pdf_file`, `video_file`, `email_subject`, `email_template`, `case_study_headline`, `case_study_summary`, `custom_fields`, `webhook_url`, `builder_layout`, `builder_content_elements`, `builder_page_data`, `webhook_field_mapping`, `content`, `seo_meta_title`, `seo_meta_description`, `seo_meta_keywords`, `scheduled_publish_date`, `webinar_date`, `hosted_by`, `platform`, `webinar_type`, `join_link`, `published_date`, `status`, `view_count`, `views_count`, `reading_time`, `admin_comment`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `is_visible_on_site`, `redirect_url`) VALUES
(937, 6, 2, 16, 'How to Ace Your Clinical Interview: Expert Tips from HCSG Recruiters', 'how-to-ace-your-clinical-interview-expert-tips-from-hcsg-recruiters', 'Prepare for your clinical interview with expert recruiter tips on research, questions, communication, experience, follow-up, and professional confidence.', '[\"clinical interview\",\"healthcare careers\",\"clinical jobs\",\"interview preparation\",\"registered dietitian\",\"dietitian career\",\"healthcare recruitment\",\"job interview tips\",\"clinical career\",\"healthcare professionals\",\"interview skills\",\"career development\",\"HCSG\",\"nutrition careers\",\"job search\"]', 'banner_image-1791230905939-444158757.png', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '[\"meta\",\"title\",\"banner\",\"content\",\"video\"]', NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h1 style=\"text-align: justify;\">How to Ace Your Clinical Interview: Expert Tips from HCSG Recruiters</h1><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A clinical interview is more than an evaluation of your qualifications. It is an opportunity to understand the organization, connect with the people you may work with, and demonstrate how your clinical knowledge can contribute to better patient and resident outcomes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Healthcare Services Group (HCSG) Clinical Recruiters Kate Boyle and Natalie Shires share practical recommendations to help clinical professionals prepare for interviews, communicate their strengths, and approach the conversation with confidence.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research the Organization Before Your Interview</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Taking time to understand an organization before an interview can make a significant difference in how confidently you communicate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research the company\'s mission, services, workplace culture, and the responsibilities associated with the position. Look beyond the job description and learn about the type of environment in which you may be working.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For a clinical role, consider how the organization delivers care, supports its clinical teams, and measures success. This preparation can help you connect your own experience and professional goals to the position.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It also gives you valuable information that can help you develop thoughtful questions for the interviewer.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prepare Meaningful Questions</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An interview should be a two-way conversation. While the employer is evaluating your qualifications, you should also determine whether the position, team, and organization are right for you.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prepare several questions before the interview and adjust them based on the conversation. Strong questions can demonstrate curiosity, preparation, critical thinking, and genuine interest in the opportunity.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Questions You Could Ask Your Interviewer</h3><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What does the onboarding and mentorship process look like for a new Registered Dietitian?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What opportunities are available for continuing clinical education and professional development?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What would you consider a successful first 90 days in this position?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How do dietitians work with other members of the care team and facility leadership?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What are some of the biggest priorities for the clinical team right now?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">How does the organization support employees who want to grow into additional responsibilities?</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Thoughtful questions can also help you better understand expectations before making a career decision.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Stay Engaged Throughout the Conversation</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Confidence does not mean having a perfect answer to every question. It means remaining present, attentive, and comfortable communicating your experience.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Before your interview begins, take a few moments to relax and organize your thoughts. During the conversation, maintain appropriate eye contact, sit with good posture, and use natural facial expressions to show that you are engaged.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Listen carefully to each question before responding. If you need a moment to think, it is perfectly acceptable to pause briefly before providing your answer.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For virtual interviews, position your camera at eye level and look toward the camera while speaking. Avoid unnecessary movements such as repeatedly adjusting your device or looking away from the screen.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For an in-person interview, offer a professional greeting and handshake when appropriate.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most importantly, allow your personality and enthusiasm for clinical care to come through naturally.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Demonstrate Your Experience and Clinical Impact</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When discussing your professional background, avoid simply listing your previous responsibilities. Instead, explain how your work produced meaningful results.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Think about situations where you:</p><ul><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Identified a clinical or nutritional concern</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Developed an appropriate intervention</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Worked with patients, residents, families, or other healthcare professionals</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Solved a challenging problem</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Improved an outcome or care process</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Communicated important clinical information effectively</p></li></ul><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use specific examples whenever possible. A strong example gives the interviewer a better understanding of how you think, make decisions, and apply your clinical knowledge.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Are You a New Graduate?</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Limited professional experience does not mean you lack valuable examples.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical rotations, internships, academic projects, volunteer experiences, and coursework can all demonstrate your capabilities.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Think about a patient or resident situation from your clinical training and be prepared to explain:</p><ol><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What was the situation or concern?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What information did you assess?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What intervention or approach did you choose?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Why did you choose that approach?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What was the result?</p></li><li><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">What did you learn from the experience?</p></li></ol><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Use the STAR Method</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The **STAR method-Situation, Task, Action, and Result-**can help you organize responses to behavioral interview questions.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Instead of giving a broad answer, describe the situation, explain your responsibility, outline the actions you took, and finish by discussing the result.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This structure makes your response easier to follow while highlighting your clinical judgment and problem-solving abilities.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Follow Up After the Interview</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Your communication should not end when the interview does.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Send a personalized thank-you message to your recruiter or hiring manager within 24 hours. Thank them for their time and mention something specific you discussed during the interview.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">You can also briefly reinforce your interest in the position and connect your clinical experience or professional values to the opportunity.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A thoughtful follow-up demonstrates professionalism and keeps the conversation moving after the interview.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Additional Ways to Prepare</h2><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Maintain Your Professional Network</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Keep your LinkedIn profile current and stay connected with colleagues, classmates, mentors, and other healthcare professionals. Professional relationships can introduce you to opportunities you may not discover through traditional job searches.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Practice With a Mock Interview</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Interview preparation becomes easier with practice. Ask a friend, colleague, mentor, or family member to conduct a mock interview.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Practice answering common questions about your experience, strengths, clinical decision-making, teamwork, and career goals. Rehearsing your responses can help reduce nervousness and make your answers feel more natural.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Choose Appropriate Professional Attire</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Select professional clothing that is appropriate for a healthcare workplace and allows you to feel comfortable and confident.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Your appearance should support a professional first impression without becoming a distraction from the conversation.</p><h3 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Arrive Early</h3><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Give yourself enough time to reach the interview location or prepare for a virtual meeting.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For an in-person interview, arriving a few minutes early gives you time to settle in. For a virtual interview, test your camera, microphone, internet connection, and meeting link before the scheduled time.</p><h2 style=\"text-align: justify;\">Make Your Clinical Interview Count</h2><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A successful interview is an opportunity to communicate more than your qualifications. It allows you to demonstrate your approach to patient and resident care, explain how you solve problems, and show the value you can bring to a clinical team.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Research the organization, prepare thoughtful questions, communicate your experience with specific examples, and remain engaged throughout the conversation. Whether you are an experienced professional or a recent graduate, preparation can help you approach the interview with greater confidence.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">With the right preparation and a clear understanding of your own strengths, you can turn your clinical interview into an opportunity to showcase your expertise and take the next step in your career.</p>', 'Clinical Interview Tips for Healthcare Professionals | HCSG', 'Learn practical clinical interview tips from HCSG recruiters covering preparation, questions, communication, experience, STAR responses, and follow-up.', 'clinical interview tips,healthcare interview tips,clinical job interview,registered dietitian interview,dietitian interview tips,healthcare careers,clinical recruitment,interview preparation,healthcare professionals,clinical career development,HCSG recruiters', '2026-10-06 00:00:00', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'live', NULL, '2026-01-28 00:00:00', 'published', 2, 1, 6, NULL, '2026-10-05 20:08:26', '2026-10-06 15:36:21', 1, NULL),
